// AUTO-GENERATED deep explanations for the revamp engine (concept from scratch + why every wrong option is wrong).
// Source: 15 parallel writer agents over met-mock-2 (180 Qs). Keyed by mock id -> ordered array (index matches the runtime question order in data-all.js; a stem guard in explainHTML falls back if order ever drifts).
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"met-mock-2":[{"q":"The resting membrane potential of a typical neuron is maintained primarily by:","concept":"The resting membrane potential (RMP) is the steady voltage across the membrane of a neuron that is not firing, about -70 mV (inside negative relative to outside). Two things create it. First, ion gradients: K+ is concentrated inside the cell, while Na+ and Cl- are concentrated outside. Second, selective permeability: at rest many K+ leak channels are open but few Na+ channels are, so the membrane is far more permeable to K+. K+ diffuses out down its gradient, leaving unbalanced negative charge inside, until the electrical pull back in balances the chemical push out. The voltage at which this balance occurs for a single ion is its equilibrium potential, given by the Nernst equation: about -90 mV for K+ and about +60 mV for Na+. The Goldman-Hodgkin-Katz equation weights each ion by its permeability, which is why the RMP sits close to, but slightly less negative than, the K+ equilibrium potential: a small Na+ leak pulls it up. Leaks would gradually run the gradients down, so the Na+/K+ ATPase, discovered by Jens Christian Skou (1957), uses ATP to pump 3 Na+ out and 2 K+ in, restoring them. Alan Hodgkin and Andrew Huxley (1952) worked out these ionic mechanisms in the squid giant axon. Exam variants contrast the RMP with the action potential (Na+ influx after threshold at about -55 mV, then K+ efflux) and ask what raising extracellular K+ does (it depolarises the cell). Option b is right because high resting K+ permeability sets the negative voltage and the Na+/K+ pump preserves the gradients that make it possible.","traps":{"a":"Equal permeability to Na+ and K+ would let the two ions pull with equal force in opposite directions, so the membrane would settle near 0 mV, roughly midway between their equilibrium potentials, instead of -70 mV. During the rising phase of an action potential the balance tips the other way: Na+ permeability dominates and the voltage swings positive. It tempts because both ions are named in every account of the resting potential, so 'both matter equally' sounds reasonable.","c":"Chloride in many neurons is distributed largely passively, sitting near its own equilibrium potential, so it shapes inhibition (through GABA-A channels) more than it sets the resting voltage. In mature neurons the transporter KCC2 keeps internal Cl- low, but no chloride pump alone produces the RMP, and the word 'only' makes the option extreme. It tempts because Cl- is a negative ion and the inside of the cell is negative.","d":"Voltage-gated Ca2+ channels are mostly closed at rest. They open when an action potential reaches the axon terminal, and the Ca2+ entry triggers vesicle release of transmitter. A continuous Ca2+ influx would carry positive charge inward and depolarise the cell, the opposite of holding it at -70 mV; internal Ca2+ is normally kept extremely low. It tempts because Ca2+ is a famous signalling ion in neurons."}},{"q":"Saltatory conduction depends most directly on:","concept":"Saltatory conduction (Latin saltare, to leap) is the way an action potential travels along a myelinated axon. Myelin is a fatty sheath wrapped around the axon in segments by glial cells: oligodendrocytes in the central nervous system (one cell myelinates segments of many axons) and Schwann cells in the peripheral nervous system (one cell per segment of one axon). Between segments are short bare gaps, the nodes of Ranvier, named after Louis-Antoine Ranvier. Voltage-gated Na+ channels are packed densely at the nodes and are sparse under the myelin. Myelin insulates the internode and lowers its capacitance, so current from one node spreads quickly and passively inside the axon to the next node, where the dense Na+ channels regenerate a full action potential. The impulse therefore seems to jump from node to node. Ichiji Tasaki (1939) and Andrew Huxley with Robert Stämpfli (1949) showed this experimentally. Two benefits follow: speed (large myelinated fibres conduct at up to about 120 m/s, unmyelinated C fibres at under 2 m/s) and energy saving, because ions cross only at the nodes so the Na+/K+ pump does less work. Conduction velocity also rises with axon diameter, which is how the unmyelinated squid giant axon gets its speed. Demyelinating diseases show why this matters: multiple sclerosis strips CNS myelin and Guillain-Barré syndrome attacks peripheral myelin, slowing or blocking conduction. In unmyelinated axons the impulse travels by continuous conduction, regenerating at every point. Option b is right because saltatory conduction depends on Na+ channels concentrated at the nodes of Ranvier between myelinated segments.","traps":{"a":"A continuous spread of Na+ channels along the whole axon is the arrangement in unmyelinated axons, where the action potential is regenerated at every adjacent patch of membrane (continuous conduction). This is slower and costs more energy. Saltatory conduction needs the opposite: channels clustered at the nodes and sparse under the myelin. It tempts because every action potential needs Na+ channels, so 'more channels everywhere' sounds like faster conduction.","c":"This option reverses the two myelinating cells. Oligodendrocytes myelinate axons in the central nervous system (brain and spinal cord) and Schwann cells myelinate axons in the peripheral nervous system. Schwann cells also guide regrowth after nerve injury, one reason peripheral nerves repair better than central ones. Even the correct pairing would describe myelin makers, not the nodal channels that conduction depends on. It tempts because both cells are genuine myelin makers.","d":"Dendrites mainly carry graded postsynaptic potentials that spread passively and fade with distance towards the cell body. A normal action potential starts at the axon hillock (initial segment) and runs one way to the terminals, because the Na+ channels just behind it are refractory. Some dendrites do show back-propagating spikes, but these have nothing to do with myelin or nodes. It tempts by pairing a true word, propagation, with the wrong compartment."}},{"q":"The mesolimbic dopamine pathway projects primarily from the ventral tegmental area to which structure, and is most implicated in:","concept":"Dopamine neurons form four classic pathways, and exams test each by origin, target and function. The mesolimbic pathway starts in the ventral tegmental area (VTA) of the midbrain and projects to the nucleus accumbens in the ventral striatum, with branches to the amygdala and hippocampus. It is the core of the reward system. Electrical self-stimulation of reward sites, first reported by James Olds and Peter Milner (1954), depends heavily on this circuit, and addictive drugs such as cocaine, amphetamine and nicotine raise dopamine in the nucleus accumbens. Wolfram Schultz and colleagues (1997) showed that VTA dopamine neurons signal reward prediction error (more firing for unexpected reward). In schizophrenia, the dopamine hypothesis, built partly on Arvid Carlsson's work on how antipsychotics act on dopamine, holds that overactive mesolimbic transmission produces positive symptoms (hallucinations, delusions); D2 blockade here is the therapeutic effect of antipsychotics. The neighbours: the mesocortical pathway (VTA to prefrontal cortex), underactive in schizophrenia and linked to negative and cognitive symptoms; the nigrostriatal pathway (substantia nigra pars compacta to caudate and putamen), which controls movement, degenerates in Parkinson's disease and causes extrapyramidal side effects when blocked; and the tuberoinfundibular pathway (arcuate nucleus of the hypothalamus to the median eminence), where dopamine inhibits prolactin, so blockade causes hyperprolactinaemia. A memory aid: limbic for reward, cortical for cognition, striatal for movement, infundibular for prolactin. Option d is right because the mesolimbic pathway runs from the VTA to the nucleus accumbens and is the pathway most implicated in reward and the positive symptoms of schizophrenia.","traps":{"a":"The caudate nucleus and putamen (together the dorsal striatum) are the target of the nigrostriatal pathway, which begins in the substantia nigra pars compacta and controls voluntary movement; its loss causes Parkinson's disease. It tempts because the nucleus accumbens is also part of the striatum (the ventral part), so 'striatum' sounds right, but the VTA projects ventrally and the function is reward, not movement.","b":"The median eminence and prolactin regulation describe the tuberoinfundibular pathway. Dopamine released by arcuate nucleus neurons into the pituitary portal blood acts as the prolactin-inhibiting factor, so D2 blockade here raises prolactin, causing galactorrhoea, amenorrhoea and sexual dysfunction. It tempts because it is a genuine dopamine pathway with a well-known antipsychotic side effect, but it starts in the hypothalamus, not the VTA.","c":"The substantia nigra is the origin of the nigrostriatal pathway, not a target of the VTA, and Parkinsonian rigidity results from loss of nigral dopamine neurons or from D2 blockade in the dorsal striatum. It tempts because the substantia nigra lies right beside the VTA in the midbrain and both are dopamine cell groups, so origin, target and function are easily mixed up across the two pathways."}},{"q":"Which structure is NOT a component of the basal ganglia?","concept":"The basal ganglia are a set of interconnected nuclei deep in the forebrain and midbrain that help select and start wanted movements, suppress unwanted ones, and support habit learning. The standard list: the striatum (caudate nucleus and putamen, with the nucleus accumbens as the ventral striatum), the globus pallidus (external and internal segments), the subthalamic nucleus, and the substantia nigra (pars compacta and pars reticulata). Useful terms: the lentiform nucleus is the putamen plus globus pallidus; the corpus striatum is the caudate plus lentiform nucleus. In the model set out by Roger Albin and colleagues (1989) and Mahlon DeLong (1990), the direct pathway (striatum to internal globus pallidus) releases the thalamus and facilitates movement, while the indirect pathway (through the external globus pallidus and subthalamic nucleus) inhibits it. Dopamine from the substantia nigra excites the direct pathway through D1 receptors and inhibits the indirect pathway through D2 receptors. Disorders map onto these nuclei: Parkinson's disease (loss of nigral dopamine), Huntington's disease (caudate degeneration, chorea), hemiballismus (subthalamic nucleus lesion) and Wilson's disease (lentiform damage). Deep brain stimulation for Parkinson's targets the subthalamic nucleus or internal globus pallidus. The red nucleus is a midbrain structure that gives rise to the rubrospinal tract and relays cerebellar output (dentate nucleus to red nucleus and thalamus); damage contributes to tremor, as in Benedikt syndrome. It is a motor structure, but not part of the basal ganglia. Option c is right because the red nucleus belongs to the midbrain and cerebellar motor circuitry, not to the basal ganglia.","traps":{"a":"The caudate nucleus is a C-shaped nucleus curving along the lateral ventricle. With the putamen it forms the striatum, the main input station of the basal ganglia, so it is a true member. It degenerates early in Huntington's disease, which is why the lateral ventricles look enlarged on brain scans. It tempts students who remember only the lentiform nucleus or who confuse the caudate with the nearby thalamus.","b":"The globus pallidus has an external segment (part of the indirect pathway) and an internal segment, which with the substantia nigra pars reticulata is the main output nucleus of the basal ganglia, inhibiting the thalamus. It is a core member and a deep brain stimulation target. It tempts because 'pallidus' sounds unfamiliar and it is sometimes taught under the separate label of lentiform nucleus.","d":"The subthalamic nucleus, lying below the thalamus, is the excitatory hub of the indirect pathway, driving the internal globus pallidus to suppress movement. A lesion here, often a small stroke, causes hemiballismus (violent flinging of the opposite limbs), and it is a common deep brain stimulation target in Parkinson's disease. It tempts because its name sounds thalamic rather than basal ganglia."}},{"q":"Wernicke's area is typically located in the:","concept":"Classical aphasia research rests on two discoveries. Paul Broca (1861) studied a patient nicknamed 'Tan' who understood speech but could barely speak, and found a lesion in the left inferior frontal gyrus (Broca's area, Brodmann areas 44 and 45). Carl Wernicke (1874) described patients with the reverse pattern and located the damage in the posterior part of the left superior temporal gyrus, next to the primary auditory cortex (posterior Brodmann area 22). Language is lateralised to the dominant hemisphere, which is the left in over 90 percent of right-handers and in most left-handers, and can be checked before surgery with the Wada test. Wernicke's aphasia is fluent but empty: normal rate and melody, many paraphasias and neologisms ('word salad'), poor comprehension, poor repetition, and often little awareness of the problem. Broca's aphasia is the opposite: non-fluent, effortful, telegraphic speech with relatively good comprehension. Norman Geschwind (1965) revived and extended this scheme into the Wernicke-Geschwind model, in which the arcuate fasciculus links the two areas; damage to it gives conduction aphasia, with fluent speech and good comprehension but very poor repetition. Global aphasia follows large lesions involving both areas, and transcortical aphasias spare repetition. A mnemonic: Broca is broken speech, Wernicke is wordy nonsense. The right hemisphere contributes to prosody and spatial attention instead. Option b is right because Wernicke's area lies in the posterior superior temporal gyrus of the language-dominant hemisphere.","traps":{"a":"The inferior frontal gyrus of the dominant hemisphere is Broca's area (Brodmann 44 and 45), described by Paul Broca (1861). Damage causes Broca's aphasia: non-fluent, effortful, telegraphic speech with fairly preserved comprehension, often with right-sided weakness because the motor cortex is nearby. It tempts because the two classic language areas are always taught together and the eponyms are easy to swap under exam pressure.","c":"The right parietal lobe is in the non-dominant hemisphere for most people. It specialises in spatial attention, and damage typically causes left hemispatial neglect and constructional or dressing apraxia, not a comprehension aphasia. Wernicke's area lies in the temporal lobe of the dominant (usually left) hemisphere. It tempts because Wernicke's area borders the parietal lobe and some definitions extend it into the left angular and supramarginal gyri.","d":"The anterior cingulate cortex, on the medial surface of the frontal lobe, is involved in conflict and error monitoring (as in the Stroop task), motivation and the emotional side of pain. Bilateral damage can cause akinetic mutism, a lack of drive to move or speak, which is very different from fluent speech with poor comprehension. It tempts because mutism sounds like a language disorder, but it is a disorder of initiation."}},{"q":"The HPA axis end-product cortisol exerts negative feedback primarily at the:","concept":"The hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis is the hormonal arm of the stress response. Hans Selye (1936) first described the body's general response to stressors, later called the general adaptation syndrome (alarm, resistance, exhaustion). The cascade has three steps. The paraventricular nucleus of the hypothalamus releases corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH) into the portal blood; CRH makes the anterior pituitary release adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) into the general circulation; ACTH makes the zona fasciculata of the adrenal cortex release cortisol. Cortisol raises blood glucose, suppresses immune and inflammatory responses, and prepares the body for sustained demand. To stop the system running away, cortisol acts on glucocorticoid receptors to inhibit its own drivers: it suppresses CRH release at the hypothalamus and ACTH release at the anterior pituitary. This is classic negative feedback, like a thermostat. The hippocampus, rich in cortisol receptors, adds a higher-level brake; the glucocorticoid cascade hypothesis of Robert Sapolsky and colleagues (1986) proposed that prolonged cortisol damages it, weakening the brake. The dexamethasone suppression test checks this feedback: a synthetic steroid should suppress ACTH and cortisol. Non-suppression was reported in melancholic depression by Bernard Carroll and colleagues (1981), and the test is also used to screen for Cushing's syndrome. Distinguish the faster sympathetic-adrenal-medullary (SAM) system, in which sympathetic nerves release adrenaline and noradrenaline from the adrenal medulla within seconds, while the HPA response takes minutes. Option a is right because cortisol's negative feedback acts mainly at the hypothalamus (on CRH) and the anterior pituitary (on ACTH).","traps":{"b":"The pineal gland secretes melatonin under the control of the suprachiasmatic nucleus and helps time sleep. The posterior pituitary releases oxytocin and vasopressin (ADH), which are made in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei and carried down axons. Neither is the main site of cortisol feedback. It tempts because the pituitary is part of the HPA axis, but it is the anterior lobe, not the posterior, that makes ACTH.","c":"The adrenal medulla is the inner part of the adrenal gland. It works like a modified sympathetic ganglion, releasing adrenaline and noradrenaline in the fast sympathetic-adrenal-medullary response. Cortisol comes from the outer adrenal cortex, and feedback works upstream on the brain and pituitary, not on the gland itself. The word 'only' adds a second error. It tempts because the adrenal gland is the obvious end point of the axis.","d":"The dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus in the medulla sends parasympathetic fibres to the heart, lungs and gut, slowing the heart and aiding digestion. It belongs to the autonomic nervous system, not the endocrine HPA loop, and cortisol does not use it as a feedback site. It tempts because stress questions often mix autonomic and endocrine responses, and the vagus is linked with calming the body after stress."}},{"q":"NMDA receptors are critical for long-term potentiation because they:","concept":"Long-term potentiation (LTP) is a lasting increase in synaptic strength after brief high-frequency stimulation. Timothy Bliss and Terje Lømo (1973) first described it in the hippocampus of anaesthetised rabbits, and it became the main cellular model of learning, fitting the idea of Donald O. Hebb (1949) that cells that fire together strengthen their connections. Glutamate synapses have two key ionotropic receptors. AMPA receptors open whenever glutamate binds and carry most ordinary fast transmission. NMDA receptors are different: at resting potential their channel is plugged by a magnesium ion (Mg2+), so glutamate binding alone lets little current through. Only when the postsynaptic membrane is already depolarised, usually by strong AMPA activity, is the Mg2+ expelled, and the channel then admits Ca2+ as well as Na+. The NMDA receptor is therefore a coincidence detector: it needs presynaptic activity (glutamate release) and postsynaptic activity (depolarisation) at the same time, which is Hebb's rule in molecular form. It also needs a co-agonist, glycine or D-serine. The Ca2+ influx activates enzymes such as CaMKII, which add more AMPA receptors to the membrane, so the synapse responds more strongly afterwards. Graham Collingridge and colleagues (1983) showed that blocking NMDA receptors with AP5 prevents LTP induction, and Richard Morris and colleagues (1986) showed that AP5 in the hippocampus impairs spatial learning in the water maze. The reverse process, long-term depression, weakens synapses. Option a is right because the Mg2+ block means NMDA receptors open only with both glutamate binding and depolarisation, which makes them the trigger for LTP.","traps":{"b":"Glycine (or D-serine) is a required co-agonist at the NMDA receptor: it binds a separate site and must be present for the channel to open. But glycine alone cannot open it; glutamate binding and depolarisation to remove the Mg2+ block are still needed. On its own, glycine is better known as the main inhibitory transmitter of the spinal cord. It tempts because it states a real fact about NMDA receptors in an exaggerated form.","c":"NMDA receptors are excitatory: they are glutamate-gated channels that let Na+ and Ca2+ in and depolarise the cell. Fast inhibition is carried by GABA-A receptors in the brain and glycine receptors in the spinal cord, which let Cl- in. Too much NMDA activity is actually harmful, causing excitotoxicity in stroke. It tempts because the Mg2+ block sounds like inhibition, and 'exclusively' is the kind of absolute word that should raise suspicion.","d":"Presynaptic vesicle fusion is triggered when an action potential opens voltage-gated Ca2+ channels in the axon terminal; Ca2+ binds synaptotagmin and SNARE proteins fuse the vesicle with the membrane. The NMDA receptor that matters for LTP sits on the postsynaptic membrane and responds to transmitter already released. It tempts because both events involve Ca2+ entry and glutamate, so the two sides of the synapse are easily confused."}},{"q":"Damage to the lateral hypothalamus typically produces:","concept":"Feeding is controlled by several hypothalamic regions, and early lesion studies in rats produced the dual-centre model. Albert Hetherington and Stephen Ranson (1940) showed that lesions of the ventromedial hypothalamus (VMH) cause hyperphagia and obesity, so the VMH was called the satiety centre. The Indian physiologist Bal K. Anand and John Brobeck (1951) showed that lesions of the lateral hypothalamus (LH) cause aphagia (refusal to eat) and adipsia (refusal to drink); animals could starve unless tube-fed, so the LH was called the feeding or hunger centre. Eliot Stellar (1954) combined these into a dual-centre theory of motivation. Stimulation gives the mirror image: LH stimulation makes sated animals eat, VMH stimulation stops hungry animals eating. Philip Teitelbaum and Alan Epstein (1962) found that LH-lesioned rats can slowly recover through stages, accepting palatable wet food before dry food. Part of the LH syndrome comes from damage to nigrostriatal dopamine fibres passing through the area. The modern view is a network rather than two centres: the arcuate nucleus senses leptin (from fat stores) and ghrelin (from the stomach), with NPY/AgRP neurons that drive eating and POMC neurons that suppress it; the LH contains orexin (hypocretin) and MCH neurons. Neighbouring nuclei for variant questions: the suprachiasmatic nucleus sets circadian rhythm, and the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei make vasopressin for water balance. Option d is right because destroying the lateral hypothalamus, the classic feeding centre, removes the drive to eat and produces aphagia and weight loss.","traps":{"a":"Hyperphagia and obesity follow lesions of the ventromedial hypothalamus, the classic satiety centre, as shown by Albert Hetherington and Stephen Ranson (1940). Such rats overeat, become very fat and also turn finicky and irritable. Damage to the paraventricular nucleus produces similar overeating. It tempts because it is the mirror image of the correct answer, and students often reverse which centre does what.","b":"Persistent thirst (polydipsia) belongs to water-balance systems: osmoreceptors near the third ventricle and angiotensin acting on the subfornical organ trigger thirst, and damage to the vasopressin-producing supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei or the posterior pituitary causes diabetes insipidus, with excess urine and compensatory thirst. Lateral hypothalamic lesions do the reverse, producing adipsia. It tempts because thirst is a hypothalamic drive just like hunger.","c":"Loss of circadian rhythm follows damage to the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN), the master clock above the optic chiasm, which receives light input from the retina and drives melatonin release from the pineal gland. Animals with SCN lesions still sleep and eat, but at scattered times across the day and night. It tempts because the SCN is another hypothalamic nucleus and examiners like to place hypothalamic functions side by side."}},{"q":"The hippocampal patient H.M. (Henry Molaison) demonstrated that bilateral medial temporal lobectomy primarily impairs:","concept":"Henry Molaison (1926 to 2008), known as H.M., had severe epilepsy. In 1953 the neurosurgeon William Beecher Scoville removed the medial temporal lobes on both sides, including much of the hippocampus, the amygdala and nearby entorhinal cortex. His seizures improved, but Scoville and Brenda Milner (1957) reported a profound, lasting anterograde amnesia: he could not form new conscious long-term memories, meeting the same people as strangers and rereading magazines as new. What he kept was just as important. His intelligence and personality were preserved. His immediate memory span was normal, so he could hold a number by rehearsing it until distracted. Milner showed he could learn mirror drawing over days, improving steadily while denying he had done the task before, which proved that procedural memory relies on other systems such as the basal ganglia and cerebellum. Old memories from childhood and early adulthood were largely intact, with retrograde loss limited mostly to the years before surgery. These findings established that the hippocampus and medial temporal lobe are needed to consolidate new declarative memories (episodic and semantic memory, as distinguished by Endel Tulving (1972)), that working memory and long-term memory are separate systems, and that remote memories are eventually stored elsewhere, in the neocortex. Larry Squire later formalised the split between declarative and non-declarative memory, and Suzanne Corkin studied H.M. for decades. Compare Korsakoff's syndrome (diencephalic amnesia with confabulation) and Clive Wearing (dense amnesia after herpes encephalitis). Option b is right because the bilateral medial temporal removal left H.M. unable to consolidate new declarative memories, a dense anterograde amnesia.","traps":{"a":"Procedural memory, for skills and habits, was spared in H.M. Brenda Milner showed that he improved at mirror drawing across days while having no memory of the earlier sessions, and later studies found other intact motor and perceptual learning. This dissociation showed that procedural learning does not depend on the hippocampus but on systems such as the basal ganglia and cerebellum. It tempts because people assume amnesia wipes out all new learning.","c":"Working memory, the short-term holding and manipulation of information modelled by Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch (1974), was intact in H.M. His digit span was normal and he could keep a number in mind by rehearsing it, but lost it as soon as his attention shifted. It tempts because 'cannot remember anything new' sounds like a short-term memory problem, though his deficit was in transfer to long-term storage.","d":"H.M. could still recognise people and events from long before his operation, including famous people who became well known before his surgery, because remote memories were largely preserved. Losing old memories is retrograde amnesia, and his retrograde loss covered mainly the years just before surgery. It tempts because amnesia in films usually means forgetting one's past, the reverse of H.M.'s main problem of being unable to lay down new memories."}},{"q":"Huntington's disease is associated with which genetic mutation?","concept":"Huntington's disease (HD) was described by the American physician George Huntington (1872). It is an autosomal dominant neurodegenerative disorder: each child of an affected parent has a 50 percent chance of inheriting the mutation, and carriers of a full mutation eventually develop the disease. In 1983 a team led by James Gusella mapped the gene to chromosome 4, and in 1993 the Huntington's Disease Collaborative Research Group identified the HTT (huntingtin) gene on the short arm of chromosome 4. The mutation is an expanded CAG trinucleotide repeat. CAG codes for glutamine, so the protein carries an abnormally long polyglutamine tract that is toxic to neurons, especially the medium spiny neurons of the caudate and putamen. Repeat counts guide diagnosis: up to 26 is normal, 27 to 35 intermediate, 36 to 39 reduced penetrance, and 40 or more full penetrance. Longer repeats mean earlier onset, and repeats tend to expand across generations, especially through the father, causing anticipation; very long repeats cause juvenile HD. Clinically HD combines chorea, a subcortical dementia with slowed thinking and poor executive function, and psychiatric symptoms such as depression, irritability and psychosis, usually starting in middle age. Exams pair HD with other trinucleotide disorders: Fragile X syndrome (CGG repeat in FMR1 on the X chromosome), myotonic dystrophy (CTG) and Friedreich's ataxia (GAA). They also use imprinting disorders of chromosome 15: Prader-Willi syndrome (paternal deletion) and Angelman syndrome (maternal deletion). Option d is right because HD is caused by a CAG trinucleotide expansion in the HTT gene on chromosome 4.","traps":{"a":"A CGG trinucleotide expansion on the X chromosome causes Fragile X syndrome, the commonest inherited cause of intellectual disability. More than 200 repeats in the FMR1 gene is a full mutation; 55 to 200 is a premutation that can expand when passed on by a mother. Features include intellectual disability, autistic traits, a long face, large ears and enlarged testes. It tempts because it is also a trinucleotide repeat disorder, differing only in the triplet and chromosome.","b":"FMR1 silencing is the mechanism of Fragile X syndrome. When the CGG repeat expands beyond about 200, the gene becomes heavily methylated and switched off, so the FMRP protein needed for normal synapse development is not made. This is a loss of function, whereas Huntington's is a toxic gain of function. It tempts because it is another well-known genetic mechanism in neuropsychiatry and sits next to Huntington's in genetics chapters.","c":"A paternal deletion of chromosome 15 (region 15q11-q13) causes Prader-Willi syndrome: floppiness in infancy, then insatiable appetite and obesity, mild intellectual disability and small hands and feet. The same deletion on the maternal chromosome causes Angelman syndrome, with severe intellectual disability, seizures and a happy demeanour, an example of genomic imprinting. It tempts because 'paternal' echoes the paternal anticipation seen in Huntington's disease."}},{"q":"REM sleep is most reliably associated with:","concept":"Sleep is staged from three recordings: the EEG, eye movements (EOG) and muscle tone (EMG). Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953) discovered rapid eye movement (REM) sleep, and William Dement and Kleitman (1957) linked it to vivid dreaming. Michel Jouvet (1959) called it paradoxical sleep, because the brain looks almost awake while the body is deeply relaxed. REM has three hallmarks. First, a low-voltage, mixed-frequency EEG, similar to waking or N1, often with sawtooth waves. Second, muscle atonia: circuits in the pons actively inhibit spinal motor neurons, paralysing skeletal muscles (except the eye muscles and diaphragm) so dreams are not acted out. Third, bursts of rapid conjugate eye movements. Heart rate and breathing become irregular. Sleep runs in cycles of about 90 minutes; REM periods lengthen towards morning and make up about 20 to 25 percent of adult sleep. Compare the other stages, scored today under the American Academy of Sleep Medicine scheme that replaced the manual of Allan Rechtschaffen and Anthony Kales (1968): relaxed wakefulness with eyes closed shows occipital alpha (8 to 13 Hz); N1 shows theta and slow rolling eye movements; N2 shows sleep spindles and K-complexes; N3 (slow-wave sleep) shows high-amplitude delta waves and the growth hormone surge. Clinical links: REM sleep behaviour disorder is a loss of atonia, narcolepsy brings REM features into wakefulness (cataplexy, sleep paralysis), and depression shortens REM latency. Option b is right because REM sleep is defined by a low-voltage mixed-frequency EEG together with muscle atonia and rapid eye movements.","traps":{"a":"Delta waves (slow, high-amplitude waves) and the nightly surge of growth hormone belong to N3, slow-wave or deep sleep, which is concentrated in the first third of the night. N3 is the hardest stage to wake from and is where sleepwalking and night terrors arise. It tempts because N3 is the other famous 'deep' stage, and students mix up deepest sleep with dreaming sleep, but in REM the EEG is fast and desynchronised.","c":"Sleep spindles (brief bursts of about 12 to 14 Hz generated by thalamocortical circuits) and K-complexes (large single biphasic waves, often triggered by sounds) are the markers of N2, light non-REM sleep, which takes up about half of adult sleep time. Spindles are also linked to memory consolidation. It tempts because spindles and K-complexes are the most memorable EEG features, so students attach them to the most memorable stage.","d":"Alpha waves (8 to 13 Hz), strongest over the occipital leads, mark relaxed wakefulness with the eyes closed. They were first described by Hans Berger (1929), who made the first human EEG recordings. Alpha blocks when the eyes open or attention increases, and drops out as N1 begins, replaced by theta. It tempts because REM is often called 'wake-like', but the REM EEG is low-voltage mixed frequency, not dominant occipital alpha."}},{"q":"The corticospinal tract crosses the midline primarily at the:","concept":"The corticospinal (pyramidal) tract carries commands for voluntary, especially skilled, movement. Its fibres start mainly in the primary motor cortex (including the giant Betz cells of layer V), the premotor and supplementary motor areas, and parietal cortex. They descend through the corona radiata, the posterior limb of the internal capsule, the cerebral peduncles of the midbrain and the basis pontis, then form the pyramids on the front of the medulla. At the lower end of the medulla, about 85 to 90 percent of the fibres cross the midline in the pyramidal decussation and run down the opposite side of the cord as the lateral corticospinal tract. The remaining 10 to 15 percent stay uncrossed as the anterior corticospinal tract, and most of these cross at their target spinal segment. This is why a stroke in the left internal capsule weakens the right side of the body, while a lesion of the tract in the spinal cord, below the decussation, causes weakness on the same side. Exams contrast where other pathways cross. The dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathway (fine touch, vibration, proprioception) also crosses in the medulla, as the internal arcuate fibres of the sensory decussation. The spinothalamic tract (pain and temperature) crosses in the spinal cord within a segment or two of entry. Hence in Brown-Séquard syndrome (hemisection of the cord) the patient loses movement and fine touch on the same side but pain and temperature on the opposite side. Option c is right because most corticospinal fibres cross the midline at the pyramidal decussation in the medulla.","traps":{"a":"Crossing in the spinal cord close to the level of entry is the pattern of the spinothalamic tract: pain and temperature fibres enter, synapse in the dorsal horn and cross in the anterior white commissure within a segment or two. In the corticospinal system, only the small uncrossed anterior tract crosses at segmental level. It tempts because it borrows a sensory crossing rule, or generalises the minor anterior tract to the whole pathway.","b":"The pons carries corticospinal fibres in scattered bundles through its basal part, the basis pontis, without crossing. The fibres that do cross in the pons belong to the corticopontocerebellar relay: pontine nuclei send transverse fibres across the midline to the opposite cerebellum through the middle cerebellar peduncle. It tempts because these transverse pontine fibres visibly cross the midline, and the pons lies on the tract's route between midbrain and medulla.","d":"The cerebral peduncles (crus cerebri) of the midbrain are a passage point: corticospinal and corticobulbar fibres run through their middle portion on the way down, still on the same side as their hemisphere of origin. The well-known midbrain crossing is the decussation of the superior cerebellar peduncles, a cerebellar pathway. It tempts because the peduncles carry the tract and look like the place where something major happens, but the crossing occurs lower."}},{"q":"Phineas Gage's well-documented post-injury changes (impulsivity, social disinhibition, poor judgement) are localised to damage of the:","concept":"Phineas Gage was a 25-year-old American railway construction foreman. On 13 September 1848 an explosion drove a tamping iron, over a metre long, up through his left cheek, behind his left eye and out through the top of his skull. He survived and could talk within minutes. His physician John Harlow (1868) recorded that he became fitful, irreverent, profane, impatient of advice and unable to keep to plans, so that friends said he was 'no longer Gage'. His memory, language and intelligence were broadly spared. His skull is preserved at Harvard. Hanna Damasio and colleagues (1994) reconstructed the path of the iron and placed the damage in the ventromedial prefrontal cortex (vmPFC), the lower inner surface of the frontal lobes; later reconstructions suggest the damage was mainly on the left. Malcolm Macmillan (2000) showed that later accounts exaggerated the changes and that Gage partly recovered, working as a stagecoach driver in Chile. Modern cases confirm the link. Antonio Damasio (1994) described a patient, Elliot, with intact IQ but chaotic decisions after vmPFC damage, and explained it with the somatic marker hypothesis: bodily emotional signals normally guide choices. Antoine Bechara and colleagues (1994) showed that vmPFC patients keep choosing risky decks on the Iowa Gambling Task. Neighbouring frontal syndromes: dorsolateral prefrontal damage impairs planning, working memory and set shifting (Wisconsin Card Sorting Test), and medial frontal damage causes apathy. Option b is right because Gage's impulsivity, social disinhibition and poor judgement are the classic signs of ventromedial prefrontal damage.","traps":{"a":"The posterior parietal cortex combines vision, touch and body position to guide attention and action. Right-sided damage causes left hemispatial neglect, left-sided damage can cause apraxia, and bilateral damage causes Balint's syndrome (optic ataxia, simultanagnosia). These are spatial and sensorimotor problems, not personality change. It tempts only if one forgets that the iron passed through the front of the skull, far from the parietal lobes.","c":"The hippocampus and amygdala are medial temporal structures. Hippocampal damage causes anterograde amnesia, as in Henry Molaison, and bilateral amygdala damage reduces fear and, with wider temporal damage, contributes to the tameness and hyperorality of Klüver-Bucy syndrome. Gage's memory was largely intact; his problem was judgement and self-control. It tempts because the amygdala is an 'emotion' structure and Gage's change looked emotional.","d":"The cerebellar vermis is the midline strip of the cerebellum that controls posture and gait. Damage, as in chronic alcohol misuse, causes truncal ataxia with a broad, unsteady gait. Jeremy Schmahmann and Janet Sherman (1998) described a cerebellar cognitive affective syndrome, but Gage's injury did not involve the cerebellum at all. It tempts as an anatomical filler for candidates unsure where the tamping iron went."}},{"q":"Which cranial nerve carries parasympathetic fibres to most thoracic and abdominal viscera?","concept":"The autonomic nervous system has two main divisions. The sympathetic division (fight or flight) leaves the spinal cord from segments T1 to L2, the thoracolumbar outflow. The parasympathetic division (rest and digest) has a craniosacral outflow: four cranial nerves plus sacral segments S2 to S4. Each cranial parasympathetic nerve has a small territory except one. The oculomotor nerve (III), from the Edinger-Westphal nucleus via the ciliary ganglion, constricts the pupil and adjusts the lens. The facial nerve (VII) drives the lacrimal, submandibular and sublingual glands. The glossopharyngeal nerve (IX) drives the parotid gland through the otic ganglion. The vagus nerve (X), whose name means wandering, is the exception: its fibres, mainly from the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus in the medulla, travel through the neck into the thorax and abdomen to supply the heart, lungs, oesophagus, stomach, liver, pancreas and intestines as far as about two-thirds along the transverse colon. The sacral outflow handles the rest of the colon, the bladder and the genitals. Vagal effects include slowing the heart, constricting the bronchi and increasing gut motility and secretion. Otto Loewi (1921) showed with two frog hearts that stimulating the vagus releases a chemical, Vagusstoff, later identified as acetylcholine, the first proof of chemical neurotransmission. Vagal tone is indexed by heart rate variability, and the polyvagal theory of Stephen Porges (1995) links it to social engagement. Vagus nerve stimulation is used for treatment-resistant epilepsy and depression. Option c is right because the vagus carries the parasympathetic supply to most thoracic and abdominal viscera.","traps":{"a":"The trigeminal nerve (V) is the main sensory nerve of the face, with ophthalmic, maxillary and mandibular branches, plus motor supply to the chewing muscles. It has no parasympathetic nucleus of its own; parasympathetic fibres from III, VII and IX only hitch a ride on its branches to reach glands. Trigeminal neuralgia is its classic disorder. It tempts because it is the largest cranial nerve and its branches spread widely.","b":"The glossopharyngeal nerve (IX) is genuinely parasympathetic, but only to the parotid salivary gland, via the inferior salivatory nucleus and the otic ganglion. It also carries taste from the back third of the tongue and sensory input from the carotid sinus and carotid body for blood pressure and oxygen reflexes. It tempts because it is a correct parasympathetic nerve, but its territory stays in the head and neck.","d":"The accessory nerve (XI) is a purely motor nerve to the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles, used to turn the head and shrug the shoulders. It carries no parasympathetic fibres. Its cranial rootlets travel briefly with the vagus, which is why older textbooks link the two. It tempts because XI sits next to X in the numbering and both leave the skull together through the jugular foramen."}},{"q":"Korsakoff's syndrome is most directly caused by deficiency of:","concept":"Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome is the classic brain disorder of thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency. Thiamine, as thiamine pyrophosphate, is a coenzyme for key enzymes of glucose metabolism (pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase and transketolase), so brain regions with high metabolic demand fail first. Carl Wernicke (1881) described the acute stage, Wernicke's encephalopathy, with the triad of confusion, ataxia and ophthalmoplegia (eye-movement palsy, nystagmus), although the full triad is seen in only a minority. Sergei Korsakoff (1887) described the chronic amnestic state. Korsakoff's syndrome shows dense anterograde amnesia, a temporally graded retrograde amnesia, confabulation (producing false memories without intent to deceive), apathy and poor insight, while working memory, general intelligence and procedural learning are relatively spared. The lesions involve the mammillary bodies and the medial dorsal and anterior thalamic nuclei, a diencephalic amnesia, unlike the medial temporal amnesia of Henry Molaison. The commonest cause is chronic alcohol use, which brings poor diet and reduced absorption and storage of thiamine, but malnutrition, hyperemesis gravidarum and bariatric surgery can also cause it. Treatment is urgent parenteral thiamine, given before glucose, because glucose loading in a thiamine-deficient patient can precipitate encephalopathy. Exams set it beside other vitamin syndromes: B12 deficiency (subacute combined degeneration, megaloblastic anaemia, dementia), niacin deficiency (pellagra), folate deficiency (megaloblastic anaemia, neural tube defects) and thiamine deficiency outside the brain (beriberi). Option c is right because Korsakoff's syndrome is most directly caused by deficiency of vitamin B1, thiamine.","traps":{"a":"Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) deficiency, often from pernicious anaemia or a strict vegan diet, causes megaloblastic anaemia and subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord (loss of position and vibration sense, with weakness). It can also cause depression, psychosis and a reversible dementia, which is why B12 is checked in dementia work-ups. It tempts because it is the B vitamin most linked with neuropsychiatric problems after thiamine.","b":"Niacin (vitamin B3) deficiency causes pellagra, remembered as the four Ds: dermatitis on sun-exposed skin, diarrhoea, dementia and death. It historically affected populations living mainly on maize and still occurs with malnutrition and alcohol misuse. Pellagra produces confusion and delirium, not the selective amnesia with confabulation of Korsakoff's syndrome. It tempts because it is another B-vitamin deficiency with mental symptoms that is also seen in alcoholism.","d":"Folate (vitamin B9) deficiency causes megaloblastic anaemia and, in early pregnancy, neural tube defects such as spina bifida, which is why folic acid is given before conception. Low folate is also associated with depression and is common in alcohol misuse and with some anticonvulsants. It does not damage the mammillary bodies or cause Korsakoff amnesia. It tempts because folate and B12 deficiencies look alike and both appear in alcohol misuse."}},{"q":"The right parietal lobe lesion most classically produces:","concept":"Hemispatial (unilateral) neglect is a failure to notice, attend to or act on stimuli on one side of space, even though vision and movement may be intact. It appears on the side opposite the lesion (contralesional) and is far more common and severe after right-hemisphere damage, typically to the right inferior parietal lobule and temporoparietal junction, often from a right middle cerebral artery stroke. So a right parietal lesion produces left-sided neglect. The asymmetry is explained by the model of Marsel Mesulam (1981): the right hemisphere attends to both sides of space, while the left attends mainly to the right side. After left damage the right hemisphere still covers both sides, so right neglect is rare and mild; after right damage nothing covers the left. Patients may eat from only the right half of the plate, shave only the right side of the face, and draw a clock with all the numbers crowded on the right. Bedside tests include line bisection (the mark is placed right of centre), cancellation tasks and figure copying. Edoardo Bisiach and Claudio Luzzatti (1978) showed representational neglect: patients describing Milan's cathedral square from memory omitted the left side from whichever viewpoint they imagined. Neglect often comes with anosognosia (unawareness of deficit, a term coined by Joseph Babinski in 1914) and with extinction to bilateral simultaneous stimulation. A homonymous hemianopia, by contrast, is a visual field loss that patients notice and compensate for by moving their eyes. Option d is right because a right parietal lesion classically produces neglect of the left side of space.","traps":{"a":"Right-sided neglect would need a left-hemisphere lesion, because neglect is contralesional. Even then it is uncommon and usually mild, since the intact right hemisphere still attends to both sides of space. Left parietal lesions are better known for apraxia and, at the angular gyrus, Gerstmann syndrome (finger agnosia, agraphia, acalculia, left-right confusion). It tempts because 'right lesion, right side' feels natural if one forgets the crossed organisation of the brain.","b":"Aphasia is an acquired language disorder that follows damage to the dominant, usually left, hemisphere: Broca's area in the inferior frontal gyrus or Wernicke's area in the posterior superior temporal gyrus. Right-hemisphere damage can disturb the emotional tone of speech (aprosodia) and discourse, but not core language. It tempts because aphasia is the most famous lesion sign, and students who forget which hemisphere is dominant may attach it to any lesion.","c":"Bilateral homonymous hemianopia means losing both visual half-fields, effectively cortical blindness, and follows damage to both occipital lobes, often from posterior cerebral artery strokes; in Anton syndrome the patient also denies being blind. A one-sided occipital or optic radiation lesion gives a single homonymous hemianopia. It tempts because parietal lesions can involve the optic radiations and cause a field cut, but neglect is an attention problem, not blindness."}},{"q":"Cones are most densely concentrated in which retinal region?","concept":"The retina contains two kinds of photoreceptor. Rods, about 120 million per eye, contain rhodopsin, are extremely sensitive, and give black-and-white vision in dim light (scotopic vision). Cones, about 6 million per eye, need brighter light and give colour and fine detail (photopic vision); they come in three types tuned to short, medium and long wavelengths, the physiological basis of the trichromatic theory of Thomas Young (1802), later extended by Hermann von Helmholtz. The fovea centralis is a small pit at the centre of the macula lutea, directly on the line of sight. Its centre is rod-free and packed with slender cones at their highest density, and the inner retinal layers are pushed aside so light reaches the cones with little scattering. Foveal cones connect almost one-to-one with ganglion cells, which gives high acuity, whereas many rods converge on each ganglion cell, which gives sensitivity but blur. This explains classic findings. Reading and recognising faces require pointing the fovea at the target. A dim star is easier to see when you look slightly to one side of it, putting its image on rod-rich retina. Dark adaptation shows a cone phase that levels off within about ten minutes and a slower rod phase lasting about half an hour. The Purkinje shift, in which blues look relatively brighter at dusk, reflects the switch from cones to rods. The optic disc, by contrast, has no photoreceptors and forms the blind spot. Option d is right because cones reach their highest density at the fovea centralis.","traps":{"a":"The optic disc is where ganglion cell axons leave the eye as the optic nerve and the retinal blood vessels enter. It has no rods or cones at all, so it forms the physiological blind spot, lying on the nasal side of the fovea. We do not notice it because the blind spots of the two eyes do not overlap and the brain fills in the gap. It tempts because it is the other famous named landmark of the retina.","b":"The peripheral retina is dominated by rods, whose density peaks roughly 20 degrees from the fovea, with only scattered cones. It supports night vision and motion detection but has poor acuity and weak colour vision, which is why you cannot read words out of the corner of your eye. Retinitis pigmentosa damages it first, causing tunnel vision. It tempts because the periphery is the larger area, but cone density there is low.","c":"The ora serrata is the jagged front edge of the retina, where the light-sensitive retina ends and the non-sensory ciliary body begins. It lies at the extreme periphery, far from the line of sight, and has very few photoreceptors of any kind. It tempts only through unfamiliarity: it is a real anatomical term that sounds technical, so candidates who cannot place it may guess it is a specialised zone."}},{"q":"Which neurotransmitter is the principal inhibitory transmitter in the mature mammalian brain?","concept":"Neurotransmitters are classed by the effect they usually have on the receiving neuron. Glutamate is the main excitatory transmitter of the brain and gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA) is the main inhibitory one; a large share of brain synapses use one or the other, and the balance between them keeps the brain stable. GABA is made from glutamate by the enzyme glutamic acid decarboxylase, which needs vitamin B6, and is released mainly by local interneurons and by projection neurons of the striatum and cerebellum (Purkinje cells). It acts on two receptor types. GABA-A receptors are ionotropic chloride channels: Cl- flows in and the neuron is hyperpolarised or held near rest, making firing less likely. Benzodiazepines increase the frequency of channel opening, barbiturates increase its duration, and alcohol and many anaesthetics also enhance GABA-A function, which is why combining them is dangerous. GABA-B receptors are metabotropic and act through G proteins to open K+ channels; baclofen is an agonist. Low GABA activity is linked to anxiety and seizures, and many anticonvulsants enhance GABA. The word 'mature' in the stem matters: in the developing brain neurons hold more internal chloride, so GABA can be depolarising, and the switch to inhibition comes as the chloride transporter KCC2 is expressed. In the spinal cord and brainstem, glycine is the main fast inhibitory transmitter, and strychnine causes convulsions by blocking its receptors. Acetylcholine, noradrenaline, dopamine and serotonin act mainly as neuromodulators whose effects depend on the receptor. Option a is right because GABA is the principal inhibitory transmitter in the mature mammalian brain.","traps":{"b":"Glutamate is the principal excitatory transmitter of the brain. It acts on AMPA, NMDA and kainate ionotropic receptors and on metabotropic receptors, drives learning through long-term potentiation, and in excess causes excitotoxic cell death in stroke and epilepsy. It is also the chemical precursor of GABA. It tempts because it is the most abundant brain transmitter and the two are always taught as a pair, so a hurried reader picks the wrong half.","c":"Acetylcholine was the first transmitter identified, by Otto Loewi (1921). It excites skeletal muscle at the neuromuscular junction through nicotinic receptors, mediates parasympathetic effects through muscarinic receptors, and in the brain supports attention and memory through basal forebrain neurons, whose loss in Alzheimer's disease is targeted by cholinesterase inhibitors. Its central effects can be excitatory or inhibitory. It tempts because it slows the heart, which looks like inhibition.","d":"Norepinephrine (noradrenaline) is released by neurons of the locus coeruleus in the pons and by sympathetic nerves. It acts on alpha and beta adrenergic receptors to raise arousal, vigilance and the fight-or-flight response, and is targeted by many antidepressants. It is a neuromodulator, not the main fast inhibitory transmitter. It tempts because alpha-2 receptors are inhibitory autoreceptors, and candidates who remember that detail may overgeneralise it."}},{"q":"The 'pleasure centre' identified by Olds and Milner (1954) corresponds to which structure?","concept":"In 1954 James Olds and Peter Milner, working at McGill University in Montreal in the department of Donald Hebb, discovered by accident that the brain contains sites where electrical stimulation is rewarding. They were implanting electrodes aimed at the reticular formation to study arousal. One electrode missed and ended up near the septal area, and the rat kept returning to the corner of the box where it had been stimulated, as if it wanted more. Olds and Milner then let rats press a lever to deliver brief pulses to their own brains (intracranial self-stimulation). Rats with septal electrodes pressed at high rates for long periods. Later work by Olds showed that the strongest sites lay along the medial forebrain bundle, a fibre tract running through the lateral hypothalamus that links the midbrain with the septal area and forebrain. There some rats pressed thousands of times an hour and chose stimulation over food even when hungry. The medial forebrain bundle carries the axons of the mesolimbic dopamine pathway from the ventral tegmental area to the nucleus accumbens, and dopamine-blocking drugs reduce self-stimulation, so the finding is seen as the start of modern reward neuroscience. Popular writing called these sites 'pleasure centres', although many researchers now think they reflect wanting (incentive salience, in the terms of Kent Berridge and Terry Robinson (1998)) more than liking. Option c is right because the reward sites identified by Olds and Milner were in the septal area and along the medial forebrain bundle.","traps":{"a":"The cerebellar tonsils are rounded lobes on the underside of the cerebellum, just above the foramen magnum. They matter clinically when they herniate downward, as in Chiari malformation or raised intracranial pressure, compressing the medulla. The cerebellum coordinates movement and balance and played no part in the self-stimulation studies. It tempts only as an unfamiliar anatomical term that might sound like a hidden deep centre.","b":"The lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN) is the thalamic relay for vision. It receives input from the retina through the optic tract and sends it through the optic radiations to the primary visual cortex in the occipital lobe; its six layers keep the inputs from the two eyes separate. The medial geniculate nucleus does the same for hearing. It tempts because it is a named deep nucleus, but it relays sensation, not reward.","d":"The internal globus pallidus is the main output nucleus of the basal ganglia. It inhibits the thalamus and so gates movement, and it is a target for deep brain stimulation and pallidotomy in Parkinson's disease and dystonia. It is not where Olds and Milner found self-stimulation. It tempts because the ventral pallidum, a different nearby region, is part of reward circuitry, so 'pallidus' sounds plausibly linked to pleasure."}},{"q":"Which feature distinguishes upper motor neuron (UMN) from lower motor neuron (LMN) lesions?","concept":"Voluntary movement uses a two-neuron chain. The upper motor neuron (UMN) runs from the motor cortex down the corticospinal and corticobulbar tracts to the brainstem or spinal cord. The lower motor neuron (LMN) runs from the anterior horn cell of the spinal cord, or a cranial nerve motor nucleus, out to the muscle; Charles Sherrington (1906) called it the final common path. Lesions of each give a recognisable picture. A UMN lesion (stroke, multiple sclerosis, spinal cord injury) removes descending control, releasing the spinal reflex circuits from inhibition. The result is weakness in a pyramidal pattern, spasticity (a velocity-dependent increase in tone, with a clasp-knife quality), hyperreflexia, clonus and an extensor plantar response, the sign described by Joseph Babinski (1896), in which the big toe goes up when the sole is stroked. Muscle wasting is only mild, from disuse. An LMN lesion (poliomyelitis, peripheral neuropathy, nerve root compression) cuts the reflex arc itself and denervates the muscle. The result is flaccid weakness, hypotonia, reduced or absent reflexes, marked wasting and fasciculations (visible twitching of denervated motor units), with a normal or absent plantar response. Two exam traps: in the first days after an acute UMN lesion, spinal shock can make the limb flaccid and areflexic; and motor neuron disease (amyotrophic lateral sclerosis) mixes UMN and LMN signs. Do not confuse spasticity with the lead-pipe or cogwheel rigidity of basal ganglia (extrapyramidal) disease. Option c is right because hyperreflexia, spasticity and a Babinski sign mark an upper motor neuron lesion.","traps":{"a":"Fasciculations, marked atrophy and hypotonia are the classic signs of a lower motor neuron lesion, because the muscle has lost its nerve supply; the twitching comes from surviving motor units firing spontaneously. UMN lesions cause only mild disuse wasting and increased, not reduced, tone. It tempts because it lists genuine motor signs and only the label is wrong, a common way examiners build distractors for this topic.","b":"Hyperreflexia is a UMN sign. In an LMN lesion the motor limb of the reflex arc is damaged, so tendon reflexes are reduced or absent (hyporeflexia or areflexia), as in poliomyelitis, Guillain-Barré syndrome or a compressed nerve root. It tempts because the sign itself is correct for one of the two lesion types, and this option simply attaches it to the wrong neuron.","d":"Absent reflexes point to an LMN lesion or to a sensory problem in the reflex arc. A UMN lesion releases the spinal reflex from cortical inhibition, so reflexes become brisk. The main time a UMN lesion gives absent reflexes is the early phase of spinal shock after acute cord injury, which later gives way to hyperreflexia. It tempts students who reason that any brain damage must weaken everything, including reflexes."}},{"q":"Which sleep stage is most associated with sleepwalking and night terrors?","concept":"Parasomnias are abnormal behaviours or experiences during sleep, and the key to this topic is which stage they come from. Roger Broughton (1968) described sleepwalking, sleep terrors and confusional arousals as disorders of arousal: the person is partly woken out of deep non-REM sleep, so motor and emotional systems switch on while much of the cortex stays asleep. They arise from N3, slow-wave sleep with high-amplitude delta waves, which dominates the first third of the night. This explains their profile. Episodes come one to three hours after sleep onset; the person is hard to wake, confused if woken, and usually has little or no memory of the event; and they are commonest in children, who have the most slow-wave sleep, often fading by adolescence. Anything that deepens or fragments N3, such as sleep deprivation, fever, stress and some sedatives, makes episodes more likely. In a sleep terror the child sits up screaming, with racing heart, sweating and dilated pupils, cannot be comforted, and does not recall it next morning. DSM-5 groups these as non-rapid eye movement sleep arousal disorders. Contrast the REM parasomnias: nightmare disorder (frightening dreams, usually in the last third of the night, with full alertness and vivid recall on waking) and REM sleep behaviour disorder (loss of normal REM atonia so dreams are acted out, mostly in older men, often an early sign of Parkinson's disease or Lewy body dementia). Sleep paralysis also involves REM atonia. Option d is right because sleepwalking and night terrors are arousal disorders arising from N3 slow-wave sleep.","traps":{"a":"REM sleep is when vivid dreams and nightmares occur, mostly in the second half of the night. Because the body is normally paralysed during REM (atonia), people do not walk in it. When atonia fails, as in REM sleep behaviour disorder, people act out dreams, but they wake easily and recall the dream, unlike sleepwalkers. It tempts because night terrors sound like nightmares, and nightmares are REM events.","b":"N1 is the brief transition from waking to sleep, with theta activity and slow rolling eye movements, and makes up only about 5 percent of the night. Its typical events are hypnic jerks (sudden starts on falling asleep) and hypnagogic imagery. People woken from N1 often deny they were asleep. It tempts because sleepwalkers seem half awake, but N1 is too light for the arousal disorders, which come out of deep N3 sleep.","c":"N2 is light non-REM sleep marked by sleep spindles and K-complexes, and it fills about half of adult sleep time. Bruxism (teeth grinding) and some sleep talking occur in N2, but sleepwalking and night terrors arise from the deeper slow-wave stage. It tempts because N2 is the most common stage across the night, so candidates may assume that most sleep events must happen there."}},{"q":"The amygdala is most central to:","concept":"The amygdala is an almond-shaped cluster of nuclei in the front of the medial temporal lobe, just in front of the hippocampus. Its core job is to detect biologically significant, especially threatening, stimuli and to organise the body's response. In classical fear conditioning a neutral tone (conditioned stimulus) is paired with a shock (unconditioned stimulus) until the tone alone produces fear; John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner (1920) showed the same principle with Little Albert. Joseph LeDoux mapped the circuit in rats: the lateral nucleus receives both signals and links them, and the central nucleus drives the outputs, freezing through the periaqueductal grey, stress hormones through the hypothalamus, and autonomic arousal. LeDoux (1996) described a quick 'low road' from the thalamus straight to the amygdala and a slower 'high road' through the cortex. Human evidence is strong. Ralph Adolphs and colleagues (1994) found that patient S.M., whose amygdalae were destroyed by Urbach-Wiethe disease, could not recognise fear in faces, and later work showed she felt little fear. Antoine Bechara and colleagues (1995) found a double dissociation: a patient with amygdala damage knew which cue predicted a loud noise but showed no conditioned skin conductance response, while a patient with hippocampal damage showed the response but could not report the facts. In monkeys, Heinrich Klüver and Paul Bucy (1937) found that bilateral temporal lobe removal produced tameness, hyperorality and hypersexuality. The amygdala is overactive in anxiety disorders and PTSD. Option c is right because the amygdala is most central to fear conditioning and threat detection.","traps":{"a":"Verbal working memory, holding and rehearsing words or digits for a few seconds, is the phonological loop of Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch (1974). It depends mainly on left-hemisphere regions: dorsolateral prefrontal cortex for control, Broca's area for subvocal rehearsal and left inferior parietal cortex for the phonological store. It tempts only as a cognitive filler, since the amygdala adds emotional weight to memories but does not hold them online.","b":"Initiating voluntary movement depends on the supplementary and premotor areas, the primary motor cortex and the basal ganglia. Hans Kornhuber and Lüder Deecke (1965) recorded the readiness potential over these areas building up before a self-chosen movement. Amygdala output can trigger freezing or startle, but it does not plan or start voluntary acts. It tempts because fear produces fast bodily reactions, which can look like the amygdala starting movement.","d":"Long-term declarative memory depends on the hippocampus for consolidation and on the neocortex for long-term storage, as the case of Henry Molaison showed. The amygdala modulates this system: James McGaugh (2004) reviewed evidence that stress hormones acting on the basolateral amygdala strengthen consolidation of emotional events, which is why such memories are vivid. It tempts because the amygdala sits right next to the hippocampus and clearly affects memory, but it is not the store."}},{"q":"Antipsychotic-induced extrapyramidal side effects arise primarily from blockade of:","concept":"All antipsychotics share one action: blocking dopamine D2 receptors. Arvid Carlsson and Margit Lindqvist (1963) first linked antipsychotic action to dopamine receptor blockade, and Philip Seeman and colleagues (1976) showed that clinical potency tracks D2 binding. Because a drug taken by mouth reaches all four dopamine pathways, its effects depend on where the blockade happens. Blockade in the mesolimbic pathway (ventral tegmental area to nucleus accumbens) reduces positive symptoms, the wanted effect. Blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway (substantia nigra pars compacta to caudate and putamen) disturbs the basal ganglia motor circuit and produces extrapyramidal side effects (EPS), so called because they arise outside the pyramidal (corticospinal) tract. They appear in a typical order: acute dystonia within hours to days (muscle spasms, oculogyric crisis, torticollis), akathisia within days to weeks (inner restlessness), drug-induced parkinsonism within weeks (tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia), and tardive dyskinesia after months or years (involuntary mouth and tongue movements, thought to involve D2 receptor supersensitivity). PET studies by Shitij Kapur and colleagues (2000) suggested that about 65 percent striatal D2 occupancy gives an antipsychotic effect and that EPS become likely above about 78 percent. High-potency first-generation drugs such as haloperidol carry the highest EPS risk, while clozapine and other second-generation drugs, with looser D2 binding and 5-HT2A blockade, carry less. Dystonia and parkinsonism respond to anticholinergics such as trihexyphenidyl; akathisia often responds to propranolol. Option b is right because extrapyramidal side effects arise primarily from D2 blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway.","traps":{"a":"Mesolimbic D2 blockade (ventral tegmental area to nucleus accumbens) is the therapeutic action of antipsychotics: it reduces positive symptoms such as hallucinations and delusions, in line with the dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia. It may also blunt reward and motivation, but it does not cause the motor side effects. It tempts because it is the best-known antipsychotic target, and candidates assume the main site of action must also be the source of side effects.","c":"The mesocortical pathway runs from the ventral tegmental area to the prefrontal cortex and is thought to be underactive in schizophrenia, contributing to negative and cognitive symptoms. D2 blockade here may worsen apathy and slowed thinking (secondary negative symptoms, sometimes called neuroleptic-induced deficit syndrome), but it does not produce motor signs. It tempts because drug-induced apathy and slowing can look like parkinsonism, which blurs the line between the two.","d":"The tuberoinfundibular pathway runs from the arcuate nucleus of the hypothalamus to the median eminence, where dopamine inhibits prolactin release. D2 blockade here causes hyperprolactinaemia, with galactorrhoea, amenorrhoea, gynaecomastia and sexual dysfunction, especially with risperidone and first-generation drugs. It tempts because it is a genuine and common antipsychotic side effect, but it is an endocrine effect, not an extrapyramidal one."}},{"q":"Which structure serves as the principal relay for nearly all sensory modalities (except olfaction) to the cortex?","concept":"The thalamus is a paired, egg-shaped mass of grey matter in the diencephalon, sitting on top of the brainstem on either side of the third ventricle. It is called the gateway or relay station to the cortex because almost every sensory pathway synapses in a specific thalamic nucleus before reaching its primary cortical area. Vision travels from the retina to the lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN) and on to primary visual cortex in the occipital lobe. Hearing passes through the medial geniculate nucleus (MGN) to primary auditory cortex in the temporal lobe. A memory aid: Lateral for Light, Medial for Music. Touch, pain and temperature from the body relay in the ventral posterolateral nucleus (VPL), and from the face in the ventral posteromedial nucleus (VPM), which also carries taste, before reaching somatosensory cortex in the postcentral gyrus. Olfaction is the exception: fibres from the olfactory bulb run in the olfactory tract directly to the piriform (primary olfactory) cortex and amygdala without a thalamic relay; a secondary route to orbitofrontal cortex passes through the mediodorsal thalamus, but the primary pathway bypasses it. The thalamus is not a passive relay. It filters and gates input, relays motor signals from the cerebellum and basal ganglia (ventral anterior and ventral lateral nuclei), and its anterior nucleus is part of the Papez circuit. Do not confuse it with the hypothalamus just below, which controls homeostasis and the pituitary. Option c is right because the thalamus is the principal relay to the cortex for every sense except smell.","traps":{"a":"The hypothalamus lies just below the thalamus (hence 'hypo') and is the master regulator of homeostasis: hunger and satiety (lateral and ventromedial nuclei), thirst, body temperature, sleep timing through the suprachiasmatic nucleus, the autonomic nervous system, and the pituitary gland through releasing hormones. It does not relay sensory information to the cortex. It tempts because the two names are almost identical and both structures belong to the diencephalon.","b":"The basal ganglia (caudate nucleus, putamen and globus pallidus, working with the subthalamic nucleus and substantia nigra) form loops with the cortex and thalamus that select and initiate voluntary movement and support habit learning. Their classic disorders are Parkinson's disease and Huntington's disease. They send output to the cortex through the thalamus and carry no primary sensory pathway. They tempt because they are deep subcortical grey matter lying close to the thalamus.","d":"The hippocampus, in the medial temporal lobe, consolidates new declarative (explicit) memories, as shown by patient H.M. (Henry Molaison), studied by Brenda Milner after his 1953 surgery. It also supports spatial memory through place cells. It receives highly processed input from association cortex through the entorhinal cortex and does not relay raw sensation. It tempts because olfactory input reaches the nearby medial temporal lobe directly, so candidates link it with the olfaction exception in the stem."}},{"q":"Split-brain studies by Sperry and Gazzaniga showed that:","concept":"The corpus callosum is the largest commissure of the brain, a band of roughly 200 million axons joining the left and right cerebral hemispheres. In the 1940s and again in the 1960s, some patients with severe, drug-resistant epilepsy had it surgically cut (commissurotomy) to stop seizures spreading from one side to the other. Roger Sperry and his student Michael Gazzaniga (first report 1962) tested these split-brain patients, work for which Sperry shared the 1981 Nobel Prize. Their method exploited the wiring of vision: each visual field projects to the opposite (contralateral) hemisphere, because fibres from the nasal half of each retina cross at the optic chiasm. A picture flashed briefly to the right visual field reaches only the left hemisphere, and the patient can name it. Flashed to the left visual field, it reaches only the right hemisphere: the patient says he saw nothing, yet his left hand can pick out the object by touch or draw it. Each disconnected hemisphere perceived, remembered and responded on its own, as if two minds shared one skull. The left hemisphere was dominant for speech and analytic tasks; the right was better at visuospatial tasks and face recognition. Gazzaniga later described a left-hemisphere 'interpreter' that invents explanations for actions started by the right hemisphere. Related exam concepts include language lateralisation (left-sided in about 95 percent of right-handers), the Wada test for checking language dominance before surgery, and alien hand behaviour. Option b is right because the central finding was that each hemisphere can process information independently once the corpus callosum is sectioned.","traps":{"a":"This reverses the finding. Split-brain patients could not name objects shown only to the right hemisphere, which showed that speech is strongly lateralised, usually to the left, as Paul Broca (1861) and Carl Wernicke (1874) had inferred from lesions. The right hemisphere understands some simple words but produces little speech. It tempts because popular accounts stress that both hemispheres contribute to language, for example prosody and metaphor on the right.","c":"In most right-handers (about 95 percent) the left hemisphere controls speech output, which is why split-brain patients could name only right-visual-field stimuli. The right hemisphere contributes emotional prosody and visuospatial skill, not verbal output. Right-hemisphere speech dominance is uncommon even in left-handers, most of whom are also left-lateralised. It tempts because of the crossed-control rule: the right hemisphere does control the left hand, which candidates confuse with speech.","d":"This contradicts the anatomy the experiments relied on. Each visual field projects to the opposite hemisphere: the left visual field to the right occipital lobe and the right visual field to the left. In an intact brain the corpus callosum then shares this information, so both hemispheres seem to receive everything. After commissurotomy that sharing stops. It tempts because each eye sends fibres to both hemispheres, which candidates confuse with each visual field doing so."}},{"q":"The default mode network is most active during:","concept":"The default mode network (DMN) is a set of brain regions that is more active when a person is resting and not focused on the outside world than when performing an external task. Hans Berger, who first recorded the human EEG (1929), already noted that the resting brain is busy. In PET studies Gordon Shulman and colleagues (1997) found the same regions consistently deactivated across many goal-directed tasks, and Marcus Raichle and colleagues (2001) proposed a 'default mode of brain function' to explain this. The core hubs are the medial prefrontal cortex, the posterior cingulate cortex and precuneus, and the angular gyrus (inferior parietal lobule), with links to the hippocampus and lateral temporal cortex. The DMN is active during mind-wandering and daydreaming, autobiographical memory, imagining the future, thinking about oneself, and inferring other people's mental states (theory of mind). It is anticorrelated with the task-positive networks (the dorsal attention network and the frontoparietal or central executive network), which switch on for external, attention-demanding tasks. The triple network model of Vinod Menon (2011) adds the salience network (anterior insula and dorsal anterior cingulate), which switches the brain between the DMN and the executive network. Clinically, DMN hyperconnectivity is linked to rumination in depression, and DMN hubs overlap with regions where amyloid builds up early in Alzheimer's disease. Abnormalities are also reported in schizophrenia, autism and ADHD. Option a is right because rest, mind-wandering and self-referential thought are exactly the conditions under which the DMN is most active.","traps":{"b":"Externally focused, goal-directed tasks such as mental arithmetic or visual search engage the task-positive networks: the dorsal attention network (intraparietal sulcus, frontal eye fields) and the frontoparietal control network (dorsolateral prefrontal cortex). During such tasks DMN activity drops below its resting level. It tempts because the brain seems most 'active' during effortful work, so candidates assume every network must be busiest then.","c":"Motor execution is driven by primary motor cortex in the precentral gyrus, premotor cortex and the supplementary motor area, with the cerebellum and basal ganglia fine-tuning movement. It is an external, action-oriented state that suppresses the DMN. Resting-state connectivity was in fact first shown in the motor system by Bharat Biswal (1995), which can tempt candidates to link 'resting network' with motor regions.","d":"Early visual processing occurs in primary visual cortex (V1, striate cortex) in the occipital lobe, which receives input from the lateral geniculate nucleus and responds to edges and orientations, as David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel (from 1959) showed. This is stimulus-driven, bottom-up activity, the opposite of the internally directed DMN. It tempts because the precuneus, a DMN hub, lies close to visual areas in the posterior medial brain."}},{"q":"Which brain area, when lesioned, classically produces transcortical motor aphasia?","concept":"Aphasia is an acquired language disorder after brain damage, usually to the left hemisphere. The classic Wernicke-Lichtheim model, set out by Ludwig Lichtheim (1885) building on Paul Broca (1861) and Carl Wernicke (1874), sorts aphasias by three bedside tests: fluency, comprehension and repetition. Broca's aphasia (left inferior frontal gyrus) has non-fluent, effortful, telegraphic speech, fairly good comprehension and poor repetition. Wernicke's aphasia (posterior superior temporal gyrus) has fluent but empty speech with paraphasias, poor comprehension and poor repetition. Conduction aphasia (arcuate fasciculus or supramarginal gyrus) has fluent speech and good comprehension but disproportionately poor repetition. The transcortical aphasias are the mirror image: repetition is preserved because the perisylvian loop (Wernicke's area, arcuate fasciculus, Broca's area) is intact but cut off from the rest of the cortex. Transcortical motor aphasia comes from a lesion in the left frontal lobe anterior or superior to Broca's area, involving dorsolateral prefrontal cortex or the supplementary motor area, often a watershed infarct between the anterior and middle cerebral artery territories. The patient has sparse, non-fluent speech with poor initiation, good comprehension and strikingly good repetition, sometimes with echolalia. Transcortical sensory aphasia (posterior temporoparietal lesion) is fluent with poor comprehension but good repetition. Mixed transcortical aphasia, or isolation of the speech area, combines both deficits with only repetition spared. Option d is right because transcortical motor aphasia arises from frontal damage anterior or superior to Broca's area that spares Broca's area itself, so repetition survives.","traps":{"a":"The posterior superior temporal gyrus of the left hemisphere is Wernicke's area, described by Carl Wernicke (1874). Damage produces Wernicke's (receptive) aphasia: fluent, well-articulated but meaningless speech full of paraphasias and neologisms, with poor comprehension, poor repetition and often poor awareness of the deficit. That is a fluent aphasia, not the non-fluent transcortical motor pattern. It tempts because it is the most famous language area after Broca's.","b":"The left angular gyrus, at the parieto-temporo-occipital junction, links written and spoken language. Damage produces alexia with agraphia and, with nearby parietal damage, Gerstmann syndrome (agraphia, acalculia, finger agnosia, left-right disorientation). Posterior lesions in this region can also cause anomic or transcortical sensory aphasia. None of these is the frontal, non-fluent transcortical motor type. It tempts because it is another classic named language region.","c":"The arcuate fasciculus is the white-matter tract linking Wernicke's and Broca's areas. Damage produces conduction aphasia, which Carl Wernicke (1874) predicted: fluent speech and good comprehension but disproportionately poor repetition with phonemic paraphasias. That is the opposite of the transcortical aphasias, where repetition is spared precisely because the arcuate loop is intact. It tempts because it is a frequently examined lesion site that sounds technical and specific."}},{"q":"The Papez circuit is a closed loop associated with emotional processing. Its components include:","concept":"In 1937 the American neuroanatomist James Papez published 'A proposed mechanism of emotion', arguing that emotion depends on an anatomical circuit rather than a single centre. His loop runs from the hippocampus (via the subiculum) into the fornix, to the mammillary bodies of the hypothalamus, then through the mammillothalamic tract to the anterior nucleus of the thalamus, on to the cingulate gyrus, then through the cingulum bundle to the parahippocampal gyrus and entorhinal cortex, and back to the hippocampus. Papez saw the hypothalamus as producing bodily emotional expression and the cingulate cortex as the seat of emotional experience, building on the thalamic theory of emotion of Walter Cannon (1927) and Philip Bard. Paul Broca (1878) had earlier named the ring of cortex around the brainstem the 'great limbic lobe'. Paul MacLean (1952) widened Papez's circuit into the 'limbic system', adding the amygdala, septal nuclei and orbitofrontal cortex. The amygdala was not in the original Papez circuit, a favourite exam detail. Today the Papez circuit is seen as more central to episodic memory than to emotion: damage to the mammillary bodies or anterior thalamus, as in Korsakoff syndrome from thiamine deficiency, produces amnesia. Contrast it with motor loops: the basal ganglia circuit (cortex, striatum, globus pallidus, thalamus, back to cortex) and the cerebellar circuit (cortex, pons, cerebellum, thalamus, cortex). Option c is right because it lists the Papez loop in order: hippocampus, fornix, mammillary bodies, anterior thalamus, cingulate gyrus, entorhinal cortex, and back to the hippocampus.","traps":{"a":"This sketches a motor loop, and not even accurately. The real cerebro-cerebellar circuit runs from motor cortex to the pontine nuclei, to the cerebellum, then to the ventral lateral nucleus of the thalamus and back to motor cortex, coordinating and timing movement. It contains no limbic structure. It tempts because the thalamus appears in both this loop and the Papez circuit, so a candidate matching on 'thalamus' alone is misled.","b":"This mixes the amygdala with the basal ganglia (caudate, putamen, globus pallidus). The amygdala was not part of Papez's 1937 circuit; Paul MacLean (1952) added it when he expanded the circuit into the limbic system. The striatum and pallidum belong to cortico-basal ganglia-thalamo-cortical loops for movement and habit. It tempts because the amygdala is the structure most associated with emotion today, so its presence looks right.","d":"Substantia nigra to striatum is the nigrostriatal dopamine pathway, part of the basal ganglia motor system. Loss of dopamine neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta causes Parkinson's disease, and blocking D2 receptors in this pathway causes the extrapyramidal side effects of antipsychotics. It concerns movement, not emotion or memory. It tempts because it is also presented as a closed loop of familiar subcortical names."}},{"q":"Which receptor subtype is the primary target of typical (first-generation) antipsychotics?","concept":"Antipsychotics treat psychosis mainly by blocking dopamine. The first, chlorpromazine, entered psychiatry when Jean Delay and Pierre Deniker (1952) used it for psychotic patients; haloperidol followed later in the 1950s. The decisive evidence for the mechanism came from receptor-binding studies by Philip Seeman and Tyrone Lee (1975) and by Ian Creese, David Burt and Solomon Snyder (1976): the usual clinical dose of each typical antipsychotic correlates closely with its affinity for the D2 receptor, but not with its D1 affinity. This finding underpins the dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia. PET work by Shitij Kapur and colleagues (2000) showed that about 65 percent D2 occupancy is needed for an antipsychotic effect, while above about 78 percent extrapyramidal symptoms become likely. Learn the four dopamine pathways: mesolimbic (blockade reduces positive symptoms, the therapeutic target), mesocortical (blockade may worsen negative and cognitive symptoms), nigrostriatal (blockade causes dystonia, parkinsonism, akathisia and tardive dyskinesia) and tuberoinfundibular (blockade raises prolactin, causing galactorrhoea and amenorrhoea). High-potency typicals such as haloperidol cause more extrapyramidal effects; low-potency typicals such as chlorpromazine block muscarinic, histamine H1 and alpha-1 receptors more, causing sedation, anticholinergic effects and postural hypotension. Atypicals such as clozapine, risperidone and olanzapine add strong serotonin 5-HT2A blockade relative to D2, and aripiprazole is a D2 partial agonist. Option c is right because dopamine D2 antagonism is the primary therapeutic mechanism of typical antipsychotics, and their potency tracks D2 affinity.","traps":{"a":"Serotonin 5-HT2A antagonism defines the second-generation (atypical) antipsychotics, which Herbert Meltzer (1989) characterised by a high 5-HT2A to D2 binding ratio. This extra blockade is thought to release dopamine in the nigrostriatal and mesocortical pathways, lowering extrapyramidal symptoms. In typicals, D2 blockade dominates. It tempts because atypicals are now prescribed far more often, so the serotonin story feels like the modern answer.","b":"D1 receptors form a separate dopamine receptor family (D1 and D5) that stimulates adenylyl cyclase, while the D2 family (D2, D3, D4) inhibits it. Phenothiazines such as chlorpromazine do bind D1, and clozapine has relatively high D1 affinity, but clinical potency does not track D1 binding, as Seeman and Lee showed. It tempts because 'dopamine' is correct and only the receptor number differs.","d":"Muscarinic M1 blockade is a side-effect mechanism, not the therapeutic one. It causes dry mouth, constipation, blurred vision, urinary retention and confusion, and is strongest in low-potency typicals such as chlorpromazine and thioridazine and in clozapine. Anticholinergic drugs such as trihexyphenidyl are deliberately given to reduce drug-induced parkinsonism. It tempts because antipsychotics clearly bind muscarinic receptors, so candidates confuse a side-effect receptor with the primary target."}},{"q":"Cushing's syndrome can produce psychiatric symptoms most commonly characterised by:","concept":"Cushing's syndrome is the clinical state produced by chronic excess of glucocorticoids (cortisol). Harvey Cushing (1912) described it, and in 1932 he linked many cases to small basophil adenomas of the pituitary. The commonest cause overall is steroid medication; among endogenous causes an ACTH-secreting pituitary adenoma (Cushing's disease) predominates, followed by adrenal tumours and ectopic ACTH from lung tumours. Cortisol is the end product of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis: corticotropin-releasing hormone from the hypothalamus releases ACTH from the pituitary, which drives the adrenal cortex. Physical signs include moon face, buffalo hump, central obesity, purple striae, hypertension, diabetes and osteoporosis. Psychiatric symptoms are common and can appear before the diagnosis. Depression is the most frequent, reported in roughly half or more of patients, typically with irritability, emotional lability, insomnia and anxiety. Cognitive impairment in memory and concentration is common, and Monica Starkman (1992) showed reduced hippocampal volume that partly recovers once cortisol is corrected. Mania, hypomania and psychosis occur in a minority and are more typical of high-dose exogenous steroids. The link runs both ways: many people with melancholic depression show HPA overactivity, which is why Bernard Carroll (1981) proposed the dexamethasone suppression test for melancholia. Compare other endocrine pictures: Addison's disease (cortisol deficiency) with apathy, fatigue and depression; hyperthyroidism with anxiety; hypothyroidism with slowing and depression. Option c is right because depression, anxiety and cognitive impairment, with occasional psychosis, is the typical psychiatric profile of Cushing's syndrome.","traps":{"a":"Mania and hypomania do occur with excess cortisol, but in a minority of patients, and they are more characteristic of high-dose exogenous corticosteroids, for example prednisolone given for asthma or autoimmune disease, especially early in treatment. Endogenous Cushing's syndrome is dominated by depression, and the word 'only' makes the option doubly wrong. It tempts because steroid-induced mania is a well-known clinical phenomenon taught under the same heading.","b":"Catatonia is a psychomotor syndrome of stupor, mutism, negativism, posturing, waxy flexibility and echo phenomena. DSM-5 (2013) allows it as a specifier of mood and psychotic disorders or as due to another medical condition, such as encephalitis, including anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis. It is not a characteristic feature of Cushing's syndrome. It tempts because candidates know medical illnesses can cause catatonia and pick the most dramatic option.","d":"A selective loss of smell has nothing to do with excess cortisol. Anosmia points to head injury, sinus or viral disease, an olfactory groove meningioma, or early neurodegeneration in Parkinson's and Alzheimer's disease. The classic endocrine condition with anosmia is Kallmann syndrome, in which hypogonadotropic hypogonadism comes with absent smell. It tempts only as an odd-one-out distractor for a candidate who knows some endocrine disorders affect smell."}},{"q":"Object permanence is typically considered fully established by what age in Piaget's framework?","concept":"Object permanence is the understanding that objects continue to exist when they cannot be seen, heard or touched. Jean Piaget built it into his account of the sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2 years) in The Construction of Reality in the Child (1937), based on close observation of his own three children. Its growth runs across six substages. Before about 4 months, out of sight is out of mind. From 4 to 8 months (secondary circular reactions) infants retrieve a partly hidden object but not a fully hidden one. From 8 to 12 months (coordination of secondary schemes) they search for a fully hidden object but make the A-not-B error: after finding a toy at A several times, they search at A again even after watching it hidden at B. From 12 to 18 months (tertiary circular reactions) they follow visible displacements but fail invisible ones, where the object is moved while hidden. From 18 to 24 months (mental representation) they infer invisible displacements, and object permanence is complete. The same substage brings deferred imitation and symbolic thought, marking the move to the preoperational stage. Later researchers argued Piaget underestimated infants: using violation-of-expectation methods, Renée Baillargeon (1987) found that infants of 3.5 to 4.5 months looked longer at impossible events involving hidden objects. Exams may therefore contrast first signs (8 months for Piaget, about 4 months for Baillargeon) with full permanence. Option c is right because Piaget held object permanence fully established, including invisible displacements, only at 18 to 24 months, at the end of the sensorimotor stage.","traps":{"a":"At 4 to 6 months, in Piaget's substage of secondary circular reactions, infants recover a partly covered toy but stop reaching once it is fully hidden, so for Piaget permanence is still absent. This range tempts because violation-of-expectation studies by Renée Baillargeon (1987) suggested some awareness of hidden objects around 4 months. That is evidence of early understanding under a different method, not Piaget's full mastery.","b":"At 8 to 10 months, in Piaget's substage of coordination of secondary schemes, infants first search for a fully hidden object, so permanence has begun. But they still make the A-not-B error and cannot track invisible displacements, so it is not fully established. It tempts because many textbooks quote 8 months as 'the age of object permanence', confusing its first appearance with its completion.","d":"By 3 years the child is well into the preoperational stage (about 2 to 7 years), using symbols, language and pretend play; object permanence was completed before this stage began. Preoperational features such as egocentrism in the three mountains task and failure of conservation belong to this later period. It tempts candidates who think 'fully established' must mean some much later, safer age."}},{"q":"Conservation of liquid volume is typically mastered during which stage?","concept":"Conservation is the understanding that a quantity stays the same when only its appearance changes. In Jean Piaget's classic liquid task, a child agrees that two identical short, wide glasses hold the same amount of water; the water from one is then poured into a tall, thin glass while the child watches. A preoperational child (about 2 to 7 years) says the tall glass has more, because of centration (attending to one dimension, height), irreversibility (being unable to mentally pour it back) and a focus on end states rather than transformations. In the concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11 years) the child conserves and justifies it with three arguments: identity (nothing was added or taken away), reversibility (pour it back and it is the same) and compensation (taller but thinner). Different conservations appear at different ages within the same stage, which Piaget called horizontal decalage: number and liquid quantity usually around 6 to 7 years, mass (substance) around 7 to 8, weight around 9 to 10, and displacement volume only around 11 to 12, at the edge of formal operations. Do not confuse liquid volume (the amount of liquid) with displacement volume (how much water an object pushes aside), a common source of wrong answers. Critics showed Piaget's method underestimated children: James McGarrigle and Margaret Donaldson (1974) found that more four to six year olds conserved number when a 'naughty teddy' disarranged the counters accidentally. Option a is right because conservation of liquid quantity is a hallmark achievement of the concrete operational stage, once reversibility and decentration appear.","traps":{"b":"The preoperational stage (about 2 to 7 years) is where conservation fails, not where it is mastered. Children centre on one salient dimension, such as water height, cannot mentally reverse the pouring, and attend to end states rather than the transformation. Its other marks are symbolic play, egocentrism and animism. It tempts because the liquid task is taught mainly as a demonstration of preoperational thinking, so the two get linked in memory.","c":"The sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2 years) is when infants learn through senses and actions, moving through circular reactions to object permanence and, near its end, mental representation and deferred imitation. There is no logical reasoning about quantity at this stage. It tempts because object permanence, its main achievement, is a kind of conservation: the object persists despite disappearing, so candidates link the two ideas.","d":"The formal operational stage (from about 11 or 12 years) brings abstract, hypothetical and propositional reasoning, such as systematic testing of variables in the pendulum task. Conservation of liquid quantity is mastered years earlier. It tempts because conservation of displacement volume appears only around 11 to 12, so a candidate who confuses 'liquid volume' with displacement volume will over-shoot to this stage."}},{"q":"Vygotsky's Zone of Proximal Development is best defined as:","concept":"Lev Vygotsky, the Soviet psychologist who died in 1934, held that higher mental functions are social before they are individual: a child first solves problems with others, then internalises the process, largely through language. The Zone of Proximal Development (ZPD), best known to English readers from Mind in Society (1978), is the distance between the child's actual developmental level, shown by independent problem solving, and the potential developmental level, shown by problem solving under adult guidance or with more capable peers. Vygotsky illustrated it with two children of the same mental age, 8 years: with help, one solved problems at a 12 year level and the other only at a 9 year level, so their zones, and their readiness to learn, differed. Teaching should therefore aim slightly ahead of independent ability. The helper is often called the more knowledgeable other (MKO). David Wood, Jerome Bruner and Gail Ross (1976) named the adjustable, gradually withdrawn support 'scaffolding', a term Vygotsky himself did not use. The ZPD also underlies dynamic assessment, such as the Learning Potential Assessment Device of Reuven Feuerstein (1979), which measures how much a child gains from help rather than giving a static score. Contrast Jean Piaget: he saw development as preceding learning and treated egocentric speech as immature, while Vygotsky saw private speech as a tool for self-regulation and learning as leading development. Option c is right because the ZPD is the gap between what a child achieves independently and what the same child can achieve with guidance.","traps":{"a":"Mental age came from the scale of Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905). William Stern (1912) proposed dividing mental age by chronological age, and Lewis Terman (1916) multiplied this by 100 to give the IQ. A mental minus chronological age gap is a static ability measure. Vygotsky criticised such measures and used children of the same mental age to show their ZPDs could differ. It tempts because mental age appears in his examples.","b":"What a child can do alone is the actual developmental level, the lower boundary of the zone, not the zone itself. The ZPD begins where independent ability ends and extends to what can be achieved with help; tasks beyond even assisted reach lie outside it. It tempts because it names a real part of Vygotsky's definition, and partial definitions are a favourite exam distractor.","d":"A fixed genetic ceiling belongs to nativist and maturational thinking, such as the reaction range idea of Irving Gottesman (1963), in which genes set upper and lower limits on how far the environment can move a trait like IQ. The ZPD is the opposite kind of idea: a changing zone that shifts upward as the child learns with social help. It tempts because the word 'potential' appears in both definitions."}},{"q":"In Erikson's framework, the crisis of adolescence is:","concept":"Erik Erikson (1950), in Childhood and Society, extended Sigmund Freud's psychosexual stages into eight psychosocial stages covering the whole lifespan. Each stage poses a crisis, a tension between two poles, and its successful resolution yields a basic virtue; the order is set by the epigenetic principle, in which each stage has its own time of ascendancy. The stages, with approximate ages and virtues, are: trust versus mistrust (infancy, hope); autonomy versus shame and doubt (about 1 to 3, will); initiative versus guilt (about 3 to 6, purpose); industry versus inferiority (about 6 to 12, competence); identity versus role confusion (adolescence, about 12 to 18, fidelity); intimacy versus isolation (young adulthood, love); generativity versus stagnation (middle adulthood, care); and integrity versus despair (old age, wisdom). In adolescence, puberty, new cognitive abilities and social expectations push young people to integrate childhood identifications into a coherent sense of who they are, what they value and where they are going. Society allows a psychosocial moratorium, a period to experiment with roles before adult commitments. Failure leads to role confusion or identity diffusion, and some adopt a negative identity built on roles they were warned against. James Marcia (1966) later turned this stage into four identity statuses: achievement, moratorium, foreclosure and diffusion. In Freud's scheme adolescence is the genital stage, and latency corresponds to Erikson's industry stage. Option b is right because Erikson placed identity versus role confusion in adolescence, the crisis of forming a stable, integrated identity.","traps":{"a":"Industry versus inferiority is Erikson's fourth stage, the school years from about 6 to 12, matching Freud's latency period. Children learn to work, master skills and compare their competence with peers; success gives the virtue of competence, while repeated failure breeds inferiority. It comes just before adolescence. It tempts because it covers schoolchildren, and many candidates picture early adolescents as still being in that school-age group.","c":"Intimacy versus isolation is Erikson's sixth stage, young adulthood, roughly from the late teens to about 40. The task is to form close, committed relationships, including love and deep friendship, without losing oneself; the virtue is love. Erikson argued that true intimacy depends on first resolving identity. It tempts because romantic relationships begin in adolescence, so candidates attach the intimacy stage to teenagers.","d":"Initiative versus guilt is Erikson's third stage, the preschool or play age from about 3 to 6, matching Freud's phallic stage. Children plan activities, make up games and take initiative; excessive control or criticism leaves them guilty about their own goals. The virtue is purpose. It tempts because 'initiative' sounds like the adolescent push for independence, but the stage belongs to early childhood."}},{"q":"In Ainsworth's Strange Situation, an infant who explores freely, shows mild distress on separation, and is easily soothed on reunion is classified as:","concept":"Attachment theory began with John Bowlby (1969), who argued that infants are biologically prepared to seek closeness to a caregiver, who acts as a secure base for exploration and a safe haven when the infant is frightened. Mary Ainsworth, who had observed mothers and infants in Uganda and Baltimore, devised the Strange Situation (Ainsworth and Barbara Wittig, 1969; fully reported in Patterns of Attachment, 1978) to measure this in 12 to 18 month olds. It is a roughly 20 minute laboratory sequence of eight short episodes in which the caregiver leaves twice and a stranger enters, testing exploration, stranger anxiety, separation distress and, most important, reunion behaviour. Three organised patterns emerged. Secure (type B, about two thirds of middle-class American samples) infants explore freely with the caregiver present, may be mildly or clearly upset when she leaves, greet her actively on return, are quickly comforted and go back to play. Insecure-avoidant (type A) infants show little distress and ignore or turn away from the caregiver on reunion. Insecure-resistant or ambivalent (type C) infants are very distressed, seek contact on reunion yet resist it angrily, and are hard to settle. Mary Main and Judith Solomon (1990) later added disorganised (type D). Ainsworth linked security to caregiver sensitivity, meaning prompt and appropriate responses to the infant's signals. A cross-cultural meta-analysis by Marinus van IJzendoorn and Pieter Kroonenberg (1988) found secure attachment the most common pattern across countries. Option a is right because free exploration, mild separation distress and easy soothing on reunion define secure (B) attachment.","traps":{"b":"Insecure-avoidant (type A) infants explore with little reference to the caregiver, show little or no distress when she leaves, treat the stranger much like her, and on reunion ignore, look away from or move away from her. Ainsworth linked this to caregivers who rebuff bids for comfort. It tempts because avoidant infants look calm and independent, which can be mistaken for the mild distress of secure infants.","c":"Insecure-resistant or ambivalent (type C) infants are clingy and explore little even before separation, become intensely distressed when the caregiver leaves, and on reunion seek contact but resist it, for example demanding to be picked up and then struggling to get down. They are hard to soothe. Ainsworth linked this to inconsistent caregiving. It tempts because these infants do seek the caregiver on reunion, as secure infants do.","d":"Disorganised (type D), added by Mary Main and Judith Solomon (1990), describes infants with no coherent strategy: contradictory approach and avoidance, freezing, dazed expressions or apprehension towards the caregiver, often linked to maltreatment or unresolved parental trauma. The stem describes a smooth, coherent, well-regulated pattern, which is the opposite. It tempts because D is the newest and most discussed category in clinical teaching."}},{"q":"Kohlberg's pre-conventional level is characterised by:","concept":"Lawrence Kohlberg (1958), extending the work of Jean Piaget (1932) on children's moral judgement, studied moral reasoning by presenting dilemmas such as the Heinz dilemma: should a man steal an overpriced drug to save his dying wife? He scored the reasoning behind the answer, not the answer itself, and proposed three levels, each with two stages, in an invariant sequence. The pre-conventional level, typical of most children under about 9 years, judges right and wrong by consequences to oneself. Stage 1 is the obedience and punishment orientation: an act is wrong if it is punished, and authority must be obeyed. Stage 2 is individualism and exchange (instrumental purpose): right is what serves one's own interests, with fair deals such as 'you scratch my back, I scratch yours'. The conventional level, typical of most adolescents and adults, rests on social approval and rules: Stage 3 is the good interpersonal relationships or 'good boy, nice girl' orientation, and Stage 4 is law and order, maintaining the social system. The post-conventional level, reached by a minority of adults, has Stage 5, social contract and individual rights, and Stage 6, universal ethical principles such as justice and human dignity. Carol Gilligan (1982) argued that this justice-based scheme undervalues an ethic of care. Piaget's earlier distinction was between heteronomous morality (rules fixed by authority, judged by consequences) and autonomous morality (rules as flexible agreements, judged by intentions). Option d is right because obedience to authority (Stage 1) and self-interest orientation (Stage 2) together define the pre-conventional level.","traps":{"a":"Universal ethical principles are Stage 6, the top of Kohlberg's post-conventional level: right is defined by self-chosen principles of justice, equality and human dignity, which can override unjust laws, as in the civil disobedience of Mahatma Gandhi. Kohlberg found it so rare that he later dropped it from his scoring manual. It tempts because it is the most quoted stage, and the two extremes of a scale are easily swapped.","b":"Social contract reasoning is Stage 5, the first post-conventional stage. Laws are seen as social agreements that serve human welfare and protect rights, so they can be changed democratically when they fail to do so. It tempts because 'contract' sounds like the deal-making of Stage 2 ('you help me, I help you'), but Stage 2 exchange is self-interested, whereas Stage 5 reasons from the perspective of society as a whole.","c":"Conformity to interpersonal expectations is Stage 3 of the conventional level, the 'good boy, nice girl' orientation: being good means meeting the expectations of family and friends, showing loyalty and earning approval, with intentions now counting. Stage 4 then shifts to law and order. It tempts because pleasing others and obeying authority both look like external control, but Stage 3 seeks approval while Stage 1 avoids punishment."}},{"q":"Harlow's experiments with infant rhesus monkeys demonstrated that:","concept":"Before the 1950s the dominant explanation of the infant's tie to the mother was secondary drive theory, held by learning theorists such as John Dollard and Neal Miller (1950): the mother becomes valued because she is repeatedly paired with feeding and the reduction of hunger, sometimes called 'cupboard love'. Harry Harlow tested this in his 1958 paper 'The Nature of Love'. Infant rhesus monkeys were separated from their mothers and given two surrogate mothers: a bare wire-mesh figure and a figure covered in soft terry cloth. In the key condition only the wire mother held a feeding bottle. The infants went to the wire mother only to feed and spent most of the day clinging to the cloth mother. When frightened by a mechanical toy they ran to the cloth mother, and in a strange room they explored more when she was present, using her as a secure base. Harlow concluded that contact comfort, not feeding, is the main basis of attachment, supporting the ethological theory of John Bowlby (1958, 1969). Later isolation studies by Harlow with Margaret Harlow and Stephen Suomi showed that monkeys reared without mothers or peers became severely disturbed: rocking, self-biting, social withdrawal, and later poor sexual and maternal behaviour, with 'motherless mothers' neglecting or abusing their infants. Younger 'therapist' monkeys partly reversed these effects. The work raised major ethical concerns and is linked with Bowlby's maternal deprivation hypothesis and with imprinting in birds. Option b is right because Harlow showed that attachment rests on contact comfort, independent of feeding.","traps":{"a":"This is secondary drive or 'cupboard love' theory, held by learning theorists such as John Dollard and Neal Miller (1950) and echoing Sigmund Freud's oral-stage view that love follows feeding. It is exactly what Harlow refuted: monkeys fed by the wire mother still preferred the non-feeding cloth mother. It tempts because the option restates the hypothesis the experiment set out to test, in the respectable language of reinforcement.","c":"Imprinting is the rapid, early following of the first moving object seen, shown by Konrad Lorenz (1935) in greylag goslings that followed him. It occurs during a critical period soon after hatching in precocial birds. Harlow studied primates and found attachment builds over weeks through contact comfort, not instantly. It tempts because Lorenz and Harlow are taught together as animal studies of attachment, and Bowlby drew on both.","d":"Harlow's isolation studies showed the opposite. Monkeys raised with adequate food but no mother or peers developed rocking, self-biting, fearfulness and social withdrawal, and as adults were sexually incompetent and often neglectful or abusive mothers. Nutrition alone did not protect them. It tempts because in the surrogate study the monkeys did survive and grow physically, so a candidate may wrongly conclude that feeding was sufficient."}},{"q":"False-belief tasks like the Sally-Anne test assess:","concept":"Theory of mind (ToM) is the ability to attribute mental states (beliefs, desires, intentions, knowledge) to oneself and others, and to use them to predict behaviour. The term comes from David Premack and Guy Woodruff (1978), who asked whether chimpanzees have a theory of mind. The acid test is false belief: understanding that someone can hold a belief that is wrong, because a true belief cannot be told apart from simply knowing reality. Heinz Wimmer and Josef Perner (1983) created the first unexpected-transfer task, with a boy, Maxi, whose chocolate is moved while he is away. Simon Baron-Cohen, Alan Leslie and Uta Frith (1985) adapted it with dolls: Sally puts her marble in her basket and leaves; Anne moves it to her box; the child is asked where Sally will look first. Most typically developing four year olds and most children with Down syndrome said the basket, but most autistic children, despite a higher mental age than the Down syndrome group, pointed to the box, supporting the idea of a ToM deficit in autism, later called mindblindness. The unexpected-contents or Smarties task tests the same concept. Children usually pass first-order false belief by about 4 years and second-order tasks ('John thinks that Mary thinks...') by about 6 or 7. Looking-time studies suggest implicit sensitivity to false belief even in infancy. Related but distinct concepts include empathy and visual perspective taking. Option a is right because false-belief tasks like Sally-Anne were designed to assess theory of mind, the capacity to represent another person's beliefs.","traps":{"b":"Working memory is the limited system for holding and manipulating information over seconds, modelled by Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch (1974) with a central executive, phonological loop and visuospatial sketchpad. It is tested with digit span, n-back or Corsi blocks. False-belief tasks do place some load on working memory and executive function, which tempts candidates, but they were designed to test belief attribution, not memory capacity.","c":"Conservation of number is a Jean Piaget task: two equal rows of counters are set out, one row is spread out, and the child is asked whether both still have the same number. Preoperational children say the longer row has more; concrete operational children conserve. It tempts because both are classic developmental tasks using objects and a 'trick' question, and both are studied around preschool age.","d":"Object permanence is the sensorimotor achievement, described by Jean Piaget (1937), of knowing that objects continue to exist when out of sight, tested by hidden-object search and the A-not-B task. In Sally-Anne the child already knows where the marble is; the question is what Sally believes. It tempts because both tasks involve a hidden object, but theory of mind concerns a hidden mental state."}},{"q":"In Thomas and Chess's temperament categories, an infant showing initial withdrawal, slow adaptation, mild reactions, and low-to-moderate activity is best classified as:","concept":"Temperament is the early-appearing, biologically based style of a child's behaviour and emotional reactivity, the raw material from which personality later develops. Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess, with Herbert Birch, began the New York Longitudinal Study in 1956, following children from infancy into adulthood through detailed parent interviews. They rated nine dimensions: activity level, rhythmicity (regularity of sleep and feeding), approach or withdrawal to new situations, adaptability, threshold of responsiveness, intensity of reaction, quality of mood, distractibility, and attention span or persistence. Clusters of these yielded three types. The easy child (about 40 percent) is regular, approaches novelty, adapts quickly, and shows mild to moderate, mostly positive reactions. The difficult child (about 10 percent) is irregular, withdraws from novelty, adapts slowly, and reacts intensely and often negatively. The slow-to-warm-up child (about 15 percent) shows low activity, initial withdrawal, slow adaptation and mild, somewhat negative reactions, but warms up with repeated, unpressured exposure. About 35 percent did not fit any type. Their key clinical idea is goodness of fit: outcomes depend on the match between the child's temperament and the demands of parents and environment, not on temperament alone. Neighbouring models include the behavioural inhibition studied by Jerome Kagan (shy, wary children distressed by novelty), the EAS model of Arnold Buss and Robert Plomin (1984), with emotionality, activity and sociability, and the dimensions of Mary Rothbart (1981), later summarised as surgency, negative affectivity and effortful control. Option d is right because initial withdrawal, slow adaptation, mild reactions and low to moderate activity make up the slow-to-warm-up profile.","traps":{"a":"The easy child, about 40 percent of the New York Longitudinal Study sample, has regular sleep and feeding rhythms, approaches new people and situations, adapts quickly and shows a mostly positive mood of mild to moderate intensity. The stem's mild reactions fit, but initial withdrawal and slow adaptation do not. It tempts because mild reactions and low activity sound like an easy, undemanding baby.","b":"The difficult child, about 10 percent of the sample, also withdraws from novelty and adapts slowly, but reacts with high intensity and frequent negative mood, and has irregular rhythms, for example unpredictable sleep and feeding with loud crying. The stem specifies mild reactions, which rules this type out. It tempts because withdrawal and slow adaptation are shared features, so candidates stop reading at the first match.","c":"'Inconsistent' is not one of the categories of Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess. Their three types were easy, difficult and slow-to-warm-up; the roughly 35 percent of children whose traits did not cluster into any type were left unclassified or described as mixed. It tempts because 'inconsistent' sounds like a natural label for such mixed children, and because inconsistent caregiving appears in attachment theory."}},{"q":"Marcia's identity status of 'foreclosure' is defined as:","concept":"James Marcia (1966) made the adolescent identity crisis of Erik Erikson (1950), identity versus role confusion, measurable. Using a semi-structured Identity Status Interview about occupation and ideology (religious and political beliefs), he rated two variables. Exploration (originally called crisis) is active questioning and trying out of alternatives. Commitment is a firm personal investment in a set of values, goals or roles. Crossing the two gives four identity statuses. Identity achievement means exploration followed by commitment: the person has weighed alternatives and chosen. Moratorium means active exploration without commitment yet: the person is in the middle of the crisis, often anxious, and it is the status most likely to lead to achievement. Foreclosure means commitment without exploration: the person has adopted values and goals handed down by parents or other authorities without examining alternatives, for example joining the family business or a career chosen by the family simply because it was always expected. Foreclosed adolescents tend to be conventional, respectful of authority and outwardly secure, but rigid when their beliefs are challenged. Identity diffusion means neither exploration nor commitment: the person is directionless and not engaged with the question. The statuses are not strict stages; people can move between them, and an achieved identity can later reopen into moratorium. A memory aid: foreclosure closes the question before it is ever opened. Exams scramble the four definitions, so learn them as a 2 by 2 grid of exploration (yes or no) against commitment (yes or no). Option a is right because foreclosure is defined as commitment without exploration.","traps":{"b":"Exploration without commitment defines moratorium, a term borrowed from Erik Erikson's psychosocial moratorium. The adolescent is actively searching, trying out beliefs, courses or careers, but has not yet settled on any. It is often an anxious period, yet the status most likely to lead to identity achievement. It tempts because foreclosure and moratorium are the two 'mixed' cells of Marcia's grid and are easily swapped.","c":"Both exploration and commitment define identity achievement, the most mature status: the person has seriously considered alternatives and then made a personal commitment. Achieved individuals tend to show higher self-esteem and more resistance to pressure to conform. It tempts because a foreclosed person also shows firm commitment, so a candidate who notices only the commitment overlooks the missing exploration that separates the two statuses.","d":"Neither exploration nor commitment defines identity diffusion, the least developed status. The person has no firm values or goals and is not actively searching, often drifting or appearing apathetic. It corresponds most closely to Erikson's role confusion. It tempts because foreclosure and diffusion both lack exploration, so a candidate who remembers only that foreclosed people did not explore may pick this option."}},{"q":"Bronfenbrenner's bioecological model places the school–family relationship within which system?","concept":"Urie Bronfenbrenner (1979), in The Ecology of Human Development, argued that development cannot be understood by studying a child alone in a laboratory; it happens inside nested environmental systems, often drawn as concentric circles. The microsystem is any setting in which the child directly takes part and has face-to-face relationships: family, classroom, peer group, neighbourhood play area. The mesosystem is the set of interrelations between two or more microsystems in which the child participates, such as links between home and school (parental involvement, parent-teacher meetings, how a quarrel at home affects classroom behaviour) or between family and peers. The exosystem contains settings the child does not enter but which affect the child indirectly, such as a parent's workplace, the school board, or local health and welfare services. The macrosystem is the overarching culture: values, laws, ideology, economic system and customs, for example norms about joint families or corporal punishment. The chronosystem adds time: life transitions such as parental divorce or a sibling's birth, and historical change. Bronfenbrenner later developed the approach into the bioecological model (with Pamela Morris, 1998), stressing proximal processes and the Process-Person-Context-Time (PPCT) model, so the child's own biology and characteristics also shape development. A simple test: if the child is inside the setting it is micro; if the question is about a link between two such settings it is meso; if the child is absent it is exo; if it is culture-wide it is macro. Option a is right because the school-family relationship links two microsystems, which is the definition of the mesosystem.","traps":{"b":"The microsystem is a single setting in which the child participates directly, such as the family by itself or the classroom by itself, with relationships running both ways. The school and the family are each microsystems, but the relationship between them is not. It tempts because both named settings are microsystems, so a candidate who sees 'school' and 'family' picks micro without noticing that the stem asks about their connection.","c":"The exosystem is a setting the child does not participate in that still affects the child, such as a parent's workplace (long shifts, job loss), the school management committee or local government services. The child is a direct participant in both home and school, so their link cannot be exosystem. It tempts because the relationship seems one step removed from the child, which feels like an outer layer.","d":"The macrosystem is the broad cultural blueprint: values, laws, religion, economic conditions and social customs, such as beliefs about education or discipline in a given society. It shapes all the inner systems but is not a specific relationship between two settings in a child's life. It tempts because school and family are both social institutions, and talk of institutions sounds like culture-level analysis."}},{"q":"The visual cliff experiment (Gibson & Walk, 1960) demonstrated:","concept":"Eleanor Gibson and Richard Walk (1960) built the visual cliff to ask whether depth perception is learned or present early. It is a raised table covered with strong, clear glass. On the shallow side a checkerboard pattern sits directly under the glass; on the deep side the same pattern lies some distance below, creating the visual impression of a drop, though the glass makes it perfectly safe. In their study, infants aged about 6 to 14 months were placed on a central board and their mothers called them from each side in turn. Most crawled readily to the mother across the shallow side, but only a very small number ventured onto the deep side; many cried or peered down and backed away. Young animals that walk soon after birth, such as chicks, kids and lambs, avoided the deep side as soon as they could stand. Gibson and Walk took this as evidence that depth perception is largely innate, or at least present by the time locomotion begins. The limitation is that human infants cannot be tested until they crawl, by which time they have months of visual experience. Joseph Campos and colleagues (1970) found that 2 month olds placed on the deep side showed heart-rate slowing, a sign of interest rather than fear, while later studies of older, crawling infants found heart-rate acceleration, suggesting that fear of heights develops with locomotor experience. Option a is right because the study is classically taken to show depth perception, argued to be innate, in human infants by crawling age.","traps":{"b":"Imprinting is rapid, early attachment to the first moving object, demonstrated by Konrad Lorenz (1935) in goslings and studied in precocial birds during a critical period. The visual cliff measured avoidance of an apparent drop, not bonding, and mothers were used only to call the infant across. It tempts because the mother's presence in the procedure, and the testing of newborn chicks and lambs, make it look like an attachment study.","c":"Object permanence, the understanding that objects exist when out of sight, was described by Jean Piaget (1937) and tested by hidden-object search; Renée Baillargeon (1987) later used looking-time methods with younger infants. The visual cliff hides nothing and tests whether infants perceive and avoid depth. It tempts because both are landmark infant-cognition findings around the middle of the first year, and 6 months sounds like the right sort of age.","d":"The visual cliff is a purely visual task; it tested whether infants use visual depth cues and did not study how sound and vision are combined. Audio-visual integration, such as infants matching lip movements to speech sounds, is studied with preferential-looking methods. It tempts because the mother called the infant, so sound was present in the procedure, but it served only to motivate crawling."}},{"q":"Babinski reflex (toes fan upward to plantar stimulation) is normal until approximately:","concept":"Newborns arrive with a set of primitive reflexes, automatic responses organised in the brainstem and spinal cord. As the cerebral cortex and the corticospinal (pyramidal) tract mature and myelinate during the first year, higher centres suppress most of them, so their timely disappearance is a sign of healthy neurological development. The Babinski reflex, described by the French neurologist Joseph Babinski (1896), is elicited by firmly stroking the outer edge of the sole from heel towards toes: in infants the big toe extends (moves upward) and the other toes fan out. This extensor plantar response is normal in infancy because the corticospinal tract is immature. As it matures, the response changes to the adult flexor pattern, with the toes curling downward. Developmental psychology textbooks place its normal disappearance at around 8 to 12 months, usually summarised as about one year; some medical texts allow up to 2 years. In older children and adults, an upgoing toe is the Babinski sign, a classic marker of an upper motor neuron lesion, alongside spasticity, hyperreflexia and clonus. Compare other primitive reflexes: Moro (startle with arms flung out then brought in, fading by about 4 to 6 months); palmar grasp (fading around 3 to 4 months); stepping (about 2 months); and the asymmetric tonic neck reflex (about 4 to 6 months). Do not confuse it with the plantar grasp, in which pressing the ball of the foot makes the toes curl. Option b is right because the upgoing Babinski response is considered normal until approximately 12 months of age.","traps":{"a":"At 3 months the extensor plantar response is still entirely normal, because corticospinal myelination has a long way to go. Three to four months is when other reflexes, such as the palmar grasp, typically fade, and the Moro reflex fades by about 4 to 6 months. It tempts because candidates who know that primitive reflexes disappear in the first few months apply that rule to all of them.","c":"At 6 months an upgoing plantar response is still normal, so 6 months lies inside the expected window rather than at its end. Six months is about when the Moro and asymmetric tonic neck reflexes fade, which is why it sounds like a reflex deadline. It tempts as a plausible midpoint for candidates who remember that the Babinski reflex outlasts the earliest reflexes but not by how long.","d":"Around 2 years is usually given as the outer limit: some medical texts accept an upgoing toe up to this age, but persistence beyond it, or its return in an adult, points to an upper motor neuron (corticospinal) lesion, as in cerebral palsy. Developmental psychology texts give about 1 year as the normal end point. It tempts because 24 months is the pathology threshold and appears in medical sources."}},{"q":"Chomsky's Language Acquisition Device theory argues most directly that:","concept":"Noam Chomsky transformed the study of language with Syntactic Structures (1957) and his 1959 review of Verbal Behavior (1957) by B. F. Skinner. Skinner had explained language as operant behaviour shaped by reinforcement, with units such as mands (requests) and tacts (labels). Chomsky argued this could not work. Children hear a limited, often fragmentary and error-filled sample of speech, yet within a few years they master an abstract rule system and can produce and understand sentences they have never heard. Parents rarely correct grammar, mostly correcting truth or meaning instead. This is the poverty of the stimulus argument. Chomsky therefore proposed, in Aspects of the Theory of Syntax (1965), an innate Language Acquisition Device (LAD): a biologically prepared, species-specific capacity containing Universal Grammar, the principles shared by all human languages, which the child uses to work out the particular grammar of the language around them. Supporting evidence includes the similar timing and sequence of milestones across cultures, overregularisation errors such as 'goed' and 'mouses' that children could not have imitated, the critical period hypothesis of Eric Lenneberg (1967), and the emergence of grammar in new sign languages created by deaf children. Neighbouring theories: Skinner's behaviourism (reinforcement and imitation), the social interactionist view of Jerome Bruner (1983), who proposed a Language Acquisition Support System (LASS) provided by caregivers, and the cognitive view of Jean Piaget, in which language follows thought. Steven Pinker (1994) popularised the nativist view as The Language Instinct. Option c is right because the LAD claims that children have an innate, biologically prepared capacity for grammar.","traps":{"a":"Pure operant conditioning is the position of B. F. Skinner (1957) in Verbal Behavior: language is learned through reinforcement of utterances, shaping and imitation, with categories such as mands, tacts and echoics. Chomsky's 1959 review attacked exactly this view, citing children's novel sentences and overregularisation errors that were never reinforced. It tempts because Skinner and Chomsky are always taught as a pair, and the option names the rival theory.","b":"Chomsky argued the opposite: grammar is not explicitly taught. Parents rarely correct grammatical errors, tending to respond to the truth of what a child says, and most cannot state the rules they themselves follow. Children still acquire complex syntax on a similar timetable. It tempts because adults assume children learn language like a school subject, and because the word 'grammar' links it superficially to Chomsky's Universal Grammar.","d":"This is not part of Chomsky's theory and is not an established finding. Bilingual children reach the major language milestones within the normal range, though vocabulary in each separate language may be smaller than a monolingual child's; total vocabulary across both languages is comparable. If anything, an innate LAD predicts that a child can acquire more than one language naturally. It tempts as a strong, absolute-sounding claim about language development."}},{"q":"Mary Main's addition of the 'disorganised' attachment classification (D) was based on infants showing:","concept":"The Strange Situation of Mary Ainsworth (1978) classified infants into three organised patterns: secure (B), insecure-avoidant (A) and insecure-resistant or ambivalent (C). Each is a coherent strategy: avoidant infants minimise attachment behaviour, resistant infants maximise it, and secure infants use the caregiver flexibly. Yet some infants, especially in high-risk samples, could not be classified or were forced into the secure category. Mary Main and Judith Solomon (1986, 1990) reviewed these cases and found that what they shared was a breakdown of any consistent strategy, usually brief and seen on reunion. They named this disorganised or disoriented attachment (D). Indicators include contradictory behaviour at the same time or in quick succession (approaching the parent with the head turned away, crying for the parent then freezing), undirected or incomplete movements, stereotypies, freezing or stilling with a dazed expression, and direct signs of apprehension of the parent. Main and Erik Hesse (1990) explained it through frightened or frightening parental behaviour, later summarised as 'fright without solution': the infant's source of safety is also the source of alarm. Disorganised attachment is over-represented among maltreated infants and those whose parents show unresolved loss or trauma, and it predicts later controlling behaviour, dissociation and externalising problems. In low-risk samples it is found in roughly 15 percent of infants. The parental categories of the Adult Attachment Interview, which Main helped develop (autonomous, dismissing, preoccupied, unresolved), parallel infant B, A, C and D. Option b is right because contradictory, freezing or apprehensive reunion behaviour, often with maltreatment histories, is the basis of the D classification.","traps":{"a":"Consistent avoidance of the caregiver defines insecure-avoidant attachment (type A) in the scheme of Mary Ainsworth (1978). These infants show little distress on separation and ignore or turn away from the caregiver on reunion. Avoidance is an organised strategy for staying near a rejecting caregiver without provoking rejection. The word 'consistent' is the giveaway: D is defined by the breakdown of any consistent strategy. It tempts because avoidance is also an insecure pattern.","c":"Easy soothing on reunion is the hallmark of secure attachment (type B): the infant greets the caregiver, accepts comfort quickly and returns to play. Disorganised infants are not readily soothed and show confused or fearful responses. It tempts because some disorganised infants had been classified as secure before Mary Main and Judith Solomon introduced the D category, so the two were historically confused in coding.","d":"Resistance defines insecure-resistant or ambivalent attachment (type C), but these infants are highly distressed: they cry intensely on separation, then seek contact on reunion while angrily resisting it. Resistance without distress matches no recognised category. It tempts because resistance is a genuine Strange Situation behaviour, and 'without distress' sounds like the odd, incoherent picture of disorganisation."}},{"q":"According to Piaget, hypothetico-deductive reasoning first emerges in the:","concept":"Jean Piaget's fourth and final stage, formal operations, begins around 11 or 12 years. Its classic description is in The Growth of Logical Thinking from Childhood to Adolescence by Bärbel Inhelder and Jean Piaget (1958). Thinking is freed from concrete reality: adolescents can reason about abstractions, possibilities and propositions that are contrary to fact. The signature is hypothetico-deductive reasoning: starting from a general theory, the adolescent deduces specific hypotheses ('if length matters, then a shorter string should swing faster') and tests them systematically, varying one factor at a time while holding the others constant. In the pendulum problem, children are asked what determines how fast a pendulum swings: string length, weight, height of release or force of push. Concrete operational children vary several factors together and draw muddled conclusions; formal operational adolescents isolate variables and find that only length matters. Related abilities are propositional logic (judging the logic of statements regardless of their content), combinatorial reasoning (the colourless chemicals task) and abstract thinking about justice, identity and the future. David Elkind (1967) linked this stage to adolescent egocentrism, with the imaginary audience and personal fable. Contrast the concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11 years): logical operations such as conservation, classification, seriation and transitive inference, applied to concrete objects, with mostly inductive reasoning from specific instances. Later research showed that many adults use formal reasoning only in familiar domains, and Piaget himself (1972) accepted this. Option a is right because hypothetico-deductive reasoning first emerges in the formal operational stage.","traps":{"b":"The preoperational stage (about 2 to 7 years) brings symbolic thought, language and pretend play, but reasoning is intuitive, egocentric and centred on one dimension, so children fail conservation. Piaget described their reasoning as transductive, from one particular to another (for example, 'I haven't had my nap, so it isn't afternoon'). It tempts only candidates who confuse 'hypothetical' with the make-believe of pretend play.","c":"The concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11 years) brings true logical operations: conservation, classification, seriation and transitive inference. But the logic is tied to concrete, present objects and mostly works inductively from specific cases to general rules. Concrete thinkers struggle with purely hypothetical premises and do not test variables systematically. It tempts because it is the first stage of logical thought, so candidates assume all logic, including deduction, begins here.","d":"The sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2 years) is the period in which infants understand the world through senses and actions, moving through circular reactions to object permanence and, by its end, mental representation and deferred imitation. There is no language-based or abstract reasoning yet. It tempts only as the opposite extreme, for candidates who confuse the infant's trial-and-error experiments in tertiary circular reactions with hypothesis testing."}},{"q":"The Strange Situation classification typically holds across cultures, but in northern German samples, classification (A) is over-represented because:","concept":"The Strange Situation is a laboratory procedure devised by Mary Ainsworth and Barbara Wittig (1969) and reported in full by Ainsworth, Mary Blehar, Everett Waters and Sally Wall (1978) after the Baltimore study. In eight short episodes, an infant of about 12 to 18 months meets a stranger, is left twice by the mother, and is reunited with her twice. Coders focus mainly on reunion behaviour. Type B (secure) infants are upset by separation, seek the mother on reunion and are quickly comforted. Type A (insecure-avoidant) infants show little visible distress and ignore or avoid the mother when she returns. Type C (insecure-resistant or ambivalent) infants are very distressed and both seek and resist contact. Type D (disorganised) was added later by Mary Main and Judith Solomon. Marinus van IJzendoorn and Pieter Kroonenberg (1988) pooled studies from eight countries: secure attachment was the most common pattern everywhere, and variation within cultures exceeded variation between them. The insecure split did differ. Klaus and Karin Grossmann's sample from Bielefeld in northern Germany had about half the infants classed as avoidant, while Japanese and Israeli kibbutz samples showed more resistant (C) infants. The Grossmanns explained the German pattern through parental values: mothers encouraged early self-reliance and discouraged clinging, so infants showed little distress and little reunion-seeking. This supports the imposed etic critique: an American coding scheme can misread a cultural norm as insecurity. Option a is right because the avoidant excess reflects a cultural emphasis on early independence, which lowers observable distress and reunion-seeking, not a defect in the infants.","traps":{"b":"This option treats the German pattern as a biological deficit. Attachment classification describes the quality of a relationship, not an inborn amount of attachment, and twin studies of infant attachment security generally find little genetic influence, with caregiving and shared environment mattering more. John Bowlby (1969) saw the capacity to attach as a universal evolved system. It tempts because 'genetically' sounds scientific, but it ignores the cultural explanation the Grossmanns gave.","c":"The Grossmanns used the standard eight-episode Strange Situation, so a modified protocol cannot explain the avoidant excess. Method critiques do exist elsewhere: Japanese infants who were rarely separated from their mothers became extremely distressed, which suggests the procedure is not culturally neutral. That debate concerns validity across cultures, not a change of protocol in Germany. It tempts because a methodological explanation sounds like a careful researcher's answer.","d":"Infants cannot 'pass directly' into adult attachment. Adult attachment is a separate research line, assessed by the Adult Attachment Interview of Carol George, Nancy Kaplan and Mary Main (secure-autonomous, dismissing, preoccupied, unresolved) and by Cindy Hazan and Phillip Shaver's (1987) work on romantic attachment. Bowlby described internal working models carried forward from infancy, not skipped stages. It tempts only as an impressive-sounding technical term."}},{"q":"Mahler's separation-individuation phase 'rapprochement' (~15–24 months) is characterised by:","concept":"Margaret Mahler, with Fred Pine and Anni Bergman, set out her theory in The Psychological Birth of the Human Infant (1975), based on observing mothers and young children. Mahler argued that psychological birth, the sense of being a separate individual, unfolds gradually over the first three years. She first described a normal autistic phase in the first weeks (a concept she later questioned), then a normal symbiotic phase (about 2 to 5 months) in which the infant behaves as if fused with the mother. The separation-individuation phase follows in four subphases. Differentiation or 'hatching' (about 5 to 10 months) brings scanning of strangers' faces and stranger reactions. Practising (about 10 to 16 months) is the toddler's 'love affair with the world' as crawling and walking carry the child away from the mother, returning briefly to her for emotional refuelling. Rapprochement (about 15 to 24 months) begins when the toddler realises being separate and small. Separation anxiety returns, and the child swings between darting away to explore and shadowing the mother for comfort, the 'rapprochement crisis'. The fourth subphase, consolidation of individuality and the beginnings of emotional object constancy (about 24 to 36 months), gives a stable inner image of the mother that survives absence and frustration. Later writers such as James Masterson linked unresolved rapprochement conflict to borderline pathology. Option d is right because rapprochement is defined by the ambivalent swing between independent exploration and returning to the caregiver for emotional refuelling.","traps":{"a":"Symbiotic merger describes Mahler's normal symbiotic phase, roughly 2 to 5 months, when the infant behaves as though infant and mother form one omnipotent system within a shared boundary. It comes before separation-individuation begins, so rapprochement has long left it behind. It tempts because rapprochement involves a renewed wish to be close to the mother, which can look like a return to merger.","b":"Stranger anxiety belongs to the differentiation subphase (about 5 to 10 months), when the infant scans and compares strangers' faces with the mother's, a behaviour Mahler called 'customs inspection'. René Spitz described the same reaction as eight-month anxiety. It appears months before rapprochement. It tempts because rapprochement brings heightened separation anxiety, and candidates mix up separation anxiety with stranger anxiety.","c":"Full object constancy is not reached in rapprochement. Mahler placed only the beginnings of emotional object constancy in the fourth subphase (about 24 to 36 months) and saw it as an open-ended achievement. It means holding a stable, unified inner image of the mother, good and bad together, even when she is absent. It tempts because it is the goal rapprochement leads toward, and it echoes Jean Piaget's object permanence."}},{"q":"The 'still-face paradigm' (Tronick) demonstrates:","concept":"The still-face paradigm was introduced by Edward Tronick, with Heidelise Als, Lauren Adamson, Susan Wise and T. Berry Brazelton (1978). It has three short episodes, each about two minutes. First the mother and infant play face to face as usual. Then the mother adopts a still, neutral face: she keeps looking at the infant but does not smile, talk or touch. Finally normal play resumes in a reunion episode. During the still face, even infants of a few months first try to re-engage the mother by smiling, cooing and reaching. When these bids fail, they look away, lose postural control, comfort themselves and show negative affect. In the reunion some distress carries over, showing that the infant registers the breakdown. This still-face effect led Tronick to his mutual regulation model: infant and caregiver jointly regulate emotion, with frequent mismatches followed by repair, and repeated repair builds the infant's trust and coping. Infants of depressed mothers, studied by Tiffany Field and others, show more withdrawn patterns. Related ideas an examiner may substitute include Colwyn Trevarthen's primary intersubjectivity, Daniel Stern's (1985) affect attunement, and social referencing, in which a baby from late in the first year checks a parent's face before acting, as on the visual cliff. All share the theme that infants are active social partners. Option c is right because the infant's active attempts to restore the interaction show that even very young infants expect and seek affective reciprocity.","traps":{"a":"This is the view the paradigm refutes. If infants were passive recipients of caregiver mood, they would simply mirror the flat face and become quiet. Instead they escalate smiles, vocalisations and gestures to repair the interaction, and only then withdraw. It tempts because infants do pick up caregiver emotion, as studies of maternal depression show, but picking up emotion is different from passively receiving it.","b":"Face recognition starts long before 12 months. Robert Fantz's preferential-looking studies in the early 1960s showed that newborns look longer at face-like patterns, and within days babies prefer their mother's face to a stranger's. The still-face effect itself depends on infants of a few months reading the caregiver's expression. It tempts because face processing keeps improving through the first year, so a late cutoff sounds plausible.","d":"Distressed infants seek their attachment figure, not strangers. Mary Ainsworth's Strange Situation showed that the caregiver serves as a secure base and safe haven, and wariness of strangers grows from about 6 to 8 months. In the still-face reunion infants return to engaging the mother. It tempts by reversing a real observation: during the still face the infant turns away from the mother, which can be misread as preferring someone else."}},{"q":"Which scale is used to assess infant developmental level from birth to 42 months across cognitive, language, and motor domains?","concept":"Infant and toddler assessment cannot rely on verbal instructions or reading, so it uses structured play, observation and caregiver report, and it yields developmental quotients or composite scores rather than IQ. The best known instrument was created by the American psychologist Nancy Bayley. The first edition, the Bayley Scales of Infant Development (1969), had a Mental Scale, a Motor Scale and an Infant Behaviour Record. The third edition (2006) was renamed the Bayley Scales of Infant and Toddler Development and organised into five domains: cognitive, language (receptive and expressive) and motor (fine and gross), tested directly, plus social-emotional and adaptive behaviour, rated by the parent. The current Bayley-4 (2019) keeps this structure. Its age range, from the first month of life to 42 months, matches the stem. In India, Pramila Phatak standardised the Bayley on Baroda infants, which led to the Developmental Assessment Scales for Indian Infants (DASII), covering birth to 30 months. Neighbouring tools an examiner may use: the Gesell Developmental Schedules of Arnold Gesell; the Denver Developmental Screening Test (now Denver II), a brief screener rather than a full assessment; and the Vineland Social Maturity Scale of Edgar Doll, adapted in India by A. J. Malin, which measures social and adaptive competence. The preschool intelligence tests in the other options all begin at about 2 years or later. Option d is right because the Bayley is the standard individually administered scale covering birth to 42 months across cognitive, language and motor domains.","traps":{"a":"The WPPSI was introduced by David Wechsler in 1967 as a downward extension of his children's scale; the current WPPSI-IV covers ages 2 years 6 months to 7 years 7 months. It yields a Full Scale IQ and index scores such as verbal comprehension. It cannot assess a newborn. It tempts because 'Preschool and Primary' sounds like early childhood and Wechsler scales are the most familiar test family.","b":"The McCarthy Scales of Children's Abilities, published by Dorothea McCarthy in 1972, cover roughly 2 years 6 months to 8 years 6 months. They yield a General Cognitive Index plus verbal, perceptual-performance, quantitative, memory and motor scales. The motor scale and child-friendly tasks make it tempting, but it starts far past birth, measures preschool and early school abilities, and is now rarely used.","c":"The Stanford-Binet began as Lewis Terman's 1916 American revision of the Binet-Simon scale and brought the IQ into wide use. The fifth edition by Gale Roid (2003) covers ages 2 to 85 years and above, with five factors such as fluid reasoning and working memory. It tempts because it is the classic intelligence test, but it neither starts at birth nor targets infant development."}},{"q":"Kohlberg's Stage 3 ('good boy/nice girl') reasoning emphasises:","concept":"Lawrence Kohlberg built his theory from his 1958 doctoral research, in which he gave boys moral dilemmas such as the Heinz dilemma (should a man steal an overpriced drug to save his dying wife?) and scored the reasoning behind the answer, not the answer itself. Extending Jean Piaget's (1932) work on children's moral judgement, he proposed three levels with two stages each, passed in an invariant sequence. Preconventional level: Stage 1, obedience and punishment orientation (wrong is what gets punished), and Stage 2, instrumental exchange (right is what serves my interests, 'you scratch my back'). Conventional level: Stage 3, interpersonal concordance or 'good boy, nice girl' orientation, where being good means pleasing others, keeping trust and loyalty, and having good intentions; and Stage 4, law and order or social system orientation, where duty means upholding rules and authority so that society functions. Postconventional level: Stage 5, social contract and individual rights, and Stage 6, universal ethical principles such as justice and human dignity, which few adults reach. To tell Stage 3 from Stage 4, ask whose expectations matter: Stage 3 looks to family, friends and the approval of particular people, Stage 4 to the wider society and its laws. Carol Gilligan (1982) argued that the scheme reflected a male justice orientation and undervalued an ethic of care, since women were often scored at Stage 3. James Rest's Defining Issues Test is an objective measure derived from the theory. Option d is right because Stage 3 reasoning centres on gaining approval and meeting the expectations of people close to one.","traps":{"a":"Avoiding punishment defines Stage 1, the obedience and punishment orientation at the preconventional level. Here an act is wrong simply because it is punished, and the size of the punishment shows how bad it was. There is no concern yet with being seen as a good person. It tempts because young children's moral talk often mentions punishment, and Stage 3 children also fear disapproval, which feels similar.","b":"Universal ethical principles define Stage 6, the second postconventional stage, where a person follows self-chosen principles of justice, equality and dignity even against law or majority opinion. Kohlberg found it so rarely that he later treated it as largely theoretical. It tempts because it is the most admirable-sounding option, but it is the opposite of Stage 3's dependence on others' approval.","c":"Maintaining social order through rules, laws and respect for authority is Stage 4, the law and order or social system orientation, the second conventional stage. The person now takes the viewpoint of society as a whole and sees duty as keeping the system working. It tempts because Stages 3 and 4 share the conventional level and both involve conformity; the difference is approval of close others versus obligation to society."}},{"q":"Which developmental milestone typically appears LAST?","concept":"Developmental milestones are skills that most children acquire within a predictable age window, grouped into gross motor, fine motor, language and social-adaptive domains. Ordering them is a frequent exam task, so learn approximate ages. Gross motor: head control by about 3 months, sitting without support around 6 to 8 months, walking alone around 12 to 15 months. Fine motor: palmar (whole-hand) grasp around 4 to 5 months, transfer between hands around 6 months, an immature pincer grasp around 9 months and a neat thumb-index pincer by about 12 months. Language: cooing around 2 to 3 months, babbling around 6 months, first words near 12 months, two-word phrases around 18 to 24 months. Social: the social smile at about 6 to 8 weeks; stranger anxiety appears around 6 to 9 months and is strong toward the end of the first year. Symbolic or pretend play, where one object stands for another (a block used as a phone), begins with simple pretend acts in the second year and becomes clear at about 18 to 24 months. Jean Piaget linked it to the sixth sensorimotor substage, when mental representation and deferred imitation appear, and saw it flourish in the preoperational stage. Mildred Parten (1932) separately described the social stages of play: unoccupied, solitary, onlooker, parallel, associative and cooperative. Absence of pretend play by about 18 months is a recognised warning sign in autism screening. Option b is right because symbolic play, at about 18 to 24 months, appears well after the social smile, stranger anxiety and pincer grasp.","traps":{"a":"The social smile, a smile directed at a human face or voice rather than a reflex smile during sleep, appears at about 6 to 8 weeks. It is one of the earliest social milestones and marks the start of face-to-face interaction. It tempts only if the candidate reads the question as asking which comes 'first' rather than 'last', so always underline the direction word in ordering questions.","c":"Stranger anxiety, wariness or crying when an unfamiliar person approaches, emerges around 6 to 9 months and is strong near the end of the first year. In John Bowlby's (1969) scheme it marks the clear-cut attachment phase, and René Spitz called it eight-month anxiety. It tempts because it needs a cognitive advance, comparing a new face with a stored memory of the caregiver, so it can feel like a late achievement.","d":"The pincer grasp is the fine-motor ability to pick up a small object between the tips of thumb and index finger. An immature (inferior) pincer appears around 9 months and a neat pincer around 12 months, after the earlier palmar grasp. It tempts because it is the latest of the motor skills listed and refined finger control sounds advanced, but symbolic play still follows it."}},{"q":"Bowlby's clear-cut attachment phase, in which infants show clear preferences for primary caregivers and stranger anxiety, is typically:","concept":"John Bowlby, in Attachment (1969), the first volume of Attachment and Loss, proposed that attachment is an evolved behavioural system that keeps an infant close to a protective caregiver, and that it develops in four phases. Phase 1, pre-attachment (birth to about 6 to 8 weeks): the baby signals by crying, grasping and smiling to anyone, with no clear preference. Phase 2, attachment-in-the-making (about 6 to 8 weeks to 6 to 8 months): the infant responds differently to familiar people, smiling and babbling more to the main caregiver, but does not yet protest when left with others. Phase 3, clear-cut attachment (about 6 to 8 months to 18 to 24 months): the infant has a clear preferred figure, shows separation protest, becomes wary of strangers, and uses the caregiver as a secure base for exploring. Phase 4, formation of a reciprocal relationship or goal-corrected partnership (from about 18 to 24 months): language and representation let the child understand the caregiver's plans and negotiate, so separation protest declines. Experiences across these phases build internal working models of self and others. A parallel scheme, often swapped into variants, comes from Rudolph Schaffer and Peggy Emerson (1964), who followed Glasgow infants: asocial, indiscriminate attachment, specific attachment from about 7 months, and multiple attachments soon after. Related background includes Konrad Lorenz's imprinting and Harry Harlow's (1958) contact comfort studies with infant monkeys. Option c is right because clear preference for the primary caregiver plus stranger anxiety marks the clear-cut phase, roughly 6 months to 2 years.","traps":{"a":"Birth to about 2 months is Bowlby's pre-attachment phase. The newborn's signals, crying, rooting, grasping and gazing, are aimed at anyone, and babies at this age generally accept comfort from any responsive adult. There is no focused preference and no stranger anxiety. It tempts because newborns already orient to faces and voices, which can be mistaken for attachment.","b":"About 2 to 6 months is attachment-in-the-making. The infant now recognises familiar people and smiles more at the main caregiver, but still accepts care from strangers and does not protest separation. In Rudolph Schaffer and Peggy Emerson's (1964) terms this is indiscriminate attachment. It tempts because preference is beginning, but the stem's strong preference plus stranger anxiety comes later.","d":"From about 2 years onward is the goal-corrected partnership, Bowlby's fourth phase. Language and mental representation let the child understand that the caregiver has separate goals, so the child negotiates ('come back after lunch') and tolerates separation better. Stranger anxiety and separation protest have usually eased. It tempts because 2 to 4 years still involves strong attachment, but the relationship is now reciprocal rather than protest-driven."}},{"q":"Authoritative parenting (Baumrind) is best characterised by:","concept":"Diana Baumrind studied parents of preschool children in Berkeley, California in the 1960s, combining home observation and interviews, and described three parenting styles (1966, 1967, 1971). Eleanor Maccoby and John Martin (1983) reorganised her work into two independent dimensions and added a fourth style. Demandingness is the amount of control, supervision, maturity demands and consistent rules. Responsiveness is warmth, acceptance and sensitivity to the child's needs. Crossing them gives a two by two grid. Authoritative parents are high on both: they set clear standards and enforce them, but explain their reasons, listen to the child and encourage independence. Authoritarian parents are high in demandingness and low in responsiveness: obedience is valued for its own sake, with little explanation ('because I said so'). Permissive or indulgent parents are warm but make few demands. Uninvolved or neglectful parents, the style added by Maccoby and Martin, are low on both. In Western samples, authoritative parenting predicts the best outcomes: self-reliance, social competence and school achievement, a pattern supported in adolescent studies by Laurence Steinberg and colleagues. Uninvolved parenting predicts the poorest. Culture matters: Ruth Chao (1994) argued that strict Chinese parenting reflects 'training' within a warm relationship, and similar points are made about Indian families, so high control does not always mean coldness. Do not confuse these parenting styles with infant attachment types. Option a is right because authoritative parenting combines high demandingness (firm, consistent expectations) with high responsiveness (warmth and reasoning).","traps":{"b":"High demandingness with low responsiveness is the authoritarian style: strict rules, obedience for its own sake, little warmth or explanation, and often harsh punishment. In Diana Baumrind's work its children tended to be more withdrawn, anxious or less spontaneous. It tempts because 'authoritarian' and 'authoritative' look almost identical and both share high demandingness; the difference lies entirely in responsiveness.","c":"Low demandingness with high responsiveness is the permissive or indulgent style. Parents are affectionate and accepting but set few rules, rarely enforce limits and let children regulate themselves. Their children tend to show poorer impulse control and more immaturity. It tempts because warmth is the ingredient everyone associates with good parenting, but authoritative parenting adds firm demands to that warmth.","d":"Low demandingness with low responsiveness is the uninvolved or neglectful style, added by Eleanor Maccoby and John Martin (1983) when they recast Baumrind's work as a two by two grid. These parents give little warmth and little supervision, often because of stress, depression or disengagement. It predicts the poorest outcomes. It tempts only through confusion about which corner of the grid is which."}},{"q":"Centration in preoperational thought refers to:","concept":"Jean Piaget's preoperational stage runs from about 2 to 7 years. The child can now use symbols, language and pretend play, but cannot yet perform operations, which are internalised, reversible mental actions. Piaget divided it into a preconceptual substage (about 2 to 4) and an intuitive substage (about 4 to 7). Several linked limitations define the stage. Centration is the tendency to focus, or centre, on one striking feature of a situation and ignore other relevant features. In the conservation of liquid task, water from a short wide glass is poured into a tall thin glass; the child fixes on the higher water level and says the tall glass has more, ignoring that it is narrower. Irreversibility is the failure to mentally undo the pouring. Focus on states rather than transformations means attending to the before and after displays rather than the pouring itself. Egocentrism is difficulty taking another's viewpoint, shown in Piaget and Bärbel Inhelder's three mountains task. Animism attributes life and feelings to objects, artificialism holds that natural things were made by people, and transductive reasoning links two particular events as cause and effect. In the concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11) decentration appears and the child conserves, though number, mass, weight and volume are mastered at different ages (horizontal décalage). Later work, such as the 'naughty teddy' study of James McGarrigle and Margaret Donaldson (1974), suggested children conserve earlier when tasks make sense to them. Option c is right because centration is focusing on one salient feature while ignoring the others.","traps":{"a":"Inability to mentally reverse an operation is irreversibility: the child cannot imagine pouring the water back into the original glass to see that the amount is unchanged. Jean Piaget treated it as a separate preoperational limitation from centration, though the two work together to produce conservation failure. It tempts because both explain the same task, but the stem asks for the definition of centration.","b":"Believing that inanimate objects are alive or have feelings is animism, as when a child says the sun follows her or a chair feels hurt when kicked. Jean Piaget described it alongside artificialism and egocentrism as typical preoperational thinking. It tempts because it is one of the best known features of the stage, but it concerns beliefs about life, not focusing on one feature.","d":"Difficulty taking another person's spatial perspective is egocentrism, shown in Piaget and Bärbel Inhelder's three mountains task, where a preoperational child picks the view from their own seat when asked what a doll sees. Martin Hughes's policeman doll studies later suggested children manage this earlier when the task makes sense. It tempts because egocentrism and centration both mean being stuck on one viewpoint."}},{"q":"Per DSM-5-TR, the minimum duration of symptoms for a diagnosis of schizophrenia is:","concept":"DSM-5-TR (2022) separates the psychotic disorders largely by duration, so knowing the time rules answers many variants. Schizophrenia has three linked requirements. Criterion A: two or more of five symptoms (delusions, hallucinations, disorganised speech, grossly disorganised or catatonic behaviour, negative symptoms), each present for a significant portion of time during a one-month period (less if successfully treated), and at least one must be delusions, hallucinations or disorganised speech. Criterion B: a marked fall in work, relationships or self-care. Criterion C: continuous signs of disturbance for at least six months, including that month of active-phase symptoms; the rest may be prodromal or residual (negative symptoms or attenuated positive symptoms). Neighbouring disorders follow the same clock. Brief psychotic disorder lasts at least one day but less than one month, with full return to previous functioning. Schizophreniform disorder lasts at least one month but less than six months, and impaired functioning is not required. Delusional disorder needs delusions for one month or longer, without the other Criterion A features. Schizoaffective disorder needs delusions or hallucinations for two weeks or more without a major mood episode. ICD-11 differs: schizophrenia needs symptoms for at least one month, and acute and transient psychotic disorder lasts up to three months. The six-month rule entered official use with DSM-III (1980), following the research-oriented Feighner criteria (1972). Option a is right because DSM-5-TR requires one month of active-phase symptoms within at least six months of continuous disturbance.","traps":{"b":"One week is not a threshold for any psychotic disorder. Brief psychotic disorder runs from one day to less than one month, so a one-week psychosis that resolves would be brief psychotic disorder, not schizophrenia. Seven days is actually the minimum for a manic episode in bipolar I. It tempts because a short, precise figure feels clinical, and mania can present with psychosis.","c":"Three months matches no DSM-5-TR schizophrenia rule. A three-month psychosis that then resolves would be schizophreniform disorder (one to six months). In ICD-11, three months is the upper limit for acute and transient psychotic disorder. It tempts candidates who half-remember a middle band of durations and those who mix ICD-11 timelines with DSM ones.","d":"Twelve months is not required for any psychotic disorder; six months of continuous disturbance is the DSM-5-TR anchor for schizophrenia. One year appears elsewhere, as the minimum for persistent depressive disorder and cyclothymic disorder in children and adolescents, and as the window for substance use disorder criteria. It tempts because schizophrenia is a chronic illness, so a long period sounds right."}},{"q":"Bleuler's '4 As' of schizophrenia are:","concept":"Emil Kraepelin (1899) grouped chronic psychoses with a deteriorating course under the name dementia praecox, defining the illness mainly by its downhill course. Eugen Bleuler, director of the Burghölzli clinic in Zurich, proposed the name schizophrenia in 1908 and developed it in his 1911 monograph Dementia Praecox or the Group of Schizophrenias. The name means 'split mind', referring to a splitting between psychic functions (thought, emotion and will), not to split or multiple personality. Bleuler divided symptoms into fundamental symptoms, present in every case and specific to the illness, and accessory symptoms, which could appear in other conditions. The fundamental symptoms are remembered as the four As: loosening of Associations (thought jumps between weakly related ideas), Affective disturbance, usually flat or inappropriate affect, Ambivalence (holding contradictory feelings, wishes or intentions at once), and Autism (withdrawal from reality into an inner fantasy life). The 'four As' label is a later teaching mnemonic, and some versions add Attention and Avolition. Hallucinations, delusions and catatonia were accessory. Kurt Schneider, whose Clinical Psychopathology appeared in English in 1959, later reversed this emphasis with his first-rank symptoms, and modern criteria rely mostly on positive symptoms. Leo Kanner (1943) later borrowed 'autism' for the childhood disorder. Do not confuse Bleuler's list with the negative symptoms in Nancy Andreasen's (1982) Scale for the Assessment of Negative Symptoms: affective flattening, alogia, avolition-apathy, anhedonia-asociality and attention. Option b is right because loose associations, flat affect, ambivalence and autism are exactly Bleuler's fundamental four As.","traps":{"a":"Affect (flattening), anhedonia, avolition and alogia are modern negative symptoms of schizophrenia, the framework of Nancy Andreasen's Scale for the Assessment of Negative Symptoms and of DSM-5-TR Criterion A5. Alogia is poverty of speech; avolition is lack of goal-directed activity. It tempts because every term begins with A and fits schizophrenia, but only affect overlaps with Bleuler's fundamental four.","c":"Apathy, anxiety, anger and aphasia form an invented list. Aphasia is an acquired language disorder caused by brain damage, such as Broca's or Wernicke's aphasia after a stroke, not a feature of schizophrenia. Anxiety and anger are nonspecific emotions found in many disorders. It tempts only because each word starts with A, and apathy loosely resembles avolition.","d":"Audible thoughts (Gedankenlautwerden) is one of Kurt Schneider's first-rank symptoms, not a Bleulerian fundamental symptom. Anosognosia is unawareness of one's own deficit, a term coined by Joseph Babinski (1914) for stroke patients and sometimes applied to poor insight in psychosis. Anhedonia and asociality are modern negative symptoms. It tempts by mixing real psychiatric terms beginning with A."}},{"q":"Persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia) in adults requires depressed mood for at least:","concept":"Persistent depressive disorder (PDD) is the DSM-5 (2013) name for chronic depression. It merged two DSM-IV categories, dysthymic disorder and chronic major depressive disorder. The term dysthymic disorder had entered DSM-III (1980), largely replacing the older label depressive neurosis. DSM-5-TR requires depressed mood for most of the day, on more days than not, for at least two years in adults; in children and adolescents the period is at least one year and the mood may be irritable. While depressed, the person has at least two of six symptoms: poor appetite or overeating, insomnia or hypersomnia, low energy or fatigue, low self-esteem, poor concentration or difficulty making decisions, and hopelessness. During the two years the person has never been free of symptoms for more than two months at a time. Full major depressive episode criteria may be present continuously, and specifiers record this (for example, with intermittent major depressive episodes). There must never have been a manic or hypomanic episode, and cyclothymic criteria must never have been met. Onset before age 21 is specified as early onset. When a major depressive episode develops on top of long-standing dysthymia, clinicians call it double depression. Compare the duration rules: a major depressive episode needs two weeks; cyclothymic disorder, like PDD, needs two years in adults and one year in youth; ICD-11 dysthymic disorder also uses two years. Option d is right because adult PDD needs at least two years of depressed mood, reduced to one year in children and adolescents.","traps":{"a":"Six months is the duration rule for generalised anxiety disorder and for the continuous disturbance of schizophrenia, among others. It is far too short for persistent depressive disorder, whose essence is chronicity. Two weeks of more severe symptoms would make a major depressive episode, but chronic low-grade depression must last years. It tempts because six months is the most common duration threshold in DSM.","b":"One year is the persistent depressive disorder requirement for children and adolescents, in whom the mood may also be irritable rather than sad. It does not apply to adults, who need two years. It tempts because it is a genuine criterion from the same disorder, and option d itself mentions it in brackets, so a candidate reading quickly recognises the figure.","c":"Five years is not a threshold in DSM-5-TR or ICD-11 for persistent depressive disorder or any other mood disorder. It overshoots the two-year adult requirement. It tempts because the disorder is chronic and many patients do report depression lasting many years, so a long figure sounds plausible to a candidate who remembers only that the condition is long-lasting."}},{"q":"Bipolar I disorder is distinguished from bipolar II by:","concept":"Emil Kraepelin (1899) grouped recurrent mood illness as manic-depressive insanity; Karl Leonhard (1957) separated bipolar from unipolar forms, and bipolar II was proposed by David Dunner and colleagues in the 1970s. DSM-5-TR defines the bipolar disorders by the highest mood episode a person has ever had. A manic episode is a distinct period of abnormally and persistently elevated, expansive or irritable mood plus persistently increased activity or energy, lasting at least one week, most of the day nearly every day, or any duration if hospitalisation is necessary. At least three symptoms are needed (four if mood is only irritable): inflated self-esteem or grandiosity, decreased need for sleep, pressured speech, flight of ideas, distractibility, increased goal-directed activity or agitation, and risky pleasurable activities. It causes marked impairment, needs hospitalisation, or has psychotic features. A hypomanic episode has the same symptoms for at least four consecutive days but is not severe enough for marked impairment or hospitalisation, and if psychotic features occur the episode is by definition manic. Bipolar I needs at least one lifetime manic episode; depressive episodes are common but not required. Bipolar II needs at least one hypomanic episode and at least one major depressive episode, and never a manic episode. Cyclothymic disorder involves two years (one in youth) of hypomanic and depressive symptoms that never meet full episode criteria. Option b is right because a single lifetime manic episode, lasting a week or needing hospitalisation, is what makes the diagnosis bipolar I rather than bipolar II.","traps":{"a":"A major depressive episode cannot separate the two disorders. It is required for bipolar II but optional for bipolar I, so a person with bipolar I may never have been depressed, though most have. Because both groups usually have depressive episodes, depression is shared ground, not the dividing line. It tempts because depression is the commonest presentation of both and the usual reason patients seek help.","c":"Rapid cycling is a course specifier meaning at least four mood episodes (manic, hypomanic or major depressive) within 12 months. It can be applied to bipolar I or bipolar II, so it does not distinguish them; it signals a more difficult course. It tempts because it is a well-known bipolar term and sounds like a defining feature of one particular type.","d":"Absence of psychotic features does not define either disorder. Psychosis during a high-mood episode makes that episode manic by definition, which points to bipolar I. Yet a bipolar II patient can have psychotic features during a major depressive episode, and many bipolar I patients never become psychotic. It tempts because hypomania, the bipolar II high, never includes psychosis."}},{"q":"Per DSM-5-TR, the minimum duration for generalised anxiety disorder is:","concept":"Generalised anxiety disorder (GAD) is chronic, excessive and hard-to-control worry about many everyday matters, such as work, health, money and family, rather than one feared object or situation. DSM-5-TR Criterion A requires excessive anxiety and worry (apprehensive expectation) occurring more days than not for at least six months. The person finds the worry difficult to control, and it comes with at least three of six symptoms (only one in children): restlessness or feeling keyed up, being easily fatigued, difficulty concentrating or mind going blank, irritability, muscle tension, and sleep disturbance. It must cause distress or impairment and not be better explained by substances, a medical condition such as hyperthyroidism, or another disorder. History explains the number: when DSM-III (1980) introduced GAD the minimum was one month, and DSM-III-R (1987) raised it to six months and made worry the core feature. ICD-11 asks for symptoms on more days than not for at least several months. Compare other anxiety durations: panic disorder requires at least one month of persistent concern or behaviour change after an attack; specific phobia, social anxiety disorder and agoraphobia typically last six months or more; separation anxiety disorder needs four weeks in children and typically six months in adults; selective mutism needs one month, not counting the first month of school. Adrian Wells (1995) explained GAD through a metacognitive model separating worry about everyday matters from worry about worry. Option b is right because DSM-5-TR sets six months as the minimum duration of worry for generalised anxiety disorder.","traps":{"a":"One month was the GAD duration when DSM-III (1980) first introduced the diagnosis, before DSM-III-R raised it to six months. Today one month belongs to other rules: persistent concern after panic attacks, the PTSD duration, selective mutism, and the active phase of schizophrenia. It tempts candidates using older textbooks and those who confuse anxiety disorder timelines.","c":"Three months is not a GAD threshold. It appears in other DSM-5-TR rules: adjustment disorder symptoms begin within three months of a stressor, and bulimia nervosa and binge-eating disorder require behaviours at least weekly for three months. It tempts as a middle value, and because ICD-11's vague 'several months' makes candidates think a shorter period is enough.","d":"Twelve months overshoots: GAD needs six months of worry, not a full year. Twelve months is the window for substance use disorder criteria and the minimum for persistent depressive disorder and cyclothymia in children and adolescents. It tempts because GAD is often chronic, so a candidate may assume a year of symptoms is needed before worry can be called generalised."}},{"q":"Capgras syndrome is characterised by the delusional belief that:","concept":"Delusional misidentification syndromes are delusions about the identity of people, places or objects. The best known is Capgras syndrome, first described by the French psychiatrists Joseph Capgras and Jean Reboul-Lachaux (1923) as 'l'illusion des sosies', the illusion of doubles, in a woman who believed her husband, children and others had been replaced by look-alikes. The patient recognises the face and appearance of a familiar person, often a spouse or parent, yet insists that person is an impostor or double. The neighbouring syndromes are easy to swap in a variant. Fregoli syndrome, reported by Paul Courbon and G. Fail (1927) and named after the quick-change actor Leopoldo Fregoli, is the reverse: a familiar person, usually a persecutor, is believed to be disguising themselves as various strangers. Intermetamorphosis is the belief that people swap identities with one another, physically and psychologically. Subjective doubles is the belief that one has a double living independently. Reduplicative paramnesia applies the idea to places, as when a patient insists the hospital has been duplicated. Capgras occurs in schizophrenia, in Lewy body and Alzheimer's dementia, and after right hemisphere injury. Hadyn Ellis and Andrew Young (1990) proposed a two-route account: overt face recognition is intact, but the covert emotional response, measured by skin conductance, is missing, so the familiar face feels strange and the mind concludes it belongs to an impostor. They saw it as a mirror image of prosopagnosia. Option a is right because Capgras is the belief that a familiar person has been replaced by an identical-looking impostor.","traps":{"b":"Being persecuted by a secret organisation is a persecutory delusion, the most common delusional theme, seen in schizophrenia, delusional disorder (persecutory type) and psychotic mood disorders. It concerns threat and intent, not identity. It tempts because Capgras patients often feel threatened by the supposed impostor and may become suspicious or even violent towards them, so persecutory content frequently accompanies misidentification.","c":"A stranger being a familiar person in disguise is Fregoli syndrome, described by Paul Courbon and G. Fail (1927) and named after the quick-change actor Leopoldo Fregoli. Here unfamiliar faces are wrongly felt to be familiar, the exact reverse of Capgras, where a familiar face feels unfamiliar. It tempts because both involve doubles and disguise, and examiners like to swap the two.","d":"Believing that one's own body has been stolen or replaced is a somatic delusion about the self, closer to the nihilistic delusion described by Jules Cotard (1880), in which patients believe they are dead, rotting or missing organs. Capgras concerns the identity of another, familiar person, not one's own body. It tempts because 'replaced' appears in both this option and the Capgras definition."}},{"q":"Schneider's first-rank symptoms include all EXCEPT:","concept":"Kurt Schneider, a German psychiatrist whose Clinical Psychopathology appeared in English in 1959, listed symptoms of 'first rank' that, in the absence of organic brain disease, he regarded as strongly suggestive of schizophrenia. Most share a loss of the boundary between self and world, so that one's own thoughts, feelings or actions seem controlled by or exposed to an outside agency. The usual list has eleven items. Three are auditory hallucinations: audible thoughts (Gedankenlautwerden or écho de la pensée, hearing one's own thoughts spoken aloud), voices arguing or discussing the patient in the third person, and voices giving a running commentary on the patient's actions. Three concern thought: thought insertion, thought withdrawal and thought broadcasting. Four are passivity experiences: made feelings, made impulses, made volitional acts, and somatic passivity (bodily sensations imposed from outside). The last is delusional perception, a two-stage symptom in which a normal perception is suddenly given a delusional, self-referred meaning with no understandable link. First-rank symptoms are not pathognomonic; they occur in mania and other psychoses and are absent in some patients with schizophrenia. ICD-10 relied heavily on them, and DSM-5 removed the special weight DSM-IV had given to bizarre delusions and certain voices. Loose associations, a formal thought disorder, belongs to Eugen Bleuler (1911), whose fundamental four As begin with loosening of associations. Schneider's second-rank symptoms include other hallucinations, perplexity and emotional blunting. Option a is right because loose associations is a Bleulerian fundamental symptom, not one of Schneider's first-rank symptoms.","traps":{"b":"Audible thoughts (Gedankenlautwerden, also called thought echo or écho de la pensée) is the first item on Kurt Schneider's list: the patient hears their own thoughts spoken aloud, as they think them or just after. It is a genuine first-rank symptom, so it cannot be the exception. It tempts because the German term is unfamiliar and sounds like a technical exclusion.","c":"Thought insertion, the experience that thoughts which are not one's own are being placed into one's mind by an outside agency, is a classic first-rank symptom and so is not the exception. It differs from obsessions in OCD, where the person recognises intrusive thoughts as products of their own mind. It tempts because it is sometimes grouped under thought alienation rather than hallucinations.","d":"Delusional perception is a genuine first-rank symptom and so not the exception. A normal perception, such as seeing a red car pass, is suddenly given a delusional meaning, such as 'I have been chosen', with no understandable link. Schneider considered it among the most important first-rank symptoms. It tempts because candidates expect first-rank symptoms to be only hallucinations or passivity experiences."}},{"q":"PTSD per DSM-5-TR requires symptoms from how many clusters?","concept":"Post-traumatic stress disorder entered the official classification in DSM-III (1980). DSM-5 (2013) moved it from the anxiety disorders into a new chapter, trauma- and stressor-related disorders, and reorganised its symptoms. Criterion A defines the trauma: exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury or sexual violence, by direct experience, witnessing, learning that it happened to a close family member or friend, or repeated exposure to aversive details, as in first responders. The 20 symptoms then fall into four clusters. B, intrusion (at least one): intrusive memories, nightmares, flashbacks, and distress or physiological reactions to reminders. C, avoidance (at least one): of thoughts and feelings, or of external reminders. D, negative alterations in cognitions and mood (at least two), such as dissociative amnesia, negative beliefs, distorted blame, persistent negative emotions, loss of interest, detachment and inability to feel positive emotions. E, alterations in arousal and reactivity (at least two), such as irritability, recklessness, hypervigilance, exaggerated startle, poor concentration and sleep disturbance. Symptoms must last more than one month (Criterion F). DSM-IV had three clusters, re-experiencing, avoidance and numbing, and hyperarousal, with 17 symptoms; DSM-5 split avoidance and numbing into C and D. Specifiers include with dissociative symptoms and with delayed expression, and there is a separate preschool subtype for children aged six and under. ICD-11 keeps three core elements for PTSD and adds complex PTSD. Option d is right because DSM-5-TR requires symptoms from four clusters: intrusion, avoidance, negative cognitions and mood, and arousal and reactivity.","traps":{"a":"Two clusters matches no edition of DSM or ICD. A candidate who remembers only the most famous symptoms, flashbacks and avoidance of reminders, may think of PTSD as a two-part disorder. It tempts as a minimal count, but even the oldest scheme had three clusters, and DSM-5 explicitly added a separate cluster for negative changes in cognitions and mood.","b":"Three clusters is the DSM-IV structure: re-experiencing, avoidance and numbing, and hyperarousal. ICD-11 also describes PTSD with three core elements: re-experiencing in the present, avoidance, and a persistent sense of current threat. DSM-5 split avoidance from negative changes in mood and cognition, producing four. It tempts candidates relying on older books or blending ICD-11 with DSM-5-TR.","c":"Five is the number of symptom categories in DSM-5-TR acute stress disorder: intrusion, negative mood, dissociation, avoidance and arousal, from which at least nine of 14 symptoms are needed. It is not the PTSD cluster count. It tempts because acute stress disorder and PTSD share the same chapter, similar symptoms and the same trauma criterion, so their structures are easily confused."}},{"q":"Acute stress disorder is distinguished from PTSD primarily by:","concept":"Acute stress disorder (ASD) was introduced in DSM-IV (1994) to identify people with severe reactions in the first weeks after trauma, before PTSD can be diagnosed. In DSM-5-TR both sit in the trauma- and stressor-related disorders chapter, share the same Criterion A trauma (actual or threatened death, serious injury or sexual violence), and draw on similar symptoms. The main dividing line is time. ASD is diagnosed when symptoms last from three days to one month after the trauma; symptoms usually begin immediately but must persist at least three days. PTSD requires that symptoms last more than one month, and it can begin later, with the specifier 'with delayed expression' when full criteria are met only six months or more after the event. ASD requires at least nine of 14 symptoms from five categories: intrusion, negative mood, dissociation, avoidance and arousal. PTSD requires a set number from each of four clusters. DSM-IV ASD required at least three dissociative symptoms, such as numbing, derealisation or depersonalisation, but DSM-5 dropped that requirement because many people with severe acute distress do not dissociate. Many people with ASD go on to develop PTSD, but many with later PTSD never had ASD. Adjustment disorder differs again: the stressor need not be traumatic, symptoms begin within three months, and they usually resolve within six months after the stressor ends. ICD-11 treats acute stress reaction as a normal response, not a mental disorder. Option c is right because the three-day to one-month duration window is the defining difference between acute stress disorder and PTSD.","traps":{"a":"Required dissociative symptoms was the DSM-IV rule: acute stress disorder needed at least three dissociative symptoms such as numbing, reduced awareness, derealisation, depersonalisation or dissociative amnesia. DSM-5 removed this requirement, so dissociation is now just one of five symptom categories. It tempts candidates using older textbooks, since ASD was historically seen as a dissociative reaction to trauma.","b":"Neither disorder is defined by age of onset; both follow a specific traumatic event at any age. DSM-5-TR does include a separate PTSD subtype for children six years and younger, with fewer and more behavioural criteria, but that is a modification of PTSD, not a distinction from ASD. It tempts because age of onset distinguishes many other disorders.","d":"Both acute stress disorder and PTSD are in the trauma- and stressor-related disorders chapter of DSM-5-TR, along with adjustment disorders, reactive attachment disorder and disinhibited social engagement disorder. The dissociative disorders chapter holds dissociative identity disorder, dissociative amnesia and depersonalisation/derealisation disorder. It tempts because ASD once demanded dissociative symptoms and the dissociative chapter sits next to the trauma chapter."}},{"q":"OCD vs OCPD: a key clinical distinction is that OCD obsessions/compulsions are:","concept":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) and obsessive-compulsive personality disorder (OCPD) share a name but are different conditions. OCD has obsessions, recurrent and persistent thoughts, urges or images experienced as intrusive and unwanted, which cause anxiety and which the person tries to ignore, suppress or neutralise; and compulsions, repetitive behaviours (washing, checking) or mental acts (counting, praying) performed to reduce that anxiety or prevent a feared event. They are time-consuming (over an hour a day) or impairing. Since DSM-5 (2013), OCD has had its own chapter with body dysmorphic disorder, hoarding disorder, trichotillomania and excoriation disorder. OCPD is a Cluster C personality disorder: a pervasive pattern, from early adulthood, of preoccupation with orderliness, perfectionism and control at the cost of flexibility, shown by at least four of eight features such as rules and lists, excessive devotion to work, rigidity, miserliness and inability to discard worthless objects. The key clinical contrast is how the person experiences the symptoms. OCD symptoms are ego-dystonic: they feel alien to the person's values and wishes, distressing and resisted, even though the person knows they come from their own mind (unlike thought insertion). OCPD traits are ego-syntonic: they feel correct and justified, so help is often sought because others complain. DSM-5 adds an insight specifier for OCD (good or fair, poor, absent or delusional), so ego-dystonicity is typical rather than absolute. Exposure and response prevention, pioneered by Victor Meyer (1966), and SSRIs are first-line for OCD. Option a is right because OCD obsessions and compulsions are characteristically ego-dystonic, experienced as intrusive and distressing.","traps":{"b":"Ego-syntonic describes OCPD traits, not OCD symptoms. A person with OCPD feels that perfectionism, order and control are simply the right way to do things, so they rarely resist them and often blame others for being careless. It tempts because the two disorders share a name, and because OCD patients with poor insight may defend their rituals.","c":"OCD obsessions are not always reality-grounded. Many concern unlikely harms (a stove causing a fire despite repeated checking) or taboo images the person finds repugnant, and insight ranges from good to absent, with DSM-5 allowing absent insight with delusional beliefs. The absolute word 'always' should raise suspicion. It tempts because some fears, such as contamination, do have a realistic core.","d":"OCD is not restricted to childhood. It often begins in late adolescence or early adulthood, although a substantial minority start in childhood, sometimes with tics, and new cases continue to appear in adult life. OCPD, by definition, is evident by early adulthood. It tempts because childhood-onset OCD is well described, including a tic-related specifier, and examiners like age-restricted distractors."}},{"q":"Conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder) requires:","concept":"Conversion disorder takes its name from Sigmund Freud and Josef Breuer's Studies on Hysteria (1895), in which an unacceptable psychological conflict was thought to be 'converted' into a bodily symptom; Jean-Martin Charcot and Pierre Janet had studied hysteria in France in the late nineteenth century. DSM-5 (2013) called it conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder), and DSM-5-TR (2022) puts functional neurological symptom disorder first. Criterion A: one or more symptoms of altered voluntary motor or sensory function, such as weakness or paralysis, tremor or gait problems, swallowing or speech difficulty, non-epileptic seizures, numbness, blindness or deafness. Criterion B: clinical findings show incompatibility between the symptom and recognised neurological or medical conditions. Examples are Hoover's sign, where a 'paralysed' leg extends at the hip when the healthy leg is flexed against resistance, or non-epileptic attacks with a normal EEG during the event. Criterion C: not better explained by another disorder; Criterion D: distress or impairment. DSM-5 dropped the earlier requirements to identify a psychological stressor and to show that symptoms are not feigned, because both were hard to judge; diagnosis now rests on positive physical signs. Specifiers cover symptom type, acute (under six months) or persistent episode, and with or without a psychological stressor. La belle indifférence is not diagnostic. ICD-11 places the condition among dissociative disorders as dissociative neurological symptom disorder. Factitious disorder and malingering, where symptoms are produced deliberately, are the main contrasts. Option b is right because the core criterion is one or more neurological symptoms incompatible with recognised medical or neurological conditions.","traps":{"a":"Intentional production of symptoms points away from conversion disorder. In factitious disorder, symptoms are deliberately produced or exaggerated to occupy the sick role, the pattern Richard Asher (1951) called Munchausen syndrome. In malingering, they are feigned for external rewards such as money or avoiding duty. It tempts because DSM-5 no longer requires proof that symptoms are not feigned, but clear evidence of deliberate production points to those diagnoses instead.","c":"Concomitant substance use is not a criterion. If a substance (intoxication, withdrawal or a medication side effect) better explained the neurological symptom, a substance-induced or medical diagnosis would be made instead. Functional symptoms do co-occur with many conditions, including epilepsy, but nothing has to accompany them. It tempts because many DSM-5-TR criteria sets mention substances, usually as exclusions rather than requirements.","d":"There is no onset age requirement. Functional neurological symptoms can begin at any point in life; non-epileptic attacks often start in young adulthood, motor symptoms somewhat later, and onset in very young children is uncommon. An onset-before-age-5 rule resembles nothing in the criteria. It tempts because neurodevelopmental disorders, such as ADHD, do carry age-of-onset criteria, and candidates over-generalise."}},{"q":"Dissociative identity disorder requires:","concept":"Dissociative identity disorder (DID), called multiple personality disorder until DSM-IV (1994), is the most severe dissociative disorder. Dissociation means a disruption in the normal integration of consciousness, memory, identity, emotion, perception and behaviour. DSM-5-TR Criterion A requires a disruption of identity marked by two or more distinct personality states, which some cultures describe as possession. It involves marked discontinuity in sense of self and agency, with changes in affect, behaviour, consciousness, memory, perception, cognition or sensory-motor functioning, observed by others or reported by the person. Criterion B requires recurrent gaps in recall of everyday events, important personal information or traumatic events, beyond ordinary forgetting. Criterion C is distress or impairment. Criterion D excludes states that are a normal part of broadly accepted cultural or religious practice and, in children, imaginary playmates or fantasy play. Criterion E excludes substances (such as blackouts during intoxication) and medical conditions such as complex partial seizures. Neighbouring dissociative disorders are dissociative amnesia, in which dissociative fugue is now a specifier rather than a separate diagnosis, and depersonalisation/derealisation disorder. ICD-11 adds partial dissociative identity disorder and trance and possession trance disorders, relevant in Indian clinical settings. Two explanations compete: the trauma model links DID to severe, repeated childhood abuse, while Nicholas Spanos's (1994) sociocognitive model argues that identities are shaped by therapist suggestion, media and cultural scripts. A famous case is Corbett Thigpen and Hervey Cleckley's The Three Faces of Eve (1957). Option c is right because DID requires two or more distinct personality states together with recurrent gaps in autobiographical memory.","traps":{"a":"No EEG finding defines DID; it is diagnosed clinically from history, observation and structured interviews. Loss of consciousness with EEG changes suggests epilepsy, and DSM-5-TR actually requires that the symptoms not be due to a medical condition such as complex partial seizures. It tempts because switching between identities can resemble seizure-like lapses, and EEG sounds like an objective test.","b":"A single brief fugue describes dissociative fugue, sudden purposeful travel or bewildered wandering with amnesia for identity. In DSM-5-TR it is a specifier of dissociative amnesia, not a separate disorder, and no 24-hour limit exists. DID needs two or more personality states plus recurrent memory gaps. It tempts because fugue episodes can occur within DID and both disorders involve amnesia.","d":"No specific precipitating event is required. Most people with DID report severe, repeated childhood trauma, often physical or sexual abuse, but DSM-5-TR lists no trauma criterion for DID, unlike Criterion A for PTSD, and the disorder is usually traced to childhood rather than a single adult assault. It tempts because the trauma model is widely taught and sexual abuse is a commonly reported antecedent."}},{"q":"Anorexia nervosa per DSM-5 requires:","concept":"Anorexia nervosa was named by William Gull (1874), while Charles Lasègue (1873) described the same condition in France. DSM-5 (2013) defines it with three criteria. A: restriction of energy intake relative to requirements, leading to significantly low body weight for age, sex, developmental trajectory and physical health, meaning less than minimally normal (or, in children, less than minimally expected). B: intense fear of gaining weight or becoming fat, or persistent behaviour that interferes with weight gain, even at low weight. C: disturbed experience of body weight or shape, undue influence of weight or shape on self-evaluation, or persistent lack of recognition of the seriousness of the low weight. DSM-5 removed the DSM-IV amenorrhoea criterion (absence of at least three consecutive menstrual cycles), since it could not apply to males, premenarchal girls or women on hormonal contraception, and cases without amenorrhoea were otherwise similar. Subtypes, judged over the last three months, are restricting and binge-eating/purging. Severity for adults uses BMI: mild 17 or above, moderate 16 to 16.99, severe 15 to 15.99, extreme below 15. ICD-10 used a BMI of 17.5 or less, while ICD-11 uses a guide of below 18.5 and flags dangerously low weight below 14. Neighbouring diagnoses: bulimia nervosa (binge-purge at normal or higher weight), avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder (restriction without body-image concern), and atypical anorexia under other specified feeding or eating disorder, where all criteria are met but weight stays in the normal range. Option a is right because DSM-5 requires significantly low weight, fear of gain or weight-gain-blocking behaviour, and body-image disturbance.","traps":{"b":"BMI below 17 is not a diagnostic cutoff. DSM-5 judges significantly low weight against age, sex, development and health, and uses BMI only to grade severity; 17 or above counts as mild, so many diagnosed adults have BMIs above 17. ICD-10 used 17.5 and ICD-11 uses 18.5 as guides. It tempts because a hard number feels objective, and 17 is a real severity boundary.","c":"Amenorrhoea, the absence of at least three consecutive menstrual cycles, was a DSM-IV criterion and is not required in DSM-5. It was dropped because it could not apply to males, premenarchal girls, postmenopausal women or those on hormonal contraception, and patients who still menstruated were otherwise similar. The word 'still' in the option tempts candidates who learned from older textbooks.","d":"Compensatory purging (self-induced vomiting, laxatives, diuretics) is not required in all cases. It characterises the binge-eating/purging subtype, while the restricting subtype loses weight by dieting, fasting or excessive exercise. Binge-purge episodes at normal weight would point to bulimia nervosa instead. It tempts because purging is strongly associated with eating disorders in general and with dramatic clinical cases."}},{"q":"Bulimia nervosa per DSM-5 requires binge-purge episodes occurring on average at least:","concept":"Bulimia nervosa was described by Gerald Russell (1979) as 'an ominous variant of anorexia nervosa'. DSM-III (1980) listed 'bulimia', DSM-III-R (1987) adopted the name bulimia nervosa, and DSM-5 (2013) kept it with a lower frequency threshold. Criterion A is recurrent binge eating. A binge means eating, in a discrete period (for example within two hours), an amount definitely larger than most people would eat in similar circumstances, with a sense of loss of control. Criterion B is recurrent inappropriate compensatory behaviour to prevent weight gain: self-induced vomiting, misuse of laxatives, diuretics or other medications, fasting, or excessive exercise. Criterion C is the frequency rule: both bingeing and compensatory behaviour occur, on average, at least once a week for three months. DSM-IV required twice a week for three months; DSM-5 lowered it because people with less frequent episodes had similar impairment. Criterion D is that self-evaluation is unduly influenced by body shape and weight, and Criterion E that the disturbance does not occur only during anorexia nervosa. Severity is graded by weekly compensatory episodes: mild 1 to 3, moderate 4 to 7, severe 8 to 13, extreme 14 or more. Binge-eating disorder uses the same once a week for three months rule, but without regular compensatory behaviour and with marked distress. Russell's sign, calluses on the knuckles from inducing vomiting, and dental erosion are clinical clues. Option b is right because DSM-5 requires binge eating and compensation at least once a week, on average, for three months.","traps":{"a":"Twice per week is the old DSM-IV frequency, but DSM-IV paired it with three months, not six. This option therefore mixes an outdated frequency with an incorrect duration and matches neither edition. Six months is the generalised anxiety disorder and schizophrenia figure. It tempts candidates who remember that the earlier rule was stricter and assume both numbers were higher.","c":"Once per day for one month matches no edition. Daily bingeing with compensation would count as severe or extreme in the DSM-5 frequency-based severity grading, but the diagnostic minimum is far lower and the required duration far longer. It tempts because severe cases may binge daily, and one month is a common DSM duration in other disorders.","d":"Three times per week for one month matches no DSM edition. The frequency is higher than the once weekly DSM-5 threshold and the duration shorter than the three months required by both DSM-IV and DSM-5. Three compensatory episodes a week would fall in the mild severity band. It tempts by sounding precise and by mixing real-looking numbers."}},{"q":"ADHD per DSM-5-TR requires symptoms to be present before what age?","concept":"Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) is a neurodevelopmental disorder defined by a persistent pattern of inattention and/or hyperactivity-impulsivity that interferes with functioning or development. DSM-5 (2013) and DSM-5-TR (2022) list nine inattention symptoms (careless mistakes, poor sustained attention, not listening, not finishing tasks, disorganisation, avoiding mental effort, losing things, distractibility, forgetfulness) and nine hyperactive-impulsive symptoms (fidgeting, leaving one's seat, running or climbing, unable to play quietly, being 'on the go', talking excessively, blurting answers, difficulty waiting, interrupting). Children up to age 16 need six or more symptoms in a domain; those aged 17 and older need five. Symptoms must last at least six months, several must be present before age 12, several must appear in two or more settings (home, school, work), and they must clearly reduce functioning. The onset rule is the classic exam change: DSM-IV (1994) required impairing symptoms before age 7, but adults could rarely date onset that precisely, so DSM-5 raised the age to 12 and asked only for several symptoms, not impairment, by then. Presentations are combined, predominantly inattentive and predominantly hyperactive-impulsive, replacing the old DSM-IV subtypes. DSM-5 also allowed ADHD and autism spectrum disorder to be diagnosed together, which DSM-IV had barred. ICD-11 describes onset during the developmental period, typically early to mid-childhood, without a fixed age. Option a is right because DSM-5-TR requires several inattentive or hyperactive-impulsive symptoms to have been present before age 12.","traps":{"b":"Age 7 is the DSM-IV (1994) onset rule, which required some impairing symptoms before age 7. DSM-5 (2013) dropped it because adults could not reliably recall such early onset and many later-recognised, mainly inattentive cases were being missed. It tempts because older textbooks, many guidebooks and past question papers still quote 'before 7', so it feels like the textbook answer.","c":"Age 6 is not an ADHD onset criterion in any DSM edition. It is the lower age limit for diagnosing disruptive mood dysregulation disorder, which cannot be first diagnosed before age 6 or after 18, and roughly the age when formal schooling begins. It tempts because it sits close to the old cutoff of 7 and to the age when classroom inattention first gets noticed.","d":"Age 16 is not the onset limit. In ADHD it marks the symptom-count boundary: individuals up to age 16 need six symptoms per domain, while those aged 17 and above need five. A candidate who remembers '16' from the criteria may misplace it onto onset. Onset by 16 would also be far too late for a disorder defined as neurodevelopmental."}},{"q":"Antisocial personality disorder cannot be diagnosed before what age, and requires evidence of conduct disorder before what age?","concept":"Antisocial personality disorder (ASPD) is the Cluster B personality disorder defined in DSM-5 (2013) and DSM-5-TR (2022) as a pervasive pattern of disregard for and violation of the rights of others occurring since age 15. Criterion A needs three or more of seven features: failure to conform to lawful behaviour, deceitfulness, impulsivity, irritability and aggressiveness, reckless disregard for safety, consistent irresponsibility, and lack of remorse. Criterion B says the person must be at least 18 years old. Criterion C needs evidence of conduct disorder with onset before age 15, and Criterion D says the behaviour does not occur only during schizophrenia or bipolar disorder. The logic is developmental: personality is still forming in adolescence, so a young person with the same pattern is diagnosed with conduct disorder, and ASPD is reserved for adults whose antisocial pattern began in youth. Other personality disorders can be diagnosed before 18 if traits have lasted at least a year; ASPD is the single exception. Conduct disorder has a childhood-onset type (a symptom before age 10) and an adolescent-onset type, and childhood onset predicts continuity into ASPD. Keep the neighbours apart: psychopathy, measured by the Psychopathy Checklist-Revised of Robert Hare (1991), adds affective and interpersonal traits such as glibness and callousness; ICD-11 drops named types for a severity rating plus trait domains, with dissociality the matching domain. Option b is right because DSM-5 sets the minimum age at 18 and requires evidence of conduct disorder with onset before 15.","traps":{"a":"This option gets both numbers wrong. ASPD can never be diagnosed before 18, and the conduct disorder evidence must show onset before 15, not 12. The age that does split conduct disorder into childhood-onset and adolescent-onset types is 10, not 12. It tempts candidates who vaguely remember that early conduct problems matter and simply pick younger-sounding ages.","c":"Half of this option is correct: conduct disorder onset before 15 is exactly the DSM-5 rule. The other half reverses the age bar, because ASPD is given only at or after 18; before 18 the same pattern is called conduct disorder. It tempts because the correct '15' catches the eye and a hurried reader skims past 'before 18' without noticing the reversal.","d":"Twenty-one is not a DSM threshold for ASPD; the minimum age is 18, the usual age of legal adulthood. The option also omits the second requirement, conduct disorder before 15, so it cannot answer a two-part stem. It tempts because 21 sounds like a safely 'fully adult' age and because some candidates think personality disorders need a long adult track record."}},{"q":"Borderline personality disorder requires how many of nine criteria for diagnosis?","concept":"Borderline personality disorder (BPD) is a Cluster B personality disorder defined in DSM-5 (2013) as a pervasive pattern of instability of interpersonal relationships, self-image and affects, with marked impulsivity, beginning by early adulthood and present in a variety of contexts. Nine criteria are listed and five or more are needed: frantic efforts to avoid real or imagined abandonment; unstable, intense relationships alternating between idealisation and devaluation; identity disturbance; impulsivity in at least two self-damaging areas (spending, sex, substances, reckless driving, binge eating); recurrent suicidal behaviour, gestures, threats or self-mutilation; affective instability from marked reactivity of mood; chronic emptiness; inappropriate, intense anger; and transient stress-related paranoid ideation or severe dissociative symptoms. Because only five of nine are required, two people can share the diagnosis while overlapping on just one criterion, a common criticism of the category. The term 'borderline' comes from Adolph Stern (1938), who described patients on the border between neurosis and psychosis. Marsha Linehan's dialectical behaviour therapy (1993) is the best-known treatment. Learn the neighbouring thresholds: narcissistic and schizotypal need five of nine, histrionic and dependent five of eight, antisocial three of seven (plus age 18), paranoid, schizoid and avoidant four of seven, and obsessive-compulsive four of eight. ICD-11 keeps only a 'borderline pattern' qualifier added to a severity rating. Option c is right because DSM-5 requires at least five of the nine BPD criteria.","traps":{"a":"Three is a real DSM-5 threshold, but for antisocial personality disorder, which needs three of seven criteria plus age 18 and conduct disorder before 15. For BPD, three criteria would be subthreshold and would label far too many people. It tempts because ASPD and BPD are both Cluster B and their criterion counts are often learned together and then swapped.","b":"Four is the threshold for several other personality disorders: paranoid, schizoid and avoidant need four of seven, and obsessive-compulsive personality disorder needs four of eight. BPD has nine criteria and needs five. It tempts because four is the most frequent personality disorder cut-off overall, so a guess based on general familiarity lands on it.","d":"Six is not a DSM-5 personality disorder threshold. Six symptoms per domain is the ADHD requirement for children up to 16, while BPD needs only five of nine. Raising the bar to six would exclude many people the field regards as having the disorder. It tempts candidates who overestimate how strict the criteria are, or who carry over the ADHD number."}},{"q":"In ICD-10, folie à deux is classified under:","concept":"Folie à deux ('madness of two') is a rare condition in which a delusion passes from a person with an established psychotic illness, the primary or inducer, to someone in a close, emotionally dependent and often socially isolated relationship with them, the secondary or induced case. Charles Lasègue and Jules Falret described it in 1877. Typically the primary is dominant and the secondary more passive or suggestible; separating the two often lets the secondary's belief fade, while the primary needs treatment. Variants include folie imposée (the commonest, a delusion imposed on a healthy partner), folie simultanée (both become psychotic together) and folie communiquée; folie à trois or à famille describes larger groups. ICD-10 (1992) coded it as F24 induced delusional disorder, and DSM-IV (1994) called it shared psychotic disorder. DSM-5 (2013) removed the separate category: the induced partner is coded as delusional disorder if criteria are met, otherwise as other specified schizophrenia spectrum disorder ('delusional symptoms in partner of individual with delusional disorder'). ICD-11 also dropped the separate category and folded induced delusions into 6A24 delusional disorder. Distinguish it from schizotypal disorder (a lasting eccentric pattern) and acute and transient psychotic disorder (sudden onset, quick remission). Option d is right because ICD-10 coded folie à deux as induced delusional disorder (F24), the same condition DSM-IV called shared psychotic disorder; ICD-11 and DSM-5 have both dropped it as a separate category.","traps":{"a":"This is not an ICD-10 heading. It is close to what ICD-11 later did: the separate induced category was removed, and a delusion adopted from a close partner is now diagnosed as delusional disorder (6A24) if criteria are met. DSM-5 similarly lists 'delusional symptoms in partner of individual with delusional disorder'. It tempts anyone who has read the ICD-11 changes, but the stem asks about ICD-10.","b":"'Brief and limited psychotic disorder' is not an ICD-11 label. The real neighbour is acute and transient psychotic disorder (6A23): sudden onset of rapidly changing psychotic symptoms that remit within about three months, often within weeks. DSM-5 has brief psychotic disorder, lasting one day to under one month. Neither is defined by a delusion passed between two people. It tempts because the induced partner's belief often fades quickly after separation.","c":"Schizotypal disorder (ICD-11 6A22; schizotypal personality disorder in DSM-5) is an enduring pattern of eccentric behaviour, odd beliefs or magical thinking, unusual perceptions and social anxiety, without sustained clear delusions. Its odd beliefs belong to the person, not to someone else. It tempts because it sits in the same ICD-11 grouping of primary psychotic disorders and its 'odd beliefs' sound like shared delusions."}},{"q":"Conduct disorder with limited prosocial emotions specifier requires features such as:","concept":"Conduct disorder (CD) is a repetitive and persistent pattern of behaviour that violates the basic rights of others or major age-appropriate norms. DSM-5 (2013) lists 15 criteria in four groups: aggression to people and animals, destruction of property, deceitfulness or theft, and serious violations of rules (such as staying out at night or truancy beginning before 13). Three or more in the past 12 months, with at least one in the past six months, are needed. Onset subtypes are childhood-onset (at least one symptom before age 10), adolescent-onset (none before 10) and unspecified. DSM-5 added the specifier 'with limited prosocial emotions', based on research on callous-unemotional (CU) traits. It requires at least two of four characteristics shown persistently over at least 12 months and in multiple relationships and settings: lack of remorse or guilt; callous lack of empathy; unconcern about performance at school, work or other important activities; and shallow or deficient affect. Information should come from several informants, because the child may not report these traits. Youth with CU traits show more planned, instrumental aggression, respond less to punishment, and more often continue into antisocial personality disorder and adult psychopathy, the construct measured by the Psychopathy Checklist-Revised of Robert Hare (1991). Neighbouring items: oppositional defiant disorder shows angry mood, argumentativeness and vindictiveness without serious rule violations, and ADHD often co-occurs with CD but is a separate diagnosis. Option c is right because lack of remorse, callous lack of empathy, unconcern about performance and shallow affect are the four features of this specifier.","traps":{"a":"Hyperactivity and inattention are the two symptom domains of ADHD, a neurodevelopmental disorder that often co-occurs with conduct disorder and, together with it, predicts a more severe course. They are not part of the limited prosocial emotions specifier, which concerns emotional coldness. It tempts because the hyperactive, impulsive, disruptive child overlaps heavily with conduct problems in clinic samples.","b":"Major depression can co-occur with conduct disorder, especially in adolescents, and raises suicide risk, but comorbidity is recorded as a separate diagnosis and never defines a specifier. The limited prosocial emotions specifier is about missing guilt and empathy, not low mood. It tempts because irritability is common to both conditions and because 'specifier' sounds like any added clinical feature.","d":"Onset before age 5 is not a conduct disorder rule. DSM-5 onset subtypes use age 10: childhood-onset needs at least one symptom before 10, adolescent-onset none before 10. Onset age is also a separate specifier from limited prosocial emotions. It tempts because early onset and callous-unemotional traits both mark a worse prognosis, so candidates blend the two specifiers into one."}},{"q":"Pibloktoq, brain fag, and dhat are examples of:","concept":"Culture shapes how distress is felt, voiced and explained. DSM-IV (1994) listed 'culture-bound syndromes' in a glossary, including pibloktoq, brain fag, dhat, koro, amok and susto. DSM-5 (2013) replaced that term with 'cultural concepts of distress', arguing that no syndrome is bound to a single culture and that Western disorders are also culturally shaped. It separates three kinds: cultural syndromes (symptom clusters recognised locally), cultural idioms of distress (shared ways of talking about suffering, such as 'thinking too much') and cultural explanations or perceived causes. DSM-5 also added the 16-question Cultural Formulation Interview. Pibloktoq, or 'Arctic hysteria', described among the Inuit of Greenland, involves sudden episodes of screaming, tearing off clothes and running into the cold, followed by amnesia. Brain fag, described by Raymond Prince (1960) in Nigerian students, involves head and neck pain, blurred vision and inability to study under academic pressure. Dhat syndrome, named by Narendra Nath Wig (1960), is common in young South Asian men: anxiety, weakness and fatigue blamed on loss of semen in urine or nocturnal emission; ICD-10 listed it under F48.8. Other examples are koro (fear that the penis will retract into the body, seen in Southeast Asia and in Indian outbreaks), susto (fright causing soul loss, Latin America), ataque de nervios (Caribbean) and taijin kyofusho (Japan, fear of offending others). ICD-11 has no separate list and adds culture-related notes within each disorder. Option d is right because all three are classic culture-bound syndromes, now called cultural concepts of distress.","traps":{"a":"DSM-5 anxiety disorders are a fixed list: separation anxiety disorder, selective mutism, specific phobia, social anxiety disorder, panic disorder, agoraphobia and generalised anxiety disorder, plus substance-induced and medical-condition types. None of the three named states is a subtype. It tempts because anxiety is prominent in dhat and brain fag, and DSM-5 discusses taijin kyofusho alongside social anxiety disorder.","b":"Substance-induced disorders are mental disorders caused by intoxication, withdrawal or medication exposure, such as alcohol-induced psychotic disorder, and DSM-5 requires evidence that the substance can produce the symptoms. Pibloktoq, brain fag and dhat have cultural and psychological explanations, not a drug cause. It tempts only as a broad-sounding category for candidates who do not recognise the three unfamiliar names.","c":"ICD-11 dissociative disorders include dissociative neurological symptom disorder, dissociative amnesia, trance disorder, possession trance disorder and dissociative identity disorder; trance that is part of accepted cultural or religious practice is excluded. Pibloktoq looks dissociative, with amnesia afterwards, but dhat and brain fag are not dissociative at all, so the group does not fit. It tempts because trance and possession are classic examples in culture chapters."}},{"q":"Major depressive episode requires how many of nine SIG-E-CAPS symptoms?","concept":"A major depressive episode (MDE) is the building block of major depressive disorder and also appears in bipolar I and II disorder. DSM-5 (2013) lists nine symptoms: depressed mood, markedly diminished interest or pleasure (anhedonia), significant weight or appetite change, insomnia or hypersomnia, psychomotor agitation or retardation observable by others, fatigue or loss of energy, worthlessness or excessive guilt, poor concentration or indecisiveness, and recurrent thoughts of death or suicidal ideation, plan or attempt. The mnemonic SIG E CAPS (Sleep, Interest, Guilt, Energy, Concentration, Appetite, Psychomotor, Suicide) plus depressed mood covers all nine. Criterion A needs five or more symptoms during the same two-week period, representing a change from previous functioning, and at least one must be depressed mood or loss of interest; in children and adolescents mood can be irritable. Symptoms must cause clinically significant distress or impairment and not be due to a substance or medical condition. DSM-5 removed the bereavement exclusion, so recent grief no longer automatically rules out an MDE. Compare the neighbours: persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia) needs depressed mood for at least two years (one year in children) plus two of six symptoms; a manic episode needs one week of elevated or irritable mood plus three symptoms (four if mood is only irritable); a hypomanic episode needs four days. ICD-11 also requires two weeks and at least five of ten symptoms, adding hopelessness. Option c is right because DSM-5 needs five or more of the nine symptoms, with at least one being depressed mood or anhedonia.","traps":{"a":"Three is a real DSM-5 number, but for other things: a manic episode needs three of seven symptoms beyond elevated mood, and the 'with mixed features' specifier needs at least three symptoms of the opposite pole. Three depressive symptoms would capture much ordinary sadness. It tempts candidates who recall 'three' from the bipolar criteria and carry it over to depression.","b":"Four symptoms falls below the DSM-5 threshold. The DSM-IV research appendix used two to four depressive symptoms over two weeks for minor depressive disorder, a subthreshold condition, and DSM-5 lists 'depressive episode with insufficient symptoms' under other specified depressive disorder. Four is also the count needed for mania when mood is only irritable. It tempts as a near-miss for a half-remembered cut-off.","d":"Requiring all nine symptoms would make major depression extremely rare and would miss most patients seen in clinics. No DSM or ICD edition sets such a rule; polythetic criteria deliberately require only a subset, so people with different symptom mixes can share a diagnosis. It tempts candidates who misread 'nine symptoms' in the stem as nine required symptoms."}},{"q":"Body dysmorphic disorder is classified in DSM-5 under:","concept":"Body dysmorphic disorder (BDD) is a preoccupation with one or more perceived defects or flaws in physical appearance that are not observable or appear slight to others. The Italian psychiatrist Enrico Morselli described it in 1891 as 'dysmorphophobia'. DSM-5 (2013) requires the preoccupation; repetitive behaviours (mirror checking, excessive grooming, skin picking, reassurance seeking) or mental acts (comparing one's looks with others) at some point; clinically significant distress or impairment; and that the concern is not better explained by worry about body fat or weight in someone who meets eating disorder criteria. Specifiers are 'with muscle dysmorphia' (belief that one's build is too small) and insight: good or fair, poor, or absent insight/delusional beliefs. When conviction is delusional, DSM-5 still diagnoses BDD rather than delusional disorder. DSM-5 created a new chapter, obsessive-compulsive and related disorders, grouping OCD, BDD, hoarding disorder, trichotillomania (hair-pulling) and excoriation (skin-picking) disorder, because they share intrusive preoccupations, repetitive behaviours, family loading and response to SSRIs. In DSM-IV (1994) BDD was a somatoform disorder, and ICD-10 placed it under hypochondriacal disorder. ICD-11 also puts it in obsessive-compulsive or related disorders, with olfactory reference disorder and hypochondriasis. Do not confuse BDD with illness anxiety disorder (fear of having a serious disease) or social anxiety disorder (fear of scrutiny not limited to a perceived flaw). Option d is right because DSM-5 classifies body dysmorphic disorder under obsessive-compulsive and related disorders.","traps":{"a":"Somatic symptom and related disorders in DSM-5 cover somatic symptom disorder, illness anxiety disorder, conversion disorder, factitious disorder and psychological factors affecting other medical conditions. They centre on bodily symptoms or fear of illness, not a perceived flaw in looks. It tempts because DSM-IV (1994) listed BDD among the somatoform disorders, so older books still group it there.","b":"Personality disorders are enduring, pervasive patterns of inner experience and behaviour that begin by early adulthood and show across many situations, such as narcissistic or avoidant personality disorder. BDD is a specific, focused preoccupation with appearance, not a whole personality style. It tempts because the constant self-focus, shame and social avoidance in BDD can resemble a narcissistic or avoidant personality.","c":"DSM-5 anxiety disorders, such as social anxiety disorder, specific phobia and panic disorder, are defined by excessive fear and avoidance of particular situations or objects. BDD involves heavy anxiety and social avoidance, but its structure is an obsession about appearance plus compulsive checking, which is why DSM-5 grouped it with OCD. It tempts because the older name 'dysmorphophobia' sounds like a phobia."}},{"q":"Narcissistic personality disorder's DSM-5 core triad is best summarised as:","concept":"Narcissistic personality disorder (NPD) is a Cluster B personality disorder defined in DSM-5 (2013) as a pervasive pattern of grandiosity (in fantasy or behaviour), need for admiration and lack of empathy, beginning by early adulthood and present in a variety of contexts. Five or more of nine criteria are needed: grandiose sense of self-importance; preoccupation with fantasies of unlimited success, power, brilliance or ideal love; belief that one is special and should associate only with high-status people; need for excessive admiration; sense of entitlement; interpersonal exploitativeness; lack of empathy; envy of others or belief that others envy them; and arrogant, haughty behaviour. The opening sentence of each DSM personality disorder states its core, and exams test exactly that sentence. The name comes from the Greek myth of Narcissus. Sigmund Freud wrote 'On Narcissism' (1914); Heinz Kohut (1971) traced narcissism to unmet childhood needs for mirroring, and Otto Kernberg (1975) described a more aggressive, pathological grandiose self. Researchers now separate grandiose (overt) from vulnerable (covert) narcissism, the latter marked by hypersensitivity and shame. Learn the other cores: borderline is instability of relationships, self-image and affect with impulsivity; histrionic is excessive emotionality and attention seeking; antisocial is disregard for others' rights; paranoid is distrust and suspiciousness; schizotypal adds eccentricity and perceptual distortions; dependent is submissive, clinging behaviour and fear of separation. Option a is right because grandiosity, need for admiration and lack of empathy are the three core features in the DSM-5 definition of NPD.","traps":{"b":"Impulsivity, identity disturbance and intense anger are three of the nine criteria for borderline personality disorder, whose core is instability of relationships, self-image and affect. People with NPD can show rage when criticised, but their self-image is inflated and fairly stable, not diffuse. It tempts because both are Cluster B disorders and both can show anger and stormy relationships.","c":"Suspiciousness, social anxiety and eccentricity describe Cluster A, the odd or eccentric cluster: suspiciousness is the core of paranoid personality disorder, and excessive social anxiety with odd behaviour and magical thinking defines schizotypal personality disorder. NPD belongs to Cluster B. It tempts because a narcissistic person's sensitivity to criticism can look like suspicion of other people's motives.","d":"Submissiveness, fear of separation and reassurance seeking describe dependent personality disorder, a Cluster C (anxious or fearful) disorder defined by an excessive need to be taken care of; DSM-5 needs five of eight criteria. It is almost the opposite of narcissistic entitlement. It tempts because both disorders lean heavily on other people, one for admiration, the other for care and reassurance."}},{"q":"La belle indifférence — relative emotional indifference to a severe disability — is classically associated with:","concept":"La belle indifférence ('beautiful indifference') is a lack of concern, sometimes a calm or even cheerful attitude, about the nature or implications of a serious symptom such as a paralysed limb or sudden blindness. It became a textbook sign of hysteria in nineteenth-century French neurology. Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud, in Studies on Hysteria (1895), explained hysterical symptoms as conversion: an unbearable conflict is 'converted' into a bodily symptom, and this primary gain (keeping the conflict out of awareness) would explain why the patient seems unworried. Secondary gain means outside benefits such as attention or release from duties. DSM-5 (2013) names the condition conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder): symptoms of altered voluntary motor or sensory function, with clinical findings showing incompatibility with recognised neurological disease, for example a positive Hoover's sign or non-epileptic seizures. DSM-5 dropped the need to identify a psychological stressor and to rule out feigning. DSM-5-TR (2022) states that la belle indifférence is not specific to conversion disorder and should not be used to make the diagnosis; studies find it in organic illness too and missing in many conversion patients. ICD-11 calls the condition dissociative neurological symptom disorder. Keep the neighbours apart: somatic symptom disorder shows excessive worry about symptoms; factitious disorder involves deliberate deception to take the sick role; malingering is deliberate deception for external gain. Option d is right because la belle indifférence is the sign classically linked with conversion disorder, even though it is no longer considered diagnostic.","traps":{"a":"Malingering is the intentional production of false or grossly exaggerated symptoms for an external incentive, such as compensation, avoiding duty or obtaining drugs; DSM-5 lists it among other conditions that may be a focus of clinical attention, not as a mental disorder. A malingerer usually stresses and displays the symptom rather than appearing unconcerned. It tempts because both conditions involve symptoms without organic disease.","b":"Somatic symptom disorder (DSM-5) is one or more distressing bodily symptoms plus excessive thoughts, feelings or behaviours about them: disproportionate worry, high health anxiety or excessive time spent on health concerns, typically for more than six months. That is the opposite of indifference. It tempts because it shares the same DSM-5 chapter as conversion disorder and replaced the older somatisation disorder.","c":"Factitious disorder is the deliberate falsification or induction of signs and symptoms, with identified deception, even without obvious external reward; the aim is to assume the sick role. Such patients are often eager for tests and procedures, not unconcerned. It tempts because conversion and factitious disorder share the somatic symptom chapter and both present symptoms that do not fit medical disease."}},{"q":"Factitious disorder imposed on another (Munchausen by proxy) is distinguished from malingering by:","concept":"Factitious disorder (DSM-5, 2013) is the falsification of physical or psychological signs or symptoms, or induction of injury or disease, with identified deception; the person presents as ill, impaired or injured, and the deceptive behaviour is evident even without obvious external rewards. In factitious disorder imposed on another, a caregiver falsifies or induces illness in someone else, usually a child, a dependent adult or a pet, and presents that victim as ill. The diagnosis goes to the perpetrator, not the victim, and the situation also calls for child-protection action. Richard Asher (1951) named Munchausen syndrome after the fictional Baron Munchausen, teller of tall tales, and Roy Meadow (1977) described Munchausen syndrome by proxy. The key contrast is motive. In malingering, symptoms are also produced intentionally, but for an external incentive such as money, avoiding military duty or work, evading prosecution or obtaining drugs; DSM-5 treats malingering as a condition that may be a focus of clinical attention, not a mental disorder. In conversion disorder, symptoms are not consciously produced at all. The classic triangle is: conscious production for external gain (malingering), conscious production for an internal, psychological need (factitious), and no conscious production (conversion and somatic symptom disorders). Clues to factitious disorder include dramatic but inconsistent histories, eagerness for invasive procedures and visits to many hospitals. ICD-11 uses the terms factitious disorder imposed on self and imposed on another. Option d is right because factitious disorder lacks obvious external incentives, the motive being the sick role, whereas malingering is driven by external gain.","traps":{"a":"Dissociation is a disruption of the normal integration of consciousness, memory, identity, perception or behaviour, as in dissociative amnesia or dissociative identity disorder. Both factitious disorder and malingering involve conscious, deliberate deception, so dissociation does not separate them. It tempts because dramatic, puzzling presentations and the old label 'hysteria' link deception, conversion and dissociation in many minds.","b":"Substance use can occur in both conditions: a malingerer may fake pain to obtain opioids, and a person with factitious disorder may secretly take drugs such as insulin or anticoagulants to create symptoms. Because it appears in both, it cannot distinguish them, and it is not a criterion of either. It tempts because obtaining drugs is listed among the external incentives in malingering.","c":"Neither condition has an adolescence-only onset rule. Factitious disorder imposed on self usually begins in early adulthood, often after a hospital stay, and the perpetrator in the imposed-on-another form is typically an adult caregiver, most often a parent. An adolescent-only onset would make the 'by proxy' form almost impossible. It tempts because invented age criteria look like real DSM-style specifications."}},{"q":"DSM-5 specifier 'with mixed features' applied to a manic or hypomanic episode requires concurrent symptoms of:","concept":"DSM-IV (1994) defined a 'mixed episode' as meeting full criteria for both a manic episode and a major depressive episode nearly every day for at least one week. This was so strict that it was rarely used. DSM-5 (2013) dropped the mixed episode and introduced the specifier 'with mixed features', which can be attached to manic, hypomanic or major depressive episodes in bipolar I, bipolar II and major depressive disorder. For a manic or hypomanic episode with mixed features, full criteria for that episode are met and at least three depressive symptoms are present on most days: prominent dysphoria or depressed mood, diminished interest or pleasure, psychomotor retardation, fatigue or loss of energy, worthlessness or excessive guilt, and recurrent thoughts of death or suicide. For a depressive episode with mixed features, at least three manic or hypomanic symptoms are needed, such as elevated mood, inflated self-esteem, pressured speech, racing thoughts, increased energy or goal-directed activity, risky behaviour or decreased need for sleep. Symptoms shared by both poles, such as distractibility, irritability and psychomotor agitation, are not counted. If full criteria for mania and depression are met together, the episode is called manic with mixed features. Mixed states carry a higher suicide risk. Do not confuse this with other specifiers: with anxious distress, with psychotic features, with rapid cycling (four or more episodes in 12 months), with melancholic features and with peripartum onset. ICD-11 keeps a separate mixed episode. Option c is right because a manic or hypomanic episode with mixed features requires concurrent depressive symptoms.","traps":{"a":"Psychosis is recorded with the separate 'with psychotic features' specifier, which can be mood-congruent (grandiose delusions in mania, guilt or nihilistic delusions in depression) or mood-incongruent. Psychotic symptoms can appear during mixed states, but they are not what 'mixed' means. It tempts because severe mania often becomes psychotic and the two specifiers are listed side by side in DSM-5.","b":"Anxiety is captured by the 'with anxious distress' specifier: at least two of feeling keyed up or tense, unusual restlessness, difficulty concentrating because of worry, fear that something awful may happen, and feeling of losing control. It applies to both bipolar and depressive episodes, but it describes anxiety, not the opposite mood pole. It tempts because agitated mixed states often look anxious.","d":"Substance withdrawal belongs to differential diagnosis. If mood symptoms are better explained by intoxication or withdrawal, the diagnosis becomes a substance/medication-induced bipolar or depressive disorder, not a specifier on a primary episode. It tempts because stimulant withdrawal can bring dysphoria after a high, which can look like mania and depression mixed together in the same person."}},{"q":"Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder (DMDD) onset must be before age:","concept":"Disruptive mood dysregulation disorder (DMDD) was introduced in DSM-5 (2013) and placed in the depressive disorders chapter. It responded to a sharp rise, especially in the United States, in diagnoses of paediatric bipolar disorder in children with chronic, non-episodic irritability rather than distinct manic episodes. Research by Ellen Leibenluft and colleagues (2003) on 'severe mood dysregulation' showed that such children tend to develop depression and anxiety as adults, not bipolar disorder. Criteria: severe recurrent temper outbursts, verbal or behavioural, grossly out of proportion to the situation and inconsistent with developmental level, on average three or more times a week; persistently irritable or angry mood most of the day, nearly every day, between outbursts; symptoms for 12 months or more without a symptom-free gap of three or more consecutive months; present in at least two of three settings (home, school, peers) and severe in at least one. The diagnosis should not be made for the first time before age 6 or after age 18, and onset must be before 10. DMDD cannot coexist with oppositional defiant disorder, intermittent explosive disorder or bipolar disorder; if ODD criteria are also met, only DMDD is diagnosed. Compare intermittent explosive disorder (outbursts without persistent irritable mood between them, minimum age 6) and ODD (angry or irritable mood plus defiance and vindictiveness, at least six months). ICD-11 has no DMDD and instead uses ODD with chronic irritability-anger. Option d is right because DSM-5 requires the onset of DMDD symptoms before age 10.","traps":{"a":"Six is a real DMDD number, but it is the lower limit for making the diagnosis, not the onset cut-off. DSM-5 says DMDD should not be diagnosed for the first time before age 6 (or after 18), because frequent tantrums are developmentally normal in preschoolers. It tempts because 6 and 10 sit in the same part of the criteria and are easily swapped.","b":"Sixteen does not appear in the DMDD criteria. The diagnosis may be first made up to age 18, but symptoms must have started before 10, so mid-adolescent onset rules DMDD out. In DSM-5, 16 is the age up to which ADHD needs six symptoms per domain. It tempts candidates who recall that DMDD extends into adolescence and pick a teenage number.","c":"Twelve is the DSM-5 onset age for ADHD, which requires several symptoms before age 12, and 12 months is the minimum duration of DMDD. It is not the DMDD onset cut-off, which is 10. It tempts because ADHD and DMDD often co-occur in irritable, hyperactive children, and 12 does appear in the DMDD criteria, but as a duration in months."}},{"q":"Anorexia nervosa is associated with which medical finding?","concept":"Anorexia nervosa (AN) is defined in DSM-5 (2013) by restriction of energy intake leading to significantly low body weight for age, sex, development and physical health; intense fear of gaining weight or persistent behaviour that interferes with weight gain; and disturbed experience of body weight or shape, undue influence of weight on self-evaluation, or failure to recognise the seriousness of low weight. Subtypes are restricting and binge-eating/purging. Severity in adults uses body mass index: mild 17 or more, moderate 16 to 16.99, severe 15 to 15.99, extreme below 15. DSM-5 removed the amenorrhoea criterion that DSM-IV (1994) required, so missing periods are now a common finding rather than a requirement. Starvation slows the body to save energy, giving the classic picture: bradycardia, hypotension, hypothermia, cold intolerance, lanugo (fine downy body hair), dry skin, constipation, amenorrhoea from low gonadotropins, osteopenia, and a low T3 or 'sick euthyroid' pattern. Purging adds hypokalaemia with metabolic alkalosis, enlarged salivary glands, raised amylase and Russell's sign (knuckle calluses from self-induced vomiting), described by Gerald Russell (1979) in his paper on bulimia nervosa. Low potassium and QT prolongation make cardiac arrhythmia a major cause of death, and AN has one of the highest mortality rates of any psychiatric disorder. Rapid refeeding can cause refeeding syndrome, driven by a fall in phosphate. Bulimia nervosa differs because weight is normal or above. Option b is right because lanugo, bradycardia, hypokalaemia and amenorrhoea are classic medical findings of anorexia nervosa.","traps":{"a":"Hyperthyroidism, as in Graves' disease, causes weight loss with increased appetite, heat intolerance, tachycardia, tremor and sweating. It is a medical differential for weight loss, not a feature of AN, where starvation produces a low T3, sick euthyroid picture with cold intolerance and a slow pulse. It tempts because a thin body and weight loss suggest an overactive thyroid.","c":"Polyphagia means excessive hunger and eating, a classic sign of uncontrolled diabetes mellitus (with polyuria and polydipsia) and of hyperthyroidism. AN is defined by restriction of intake despite hunger. Binge eating can occur in the binge-eating/purging subtype, but polyphagia is not the characteristic medical finding. It tempts because bingeing in eating disorders sounds like overeating.","d":"This option reverses both findings. AN causes hypokalaemia, especially with vomiting or laxative and diuretic misuse, and resting bradycardia. Hyperkalaemia is typical of kidney failure or adrenal insufficiency. Tachycardia can appear with dehydration or during refeeding, but it is not the classic picture. It tempts careless readers who only notice that potassium and heart rate are mentioned."}},{"q":"Specific phobia per DSM-5 requires duration of:","concept":"A specific phobia is marked fear or anxiety about a specific object or situation, such as flying, heights, animals, injections or seeing blood. In DSM-5 (2013) the phobic object or situation almost always provokes immediate fear, is actively avoided or endured with intense fear, the fear is out of proportion to the actual danger and sociocultural context, and it is persistent, typically lasting six months or more, with clinically significant distress or impairment. DSM-5 lists five specifiers: animal, natural environment (heights, storms, water), blood-injection-injury, situational (planes, lifts, enclosed places) and other (choking, vomiting, loud sounds in children). Blood-injection-injury phobia is unusual because of a vasovagal response, a brief rise and then a sharp fall in heart rate and blood pressure that can cause fainting. DSM-IV (1994) applied the six-month rule only to people under 18 and required adults to recognise that their fear was excessive; DSM-5 dropped the insight requirement and extended six months to all ages, so the clinician now judges proportion. Learn the other durations: social anxiety disorder, agoraphobia and generalised anxiety disorder need six months; panic disorder needs one month of worry or behaviour change after an attack; separation anxiety disorder needs four weeks in children and typically six months in adults; selective mutism needs one month. Martin Seligman (1971) proposed 'preparedness' to explain why phobias of snakes and heights are common, and exposure therapy is the treatment of choice. Option a is right because DSM-5 requires the phobic fear to be persistent, typically lasting six months or more.","traps":{"b":"One month is the panic disorder requirement: after at least one attack, a month or more of persistent worry about further attacks or maladaptive change in behaviour. One month is also the upper limit of acute stress disorder and the minimum for selective mutism. It tempts because panic attacks often occur in phobic situations, so the two disorders get mixed up.","c":"Three months is not a duration for specific phobia. It appears elsewhere: adjustment disorder symptoms must begin within three months of the stressor, and three months was the DSM-IV cut-off between acute and chronic PTSD. It tempts as a middle value for candidates who know the fear must be lasting but cannot recall the exact rule.","d":"One week is far too short for any anxiety disorder; it would label ordinary short-lived fears as illness, which the DSM-5 six-month rule was meant to prevent. One week is the minimum duration of a manic episode, not an anxiety criterion. It tempts candidates who think a single intense, recent fear reaction is enough to diagnose a phobia."}},{"q":"Schizoaffective disorder per DSM-5 requires:","concept":"Schizoaffective disorder was named by Jacob Kasanin (1933) for patients whose illness mixed schizophrenia-like psychosis with strong mood symptoms and a relatively good outcome. It sits across the divide Emil Kraepelin (1899) drew between dementia praecox and manic-depressive insanity. DSM-5 (2013) defines it with four criteria. A: an uninterrupted period of illness during which a major mood episode (major depressive or manic) is concurrent with Criterion A of schizophrenia; a depressive episode must include depressed mood. B: delusions or hallucinations for two or more weeks in the absence of a major mood episode at some point during the lifetime of the illness. C: symptoms meeting criteria for a major mood episode are present for the majority of the total duration of the active and residual portions of the illness, a rule DSM-5 tightened to make the diagnosis less frequent and more reliable. D: not due to a substance or medical condition. Subtypes are bipolar type and depressive type. Criterion B separates it from a mood disorder with psychotic features, where psychosis occurs only during mood episodes. Criterion C separates it from schizophrenia, where mood episodes occupy only a minority of the illness. ICD-11 (6A21) uses an episode approach instead: schizophrenia requirements and a moderate or severe mood episode are met within the same episode, starting together or within a few days of each other. Option a is right because DSM-5 requires a major mood episode concurrent with active-phase schizophrenia symptoms plus at least two weeks of psychosis without prominent mood symptoms.","traps":{"b":"If psychotic symptoms occur only during mood episodes, the diagnosis is a mood disorder with psychotic features, such as bipolar I disorder or major depressive disorder with psychotic features. Schizoaffective disorder needs a stretch of at least two weeks of delusions or hallucinations without a major mood episode. It tempts because overlap of mood and psychosis is part of the definition, so the missing half goes unnoticed.","c":"Lifetime absence of psychosis contradicts the diagnosis, which requires schizophrenia Criterion A symptoms such as delusions, hallucinations or disorganised speech. A person with only mood episodes and no psychosis has a mood disorder. It tempts because absence of psychosis is a real rule elsewhere, for example in hypomanic episodes, where psychotic features would make the episode manic by definition.","d":"Bipolar features alone describe bipolar disorder. Schizoaffective disorder does have a bipolar type, used when a manic episode is part of the presentation, and a depressive type, but the subtype only labels the mood component; the psychosis criteria still apply. It tempts because 'bipolar type' is a real schizoaffective subtype, so the phrase looks familiar to a hurried reader."}},{"q":"WAIS-IV yields four index scores:","concept":"David Wechsler (1939) published the Wechsler-Bellevue Intelligence Scale, defining intelligence as the global capacity to act purposefully, think rationally and deal effectively with the environment. The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale followed in 1955, and the series used deviation IQs (mean 100, SD 15) instead of the ratio IQ of the Stanford-Binet. Up to WAIS-R, scores were reported as Verbal IQ, Performance IQ and Full Scale IQ. WAIS-III (1997) added four index scores alongside these IQs, and WAIS-IV (2008), for ages 16 to 90, dropped Verbal and Performance IQ altogether, keeping four indices that combine into the Full Scale IQ. Verbal Comprehension (Similarities, Vocabulary, Information; Comprehension supplemental) measures verbal reasoning and acquired knowledge. Perceptual Reasoning (Block Design, Matrix Reasoning, Visual Puzzles; Picture Completion and Figure Weights supplemental) measures nonverbal, fluid reasoning. Working Memory (Digit Span, Arithmetic; Letter-Number Sequencing supplemental) measures holding and manipulating information. Processing Speed (Symbol Search, Coding; Cancellation supplemental) measures speed of simple visual scanning and copying. Ten core subtests yield the indices, and an optional General Ability Index uses only Verbal Comprehension and Perceptual Reasoning, reducing the effect of working memory and speed problems. Variant questions also ask about later editions: WISC-V (2014) splits Perceptual Reasoning into Visual Spatial and Fluid Reasoning, giving five primary indices, and WAIS-5 (2024) adopted the same five-index structure. Option c is right because the four WAIS-IV indices are Verbal Comprehension, Perceptual Reasoning, Working Memory and Processing Speed.","traps":{"a":"Verbal IQ, Performance IQ and Full Scale IQ were the summary scores of the Wechsler-Bellevue, WAIS, WAIS-R and WAIS-III; WAIS-IV (2008) dropped VIQ and PIQ. Memory Quotient came from the original Wechsler Memory Scale (1945), a separate test. It tempts because VIQ-PIQ differences fill older textbooks and neuropsychology papers, and Wechsler's name is on both tests.","b":"Fluid and crystallised intelligence come from Raymond Cattell (1963), later extended with John Horn: fluid ability is reasoning with novel problems and crystallised ability is acquired knowledge. They inform test design but are not WAIS-IV index names, and 'Visual IQ' and 'Auditory IQ' are not Wechsler scores. It tempts because Perceptual Reasoning and Verbal Comprehension roughly map onto fluid and crystallised ability.","d":"Cognitive, behavioural, emotional and adaptive are broad domains of a full clinical assessment. Adaptive behaviour is measured by scales such as the Vineland, and emotional and behavioural problems by checklists or personality tests. They are not intelligence test indices. It tempts because the words sound like a complete picture of functioning and because intellectual disability diagnosis combines cognitive and adaptive scores."}},{"q":"On the MMPI, the L (Lie) scale primarily detects:","concept":"The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI) was developed by Starke R. Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) using empirical criterion keying: items were kept if they separated patient groups from normal controls, whatever their content. Because self-report can be distorted, it includes validity scales. The L (Lie) scale has 15 items on the MMPI-2 (1989), each describing a minor fault that most people would admit, such as sometimes getting angry or putting off work; the items drew on the character studies of Hugh Hartshorne and Mark May (1928). A person who denies many of them presents an unrealistically virtuous picture, a naive, unsophisticated attempt to 'fake good'. High L suggests defensiveness and possible under-reporting of real problems; it is also raised in people from very traditional or religious backgrounds. The K (Correction) scale, added by Paul Meehl and Hathaway (1946), detects subtler defensiveness and is used to correct five clinical scales. The S (Superlative Self-Presentation) scale is another MMPI-2 fake-good measure. On the over-reporting side, the F (Infrequency) scale picks up rare responses from symptom exaggeration, severe distress or random answering, with Fb and Fp variants. Inconsistency is checked by VRIN (variable response) and TRIN (true response, for acquiescence or nay-saying), and the Cannot Say count tracks omitted items. In the MMPI-2-RF (2008) the L scale became L-r, 'Uncommon Virtues'. Option b is right because the L scale detects naive impression management, the denial of common minor faults most people would admit.","traps":{"a":"Random responding is detected by VRIN, the Variable Response Inconsistency scale, which compares answers to pairs of similar or opposite items, and it also inflates the F scale. The L scale cannot spot randomness, because a random responder endorses some L items and denies others, giving an ordinary score. It tempts because all validity scales seem to 'check honesty' in a general way.","c":"Severe psychopathology shows up on the ten clinical scales, such as Scale 2 (Depression) and Scale 8 (Schizophrenia), and a very high F can reflect severe distress or exaggeration. A high L points the other way: it suggests the person is hiding problems. It tempts because 'Lie' sounds like an antisocial trait, which is actually tapped by Scale 4 (Psychopathic Deviate).","d":"Reading ability is a precondition for valid testing and is checked before administration; the MMPI-2 needs basic reading skills, and an audio version exists for poor readers. Poor reading usually shows up as many omitted items, high VRIN inconsistency or raised F, not as high L. It tempts because invalid profiles caused by poor reading are a known practical problem on long inventories."}},{"q":"The Vineland Adaptive Behaviour Scales measure:","concept":"Adaptive behaviour is the set of conceptual, social and practical skills people learn and use in daily life: communicating, caring for themselves, managing money and safety, and getting along with others. Edgar A. Doll (1935) published the Vineland Social Maturity Scale at the Vineland Training School in New Jersey; it yields a social age and a social quotient (social age divided by chronological age, times 100), and an Indian adaptation is still widely used in Indian clinics. Sara Sparrow, David Balla and Domenic Cicchetti revised it as the Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales (1984), followed by Vineland-II (2005) and Vineland-3 (2016). Information comes mainly from a semi-structured interview or rating form completed by a parent, teacher or caregiver. The core domains are Communication (receptive, expressive, written), Daily Living Skills (personal, domestic, community), Socialization (interpersonal relationships, play and leisure, coping skills) and, mainly in younger children, Motor Skills (gross and fine); an optional Maladaptive Behavior index covers problem behaviours. The Adaptive Behavior Composite uses a mean of 100 and SD of 15, like IQ. Adaptive behaviour matters because DSM-5 (2013) and the AAIDD both require deficits in intellectual functioning and in adaptive functioning, with onset in the developmental period, to diagnose intellectual disability, and DSM-5 sets severity (mild to profound) by adaptive functioning, not IQ. Other adaptive measures include the AAMR Adaptive Behavior Scale and the Adaptive Behavior Assessment System. Option a is right because the Vineland measures adaptive functioning across communication, daily living skills, socialisation and motor skills.","traps":{"b":"Personality traits are measured by inventories such as the MMPI of Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943), the 16PF of Raymond Cattell (1949) and the NEO-PI-R of Paul Costa and Robert McCrae (1992). They describe enduring styles of feeling and behaving, not practical everyday competence. It tempts because the original Vineland was a 'social maturity' scale, and social maturity sounds like a personality trait.","c":"IQ is measured by intelligence tests such as the Wechsler scales and the Stanford-Binet. The Vineland deliberately measures something different: what a person actually does day to day, which correlates only moderately with IQ. It tempts because both yield standard scores with mean 100 and SD 15, and both are needed together for an intellectual disability diagnosis, so they are easily merged in memory.","d":"Severity of psychopathology is rated with clinician scales such as the Brief Psychiatric Rating Scale of John Overall and Donald Gorham (1962) or the Hamilton Depression Rating Scale of Max Hamilton (1960). The Vineland measures skills, not symptoms; its optional maladaptive behaviour index records problem behaviour but is outside the core composite. It tempts because adaptive deficits and psychopathology often appear together in clinical cases."}},{"q":"On the Wechsler scales, an IQ of 85 corresponds to which percentile?","concept":"Modern IQs are deviation IQs: a person's raw score is compared with others of the same age and converted to a scale with mean 100 and standard deviation (SD) 15. David Wechsler (1939) introduced this approach to replace the ratio IQ (mental age divided by chronological age, times 100) proposed by William Stern (1912) and used in the Stanford-Binet of Lewis Terman (1916). To find a percentile, convert to a z score: z equals the score minus the mean, divided by the SD. For IQ 85, z = (85 minus 100) / 15 = minus 1. In a normal curve about 34.1 percent of cases lie between the mean and one SD, so 50 minus 34.1 leaves about 15.9 percent below z = minus 1, rounded to the 16th percentile. Anchors: IQ 70 (z = minus 2) is about the 2nd percentile, IQ 100 the 50th, IQ 115 the 84th and IQ 130 about the 98th. The same logic works for other standard scores: T scores (mean 50, SD 10, used by the MMPI), where T 40 is the 16th percentile; Wechsler subtest scaled scores (mean 10, SD 3), where 7 is the 16th; and stanines (mean 5, SD about 2). Wechsler descriptive labels call 80 to 89 'low average' and 90 to 109 'average'. An IQ about two SDs below the mean, roughly 70 or below allowing for measurement error, is one part of the criteria for intellectual disability. Option c is right because IQ 85 is one SD below the mean, which is about the 16th percentile.","traps":{"a":"The 10th percentile lies at about z = minus 1.28, which on the IQ scale is roughly 81, not 85. Ten percent is a round, memorable figure, but it does not match any standard SD landmark. It tempts candidates who estimate rather than calculate, since an IQ of 85 feels as if it should sit near the bottom tenth of the population.","b":"The 25th percentile is the first quartile, at about z = minus 0.67, which corresponds to an IQ of about 90. IQ 85 is further down the curve. It tempts because a quarter looks like a natural 'below average' point and the five-point gap seems small, but percentiles bunch near the mean, so a few IQ points shift percentile rank a lot.","d":"The 50th percentile is the median, which equals the mean in a normal distribution: an IQ of 100, not 85. A score of 85 is one full SD below average. It tempts candidates who think anything in the low average band (80 to 89) counts as 'normal' and therefore must sit at the middle of the distribution."}},{"q":"The Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) was developed by:","concept":"The Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) was introduced by Christiana D. Morgan and Henry A. Murray (1935) at the Harvard Psychological Clinic, and Murray published the standard card set with a manual in 1943. 'Apperception' means interpreting a new perception in the light of past experience, so the stories a person tells are assumed to reveal inner needs and conflicts. The set has 31 cards: 30 black-and-white pictures of ambiguous scenes and one blank card. Originally about 20 were chosen by age and sex, though most clinicians now use fewer. For each card the person tells a story: what led up to the scene, what is happening, what the characters feel and think, and how it ends. Interpretation follows Murray's need-press theory from Explorations in Personality (1938): needs (achievement, affiliation, power and many others) are internal drives, presses are environmental forces, and the hero of the story is taken to stand for the storyteller. David McClelland and John Atkinson (1953) later used TAT-type pictures to score achievement motivation. Neighbouring projective tests are common distractors: the inkblot test of Hermann Rorschach (1921); the Comprehensive System for Rorschach scoring by John Exner (1974); and the Children's Apperception Test of Leopold Bellak (1949), which uses animal figures. Indian adaptations with locally drawn pictures have also been developed. The TAT is criticised for weak reliability and lack of a single standard scoring system, but it remains a classic exam topic. Option d is right because Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan developed the TAT.","traps":{"a":"Hermann Rorschach, a Swiss psychiatrist, published the inkblot test in Psychodiagnostik (1921): ten symmetrical inkblots, five achromatic and five with colour, which the person describes. It is the other major projective test, but it uses unstructured inkblots, not pictures of people in scenes. It tempts because Rorschach is the most famous name linked with projective testing.","b":"John Exner developed the Comprehensive System (1974), which combined earlier Rorschach scoring approaches into one standardised method with norms for location, determinants and content. He worked on the Rorschach, not the TAT, and did not create the inkblots or the picture cards. It tempts because Exner is the best-known name in modern projective scoring and appears in many assessment questions.","c":"Leopold Bellak created the Children's Apperception Test (1949), a TAT-style test for children aged about 3 to 10 using pictures of animals in human situations, because young children were thought to identify more easily with animals. He adapted Murray's idea; he did not originate it. It tempts because 'Apperception' appears in the CAT's name and Bellak also wrote widely on TAT interpretation."}},{"q":"On a normal distribution, what proportion of cases fall within ±2 standard deviations of the mean?","concept":"The normal distribution is the symmetric, bell-shaped curve described mathematically by Abraham de Moivre (1733) and Carl Friedrich Gauss (1809), and applied to human abilities by Francis Galton (1869). Its mean, median and mode coincide, and its spread is measured by the standard deviation (SD). The empirical rule, also called the 68-95-99.7 rule, gives the proportion of cases within fixed distances of the mean: about 68.3 percent within ±1 SD, about 95.4 percent within ±2 SD and about 99.7 percent within ±3 SD. Each half of the curve holds 50 percent, split into bands of 34.1 percent between the mean and 1 SD, 13.6 percent between 1 and 2 SD, and 2.1 percent between 2 and 3 SD, leaving about 0.1 percent beyond 3 SD on each side. Exactly 95 percent lies within ±1.96 SD, which is why 1.96 is the critical z for a two-tailed test at the 0.05 level and for 95 percent confidence intervals; ±2.58 SD contains 99 percent. Applied to IQ (mean 100, SD 15), about 95 percent of people score between 70 and 130, and about 2.3 percent fall below 70. For T scores (mean 50, SD 10) the ±2 SD range is 30 to 70. Skewness (asymmetry) and kurtosis (peakedness or tail weight) describe how far a real distribution departs from this shape. Option c is right because about 95 percent of cases fall within ±2 SD of the mean.","traps":{"a":"About 50 percent of cases fall within ±0.67 SD of the mean, the interquartile range between the 25th and 75th percentiles; on the IQ scale that is roughly 90 to 110. Fifty percent is also the share lying on either side of the mean. It tempts candidates who confuse 'half the distribution lies below the mean' with the proportion inside a two-SD band.","b":"About 68 percent of cases fall within ±1 SD, which on the IQ scale means roughly 85 to 115. It is the first number of the 68-95-99.7 rule, so it is the most remembered value. It tempts candidates who read '±2 SD' as one SD on each side, counting the two directions as the two standard deviations.","d":"About 99.7 percent of cases fall within ±3 SD, which for IQ is 55 to 145. Only about 0.3 percent lie outside, which is why scores beyond three SDs are often treated as outliers. It tempts candidates who remember the rule but shift it by one step, or who feel that nearly everyone must lie within two SDs."}},{"q":"Cronbach's alpha measures:","concept":"Reliability is the consistency of a measurement; in classical test theory it is the proportion of observed score variance that is true score variance. Lee J. Cronbach (1951) introduced coefficient alpha to estimate internal consistency: how closely the items of a scale relate to one another when they are meant to measure a single construct. It needs only one administration. Alpha rises with the average inter-item correlation and with the number of items, and Cronbach showed it equals the mean of all possible split-half coefficients. For dichotomous (right or wrong) items it is identical to the Kuder-Richardson formula 20 of G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson (1937). The older split-half method correlates two halves of a test and corrects upward with the formula of Charles Spearman and William Brown (1910). A common rule of thumb treats .70 as acceptable for research and .90 or above for individual clinical decisions. A high alpha does not prove the scale is unidimensional, and very high values can mean redundant items. Neighbouring reliability types: test-retest (same test, two occasions, stability over time), parallel or alternate forms (two equivalent versions, equivalence), and inter-rater (agreement between judges, measured by Cohen's kappa for categories or the intraclass correlation for continuous ratings). Validity is a separate question: whether the test measures what it claims, with construct validity set out by Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955). Option a is right because Cronbach's alpha is a coefficient of internal consistency reliability.","traps":{"b":"Test-retest reliability gives the same test to the same people on two occasions and correlates the scores; it measures stability over time and is called the coefficient of stability. It is affected by the time gap, practice and memory effects, and real change in the trait. Alpha uses a single administration and says nothing about stability. It tempts because both are reliability coefficients.","c":"Construct validity is the degree to which a test measures the theoretical construct it claims to, a concept set out by Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955) and tested with convergent and discriminant evidence, for example the multitrait-multimethod matrix of Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske (1959). Alpha is a reliability index, not validity. It tempts because Cronbach's name is attached to both ideas.","d":"Inter-rater reliability is the agreement between two or more independent raters scoring the same responses or behaviour, as in projective test scoring or diagnostic interviews. It is measured by Cohen's kappa (Jacob Cohen, 1960) for categorical judgments or the intraclass correlation for continuous ratings. Alpha concerns agreement among items, not among raters. It tempts because both describe consistency of scores."}},{"q":"Predictive validity is best demonstrated by:","concept":"Validity is the degree to which a test measures what it claims to measure and supports the decisions made from its scores. Textbooks group it into content validity, criterion-related validity and construct validity, the last formalised by Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955). Criterion-related validity asks how well test scores relate to an external criterion, an independent measure of the behaviour the test is meant to forecast or reflect, such as job ratings, grades or a clinician's diagnosis. It is expressed as a validity coefficient, the correlation between test and criterion. It has two forms that differ only in timing. In predictive validity the test is given first and the criterion is collected later: an entrance examination is correlated with first-year marks a year later, or an aptitude test given at hiring is correlated with supervisor ratings after six months. In concurrent validity the criterion is gathered at about the same time, for example a new depression scale against a psychiatrist's diagnosis made the same week. Predictive designs suffer from range restriction, because only selected applicants produce criterion scores, and from criterion contamination if raters know the test scores. Keep validity separate from reliability: split-half, test-retest and alternate-form methods correlate a test with itself and show consistency, not accuracy. Face validity, whether items look appropriate to test takers, is not statistical evidence at all. Option b is right because predictive validity is shown by correlating present test scores with a criterion measured at a later point in time.","traps":{"a":"Asking experts or test takers whether items 'look right' describes face validity, a judgement of surface appearance. When qualified experts systematically rate whether items cover the whole content domain, that is content validity, typically used for achievement tests. Neither involves an external criterion or the passage of time. It tempts because expert judgement sounds rigorous and content validity is a genuine validity type.","c":"Splitting a test into two halves (often odd versus even items) and correlating them gives split-half reliability, an index of internal consistency. Because each half is only half as long, the correlation is stepped up with the Spearman-Brown formula (Charles Spearman and William Brown, both 1910). It shows that items hang together, not that scores forecast anything outside the test. It tempts because it also produces a correlation coefficient.","d":"Giving the same test to the same people twice, two weeks apart, and correlating the scores is test-retest reliability, which yields a coefficient of stability. The test is compared with itself, not with an external criterion, so it says nothing about predicting future behaviour. It tempts because, like predictive validity, it involves a time gap between two measurements."}},{"q":"On the MMPI-2, T-scores of ≥65 are considered clinically significant. T=65 corresponds to:","concept":"A T score is a linear standard score with a mean of 50 and a standard deviation (SD) of 10. It is obtained from a z score by T = 50 + 10z, so any T score can be turned back into SD units by z = (T minus 50) divided by 10. For T = 65 this gives 15 divided by 10, which is 1.5, so the person scored 1.5 SD above the normative mean, roughly the 93rd percentile in a normal distribution. Useful anchors: T 60 is +1 SD (about the 84th percentile), T 70 is +2 SD (about the 98th percentile), and T 40 is 1 SD below the mean. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory was published by Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943), and the original test treated T 70 or above as clinically elevated. The restandardised MMPI-2 (James Butcher and colleagues, 1989) lowered the interpretive cutoff to T 65 and introduced uniform T scores for eight clinical scales, so that a given T score means the same percentile across those scales. The MMPI-2-RF (2008) also uses T 65. Other standard-score metrics that examiners pair with T: z scores (mean 0, SD 1), deviation IQ (mean 100, SD 15), Wechsler scaled scores (mean 10, SD 3) and stanines (mean 5, SD about 2). Option d is right because 65 is 15 points, or one and a half SDs of 10 points each, above the T mean of 50.","traps":{"a":"+0.5 SD corresponds to T 55, because half of the 10-point SD is 5 points above the mean of 50. A T of 55 sits around the 69th percentile and is well inside the normal range on the MMPI-2. It tempts only if the 10-point SD is forgotten and a 15-point gap is felt to be a small one.","b":"+1.0 SD corresponds to T 60, around the 84th percentile. This is a frequent slip carried over from IQ arithmetic, where the SD is 15, so a 15-point gap equals exactly one SD. On the T metric the SD is only 10, so 15 points is 1.5 SD. That carry-over from IQ scores is why this option is the most tempting one.","c":"+2.0 SD corresponds to T 70, about the 98th percentile. T 70 was the clinical cutoff on the original MMPI of Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943), so candidates who remember the older rule often pick it. The MMPI-2 (1989) lowered the cutoff to T 65, which is 1.5 SD, not 2 SD. The stem asks you to convert T 65, not to recall the historical cutoff."}},{"q":"The Bender Visual-Motor Gestalt Test is primarily used to screen for:","concept":"The Bender Visual-Motor Gestalt Test was published by the American child psychiatrist Lauretta Bender (1938). She took nine geometric figures from Max Wertheimer's 1923 work on the Gestalt laws of perceptual organisation and asked people to copy them on blank paper. Copying a design requires seeing it accurately, organising it as a whole, and translating that percept into controlled hand movement; this combined skill is visual-motor integration. Distortion, rotation, fragmentation, perseveration and poor integration of parts were taken as signs of maturational delay or brain dysfunction, so the test became one of the most widely used quick screens for organicity in adults. Elizabeth Koppitz (1963) developed the developmental scoring system for young children, used to judge perceptual-motor maturity and school readiness; Gerald Pascal and Barbara Suttell (1951) produced an adult scoring system; and Max Hutt promoted a projective reading of the drawings. The second edition, the Bender-Gestalt II (Gary Brannigan and Scott Decker, 2003), has 16 cards, adds Motor and Perception supplementary tests, and is normed from age 4 to over 85. Neighbouring tests that examiners use in variants: the Beery-Buktenica Developmental Test of Visual-Motor Integration (VMI), the Benton Visual Retention Test, which adds memory for designs, and the Rey-Osterrieth Complex Figure, which assesses copying, planning and recall. None of these measure mood or personality directly. Option d is right because the Bender's primary use is to screen visual-motor integration and the neurological dysfunction that disrupts it.","traps":{"a":"Personality structure is assessed by inventories such as the MMPI or 16PF, or by projective tests such as the Rorschach and the TAT. Max Hutt and others did read the Bender drawings projectively, for instance treating placement and size as signs of anxiety, but that use is secondary and poorly validated. It tempts because the Bender sits next to projective tests in assessment chapters and is drawn by hand.","b":"Severity of depression is rated with symptom scales: self-report tools such as the Beck Depression Inventory-II (Aaron T. Beck and colleagues, 1996) or clinician-rated tools such as the Hamilton Rating Scale for Depression (Max Hamilton, 1960). Copying geometric designs gives no information about mood, sleep, guilt or suicidal ideas. It tempts only because psychomotor slowing in depression can make drawings slow or careless.","c":"Reading comprehension is an academic achievement skill measured with tests such as the Wechsler Individual Achievement Test or curriculum-based reading tasks. The Bender involves no reading at all, only copying figures. It tempts because Koppitz's developmental scoring has been used to judge school readiness and to flag children at risk of learning difficulties, so the test turns up in learning disability assessments."}},{"q":"WISC-V is normed for what age range?","concept":"David Wechsler built a family of individually administered intelligence scales, each normed for a different age band, and examiners like to test which band belongs to which scale. The Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children first appeared in 1949; the fifth edition, WISC-V (2014), covers ages 6 years 0 months to 16 years 11 months, written 6:0 to 16:11 (years:months). Below it, the Wechsler Preschool and Primary Scale of Intelligence, Fourth Edition (WPPSI-IV, 2012), covers 2:6 to 7:7. Above it, the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale, Fourth Edition (WAIS-IV, 2008), covers 16:0 to 90:11. The overlaps are deliberate: a 6 or 7 year old can be given the WPPSI-IV or the WISC-V, and a 16 year old the WISC-V or the WAIS-IV, choosing by expected ability. The WISC-V yields a Full Scale IQ from seven subtests and five primary indices: Verbal Comprehension, Visual Spatial, Fluid Reasoning, Working Memory and Processing Speed. Splitting the old Perceptual Reasoning Index into Visual Spatial and Fluid Reasoning was its main structural change. Composite scores have a mean of 100 and SD of 15; subtests have a mean of 10 and SD of 3. For infants and toddlers, developmental scales such as the Bayley Scales of Infant and Toddler Development are used, covering roughly the first 42 months. Indian clinicians often use Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children (Arthur J. Malin, 1969), an adaptation of the original WISC. Option d is right because the WISC-V is normed for children aged 6:0 to 16:11.","traps":{"a":"2:6 to 7:7 is the age range of the WPPSI-IV (2012), the Wechsler Preschool and Primary Scale of Intelligence. It is split into two batteries, one for ages 2:6 to 3:11 and one for 4:0 to 7:7, because very young children cannot manage the full set of tasks. It tempts because the WPPSI-IV and the WISC-V overlap at ages 6 and 7.","b":"0 to 42 months is the band of infant developmental scales, chiefly the Bayley Scales of Infant and Toddler Development, originated by Nancy Bayley, which assess cognitive, language and motor development from early infancy to about three and a half years. No Wechsler scale starts at birth; the youngest Wechsler test begins at 2 years 6 months. It tempts because it is also a child range, written in months.","c":"16:0 to 90:11 is the range of the WAIS-IV (2008), the adult Wechsler scale, which yields Verbal Comprehension, Perceptual Reasoning, Working Memory and Processing Speed indices. Its floor of 16:0 overlaps with the WISC-V ceiling, so a 16 year old can take either test. It tempts because candidates remember the number 16 but attach it to the wrong end of the range."}},{"q":"Standard error of measurement (SEM) is calculated as:","concept":"Classical test theory, built on the work of Charles Spearman (1904), assumes that every observed score (X) is the sum of a true score (T) and random error (E), so X = T + E. Reliability (r_xx) is the proportion of observed-score variance that is true-score variance; the remaining proportion, 1 minus r_xx, is error variance. Multiplying that error proportion by the total variance and taking the square root gives the standard error of measurement: SEM = SD × √(1 minus r_xx). It is the standard deviation of the scores one person would obtain over many hypothetical retestings, so it describes the precision of an individual score. Example: on an IQ test with SD 15 and reliability .91, SEM = 15 × √.09 = 15 × .3 = 4.5. A score of 100 then has a 68 percent band of 95.5 to 104.5 (plus or minus 1 SEM) and a 95 percent band of about 91 to 109 (plus or minus 1.96 SEM). Perfect reliability gives an SEM of 0; zero reliability makes the SEM equal to the SD. Do not confuse it with its neighbours: SD × √r_xx is the standard deviation of true scores; the standard error of estimate, SD of the criterion × √(1 minus the squared validity coefficient), describes error in predicting a criterion; and the standard error of the difference, used to compare two scores, combines the SEMs of both. Option c is right because the SEM equals the test's standard deviation multiplied by the square root of one minus its reliability.","traps":{"a":"SD × √reliability is the standard deviation of true scores, because true-score variance equals reliability times total variance. It grows as reliability rises, whereas measurement error must shrink as reliability rises, so it cannot be the SEM. With SD 15 and reliability .91 it gives about 14.3, which would absurdly make a highly reliable test very imprecise. It tempts because it differs from the right formula only by the missing '1 minus'.","b":"Reliability multiplied by validity is not a defined psychometric index. Reliability does limit validity, since the maximum validity coefficient is the square root of reliability, but multiplying two coefficients gives a unitless number, while the SEM is expressed in the test's own score units, such as IQ points or T points. It tempts because reliability and validity are always taught together.","d":"Mean minus SD simply locates the point one standard deviation below the mean, for example 85 on an IQ scale or T 40 on a T scale, roughly the 16th percentile. It describes where a score falls in the distribution, not how much error surrounds an individual score, and it ignores reliability entirely. It tempts because it uses the familiar symbols of descriptive statistics."}},{"q":"The 16PF was developed by:","concept":"Raymond B. Cattell built the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF), first published in 1949, as part of the lexical, factor-analytic approach to traits. The lexical hypothesis holds that important individual differences become encoded as words in a language. Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert (1936) listed about 18,000 trait-descriptive words from an English dictionary; Cattell trimmed these to a much smaller set of trait variables and applied factor analysis to three data sources, L-data (life records and observer ratings), Q-data (questionnaire self-reports) and T-data (objective tests), to find the factors behind them. He separated surface traits, clusters of behaviour that go together, from source traits, the underlying causes revealed by factor analysis. The 16PF measures 16 source traits labelled with letters, such as A Warmth, B Reasoning, C Emotional Stability, E Dominance and Q4 Tension, and reports scores as stens (mean 5.5, SD 2, range 1 to 10). Because Cattell allowed his factors to correlate, they yield second-order global factors, such as Extraversion and Anxiety, that resemble the Big Five. Contrast this with Hans Eysenck, who argued for only three broad dimensions (PEN), and with Paul Costa and Robert McCrae, whose NEO-PI-R (1992) measures the Big Five. Allport himself proposed cardinal, central and secondary traits (1937) but distrusted factor analysis. Cattell is also known for distinguishing fluid from crystallised intelligence. Option b is right because Raymond Cattell developed the 16PF by factor analysing trait descriptions into 16 source traits.","traps":{"a":"Hans Eysenck developed the PEN model, three broad, largely biologically based dimensions: Psychoticism, Extraversion and Neuroticism. They are measured by the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire (EPQ, Hans and Sybil Eysenck, 1975), which grew out of the earlier Maudsley Personality Inventory. Eysenck argued that Cattell's 16 correlated factors collapse into his few higher-order ones. It tempts because both men were London-trained factor analysts working on traits in the same era.","c":"Paul Costa and Robert McCrae built the NEO Personality Inventory, revised as the NEO-PI-R (1992), to measure the Five-Factor Model: Neuroticism, Extraversion, Openness, Agreeableness and Conscientiousness, each with six facets. The five-factor structure was first found when Ernest Tupes and Raymond Christal (1961) re-analysed Cattell's own rating variables. It tempts because the Big Five is the most famous trait model and descends from the same lexical tradition.","d":"Gordon Allport (1937) founded modern trait psychology, distinguishing cardinal, central and secondary traits and stressing the uniqueness of each person through idiographic study. With Henry Odbert (1936) he compiled the trait-word list that Cattell later reduced, but he did not build the 16PF and was critical of factor analysis. It tempts because his lexical work is the starting point of Cattell's test."}},{"q":"Reliability sets the ceiling for validity such that the maximum possible validity coefficient equals:","concept":"Reliability limits validity. Under classical test theory an observed score contains true variance and random error, and random error cannot correlate with anything. The highest correlation a test can have with any other variable is therefore its correlation with its own true scores, called the index of reliability, which equals the square root of r_xx. More generally, the maximum validity coefficient is √(r_xx × r_yy), where r_yy is the reliability of the criterion. If the criterion is measured without error (r_yy = 1), the ceiling reduces to √r_xx. Worked examples: a test with reliability .64 can correlate at most .80 with a perfect criterion; with reliability .81 the ceiling is .90; with reliability .25 it is .50. Because reliability lies between 0 and 1, its square root is always at least as large as the reliability itself, so a validity coefficient can exceed the reliability coefficient but never its square root. Charles Spearman (1904) used the same logic in the correction for attenuation, which estimates the correlation between true scores: the observed correlation divided by √(r_xx × r_yy). Two exam points follow. First, a test can be reliable without being valid (precisely measuring the wrong construct), but it cannot be valid without some reliability. Second, an unreliable criterion, such as inconsistent supervisor ratings, also depresses observed validity coefficients. Squaring a validity coefficient gives the coefficient of determination, the proportion of criterion variance the test explains. Option a is right because, with a perfectly reliable criterion, the maximum validity coefficient equals the square root of the test's reliability.","traps":{"b":"The square of reliability would give a ceiling smaller than reliability itself; for a reliability of .64 it would cap validity at about .41, when the true ceiling is .80. Squaring matters elsewhere: squaring a validity coefficient gives the coefficient of determination, the share of criterion variance explained. It tempts because square and square root are easily swapped under exam pressure.","c":"Equating maximum validity with reliability itself is the half-remembered version of 'reliability limits validity'. Because reliability is a proportion between 0 and 1, its square root is larger, so the true ceiling is higher than r_xx: a test with reliability .64 can reach a validity of .80, not just .64. It tempts because the general principle is right while the exact formula is wrong.","d":"Reliability minus SEM mixes two incompatible quantities. Reliability is a unitless coefficient, while the standard error of measurement, SD × √(1 minus r_xx), is expressed in score units such as IQ points. Subtracting one from the other has no meaning and could even give a negative number. It tempts because both terms come from the same chapter on reliability."}},{"q":"The Rorschach Comprehensive System was developed by:","concept":"Hermann Rorschach, a Swiss psychiatrist, published the inkblot test in Psychodiagnostik (1921), using ten cards: five achromatic, two black and red, and three multicoloured. He died the next year without a complete scoring manual, so in the United States five rival systems grew up, those of Samuel Beck, Bruno Klopfer, Marguerite Hertz, Zygmunt Piotrowski, and David Rapaport with Roy Schafer. Each used different codes and norms, so research results could not be pooled and the test's reliability was widely criticised. John E. Exner Jr. compared the systems in The Rorschach Systems (1969) and then published The Rorschach: A Comprehensive System (1974), keeping the elements that had empirical support and adding standardised administration, norms and checks on inter-rater reliability. Responses are coded for location (W whole, D common detail, Dd unusual detail, S white space), determinants (form, movement, colour, shading), form quality, content, popular responses and special scores, and are summarised in a Structural Summary with indices such as the Schizophrenia Index, later replaced by the Perceptual-Thinking Index. The Comprehensive System became the dominant approach for decades. After Exner's death in 2006, his colleagues Gregory Meyer, Donald Viglione, Joni Mihura, Robert Erard and Philip Erdberg developed the Rorschach Performance Assessment System (R-PAS, 2011), now its main successor. Do not mix this up with the TAT, which comes from Henry Murray's need-press theory. Option b is right because John Exner integrated the earlier American scoring systems into the Rorschach Comprehensive System.","traps":{"a":"Samuel J. Beck developed one of the earliest American Rorschach scoring systems, with a strongly empirical, normative emphasis on form quality and frequency tables. His system was one of the five that Exner compared and drew on, but Beck did not build the integrated system. It tempts because the surname Beck is also attached to the Beck Depression Inventory, which belongs to Aaron T. Beck, a different person.","c":"Bruno Klopfer, a German emigre to the United States, developed a popular Rorschach system with a more phenomenological, clinical emphasis and detailed scoring of determinants and form level. He also founded the Rorschach Research Exchange, forerunner of the Journal of Personality Assessment. His was one of the five systems Exner integrated. It tempts because Klopfer was arguably the most influential Rorschach teacher before Exner.","d":"Zygmunt Piotrowski developed his own Rorschach approach, called perceptanalysis, and is also known for a list of organic signs used to detect brain damage from Rorschach responses. He was one of the five system builders whose work Exner reviewed, not the integrator. It tempts because his name appears in the same historical list as Beck, Klopfer, Hertz and Rapaport."}},{"q":"Concurrent validity differs from predictive validity in that:","concept":"Criterion-related validity is shown by correlating test scores with an external criterion, an independent measure of the behaviour the test is supposed to reflect or forecast. The resulting validity coefficient is an ordinary correlation, so it can be computed, squared and tested for significance. Its two subtypes were listed separately in the American Psychological Association's technical recommendations of 1954 and later grouped together, and they differ only in when the criterion is collected. In concurrent validity, test and criterion are gathered at about the same time: a new ten-minute depression screener is given to outpatients on the same day a psychiatrist makes a diagnosis or completes the Hamilton scale. Its purpose is to estimate a present status more cheaply, or to replace a longer measure. In predictive validity, the criterion is gathered later, as when an entrance test is checked against first-year grades or a selection test against job performance after six months. Its purpose is to forecast. A rarer third form, postdictive or retrospective validity, uses a criterion that already existed before testing, such as past school records. Both main forms are threatened by criterion contamination (raters knowing test scores) and range restriction (only selected people provide criterion data). Keep them separate from reliability indices such as Cronbach's alpha (Lee Cronbach, 1951) and from construct validity methods such as the multitrait-multimethod matrix of Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske (1959), which examines convergent and discriminant validity. Option b is right because concurrent validity collects test scores and criterion at the same time, whereas predictive validity collects the criterion later.","traps":{"a":"Measuring the criterion before the test describes postdictive (retrospective) validity, for example checking a new screening tool against hospital records of diagnoses made last year. It is a recognised but minor variant, not the definition of concurrent validity, in which both measurements are taken at the same time. It tempts because candidates know timing is the key difference and simply pick the wrong direction.","c":"Concurrent validity is quantified exactly like predictive validity, as a validity coefficient (usually Pearson's r) between test and criterion, and it can be squared to give the proportion of criterion variance explained. The option confuses it with face validity, which really is a non-statistical judgement. It tempts because the word 'concurrent' sounds descriptive rather than numerical.","d":"Internal consistency is a reliability concept: it asks whether the items of one test measure the same thing, using split-half correlations, KR-20 (G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson, 1937) or Cronbach's alpha (Lee Cronbach, 1951). It involves no external criterion at all. It tempts because both concurrent validity and internal consistency can be computed from data collected in a single sitting."}},{"q":"The K (correction) scale on the MMPI is used to:","concept":"The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943) has validity scales that describe test-taking attitude, separate from its ten clinical scales. The original ones were L (Lie: naive, obvious attempts to look virtuous), F (Infrequency: rare, deviant answers suggesting exaggeration, severe distress or random responding) and the Cannot Say (?) count of omitted items. Paul Meehl and Starke Hathaway (1946) added K (Correction), which detects subtler defensiveness: guarded, often well-educated people who deny problems without looking obviously dishonest. Because such people under-report symptoms, the clinical scales underestimate their difficulties. A fraction of the K raw score is therefore added to five clinical scales: 0.5K to Scale 1 (Hypochondriasis), 0.4K to Scale 4 (Psychopathic Deviate), the full K to Scales 7 (Psychasthenia) and 8 (Schizophrenia), and 0.2K to Scale 9 (Hypomania). This K-correction is retained on the MMPI-2 (1989). The MMPI-2 added further validity indices: VRIN (Variable Response Inconsistency) for random responding, TRIN (True Response Inconsistency) for yea-saying or nay-saying, F(B) for infrequent answers late in the booklet, F(p) for infrequent psychopathology, and S (Superlative Self-Presentation). Very high K suggests defensiveness; very low K suggests over-reporting or a cry for help. The MMPI-2-RF dropped the K-correction and keeps K-r only as a validity scale. Option b is right because the K scale's distinctive use is to add a correction to certain clinical scales to offset defensive under-reporting.","traps":{"a":"Random responding, answering without reading the items, is flagged on the MMPI-2 by VRIN (Variable Response Inconsistency) together with a very high F scale, since random answers endorse many rare items. K does not detect randomness; it measures subtle defensiveness. It tempts because K is a validity scale too, and candidates often lump all validity scales into one 'faking or careless' category.","c":"Depression is measured by clinical Scale 2 (D), one of the ten clinical scales, along with its Harris-Lingoes subscales and the DEP content scale on the MMPI-2. K is a validity and correction scale, and Scale 2 is not one of the five scales that receive the K-correction. It tempts because candidates confuse the validity scales with the clinical scales they correct.","d":"Inconsistent responding is detected on the MMPI-2 by VRIN, which counts contradictory answers to pairs of similar or opposite items, and TRIN, which detects indiscriminate 'true' (yea-saying) or 'false' (nay-saying) answering. These scales were added in the 1989 revision, whereas K belongs to the original validity set. It tempts because inconsistency detection is a real MMPI validity function, simply attached to the wrong scale."}},{"q":"On the WAIS-IV, an FSIQ of 70 typically corresponds to which classification (using DSM-5 framing)?","concept":"Intellectual disability (intellectual developmental disorder) in DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) has three criteria: deficits in intellectual functions such as reasoning, problem solving and abstract thinking, confirmed by clinical assessment and standardised testing; deficits in adaptive functioning in the conceptual, social and practical domains; and onset during the developmental period. On tests with a mean of 100 and SD of 15, the intellectual criterion is about two SDs below the mean, an IQ of about 70, and DSM-5 explicitly allows for the standard error of measurement, giving a range of about 65 to 75. DSM-5 also removed IQ bands from the severity levels: mild, moderate, severe and profound are now graded by adaptive functioning. So a score of 70 alone never makes the diagnosis. In the Wechsler descriptive scheme, WAIS-IV composite scores of 70 to 79 are labelled 'borderline' and 69 or below 'extremely low'. Borderline intellectual functioning appears in DSM-5 among other conditions that may be a focus of clinical attention, not as a mental disorder, and is used when low intellect matters clinically but ID criteria are not met. Adaptive behaviour is measured with tools such as the Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales or, in Indian practice, the Vineland Social Maturity Scale. Under the RPwD Act 2016, intellectual disability is one of the specified disabilities. Option b is right because an FSIQ of 70 falls in the Wechsler borderline band, and a diagnosis of intellectual disability additionally requires documented adaptive deficits.","traps":{"a":"Average intelligence on Wechsler scales means an FSIQ of 90 to 109, the middle half of the population. A score of 70 is two full SDs below the mean of 100, around the 2nd percentile, so it cannot be average. It tempts only if the stem is misread or if 'within normal limits' is loosely equated with average; the correct band for 70 is borderline.","c":"Mild intellectual disability is a real DSM-5 category, and older systems such as ICD-10 placed it at IQ 50 to 69. But DSM-5 does not allow ID to be diagnosed from an IQ score alone: adaptive deficits in conceptual, social or practical domains and developmental onset are also required, and severity is set by adaptive functioning. The word 'solely' makes this wrong. It tempts because 70 is the traditional cutoff.","d":"Profound intellectual disability is the most severe level. In older IQ-based systems it lay below about 20 to 25, and in DSM-5 it is defined by extremely limited conceptual skills, dependence on others for all aspects of daily care, and often co-occurring sensory and motor impairments. An FSIQ of 70 is nowhere near this. It tempts only as an extreme distractor for candidates who see 'intellectual disability' and stop reading."}},{"q":"Murray's need-press theory is the conceptual basis for which projective test?","concept":"Henry A. Murray developed personology at the Harvard Psychological Clinic and set out his theory in Explorations in Personality (1938). Behaviour, he argued, comes from the interaction of needs inside the person and press from the environment. Needs are motivational forces such as achievement (n Ach), affiliation (n Aff), dominance, aggression, autonomy and nurturance; Murray listed many such psychogenic needs. Press is any feature of the environment that helps or blocks need satisfaction. Alpha press is the environment as it objectively is; beta press is the environment as the person perceives it, and beta press matters most for behaviour. A combination of need and press forms a thema. To reveal these, Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray (1935) created the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT). The person tells a story about each of a series of ambiguous pictures (31 cards in the standard set, including one blank card), saying what led up to the scene, what is happening, what the characters feel and how it ends. Murray's scoring identifies the hero, the hero's needs, the press acting on the hero and the outcome. David McClelland later scored TAT-type stories for achievement motivation. Variants include the Children's Apperception Test (Leopold Bellak and Sonya Sorel Bellak, 1949), which uses animal figures, and an Indian adaptation by Uma Chowdhury. 'Apperception' means interpreting a new perception through past experience, which is the projective logic of the test. Option c is right because the TAT was built directly on Murray's need-press theory, with stories scored for the hero's needs and environmental press.","traps":{"a":"The Rorschach, published by Hermann Rorschach (1921), uses ten inkblots and is scored mainly for perceptual and structural features: location, determinants such as form, colour and movement, form quality and content. Its theory concerns how a person organises ambiguous visual input, not needs and environmental press. It tempts because it is the most famous projective test and shares the ambiguous-stimulus logic of the TAT.","b":"Sentence completion tests give stems such as 'I feel...' or 'My mother...' for the person to finish. The best known is Julian Rotter's Incomplete Sentences Blank (1950), scored for conflict and adjustment; the Sacks Sentence Completion Test is another. These are semi-structured projective tools without a need-press scoring scheme. It tempts because, like the TAT, it elicits verbal material that reveals motives.","d":"The word association test asks the person to reply to each stimulus word with the first word that comes to mind. Francis Galton experimented with it, and Carl Jung used it in the early 1900s to detect emotionally charged complexes through long reaction times, repetitions and odd responses. It belongs to Jung's analytical psychology, not to Murray. It tempts because it is one of the oldest projective methods."}},{"q":"The Beck Depression Inventory-II measures depression severity using:","concept":"The Beck Depression Inventory was created by Aaron T. Beck and colleagues (1961) to measure the intensity of depression, using symptoms Beck had observed in depressed patients. The BDI-II (Aaron T. Beck, Robert Steer and Gregory Brown, 1996) revised it to match the DSM-IV criteria for major depression. It is a self-report inventory of 21 items. Each item offers four statements of increasing severity scored 0 to 3, so totals range from 0 to 63. The time frame was lengthened from the past week, used in the BDI-IA, to the past two weeks, matching the DSM duration criterion. Four items were replaced (weight loss, body image change, work difficulty and somatic preoccupation gave way to agitation, worthlessness, concentration difficulty and loss of energy), and the sleep and appetite items now allow increases as well as decreases. Manual cutoffs are 0 to 13 minimal, 14 to 19 mild, 20 to 28 moderate and 29 to 63 severe. Internal consistency is high, around .90, and it is meant for ages 13 and above. Neighbouring scales that examiners use: the Hamilton Rating Scale for Depression (Max Hamilton, 1960), clinician-rated, with 17 items in its common version; the PHQ-9 (Kurt Kroenke, Robert Spitzer and Janet Williams, 2001), 9 items scored 0 to 3 over two weeks, range 0 to 27; and the Montgomery-Asberg Depression Rating Scale (1979), with 10 clinician-rated items. Option a is right because the BDI-II has 21 items, each rated 0 to 3, covering the past two weeks.","traps":{"b":"Thirty-one items rated 0 to 4 matches no version of the BDI. The number 31 may echo the 31 cards of the TAT, and 0 to 4 ratings are typical of Likert scales or of the Y-BOCS items, not of Beck's four-statement items scored 0 to 3. It tempts candidates who remember that the BDI is longer than the PHQ-9 but not its exact length.","c":"Ten items rated 0 to 10 does not describe the BDI-II. The best-known ten-item depression measures are the clinician-rated Montgomery-Asberg Depression Rating Scale, scored 0 to 6 per item, and the Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale (John Cox and colleagues, 1987), scored 0 to 3 per item. It tempts as a round-number guess by candidates unsure of the BDI format.","d":"A single item rated 0 to 100 resembles a visual analogue scale or a global rating such as the Global Assessment of Functioning (1 to 100), used on Axis V of DSM-IV. One rating cannot capture the cognitive, affective and somatic symptoms that a multi-item inventory like the BDI-II samples. It tempts because global ratings are quick and appear in the same clinical chapters."}},{"q":"The standard score where M=10 and SD=3 is called:","concept":"A standard score expresses a raw score as a distance from the norm-group mean, on a scale with a chosen mean and standard deviation. All start from the z score, z = (X minus mean) divided by SD, which has a mean of 0 and SD of 1. Because z scores are negative below the mean and carry decimals, they are linearly transformed: new score = new mean + (new SD × z). The common metrics are: z score, mean 0, SD 1; T score, mean 50, SD 10, used by the MMPI and many personality scales; deviation IQ, mean 100, SD 15, introduced by David Wechsler (1939) and used for Wechsler composite and index scores; scaled score, mean 10, SD 3, range 1 to 19, used for the individual subtests of the Wechsler scales such as Block Design or Digit Span; stanine (standard nine), mean 5, SD about 2, range 1 to 9, a normalised band score; and sten (standard ten), mean 5.5, SD 2, range 1 to 10, used by Raymond Cattell's 16PF. Converting between them is a favourite exam move: a scaled score of 13 is +1 SD, equal to z of 1, T 60 and IQ 115; a scaled score of 4 is 2 SD below the mean, equal to T 30 and IQ 70. Percentile ranks are not standard scores, because they are ordinal and stretch unequally across the distribution. Option b is right because the metric with a mean of 10 and SD of 3 is the scaled score used for Wechsler subtests.","traps":{"a":"A z score has a mean of 0 and an SD of 1; it is the raw score's distance from the mean measured in SD units, and every other standard score is built from it. A subtest scaled score of 10 corresponds to a z of 0, and a scaled score of 7 to a z of minus 1. It tempts because z is the first standard score students learn and the stem says 'standard score'.","c":"A T score has a mean of 50 and an SD of 10, so it runs roughly from 20 to 80. It is used by the MMPI-2, where T 65 or above marks clinical elevation, and by many other personality and neuropsychological tests. A scaled score of 10 corresponds to T 50. It tempts because T scores are the second most commonly taught metric after z.","d":"A stanine (standard nine) divides the normal distribution into nine bands with a mean of 5 and an SD of about 2; the middle band holds about 20 percent of cases. It was developed for large-scale testing in the US armed forces during the Second World War and is common in school achievement reports. It tempts because its small single-digit range resembles the 1 to 19 range of scaled scores."}},{"q":"The NEO-PI-R measures personality on:","concept":"The Five-Factor Model (Big Five) holds that most trait variation can be summarised in five broad, largely independent dimensions. It emerged from the lexical tradition: Ernest Tupes and Raymond Christal (1961) found five factors when they re-analysed trait ratings, Warren Norman (1963) replicated them, and Lewis Goldberg (1981) popularised the term 'Big Five'. Paul Costa and Robert McCrae turned the model into a questionnaire. Their inventory began with Neuroticism, Extraversion and Openness, hence the letters NEO; Agreeableness and Conscientiousness were added later, and in the revised NEO-PI-R (1992) all five domains received six facets each, giving 30 facets measured by 240 items on a five-point scale. For example, Neuroticism has the facets anxiety, angry hostility, depression, self-consciousness, impulsiveness and vulnerability; Conscientiousness has competence, order, dutifulness, achievement striving, self-discipline and deliberation. There is a self-report form (Form S) and an observer-rating form (Form R). The 60-item NEO Five-Factor Inventory (NEO-FFI) measures only the five domains, without facets. Remember the acronym OCEAN. Contrast it with Hans Eysenck's three PEN dimensions, Raymond Cattell's 16 source traits in the 16PF, and the HEXACO model of Michael Ashton and Kibeom Lee, which adds Honesty-Humility as a sixth factor. Clinical inventories such as Theodore Millon's MCMI target personality disorders rather than normal-range traits. Option c is right because the NEO-PI-R measures five broad domains, each split into six facets.","traps":{"a":"Three dimensions called PEN (Psychoticism, Extraversion, Neuroticism) belong to Hans Eysenck's hierarchical, biologically based model, measured by the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire (Hans and Sybil Eysenck, 1975). Eysenck argued that a few higher-order factors were enough and criticised five-factor and sixteen-factor schemes. It tempts because Extraversion and Neuroticism appear in both models, so the overlap feels familiar.","b":"Sixteen source traits are measured by Raymond Cattell's Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF, 1949), derived by factor analysis of L-data, Q-data and T-data. Cattell's factors are narrower and correlated; his second-order global factors resemble the Big Five, but the 16PF is a different instrument by a different author. It tempts because both are factor-analytic trait inventories from the lexical tradition.","d":"Eleven personality disorder scales point to a clinical inventory: Theodore Millon's MCMI-III, for example, has 11 clinical personality pattern scales plus 3 severe personality pathology scales, built around the DSM personality disorders. The NEO-PI-R describes normal-range traits, although its profiles can describe personality disorders dimensionally. It tempts because both tools measure 'personality' and appear together in assessment chapters."}},{"q":"Y-BOCS is used to assess severity of:","concept":"The Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale (Y-BOCS) was developed by Wayne Goodman and colleagues (1989) at Yale and Brown universities and is the standard measure of the severity of obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) and of treatment response in clinical trials. It is a semi-structured, clinician-administered interview in two parts. First, a symptom checklist identifies the content of the person's obsessions (for example contamination, aggressive, sexual, religious or symmetry themes) and compulsions (washing, checking, counting, ordering, repeating). Second, a 10-item severity scale rates obsessions (items 1 to 5) and compulsions (items 6 to 10) on the same five dimensions: time occupied, interference with functioning, distress, resistance and degree of control. Each item is scored 0 to 4, giving subtotals of 0 to 20 and a total of 0 to 40. Common bands are 0 to 7 subclinical, 8 to 15 mild, 16 to 23 moderate, 24 to 31 severe and 32 to 40 extreme; a reduction of roughly 25 to 35 percent is often used to define treatment response. Because it rates severity independent of content, it works across very different OCD presentations. A children's version, the CY-BOCS, also exists. Pair each disorder with its scale for exam variants: schizophrenia, the PANSS (Stanley Kay, Abraham Fiszbein and Lewis Opler, 1987) and BPRS (John Overall and Donald Gorham, 1962); depression, the HAM-D (Max Hamilton, 1960) and BDI-II; mania, the Young Mania Rating Scale; PTSD, the CAPS and PCL. Option b is right because the Y-BOCS measures the severity of obsessive-compulsive disorder.","traps":{"a":"Schizophrenia symptom severity is rated with the Positive and Negative Syndrome Scale (PANSS, Stanley Kay, Abraham Fiszbein and Lewis Opler, 1987), which has 30 items, or the Brief Psychiatric Rating Scale (John Overall and Donald Gorham, 1962). Negative symptoms alone can be rated with Nancy Andreasen's SANS. It tempts because obsessions can be confused with delusions, and severe OCD with poor insight can resemble psychosis.","c":"Depression severity is measured with the clinician-rated Hamilton Rating Scale for Depression (Max Hamilton, 1960), the Montgomery-Asberg scale, or self-report tools such as the BDI-II and PHQ-9. The Y-BOCS does not rate mood, sleep or suicidality. It tempts because depression commonly co-occurs with OCD, and both are often treated with SSRIs, so their scales appear side by side in drug trials.","d":"PTSD severity is assessed with the Clinician-Administered PTSD Scale (CAPS), the gold-standard structured interview, or the self-report PTSD Checklist (PCL), both developed at the US National Center for PTSD. Intrusive memories in PTSD can look like obsessions, but they are recollections of a real trauma, not ego-dystonic thoughts that drive compulsions. It tempts because both disorders involve unwanted, repetitive thoughts."}},{"q":"A scaled score of 7 on a WAIS-IV subtest corresponds to which classification?","concept":"Wechsler subtest scores are scaled scores with a mean of 10 and a standard deviation (SD) of 3, so every 3 points is one SD. A scaled score of 7 is 10 minus 3, exactly 1 SD below the mean. That equals a z of minus 1, a T score of 40, an index-score equivalent of 85, and roughly the 16th percentile: about 84 percent of same-age peers score higher. In a normal curve about 68 percent of people fall within 1 SD either side of the mean (scaled scores 7 to 13), so a 7 sits at the lower edge of the normal range rather than in an impaired range. Many report-writing conventions call scaled scores of 8 to 12 average and 6 to 7 low average; scores of 4 to 5 correspond roughly to the borderline zone (index scores in the 70s), and 1 to 3 is extremely low, 2 or more SD below the mean. Higher scores mirror this: 13 is +1 SD and 16 is +2 SD. Clinicians interpret a single subtest cautiously, because subtests are less reliable than composites and carry a larger standard error of measurement. They give more weight to index scores and to statistically significant differences between subtests (personal strengths and weaknesses), checked against base rates in the normative sample. The WISC-V and WPPSI-IV use the same subtest metric. Option d is right because a scaled score of 7 is exactly 1 SD below the mean of 10 and still lies within the broad average or normal range.","traps":{"a":"Profound impairment would require a score at the floor of the subtest metric: a scaled score of 1 is 3 SD below the mean, below the 1st percentile. Even the extremely low range (scaled 1 to 3) is 2 SD or more below average, while a 7 is only 1 SD below. It tempts candidates who see that 7 is below 10 and assume any below-mean score signals a deficit.","b":"Significantly above average would need a scaled score of about 13 or more (+1 SD), with 16 or more (+2 SD) at the top of the range. A score of 7 is below the mean, not above it. It tempts candidates who misremember the subtest mean, or who confuse scaled scores with metrics where lower numbers mean better performance, such as ranks or error counts.","c":"Borderline functioning on Wechsler scales means composite scores of 70 to 79, about 1.4 to 2 SD below the mean; at subtest level this corresponds roughly to scaled scores of 4 to 5. A 7 is only 1 SD below the mean, equal to an index of 85, which is low average. It tempts because 7 feels well below 10, but each scaled-score point is only one third of an SD."}},{"q":"Test-retest reliability is most appropriate for:","concept":"Reliability is the consistency of measurement, and classical test theory offers several ways to estimate it, each suited to a different source of error. Test-retest reliability gives the same test to the same people on two occasions and correlates the scores; the result is called the coefficient of stability. It treats any change in people's relative standing between occasions as error. That logic only works if the attribute itself does not truly change across the interval, so test-retest is appropriate for stable traits such as intelligence, aptitude and personality dispositions. Charles Spielberger's State-Trait Anxiety Inventory (1970) shows this clearly: the trait anxiety scale has high retest correlations, while the state anxiety scale has low ones, which is correct for a state measure, not a flaw. The interval must be chosen carefully: too short, and memory or practice effects (carryover) inflate the correlation; too long, and genuine development or life events lower it. Neighbouring methods: alternate or parallel forms reliability (coefficient of equivalence), which reduces memory effects; internal consistency from a single sitting, using split-half with the Spearman-Brown correction, KR-20 for right-or-wrong items (G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson, 1937) or Cronbach's alpha (Lee Cronbach, 1951), which suits state measures but needs several items; and inter-rater reliability, such as Cohen's kappa (Jacob Cohen, 1960), for observational and interview ratings. Option b is right because test-retest reliability assumes the construct stays stable over the interval, which holds for trait measures.","traps":{"a":"Measures of rapidly changing states, such as current mood, momentary anxiety or pain, are expected to change between sessions. A low retest correlation then reflects real change, not unreliable measurement, so test-retest would wrongly make a good state measure look poor. Internal consistency within one sitting suits such measures better. It tempts because states are often re-measured in treatment studies to track change, which is a different purpose.","c":"Single-item measures can in fact be retested, and test-retest is one of the few ways to estimate their reliability, because internal consistency methods such as split-half or Cronbach's alpha need several items. But the method is not restricted to single items; it applies equally to whole scales and batteries. The word 'only' makes this wrong. It tempts because it contains a half-truth.","d":"Open-ended interviews raise a different reliability problem: agreement between interviewers or raters, estimated with inter-rater statistics such as Cohen's kappa. Test-retest can be applied to interviews, but it is not limited to them, and the word 'only' makes the option wrong. It tempts because structured clinical interviews such as the SCID are often studied with test-retest designs, so the pairing sounds familiar."}},{"q":"Beck's cognitive triad in depression refers to negative views of:","concept":"Aaron T. Beck, a psychiatrist trained in psychoanalysis, noticed that depressed patients' dreams and free associations were dominated by themes of loss and failure, not by inverted anger as Freudian theory predicted. In his book on depression (1967) he proposed a cognitive model, later turned into the treatment manual Cognitive Therapy of Depression (Aaron T. Beck, A. John Rush, Brian Shaw and Gary Emery, 1979). The model has three levels. The cognitive triad is a negative view of the self ('I am worthless, defective'), of the world or ongoing experience ('everyone rejects me, everything is an obstacle') and of the future ('it will never get better'). These views surface as automatic thoughts and are kept alive by systematic cognitive errors such as arbitrary inference, selective abstraction, overgeneralisation, magnification and minimisation, personalisation and dichotomous thinking. Underneath lie depressogenic schemas, stable core beliefs formed early in life and activated by stress, which makes this a diathesis-stress model. Hopelessness, the negative view of the future, is the leg most linked to suicide, which is why Beck developed the Beck Hopelessness Scale. Related models that examiners pair with it: learned helplessness, from Martin Seligman; its attributional reformulation (Lyn Abramson, Martin Seligman and John Teasdale, 1978), in which internal, stable and global attributions for bad events predict depression; and hopelessness theory (Lyn Abramson, Gerald Metalsky and Lauren Alloy, 1989). Option d is right because Beck's cognitive triad consists of negative views of the self, the world and the future.","traps":{"a":"Id, ego and superego are the three structures of the mind in Sigmund Freud's structural model (1923): the id seeks instinctual gratification, the superego holds internalised moral standards, and the ego mediates using reality testing and defences. In Freud's account of depression, in Mourning and Melancholia (1917), anger at a lost object is turned against the self. It tempts because it is the most famous psychological triad.","b":"Mother, father and self suggest the Oedipal triangle of psychoanalysis, or the triangles of Murray Bowen's family systems theory, in which a third person is drawn into a two-person conflict. These are relational triangles about family dynamics, not cognitive appraisals. It tempts because early family experience does shape Beck's schemas, and the trio sounds like a plausible developmental model.","c":"Past, present and future is a division of time, not Beck's triad. Only 'future' is correct; the other two legs are self and world. Depressive rumination does dwell on the past, which makes this look plausible, but the triad concerns the targets of negative evaluation, not time periods. It tempts because it shares one genuine element and has a neat, symmetrical ring."}},{"q":"The 'empty chair' technique is most associated with:","concept":"Gestalt therapy was developed by Frederick (Fritz) Perls with Laura Perls and Paul Goodman; its founding text, Gestalt Therapy by Fritz Perls, Ralph Hefferline and Paul Goodman, appeared in 1951. It borrowed the word Gestalt (whole, configuration) from perception psychology and aims at awareness, contact and the integration of split-off parts of the personality, working in the here and now rather than analysing the past. Key ideas include unfinished business (unexpressed feelings, often toward significant others, that keep intruding on the present); disturbances at the contact boundary such as introjection, projection, retroflection and confluence; and the topdog and underdog split between demanding 'shoulds' and resistant excuses. In the empty-chair technique the client imagines a person or a part of the self sitting in an empty chair and speaks to it directly, for example saying to a dead parent what was never said. In the two-chair variant the client switches chairs and answers as the other side, giving voice to both parts of a conflict until they are integrated. Perls drew on role-play ideas from Jacob Moreno's psychodrama, and Leslie Greenberg later researched chair work within emotion-focused therapy. Other Gestalt methods include dreamwork, in which every element of a dream is treated as a projected part of the self, and exaggeration of gestures. Do not confuse the therapy with the Gestalt school of perception founded by Max Wertheimer (1912). Option a is right because the empty-chair technique is a signature method of Fritz Perls's Gestalt therapy.","traps":{"b":"Cognitive behaviour therapy, rooted in Aaron T. Beck's cognitive therapy and Albert Ellis's rational emotive behaviour therapy, changes emotion and behaviour by identifying and testing distorted thoughts with thought records, Socratic questioning and behavioural experiments. It works collaboratively and logically rather than through enacted dialogues. It tempts because some later CBT-family approaches, such as schema therapy, have borrowed chair work.","c":"Person-centred therapy, developed by Carl Rogers and set out in Client-Centered Therapy (1951), relies on the core conditions of empathy, unconditional positive regard and congruence, and deliberately avoids directive techniques. The therapist follows the client rather than setting up exercises like chair dialogues. It tempts because Rogers and Perls are both humanistic therapists who stress present experience and growth.","d":"Classical psychoanalysis, developed by Sigmund Freud, uses free association, dream interpretation, and analysis of transference and resistance, with the patient on a couch and the analyst largely neutral. It explores the unconscious past rather than enacting present dialogues. It tempts because Perls trained as a psychoanalyst, and Gestalt work on unfinished business resembles working through old conflicts."}},{"q":"Carl Rogers' three core conditions for therapeutic change are:","concept":"Carl Rogers developed client-centred, later called person-centred, therapy, set out in Counseling and Psychotherapy (1942) and Client-Centered Therapy (1951). His theory holds that people have an actualising tendency toward growth, but conditions of worth imposed by others create incongruence between the self-concept and actual experience, which produces anxiety and maladjustment. In his 1957 paper 'The necessary and sufficient conditions of therapeutic personality change', Rogers listed six conditions: two persons are in psychological contact; the client is incongruent, vulnerable or anxious; the therapist is congruent or integrated in the relationship; the therapist experiences unconditional positive regard for the client; the therapist experiences empathic understanding of the client's internal frame of reference and tries to communicate it; and the client perceives, at least minimally, that regard and empathy. The three therapist conditions are the core conditions. Congruence (genuineness) means the therapist's inner experience matches outward behaviour. Unconditional positive regard means warm acceptance without conditions or judgement. Accurate empathy means sensing the client's world as if it were one's own, without losing the 'as if' quality. Rogers claimed no technique is needed beyond these, a strong position. Later common-factors research supports the importance of empathy and of the working alliance, which Edward Bordin (1979) defined as agreement on goals, agreement on tasks and an emotional bond. Typical Rogerian responses are reflection of feeling and clarification. Option c is right because Rogers's three core conditions are unconditional positive regard, congruence and empathic understanding.","traps":{"a":"Transference, free association and interpretation are the core tools of Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis. Free association is the basic rule of saying whatever comes to mind; transference is the redirection of feelings from past figures onto the analyst; interpretation makes unconscious meaning conscious. Rogers rejected this expert, interpretive stance. It tempts because these are also therapeutic essentials that appear in the same chapter.","b":"Cognitive restructuring, behavioural activation and exposure are techniques of cognitive behaviour therapy, drawing on Aaron T. Beck, Peter Lewinsohn and Joseph Wolpe respectively. They are specific procedures aimed at thoughts, activity levels and avoidance, the opposite of Rogers's claim that relationship conditions alone are sufficient. It tempts because candidates may recall 'three key components' of a therapy without linking them to the right school.","d":"Working alliance, transference and resistance interpretation are psychodynamic concepts. Ralph Greenson described the working alliance in psychoanalysis, and Edward Bordin (1979) generalised it as goals, tasks and bond; resistance interpretation is a classical analytic method. The alliance overlaps with Rogers's relational emphasis, but this trio is not his three core conditions. It tempts because 'alliance' sounds like a relationship factor."}},{"q":"Systematic desensitisation, developed by Joseph Wolpe, combines:","concept":"Joseph Wolpe, a South African psychiatrist, developed systematic desensitisation and described it in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition (1958). He had found that cats made anxious by shocks in an experimental cage could be fed in rooms increasingly similar to that cage until the anxiety disappeared. His principle of reciprocal inhibition states that if a response incompatible with anxiety occurs in the presence of anxiety-provoking stimuli, the bond between those stimuli and anxiety is weakened. Deep muscle relaxation, adapted from Edmund Jacobson's progressive relaxation (1929), was his main incompatible response; assertive and sexual responses were others. The procedure has three steps: train the client in relaxation; build an anxiety hierarchy of about 10 to 20 situations ranked in Subjective Units of Disturbance (SUDS, 0 to 100); then present items from least to most frightening, usually in imagination, moving up only when an item no longer produces anxiety. This is counterconditioning, foreshadowed by Mary Cover Jones's (1924) treatment of Little Peter's fear of rabbits. Neighbours examiners contrast: flooding and implosive therapy (Thomas Stampfl), which use prolonged, intense exposure with no hierarchy or relaxation; graded in vivo exposure, now preferred for phobias; and exposure and response prevention for OCD. Later research suggests that exposure, not relaxation, is the active ingredient, working through extinction and inhibitory learning. Option c is right because systematic desensitisation combines graded exposure along a fear hierarchy with deep relaxation to reciprocally inhibit anxiety.","traps":{"a":"Aversive conditioning, or aversion therapy, pairs an unwanted behaviour or stimulus with an unpleasant experience such as drug-induced nausea or mild shock, so the stimulus itself becomes aversive; covert sensitisation (Joseph Cautela, 1967) does this in imagination. It aims to create discomfort, whereas desensitisation removes it. It tempts because both are classical conditioning techniques from the same era of behaviour therapy.","b":"Flooding exposes the client to the most feared stimulus at full intensity, without a hierarchy and without relaxation, until anxiety subsides. Cognitive disputation is the method of Albert Ellis's rational emotive behaviour therapy, in which irrational beliefs are actively challenged. Neither is part of Wolpe's procedure. It tempts because flooding is also an exposure method for phobias, and the two are often taught side by side.","d":"Operant shaping reinforces successive approximations to a target behaviour, and token reinforcement gives tokens exchangeable for rewards, as in the token economy of Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin (1968). Both are operant procedures based on B. F. Skinner's work that change behaviour through its consequences. Systematic desensitisation is a classical counterconditioning method that changes emotional responses. It tempts because all are behaviour therapy techniques."}},{"q":"Ayllon and Azrin pioneered which behavioural intervention in inpatient settings?","concept":"A token economy is a behaviour modification system in which target behaviours earn tokens, such as plastic chips, points or stamps. Tokens are conditioned (secondary) reinforcers: they have no value in themselves but can later be exchanged for backup reinforcers such as snacks, privileges, outings or a private room. It applies B. F. Skinner's operant conditioning to a whole institution. Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin set up the classic programme on a ward of chronic, long-stay psychiatric patients at Anna State Hospital in Illinois. Patients earned tokens for self-care and ward jobs; their work rose sharply when tokens were contingent on it and fell when reinforcement was given regardless of behaviour. They reported this in a 1965 paper and in the book The Token Economy (1968). Gordon Paul and Robert Lentz (1977) later showed that a social learning programme built on a token economy outperformed milieu therapy and routine hospital care for chronic patients. Strengths: tokens bridge the delay between behaviour and reward and resist satiation. Problems: gains may not generalise outside the ward once tokens stop, and ethical concerns arise if basic rights are made contingent. Related operant tools that examiners use as distractors include shaping, the Premack principle, time-out from positive reinforcement, response cost (losing tokens for misbehaviour), and contingency management for substance use. Ayllon had earlier worked with Jack Michael on the psychiatric nurse as a behavioural engineer (1959). Option b is right because Ayllon and Azrin pioneered the token economy on inpatient psychiatric wards.","traps":{"a":"Aversion therapy reduces an unwanted behaviour by pairing it with an unpleasant stimulus, for example a nausea-producing drug paired with alcohol, or mild shock. It relies on classical conditioning and punishment, whereas the token economy builds desirable behaviour through positive reinforcement. Ayllon and Azrin are not associated with it. It tempts because it was another prominent behavioural treatment used in hospitals in the same period.","c":"Time-out, short for time-out from positive reinforcement, briefly removes a person from a reinforcing situation after problem behaviour, a form of negative punishment widely used with children. It is an operant technique and may sit inside a token system, but it was not Ayllon and Azrin's contribution. It tempts because Azrin is well known for other operant programmes, such as rapid toilet training with Richard Foxx.","d":"Modelling, learning by watching others perform a behaviour and its consequences, is the core of Albert Bandura's social learning theory, shown in his Bobo doll studies (1961). Participant modelling is used to treat phobias. It is observational learning, not reinforcement through exchangeable tokens. It tempts because Paul and Lentz's social learning programme combined modelling with a token economy."}},{"q":"DBT was developed for the primary treatment of:","concept":"Dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT) was developed by Marsha Linehan, an American psychologist, from the late 1970s and published in her two 1993 manuals. She first tried standard cognitive behaviour therapy with chronically suicidal, self-harming women, most of whom met criteria for borderline personality disorder (BPD). Pure change strategies made these clients feel criticised and many dropped out, so she balanced change with acceptance drawn from Zen practice; holding these opposites together is the 'dialectic'. Her biosocial theory says BPD arises when a biological emotional vulnerability (high sensitivity, intense reactions, slow return to baseline) meets an invalidating environment that dismisses or punishes emotional expression, producing pervasive emotion dysregulation. Standard DBT has four modes: weekly individual therapy, a weekly skills training group, telephone coaching between sessions, and a therapist consultation team. Treatment targets follow a hierarchy: life-threatening behaviour first, then therapy-interfering behaviour, then quality-of-life problems. Its first randomised trial (Linehan and colleagues, 1991) showed fewer parasuicidal acts and fewer inpatient days than treatment as usual. Later adaptations cover adolescents, substance use, and binge eating and bulimia. Examiners also ask about other BPD treatments: mentalisation-based treatment (Anthony Bateman and Peter Fonagy), transference-focused psychotherapy (Otto Kernberg), and schema therapy (Jeffrey Young). Option c is right because DBT was built for chronically suicidal patients with borderline personality disorder, which remains its primary and best-evidenced indication.","traps":{"a":"Schizophrenia is a psychotic disorder treated mainly with antipsychotic medication, supported by cognitive behaviour therapy for psychosis, family interventions that reduce high expressed emotion, and social skills training. DBT was not designed for hallucinations or delusions; its target is emotion dysregulation and suicidal behaviour. It tempts because DBT is used in inpatient and severe mental illness settings, and candidates may assume an intensive structured therapy was built for the most severe disorder.","b":"OCD is treated first-line with exposure and response prevention, first described by Victor Meyer (1966), often combined with SSRIs or clomipramine. Its core problem is obsessions and neutralising rituals, not the chronic suicidality and emotional instability DBT was built for. It tempts because DBT is a cognitive behavioural therapy and OCD is the classic disorder for behavioural techniques, so the two get linked in memory.","d":"Anorexia nervosa is treated with family-based treatment (the Maudsley approach) for adolescents and enhanced CBT (CBT-E), developed by Christopher Fairburn. DBT was later adapted for binge eating disorder and bulimia, and Thomas Lynch's radically open DBT targets overcontrolled problems including anorexia, but these are later adaptations, not the original purpose. It tempts because these eating disorder versions carry the DBT name."}},{"q":"The DBT skills training modules include:","concept":"Skills training is one of the four modes of dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT), developed by Marsha Linehan and set out in her 1993 skills training manual (second edition 2015). Skills are taught in a weekly group run like a class, with homework, while the individual therapist helps the client apply them in daily life. There are four modules. Core mindfulness, drawn from Zen practice, comes first and is repeated between the other modules: clients learn to reach 'wise mind', the integration of 'reasonable mind' and 'emotion mind', through the 'what' skills (observe, describe, participate) and the 'how' skills (non-judgementally, one-mindfully, effectively). Distress tolerance teaches surviving a crisis without making it worse, through distraction, self-soothing, pros and cons, and radical acceptance. Emotion regulation teaches naming emotions, reducing vulnerability to them, and opposite action (acting against the urge of an emotion that does not fit the facts). Interpersonal effectiveness teaches asking for what one needs and saying no while keeping relationships and self-respect, using acronyms such as DEAR MAN, GIVE and FAST. Mindfulness and distress tolerance are the acceptance skills; emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness are the change skills, which mirrors the dialectic at the heart of DBT. Adolescent DBT, developed by Alec Miller and Jill Rathus with Linehan, adds a fifth module, walking the middle path. Do not mix these modules with the six hexaflex processes of Acceptance and Commitment Therapy. Option b is right because mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness are exactly the four standard DBT skills modules.","traps":{"a":"Free association, dream work, interpretation and working through are the classical techniques of psychoanalysis, developed by Sigmund Freud. The patient says whatever comes to mind, the analyst interprets resistance, dreams and transference, and insight is repeated until it changes behaviour (working through). These are insight methods, not teachable skills. It tempts because the option lists four neat items like a module list, and all are real therapy terms.","c":"Object relations, transference, splitting and projection are psychodynamic concepts. Splitting (seeing self or others as all good or all bad) is central to Otto Kernberg's theory of borderline personality organisation, treated with his transference-focused psychotherapy. These describe defences and relational patterns that the therapist interprets, not skills that clients practise. It tempts because both DBT and Kernberg's model target borderline personality disorder.","d":"Schema analysis, mode work and limited reparenting belong to schema therapy, developed by Jeffrey Young, who extended Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy for personality disorders. It identifies early maladaptive schemas, works with modes such as the vulnerable child and punitive parent, and the therapist partly meets unmet childhood needs within professional limits. It tempts because schema therapy is also an integrative, evidence-based treatment for borderline personality disorder."}},{"q":"EMDR was developed by:","concept":"Eye Movement Desensitisation and Reprocessing (EMDR) was developed by Francine Shapiro, an American psychologist. She reported noticing in 1987 that moving her eyes rapidly from side to side seemed to reduce the distress of disturbing thoughts, and she published her first controlled study in 1989. The client holds a traumatic image, a negative belief about the self, and the linked body sensations in mind while following bilateral stimulation, usually the therapist's moving fingers, though taps or tones are also used. Treatment follows an eight-phase protocol: history taking, preparation, assessment, desensitisation, installation, body scan, closure, and reevaluation. Distress is rated on the Subjective Units of Disturbance scale (0 to 10, adapted from Joseph Wolpe), and belief in a positive self-statement on the Validity of Cognition scale (1 to 7). Shapiro's explanation is the adaptive information processing model: trauma memories are stored unprocessed, and the procedure helps the brain integrate them with other memories. The World Health Organization (2013) recommended EMDR alongside trauma-focused CBT for adults with PTSD. A key controversy is whether the eye movements add anything beyond exposure to the memory, since dismantling studies give mixed results. For variant questions, link each trauma therapy to its founder: prolonged exposure (Edna Foa), cognitive processing therapy (Patricia Resick), stress inoculation training (Donald Meichenbaum), and narrative exposure therapy (Maggie Schauer, Frank Neuner and Thomas Elbert). Option a is right because Francine Shapiro is the originator of EMDR.","traps":{"b":"Edna Foa developed prolonged exposure (PE) for PTSD, based on emotional processing theory (Edna Foa and Michael Kozak, 1986). PE uses repeated imaginal exposure to the trauma memory and in vivo exposure to avoided but safe situations, with no eye movements. It tempts because Foa and Shapiro are the two names most linked with trauma therapy, and both treatments involve recalling the traumatic memory in session.","c":"Aaron Beck founded cognitive therapy, first for depression, built on the cognitive triad and automatic thoughts. Trauma-focused CBT later grew from this tradition, but EMDR came from Shapiro's own observation and her adaptive information processing model, not from Beck. It tempts because Beck is the most famous name in modern psychotherapy, so candidates guess him for any structured, evidence-based treatment.","d":"Marsha Linehan developed dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT) for chronically suicidal people with borderline personality disorder, combining behavioural change with Zen-derived acceptance. DBT does not use bilateral stimulation. It tempts because Linehan and Shapiro are both American women psychologists who created branded therapies in the same era, and a combined DBT and prolonged exposure protocol for PTSD links Linehan to trauma work."}},{"q":"Prolonged exposure for PTSD was developed primarily by:","concept":"Prolonged exposure (PE) therapy for post-traumatic stress disorder was developed by Edna Foa and her colleagues, with the therapist guide written by Foa, Elizabeth Hembree and Barbara Rothbaum (2007). Its theory is emotional processing theory (Edna Foa and Michael Kozak, 1986): fear is stored as a memory network of stimuli, responses and meanings, and it changes only when the network is activated and new, incompatible information is added. Avoidance blocks this, so PTSD persists. PE usually runs about 8 to 15 weekly sessions of around 90 minutes and has four parts: psychoeducation about common trauma reactions, breathing retraining, in vivo exposure to safe but avoided situations arranged in a hierarchy, and imaginal exposure, in which the client recounts the trauma aloud in the present tense, followed by processing of thoughts and feelings. The imaginal exposure is recorded so the client can listen to it as homework. Its main rival is cognitive processing therapy (CPT), developed by Patricia Resick for rape survivors, which uses about 12 sessions to challenge 'stuck points' in themes of safety, trust, power and control, esteem, and intimacy; exposure there is secondary. Both are first-line trauma-focused treatments in major guidelines, together with EMDR (Francine Shapiro) and, for children, trauma-focused CBT (Judith Cohen, Anthony Mannarino and Esther Deblinger). Option a is right because PE was developed primarily by Edna Foa and her colleagues.","traps":{"b":"Patricia Resick developed cognitive processing therapy (CPT), first for rape survivors. CPT is mainly cognitive: the client writes an impact statement and challenges 'stuck points', such as self-blame, in five themes (safety, trust, power and control, esteem, intimacy). A written trauma account is optional. It tempts because CPT and PE are the two leading trauma-focused CBT protocols, often taught side by side with similar guideline status.","c":"Francine Shapiro developed Eye Movement Desensitisation and Reprocessing (EMDR) after a 1987 observation, publishing her first study in 1989. EMDR asks the client to hold the trauma memory in mind briefly while attending to bilateral stimulation, within an eight-phase protocol. It contains an exposure element but is not a prolonged, repeated exposure protocol. It tempts because EMDR is also first-line for PTSD and its full name includes 'desensitisation'.","d":"Marsha Linehan developed dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT) for chronically suicidal people with borderline personality disorder. DBT's core is skills training in mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness, not trauma exposure. A combined protocol, DBT with prolonged exposure, was later developed for clients with both problems. It tempts because that combined protocol puts Linehan's name next to PE."}},{"q":"Ellis's REBT 'ABC' model stands for:","concept":"Albert Ellis, an American psychologist first trained in psychoanalysis, founded rational therapy in the mid-1950s; it was later called rational-emotive therapy and, in the 1990s, rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT). It is usually counted as the first of the cognitive behavioural therapies, ahead of Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy. Its core is the ABC model, set out in his book Reason and Emotion in Psychotherapy (1962). A is the activating event (an adversity, such as failing an exam). B is the belief about A. C is the emotional and behavioural consequence (depression, avoidance). The key teaching point is that A does not cause C directly; B does. Ellis drew this from the Stoic philosopher Epictetus: people are disturbed not by things but by the views they take of them. Irrational beliefs are rigid demands ('musts' and 'shoulds'), with derivatives: awfulising, low frustration tolerance ('I can't stand it'), and global rating of self or others. Rational beliefs are flexible preferences. Therapy extends the model to D, actively disputing the irrational belief (asking where the evidence is, whether it is logical, and whether it helps), and E, the effective new philosophy, with healthier emotions. Ellis also stressed unconditional self-acceptance. Do not confuse this with the behavioural ABC of applied behaviour analysis (antecedent, behaviour, consequence) or the ABC model of attitudes (affect, behaviour, cognition). Option c is right because in REBT, A is the activating event, B the belief, and C the consequence.","traps":{"a":"Antecedent, behaviour, consequence is the three-term contingency of operant conditioning from B. F. Skinner, used in functional behavioural assessment: what happens before a behaviour, the behaviour itself, and what follows and maintains it. Here B means an observable behaviour, not a belief, and the model leaves out thoughts. It tempts because it shares the letters A, B and C and even the word consequence, differing only in the middle term.","b":"Attention, behaviour, cognition is not a recognised model in REBT or elsewhere; it simply recombines familiar psychology words. Attention matters in other models, such as Albert Bandura's observational learning (attention, retention, reproduction, motivation), but it is not part of Ellis's ABC. It tempts because 'cognition' sounds right for a cognitive therapy, and candidates who remember that REBT is about thinking may grab it.","d":"Affect, behaviour, coping is not Ellis's model. The nearest real model is the tripartite ABC model of attitudes (affect, behaviour, cognition) from social psychology, and coping belongs to the stress and coping theory of Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman (1984). Emotion does appear in REBT, but as the consequence, C. It tempts because it mixes real terms that feel clinically relevant."}},{"q":"Yalom's existential 'four ultimate concerns' are:","concept":"Irvin Yalom, an American psychiatrist at Stanford, set out a clinical form of existential psychotherapy in his textbook Existential Psychotherapy (1980). He drew on existential philosophers such as Soren Kierkegaard, Friedrich Nietzsche, Martin Heidegger and Jean-Paul Sartre. His central claim is that anxiety and symptoms come from conflict with four 'givens' or ultimate concerns of existence. Death: we know we will die, yet wish to go on living; much anxiety is disguised death anxiety, held off by defences such as belief in one's own specialness or in an ultimate rescuer. Freedom: there is no external structure, so we are responsible for our choices and lives, which brings a sense of groundlessness; avoiding responsibility, wishing and willing are discussed here. Isolation: existential isolation is an unbridgeable gap between oneself and any other being, distinct from interpersonal isolation (loneliness) and intrapersonal isolation (being cut off from parts of oneself). Meaninglessness: we need meaning in a universe that supplies none, so it must be created. A mnemonic is 'Death Frees Isolated Minds'. Therapy aims at awareness of these givens and authentic choice, not at removing all anxiety. Neighbouring examinable ideas: Viktor Frankl's logotherapy (the will to meaning), Rollo May's work on normal and neurotic anxiety, and Yalom's separate list of eleven therapeutic factors in group therapy, one of which is 'existential factors'. Option b is right because Yalom named death, freedom, isolation and meaninglessness as the four ultimate concerns.","traps":{"a":"Past, present, future and eternity are categories of time, not Yalom's concerns. Time does feature in existential thought, for example Martin Heidegger's being-towards-death and the here-and-now focus of humanistic therapies, but Yalom organised his theory around givens of existence, not tenses. It tempts because 'existential' and 'ultimate' sound philosophical and abstract, and eternity hints at death.","c":"Love and work echo the saying attributed to Sigmund Freud by Erik Erikson, that a healthy person should be able to love and to work, and Alfred Adler named work, friendship and love as the life tasks. Transcendence recalls Abraham Maslow's later idea of self-transcendence beyond self-actualisation. None is one of Yalom's four. It tempts because these are positive life themes from humanistic and neo-Freudian theory.","d":"Birth, life, death and rebirth describe a cyclical, spiritual view of existence, close to the Indian idea of samsara or Carl Jung's archetype of rebirth. Yalom's existentialism is secular and treats death as final, which is exactly why it causes anxiety. It tempts because 'death' appears in both lists, and candidates may link 'ultimate concerns' to religious ideas of the life cycle."}},{"q":"Yalom's therapeutic factors in group therapy include:","concept":"Irvin Yalom described the therapeutic factors, the mechanisms through which group psychotherapy helps, in The Theory and Practice of Group Psychotherapy (first edition 1970). His standard list has eleven: instillation of hope (seeing others improve), universality (discovering that others share one's problems: 'I am not alone'), imparting information (advice and psychoeducation), altruism (gaining self-worth by helping others), corrective recapitulation of the primary family group (old family patterns are re-enacted in the group and corrected), development of socialising techniques, imitative behaviour (modelling on the therapist and other members), interpersonal learning, group cohesiveness, catharsis (expressing strong feelings), and existential factors (accepting responsibility and life's limits). Yalom treats interpersonal learning and cohesiveness as central. The group becomes a social microcosm in which members' usual interpersonal patterns appear, and feedback in the here and now lets them change. Cohesiveness is the group equivalent of the therapeutic alliance in individual therapy. Instillation of hope matters most early, keeping members attending. Exam variants test single factors from short vignettes: 'others have the same feelings as me' is universality, 'helping a new member made me feel useful' is altruism, and 'I copied how she handled criticism' is imitative behaviour. Do not confuse these with Yalom's four ultimate concerns of existential therapy, or with the basic assumptions (dependency, fight-flight, pairing) that Wilfred Bion described in group dynamics. Option b is right because universality, instillation of hope, altruism, corrective recapitulation and cohesiveness are all on Yalom's list of therapeutic factors.","traps":{"a":"Free association and interpretation are the core techniques of Sigmund Freud's individual psychoanalysis: the patient voices every thought without censorship, and the analyst interprets resistance, dreams and transference. They are therapist techniques, whereas Yalom's factors are processes that happen among group members. It tempts because psychoanalytic group therapy exists, so candidates may think group work inherited these methods as its core.","c":"Token reinforcement and shaping are operant procedures from B. F. Skinner's work. A token economy (Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin, 1968) gives exchangeable tokens for target behaviours, and shaping reinforces successive approximations to a goal. These are therapist-controlled contingencies, not interpersonal healing processes within a group. It tempts because token economies are applied to whole groups of patients on hospital wards.","d":"Schema reconstruction and mode work belong to schema therapy, developed by Jeffrey Young, which changes early maladaptive schemas and works with modes such as the vulnerable child and punitive parent. These are treatment targets of one specific model, not general group therapeutic factors. It tempts because group schema therapy for borderline personality disorder is now in use, so the terms can appear in group therapy texts."}},{"q":"Logotherapy was founded by:","concept":"Logotherapy was founded by Viktor Frankl, an Austrian psychiatrist and neurologist from Vienna. The name comes from the Greek logos, meaning 'meaning'. It is often called the 'third Viennese school of psychotherapy', after Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis, which stressed the will to pleasure, and Alfred Adler's individual psychology, which stressed the will to power. Frankl held that the primary human motive is the will to meaning. He developed the approach before the Second World War, and his imprisonment in Nazi concentration camps, including Auschwitz, strengthened it; he described this in Man's Search for Meaning (German original 1946). He observed that prisoners who held on to a purpose, such as a loved one or unfinished work, were better able to endure. Meaning can be found in three ways: creative values (doing a deed or work), experiential values (experiencing something or loving someone), and attitudinal values (the stance taken toward unavoidable suffering). When the will to meaning is frustrated, the person feels an existential vacuum, and Frankl called the neurosis arising from this noogenic neurosis. Its techniques are examined often: paradoxical intention (deliberately wishing for the feared symptom, used for phobias and obsessions), dereflection (shifting attention away from oneself, used in sexual dysfunction and insomnia), and Socratic dialogue to uncover meaning. Separate it from Irvin Yalom's four ultimate concerns and Rollo May's American existential psychology. Option c is right because Viktor Frankl founded logotherapy.","traps":{"a":"Irvin Yalom is an American existential psychiatrist who wrote Existential Psychotherapy (1980), naming death, freedom, isolation and meaninglessness as the ultimate concerns, and The Theory and Practice of Group Psychotherapy. He wrote about meaninglessness and drew on Frankl, but he did not found logotherapy. It tempts because Yalom is the most widely read existential therapist today, and meaning is one of his four concerns.","b":"Rollo May was the leading American existential psychologist. He wrote The Meaning of Anxiety (1950) and Love and Will (1969), and co-edited Existence (1958), which brought European existential psychiatry to American readers. He distinguished normal from neurotic anxiety. He did not found logotherapy. It tempts because he belongs to the same existential school, and the word 'Meaning' even appears in one of his best-known titles.","d":"Karen Horney was a neo-Freudian psychoanalyst who described basic anxiety, ten neurotic needs, three interpersonal styles (moving toward, against and away from people), the real versus idealised self, and the 'tyranny of the shoulds'. She also challenged Freud's view of female psychology. She has no link to logotherapy. It tempts as a well-known name from the same era for candidates guessing among famous therapists."}},{"q":"Minuchin's structural family therapy focuses primarily on:","concept":"Structural family therapy was developed by Salvador Minuchin, an Argentine-born psychiatrist, from his work with poor families at the Wiltwyck School in New York (Families of the Slums, 1967) and later at the Philadelphia Child Guidance Clinic. Its key text is Families and Family Therapy (1974). The theory says a family is a system with an organisation, its structure: the repeated, invisible patterns of who relates to whom, how and when. The family is divided into subsystems, such as spousal, parental and sibling, each with its own tasks. Boundaries are the rules that define who participates in each subsystem. They can be rigid, which produces disengaged families where members are isolated and support is low; clear, which is healthy; or diffuse, which produces enmeshed families where members are over-involved and autonomy is weak. A healthy family also needs a clear hierarchy, with parents in charge. Symptoms often reflect structural problems, such as a cross-generational coalition of a parent and child against the other parent. Minuchin's later work on 'psychosomatic families' examined anorexia nervosa. The therapist first joins the family, maps its structure, and then restructures it through enactment (having members act out a conflict in session), boundary making, unbalancing and reframing. Contrast neighbouring schools: Murray Bowen (differentiation, multigenerational transmission), the strategic therapy of Jay Haley (directives, paradox), and the Milan systemic group (circular questioning). Option b is right because Minuchin's model focuses on subsystems, boundaries and hierarchies within the family.","traps":{"a":"Multigenerational transmission of anxiety is a core concept of Murray Bowen's family systems theory: levels of differentiation and patterns of emotional reactivity pass down through generations, mapped with a genogram. Bowen focused on the emotional system across three or more generations, not on the present-day organisation of subsystems. It tempts because Bowen and Minuchin are both systems family therapists, often taught in the same lecture.","c":"Cognitive distortions are systematic thinking errors, such as overgeneralisation and personalisation, described by Aaron Beck in individual cognitive therapy. Cognitive behavioural family therapy exists, but distortions are a property of individual minds, whereas Minuchin located problems in the family's organisation, not in anyone's thinking. It tempts because 'cognitive distortions' is a familiar textbook phrase that seems to fit almost any therapy question.","d":"Paradoxical injunctions, such as prescribing the symptom, belong to strategic family therapy (Jay Haley, the Mental Research Institute group in Palo Alto) and the Milan systemic group. The idea grew from the double bind theory of Gregory Bateson and colleagues (1956). Minuchin used direct, active restructuring rather than paradox. It tempts because Haley worked alongside Minuchin in Philadelphia, so their methods are easily mixed."}},{"q":"Bowen family systems therapy emphasises:","concept":"Bowen family systems theory was developed by Murray Bowen, an American psychiatrist, from his work in the 1950s, including research at the National Institute of Mental Health in which whole families of patients with schizophrenia lived on the research ward. He saw the family as an emotional unit and described eight interlocking concepts. Differentiation of self is the central one: the ability to separate thinking from feeling and to keep a stable self while staying emotionally connected; Bowen imagined it on a scale from 0 to 100. Poorly differentiated people are either fused with the family or cut off from it. Triangles: a tense two-person relationship pulls in a third person, often a child, to reduce anxiety; this is triangulation. The others are nuclear family emotional process, family projection process (parents project their anxiety onto a child), multigenerational transmission process (differentiation levels shift slightly across generations), emotional cutoff, sibling position (drawing on Walter Toman's work), and societal emotional process. Bowen collected the ideas in Family Therapy in Clinical Practice (1978). The therapist stays calm and detached, often coaching one motivated person to differentiate, and uses the genogram, a three-generation family diagram later standardised by Monica McGoldrick and Randy Gerson. Contrast Salvador Minuchin's structural therapy (boundaries, subsystems), strategic therapy (Jay Haley), narrative therapy (Michael White and David Epston), and solution-focused therapy (Steve de Shazer and Insoo Kim Berg). Option c is right because differentiation of self, triangulation and multigenerational transmission are Bowen's signature concepts.","traps":{"a":"Externalisation is the key technique of narrative therapy, developed by Michael White and David Epston (Narrative Means to Therapeutic Ends, 1990). The problem is talked about as separate from the person ('the person is not the problem; the problem is the problem'), helping clients re-author a richer life story. It is postmodern and social constructionist, not a systems theory of emotional fusion. It tempts as another famous family therapy technique.","b":"Strategic paradox and prescribing the symptom come from strategic family therapy, associated with Jay Haley and Cloe Madanes and the Mental Research Institute in Palo Alto, drawing on Milton Erickson's hypnotherapy. The therapist designs directives to change the sequence of behaviour maintaining the problem, sometimes instructing the family to continue the symptom. It tempts because strategic and Bowenian therapy are both family systems approaches from the same era.","d":"Exception questions ask when the problem did not occur or was less severe, so the client can do more of what already works. They come from solution-focused brief therapy, developed by Steve de Shazer and Insoo Kim Berg in Milwaukee, along with the miracle question and scaling questions. It is future-focused and brief, unlike Bowen's multigenerational history taking. It tempts as another well-known family therapy technique."}},{"q":"Schema therapy was developed by:","concept":"Schema therapy was developed by Jeffrey Young, an American psychologist who had worked with Aaron Beck. He found that standard cognitive therapy helped acute problems but often failed in chronic, characterological cases such as personality disorders, so he built an integrative model combining cognitive, behavioural, attachment, gestalt and psychodynamic ideas. It appeared in his 1990 book on a schema-focused approach and in Schema Therapy: A Practitioner's Guide (2003, with Janet Klosko and Marjorie Weishaar). Its core unit is the early maladaptive schema: a broad, pervasive pattern of memories, emotions, beliefs and body sensations about oneself and relationships, formed when core childhood needs were not met. Young listed 18 schemas in five domains, such as abandonment, mistrust and abuse, defectiveness and shame, and unrelenting standards. People respond with three coping styles: surrender (giving in to the schema), avoidance, and overcompensation (fighting it by acting the opposite). Modes are the moment-to-moment states that dominate a person, such as the vulnerable child, angry child, punitive parent, detached protector and healthy adult; mode work was developed mainly for borderline personality disorder. Techniques include limited reparenting, empathic confrontation, imagery rescripting and chair work, and schemas are assessed with the Young Schema Questionnaire. A Dutch trial by Josephine Giesen-Bloo and colleagues (2006) found it more effective than transference-focused psychotherapy for borderline personality disorder. Do not confuse Young's early maladaptive schemas with Beck's general cognitive schemas or core beliefs. Option b is right because Jeffrey Young developed schema therapy.","traps":{"a":"Aaron Beck founded cognitive therapy, first for depression, using automatic thoughts, the cognitive triad and the general concept of schemas or core beliefs. He also wrote Cognitive Therapy of Personality Disorders (1990, with Arthur Freeman). Schema therapy grew out of his school, but it was Young who built it into a separate model. It tempts because Beck used the word 'schema' first, and Young worked at his centre.","c":"Marsha Linehan developed dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT) for chronically suicidal people with borderline personality disorder, balancing behavioural change with Zen-derived acceptance through skills training. DBT and schema therapy are both well-supported treatments for borderline personality disorder, so they appear together in exam lists, but DBT has no schema domains or modes. It tempts because of this shared target disorder.","d":"Steven Hayes developed Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT), a third-wave behaviour therapy based on relational frame theory. ACT aims at psychological flexibility through six processes, including acceptance, cognitive defusion and values, rather than changing the content of schemas. It tempts because ACT and schema therapy are both newer extensions of CBT, often grouped together as post-Beck or third-wave approaches."}},{"q":"Acceptance and Commitment Therapy was developed by:","concept":"Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT, said as the word 'act') was developed by Steven C. Hayes with Kirk Strosahl and Kelly Wilson, and set out in their 1999 book. It is a 'third wave' behaviour therapy, a term Hayes used for therapies that change a person's relationship to thoughts and feelings rather than their content. Its basic theory is relational frame theory, a behavioural account of language: humans learn to relate events arbitrarily (bigger than, same as, because of), so words can trigger pain even when the real event is absent. Suffering, in this view, comes from psychological inflexibility, chiefly experiential avoidance (trying to escape unwanted inner experiences) and cognitive fusion (taking thoughts as literal truths). The goal is psychological flexibility, built through six core processes shown in the 'hexaflex': acceptance, cognitive defusion (seeing a thought as just a thought, for instance by repeating a word until it loses meaning), contact with the present moment, self-as-context (the observing self), values, and committed action. Metaphors and experiential exercises are used rather than disputing beliefs. ACT is used for chronic pain, anxiety, depression and psychosis. Other third-wave therapies are dialectical behaviour therapy (Marsha Linehan), mindfulness-based cognitive therapy (Zindel Segal, Mark Williams and John Teasdale, 2002), functional analytic psychotherapy (Robert Kohlenberg and Mavis Tsai), and compassion-focused therapy (Paul Gilbert). The first wave was behaviour therapy; the second was the cognitive therapy of Beck and Ellis. Option a is right because ACT was developed by Steven Hayes and his colleagues.","traps":{"b":"Marsha Linehan developed dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT) for chronically suicidal clients with borderline personality disorder. DBT is also a third-wave therapy using mindfulness and acceptance, but it is organised around four skills modules and a biosocial theory, not relational frame theory or the hexaflex. It tempts because DBT and ACT both put acceptance at the centre and are always listed together as third-wave therapies.","c":"Aaron Beck founded cognitive therapy, the core of the second wave, which identifies automatic thoughts and tests their accuracy through Socratic questioning and behavioural experiments. ACT was partly a reaction against this: it does not try to change the content of thoughts, only how one relates to them. It tempts because Beck is the default answer for any question about the founder of a CBT-related therapy.","d":"Albert Ellis founded rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT) in the 1950s, built on the ABC model and active disputing of irrational beliefs such as 'musts'. Like Beck's therapy, it aims to change belief content, the opposite of ACT's defusion. Ellis did stress unconditional self-acceptance, which can make the word 'acceptance' seem linked. It tempts for that reason and because both are known by short acronyms."}},{"q":"The 'Premack principle' states that:","concept":"The Premack principle was proposed by David Premack, an American psychologist, in a 1959 paper and developed further in 1965. It says that reinforcement is relative: of any two behaviours, the more probable one (the one done more often when free choice is allowed) will reinforce the less probable one when access to it is made contingent on doing the less probable one. A reinforcer is therefore an activity, not just a stimulus such as food. In his classic study, children who preferred pinball to candy ate more candy when eating earned pinball play, while candy-preferring children played more pinball when playing earned candy. The relation can reverse: Premack showed with rats that running could reinforce drinking, or drinking reinforce running, depending on which had been restricted. The everyday version is 'Grandma's rule': first finish your homework, then watch TV. In therapy it guides contingency contracts, activity scheduling and token economies, where preferred activities serve as backup reinforcers. The later response deprivation hypothesis (William Timberlake and James Allison, 1974) refined it: any behaviour restricted below its baseline level can become a reinforcer, even a less preferred one. Neighbouring examinable rules: Edward Thorndike's law of effect, B. F. Skinner's schedules of reinforcement, the partial reinforcement extinction effect, and the conditions for effective punishment. Option a is right because Premack held that a higher-frequency behaviour can reinforce a lower-frequency behaviour.","traps":{"b":"Immediacy is one of the conditions for effective punishment summarised from operant research, for example by Nathan Azrin and William Holz (1966): punishment works best when it is immediate, consistent and of adequate intensity from the start. The same immediacy rule applies to reinforcement. It is a real operant principle, but not Premack's, which concerns which activities can act as reinforcers. It tempts because it sounds like a textbook behavioural rule.","c":"This states the opposite of the partial reinforcement extinction effect. Behaviour learned under continuous reinforcement extinguishes fastest, because the change to no reinforcement is easy to detect. Intermittent schedules, especially variable ratio (as in gambling), produce the most resistance to extinction, as shown in Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner's Schedules of Reinforcement (1957). It tempts because continuous reinforcement is best for fast acquisition, which candidates confuse with persistence.","d":"This is a garbled classical conditioning statement, and no such rule exists. In Ivan Pavlov's paradigm a neutral stimulus, such as a bell, is paired with an unconditioned stimulus, such as food, until it becomes a conditioned stimulus. Premack's principle is about operant reinforcement between activities, not stimulus pairing. Related real ideas include higher-order conditioning and sensory preconditioning. It tempts because it uses correct conditioning vocabulary."}},{"q":"Bandura's social learning theory introduced the construct of:","concept":"Albert Bandura, a Canadian-born psychologist at Stanford, developed social learning theory, set out in Social Learning Theory (1977) and expanded as social cognitive theory in Social Foundations of Thought and Action (1986). Against strict behaviourism, he argued that most learning happens without direct reinforcement, by watching models: observational learning, or modelling. In the Bobo doll studies (Albert Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross, 1961), children who watched an adult hit an inflatable doll later copied the aggression. When a model is seen being rewarded or punished, the observer's behaviour changes too: vicarious reinforcement and vicarious punishment. Bandura also showed that learning differs from performance: children who saw the model punished imitated less, yet reproduced the acts once offered incentives. Observational learning needs four processes: attention, retention, motor reproduction and motivation. His second hallmark construct is reciprocal determinism: behaviour, personal factors (thoughts, expectations) and the environment all influence one another, so people are not only shaped by their surroundings but also change them. His later central construct was self-efficacy (1977), the belief in one's ability to carry out a behaviour, built from mastery experiences, vicarious experience, verbal persuasion and physiological states. Clinical uses include participant modelling for phobias. Neighbouring concepts: the earlier work on imitation by Neal Miller and John Dollard (1941), and Joseph Wolpe's reciprocal inhibition. Option b is right because reciprocal determinism and observational (vicarious) learning are Bandura's signature constructs.","traps":{"a":"Reciprocal inhibition is the principle Joseph Wolpe (1958) used for systematic desensitisation: if a response incompatible with anxiety, such as deep muscle relaxation, occurs in the presence of a feared stimulus, the anxiety bond is weakened. The term came from Charles Sherrington's physiology of opposing muscle reflexes. It belongs to behaviour therapy, not social learning theory. It tempts because it shares the word 'reciprocal'.","c":"Reciprocal altruism was proposed by the evolutionary biologist Robert Trivers (1971): helping an unrelated individual can evolve if the help is likely to be returned later, as in 'you scratch my back, I scratch yours'. It explains cooperation in evolutionary and social psychology, not learning by observation. It tempts because Bandura's theory is 'social', and the option repeats the word 'reciprocal'.","d":"Reciprocal interpretation is not a recognised psychological construct; it is a filler built on the 'reciprocal' pattern of the other options. Real neighbours include the norm of reciprocity (Alvin Gouldner, 1960) and reciprocal teaching in educational psychology. It tempts candidates who remember that Bandura's key term starts with 'reciprocal' but cannot recall the second word, determinism."}},{"q":"Free association is the central technique of:","concept":"Free association is the 'fundamental rule' of psychoanalysis, developed by Sigmund Freud in the 1890s. The patient, often lying on a couch with the analyst out of sight, is asked to say everything that comes to mind, without selecting, judging or censoring, however trivial, rude or embarrassing it seems. The history matters for exams. Josef Breuer's cathartic method with the patient Anna O (Bertha Pappenheim), who called it the 'talking cure', used hypnosis, and Studies on Hysteria (Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud, 1895) reported such cases. Freud found that many patients could not be hypnotised, tried a 'pressure technique' (a hand on the forehead), and then moved to free association. The logic is psychic determinism: no thought is random, so a chain of associations leads toward repressed, unconscious material. Where the flow stops or the patient changes topic, the analyst sees resistance. Other psychoanalytic techniques work alongside it: dream analysis (Freud called dreams the royal road to the unconscious, in The Interpretation of Dreams, 1900), analysis of transference and resistance, interpretation, and working through. The analyst listens with 'evenly suspended attention'. Do not confuse free association with Carl Jung's word association test, in which the subject answers single stimulus words, and delays or odd responses reveal emotionally charged complexes. Option b is right because free association is the central technique of Freudian psychoanalysis.","traps":{"a":"Behaviour therapy applies learning principles to observable behaviour, using techniques such as systematic desensitisation (Joseph Wolpe, 1958), exposure, operant reinforcement and token economies. It looks at present behaviour with its antecedents and consequences, not unconscious material, so it has no use for free association. It tempts because 'association' sounds like associative learning in classical conditioning, a basic idea in behaviour therapy.","c":"Person-centred therapy was developed by Carl Rogers (Client-Centered Therapy, 1951). The therapist offers three core conditions, empathy, unconditional positive regard and congruence, and the client leads. Rogers rejected interpretation of unconscious content. It tempts because the client talks freely in both approaches, but person-centred therapy has no rule to report every thought, and the therapist reflects feelings rather than interpreting them.","d":"Cognitive therapy, developed by Aaron Beck for depression, identifies and tests automatic thoughts using thought records, Socratic questioning and behavioural experiments. It is structured, collaborative and focused on conscious, accessible thinking. Beck trained as a psychoanalyst, which is why candidates may link him with free association, but he broke from psychoanalysis. It tempts because both therapies ask patients to report their thoughts."}},{"q":"Maxwell Jones is best associated with:","concept":"A therapeutic community is a residential or day treatment setting in which the whole community, not only the professional, is the agent of change. Maxwell Jones, a Scottish psychiatrist, is the name most closely linked with it. During the Second World War he treated soldiers with 'effort syndrome' at Mill Hill and found that group discussion and shared responsibility helped. After the war he ran a unit for industrial neurosis at Belmont Hospital in Surrey, later renamed the Henderson Hospital, which became Britain's model unit for personality disorder, and he later led Dingleton Hospital in Scotland. The term itself was coined by Tom Main (1946) after the Northfield experiments with Wilfred Bion and others, but Jones developed and popularised the method, described in Social Psychiatry (1952). Its features are daily community meetings, a flattened hierarchy between staff and patients, open communication, shared decisions, and 'living-learning' situations in which everyday behaviour is examined in the group. Robert Rapoport's study Community as Doctor (1960) summarised four principles: democratisation, permissiveness, communalism and reality confrontation. A separate, more hierarchical 'concept' model of therapeutic community grew up for drug addiction, starting with Synanon in the United States. Exam neighbours that also used the institution as therapy are moral treatment (Philippe Pinel, William Tuke), milieu therapy and the token economy. Option a is right because Maxwell Jones is best known for developing the democratic therapeutic community.","traps":{"b":"A token economy is an operant system in which patients earn tokens for target behaviours and exchange them for privileges. It was introduced on a psychiatric ward by Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin (The Token Economy, 1968), based on B. F. Skinner's principles. It is staff-controlled and hierarchical, unlike Jones's democratic community. It tempts because both are ward-wide inpatient programmes from roughly the same period.","c":"Aversion therapy pairs an unwanted behaviour or stimulus with something unpleasant, such as an emetic drug with alcohol or a mild electric shock, to make it less attractive. It is a classical conditioning technique; covert sensitisation (Joseph Cautela) is an imagined version. It has no link to Jones. It tempts as another mid-twentieth-century hospital treatment that candidates may attach to any psychiatrist's name.","d":"Eye Movement Desensitisation and Reprocessing (EMDR) is a trauma therapy developed by Francine Shapiro in the late 1980s, in which the client recalls a traumatic memory while following bilateral eye movements within an eight-phase protocol. It is an individual therapy from a much later period than Jones's work. It tempts only as a famous acronym, offered to candidates who do not recognise Jones's name."}},{"q":"Aaron Beck originally developed cognitive therapy primarily for:","concept":"Aaron T. Beck, an American psychiatrist at the University of Pennsylvania, developed cognitive therapy primarily for depression. Trained in psychoanalysis, he set out around the late 1950s and early 1960s to test the psychoanalytic idea that depression is anger turned inward, by studying the dreams and thoughts of depressed patients. Instead of hostility he found themes of loss, defeat and failure, and rapid, negative automatic thoughts. His model rests on the cognitive triad: negative views of the self ('I am worthless'), the world ('nobody cares') and the future ('it will never improve'). These come from negative schemas formed early in life and are kept going by cognitive distortions such as arbitrary inference, selective abstraction, overgeneralisation, magnification and minimisation, personalisation and dichotomous thinking. Key texts are Depression: Clinical, Experimental and Theoretical Aspects (1967) and Cognitive Therapy of Depression (Aaron Beck, John Rush, Brian Shaw and Gary Emery, 1979). He also created the Beck Depression Inventory (1961). Therapy is short-term and structured, using collaborative empiricism, Socratic questioning, thought records and behavioural experiments, with activity scheduling early on in severe depression. A 1977 trial by John Rush and colleagues found it at least as effective as the antidepressant imipramine. Only later was it extended to anxiety and panic, personality disorders, substance use and schizophrenia. Compare Albert Ellis's REBT (irrational beliefs), Martin Seligman's learned helplessness, and the hopelessness theory of Lyn Abramson and colleagues. Option d is right because Beck built cognitive therapy first for depression.","traps":{"a":"Schizophrenia was a much later target. Cognitive behaviour therapy for psychosis developed mainly in Britain from the 1990s, working on delusions as beliefs open to evidence and on distress from voices. Beck co-wrote a book on schizophrenia late in his career (2009). It tempts because Beck's name is now linked with psychosis work, but this came decades after his work on depression.","b":"Substance use disorders were another later extension. Beck and colleagues published Cognitive Therapy of Substance Abuse (1993), focusing on drug-related beliefs and craving. The best-known cognitive behavioural approach for addiction is relapse prevention, developed by G. Alan Marlatt. It tempts because CBT is now standard in addiction treatment, and candidates may not know the order in which Beck's model spread to different disorders.","c":"OCD is treated first-line with exposure and response prevention, which came from behaviour therapy (Victor Meyer, 1966), not from Beck's cognitive therapy. Cognitive models of OCD, such as Paul Salkovskis's account of inflated responsibility for harm, came later. It tempts because OCD is a classic CBT disorder, and cognitive work on obsessional thoughts is common in textbooks."}},{"q":"The behavioural intervention called 'response prevention' is most central to the treatment of:","concept":"Response prevention means stopping the patient from carrying out the ritual or safety behaviour that normally follows a trigger. It is half of exposure and response prevention (ERP, also called exposure and ritual prevention), the first-line psychological treatment for obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD). Victor Meyer (1966), at the Middlesex Hospital in London, first described it with two patients, and it was developed in the 1970s by Stanley Rachman, Ray Hodgson and Isaac Marks at the Maudsley, and later by Edna Foa and colleagues. The logic follows the two-factor theory of O. Hobart Mowrer: anxiety becomes attached to a trigger through classical conditioning, and the compulsion is maintained by negative reinforcement, because washing or checking briefly reduces anxiety. Each ritual therefore prevents the person from learning that anxiety falls on its own and that the feared disaster does not happen. In ERP the patient faces graded triggers from a hierarchy (in vivo and imaginal) and then refrains from overt rituals and from mental neutralising, such as praying or counting. Anxiety rises, peaks and declines (habituation), and the feared belief is disconfirmed (inhibitory learning). Severity is measured with the Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale (Y-BOCS), and medication options are SSRIs and clomipramine. Exposure alone, without response prevention, is enough for most phobias, which is the key difference examiners test. ERP is also adapted for related problems such as body dysmorphic disorder. Option a is right because response prevention is the central, defining component of ERP for OCD.","traps":{"b":"Specific phobia is treated with exposure: graded in vivo exposure, systematic desensitisation (Joseph Wolpe, 1958), flooding, or the one-session treatment of Lars-Göran Öst. Phobic patients mainly avoid and escape rather than perform rituals, so preventing a ritual is not the central component. Some safety behaviours may be dropped, but the core is exposure. It tempts because exposure is shared by both treatments.","c":"Depression is treated behaviourally with behavioural activation, rooted in Peter Lewinsohn's work linking depression to low rates of response-contingent positive reinforcement, and also with Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy, interpersonal therapy and antidepressants. There is no compulsive ritual to block. It tempts as a common disorder that candidates pick when unsure which behavioural technique fits which condition.","d":"Insomnia is treated with cognitive behavioural therapy for insomnia (CBT-I), whose key behavioural parts are stimulus control (Richard Bootzin) and sleep restriction (Arthur Spielman), plus sleep hygiene and cognitive work. Stimulus control means using the bed only for sleep and leaving it if not asleep within about 15 to 20 minutes. It tempts because these rules restrict what the person does, which can sound like response prevention."}},{"q":"Negative reinforcement is best illustrated by:","concept":"Negative reinforcement is an operant conditioning term from B. F. Skinner, who built on Edward Thorndike's law of effect (1911). Skinner classified consequences with two questions. First, does the behaviour become more or less likely afterwards? If it increases, the consequence is reinforcement; if it decreases, it is punishment. Second, is a stimulus added or removed? Added is positive and removed is negative, in the arithmetic sense, not good or bad. This gives four cells: positive reinforcement (add a pleasant stimulus, behaviour increases, such as praise for homework), negative reinforcement (remove an aversive stimulus, behaviour increases), positive punishment (add an aversive stimulus, behaviour decreases, such as a scolding), and negative punishment (remove a pleasant stimulus, behaviour decreases, such as time-out or confiscating a phone). Classic examples of negative reinforcement: fastening a seatbelt turns off the warning buzzer; taking a painkiller removes a headache. Two forms are tested: escape learning (the behaviour ends an aversive stimulus already present) and avoidance learning (the behaviour prevents it from starting). Negative reinforcement is central in clinical psychology because relief maintains avoidance, compulsions and substance use, as in the two-factor theory of anxiety of O. Hobart Mowrer. The most common exam error is equating negative reinforcement with punishment; both may involve aversive stimuli, but reinforcement always strengthens behaviour. Option a is right because removing an aversive stimulus contingent on a behaviour, so that the behaviour increases, is the definition of negative reinforcement.","traps":{"b":"Adding a pleasant stimulus to increase a behaviour is positive reinforcement, such as praise, money or food after the response. It is reinforcement because the behaviour increases, but 'positive' because something is added. It tempts because candidates read 'negative' as 'unpleasant' and 'positive' as 'pleasant', and because this is the most familiar form of reinforcement, so it feels like the default answer.","c":"Removing a pleasant stimulus to decrease a behaviour is negative punishment, as in time-out from reinforcement or response cost, such as a fine. It is 'negative' because something is removed, matching the word in the stem, but it reduces behaviour, so it is punishment. It tempts because it gets the removal part right, and candidates forget to check the direction in which the behaviour changes.","d":"Adding an aversive stimulus to decrease a behaviour is positive punishment, such as a scolding or a smack after misbehaviour. It is the most common confusion in this topic: many people, and even some media, wrongly call any unpleasant consequence 'negative reinforcement'. Here the aversive stimulus is added and behaviour falls, so it is punishment. It tempts because 'aversive' and 'negative' feel like synonyms."}},{"q":"Motivational Interviewing was developed by:","concept":"Motivational interviewing (MI) was developed by William R. Miller, an American psychologist, and Stephen Rollnick, a clinical psychologist working in Wales. Miller first described it in a 1983 paper on problem drinkers, and the two wrote the book Motivational Interviewing (1991), with later editions. MI is a collaborative, goal-oriented style of conversation for strengthening a person's own motivation for change by exploring and resolving ambivalence. It grew from Carl Rogers's client-centred therapy but is directive in purpose: the counsellor deliberately draws out and reinforces 'change talk' (the client's own arguments for change) and softens 'sustain talk'. Its spirit is partnership, acceptance, compassion and evocation. Core skills are summed up as OARS: open questions, affirmations, reflective listening and summaries. Earlier editions gave four principles: express empathy, develop discrepancy between goals and current behaviour, roll with resistance instead of arguing, and support self-efficacy. The third edition described four processes: engaging, focusing, evoking and planning. Change talk is often coded as DARN (desire, ability, reasons, need), leading to commitment. A brief adaptation, motivational enhancement therapy, was tested in Project MATCH for alcohol problems. MI is often paired with, but separate from, the transtheoretical (stages of change) model of James Prochaska and Carlo DiClemente: precontemplation, contemplation, preparation, action and maintenance. MI is especially useful in the precontemplation and contemplation stages. Option d is right because motivational interviewing was developed by William Miller and Stephen Rollnick.","traps":{"a":"Albert Bandura developed social learning theory and the concept of self-efficacy (1977), a person's belief that they can carry out a behaviour. MI borrows self-efficacy, since supporting confidence in change is one of its principles, but Bandura did not develop MI. It tempts because self-efficacy is central in addiction and health behaviour work, so his name feels connected to motivation for change.","b":"James Prochaska and Carlo DiClemente developed the transtheoretical model, or stages of change, from studies of smokers who quit: precontemplation, contemplation, preparation, action and maintenance, sometimes with termination. It describes when and how people change, while MI is a method to help them move forward. It tempts because the two are taught together and MI is matched to the client's stage, so their authors get swapped.","c":"G. Alan Marlatt developed relapse prevention with Judith Gordon (1985), a cognitive behavioural model of addiction that identifies high-risk situations, coping skills, and the abstinence violation effect, in which a single lapse becomes a full relapse through guilt and loss of perceived control. He also promoted harm reduction. It tempts because he is a major figure in the same addictions field as MI."}},{"q":"A Type I error in null-hypothesis testing is:","concept":"Null hypothesis significance testing combines ideas from Ronald Fisher (significance testing and the p value) and from Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1933), who framed testing as a decision with two possible kinds of error. The null hypothesis (H0) states no effect or no difference; the alternative (H1) states that there is one. Because decisions are made from samples, two errors are possible. A Type I error is rejecting H0 when it is actually true: concluding a drug works when it does not. It is a false positive, and its probability is alpha, the significance level fixed in advance, usually .05 (or .01 for a stricter test). A Type II error is retaining, or failing to reject, H0 when it is actually false: missing a real effect. It is a false negative, with probability beta. Power is 1 minus beta, the probability of detecting a real effect, conventionally set at .80 or more. Lowering alpha reduces Type I errors but increases Type II errors if nothing else changes. Running many tests inflates the familywise Type I error rate, which corrections such as Bonferroni (alpha divided by the number of tests) control. A courtroom analogy helps: H0 is 'innocent', so convicting an innocent person is a Type I error and freeing a guilty person is a Type II error. Another memory aid: Type I is a false alarm, crying wolf when there is no wolf. Option b is right because a Type I error means rejecting H0 when it is actually true, a false positive.","traps":{"a":"Retaining H0 when it is actually false is a Type II error, a false negative, with probability beta. It happens when a study misses a real effect, often because power is low, for example due to a small sample or a small true effect. It tempts because it is the mirror image of the correct answer, and the two error types are swapped more often than any other pair of terms in research methods.","c":"Using too small a sample is a design weakness, not a type of error. Small samples lower power and so raise the risk of a Type II error; when they do give significant results, the effect sizes tend to be inflated. But alpha, the Type I error rate, is set by the researcher and stays at, say, .05 regardless of sample size. It tempts because 'error' suggests any research mistake.","d":"Using a one-tailed test inappropriately, for example choosing the direction after seeing the data, effectively doubles alpha and so raises the true Type I error rate. It is a cause of extra Type I errors, not their definition. A one-tailed test is legitimate only when the direction is predicted in advance on good grounds. It tempts because it is genuinely linked to Type I error inflation."}},{"q":"Statistical power increases when:","concept":"Statistical power is the probability that a test correctly rejects a false null hypothesis, that is, detects an effect that really exists. It equals 1 minus beta, where beta is the Type II error rate. Jacob Cohen made power central to psychology, first in a 1962 review showing that typical published studies had low power, then in Statistical Power Analysis for the Behavioral Sciences (1969, second edition 1988). He suggested .80 as the minimum acceptable power. Power depends on four linked quantities, and if three are known the fourth can be calculated, which is what an a priori power analysis does to find the needed sample size. First, effect size: larger true differences are easier to detect. Second, sample size: a larger n reduces the standard error, so power rises. Third, alpha: a more lenient alpha (.05 rather than .01) makes rejecting H0 easier, so power rises, at the cost of more Type I errors. Fourth, variability: less noise, through reliable measures, homogeneous samples or within-subjects designs, raises power. A one-tailed test, used correctly, also has more power than a two-tailed test for an effect in the predicted direction. Note the trade-off examiners like: making alpha stricter, as with a Bonferroni correction, lowers Type I error but also lowers power. Low-powered studies miss real effects and, when significant, tend to overestimate effect size, a factor in psychology's replication crisis. Option d is right because increasing effect size, sample size and alpha each raises power, so increasing all three raises it.","traps":{"a":"Decreasing sample size lowers power. With a smaller n the standard error is larger, so the same true effect is less likely to reach significance, and Type II errors become more common. This is the opposite of the correct relationship. Power analysis exists precisely to find the minimum sample size needed for adequate power, usually .80. It tempts mainly through misreading, or the mistaken belief that significance in a small sample is more impressive.","b":"Moving alpha from .05 to .01 makes the test stricter: a larger test statistic is needed to reject H0. This reduces the Type I error rate but increases beta, so power falls. This is the trade-off behind corrections such as Bonferroni. It tempts because a 'more conservative' or 'more rigorous' test sounds better and stronger in everyday language, so candidates assume it must also be more powerful.","c":"Greater variability, meaning more spread or noise in the scores, makes the standard error larger and hides the effect, so power decreases. Researchers raise power by reducing variability through reliable measures, standardised procedures, homogeneous samples, or within-subjects designs where each person acts as their own control. It tempts because more variation can sound like more information, or because candidates confuse it with a larger effect."}},{"q":"Cohen's d of 0.5 is conventionally considered:","concept":"Cohen's d is a standardised effect size for the difference between two means, introduced by Jacob Cohen in his work on power analysis (Statistical Power Analysis for the Behavioral Sciences, 1969, second edition 1988). It is the difference between the two group means divided by the pooled standard deviation, so it tells you how many standard deviations apart the groups are, independent of the measurement scale. Unlike a p value, which depends heavily on sample size, d describes how big the effect is. Cohen offered rough benchmarks for use when no better context exists: d = 0.2 is small, 0.5 is medium, and 0.8 is large. He described a medium effect as one visible to the naked eye of a careful observer. A d of 0.5 means the average person in the treated group scores higher than about 69 percent of the control group. Related indices: Hedges' g corrects d for small-sample bias, and Glass's delta uses only the control group's standard deviation. Cohen's benchmarks for other indices are also examinable: for the correlation r, .10, .30 and .50; for eta squared, .01, .06 and .14; and for f squared in regression, .02, .15 and .35. Effect sizes are pooled across studies in meta-analysis and are needed as inputs for power analysis. Cohen himself warned that the labels are arbitrary and that context matters. Option d is right because a Cohen's d of 0.5 is conventionally described as a medium effect.","traps":{"a":"Small is Cohen's label for d = 0.2, a difference of one fifth of a standard deviation, which he illustrated with the small height difference between 15 and 16 year old girls. A d of 0.5 is two and a half times that. It tempts because 0.5 looks like a small number, and candidates who forget the benchmarks think anything below 1 must be small.","b":"Negligible is not one of Cohen's three labels. It is used informally for effects well below d = 0.2, close to zero, where the two groups overlap almost completely. A d of 0.5 is a clearly meaningful difference that a careful observer could notice. It tempts candidates who confuse effect size with statistical significance, or who think any value below 1 is unimportant.","c":"Large is Cohen's label for d = 0.8, almost a full standard deviation between the means. It tempts because 0.5 is the large benchmark for the correlation coefficient r, where Cohen's values are .10, .30 and .50. Candidates who remember the r scale apply it to d by mistake. Always check which effect size index the question uses before choosing a label."}},{"q":"The Mann-Whitney U test is the non-parametric equivalent of:","concept":"Non-parametric tests make few assumptions about the shape of the population distribution. Most work on ranks rather than raw scores, so they suit ordinal data, small samples, skewed distributions and outliers, at the cost of some power when parametric assumptions actually hold. The Mann-Whitney U test was published by Henry B. Mann and Donald R. Whitney (1947); it is equivalent to the rank-sum test of Frank Wilcoxon (1945), so books also call it the Wilcoxon-Mann-Whitney test. It compares two independent groups: all scores are pooled and ranked together, the ranks are summed for each group, and U counts how often a score in one group precedes a score in the other. If the groups do not differ, the ranks should be evenly mixed. It is therefore the rank-based counterpart of the independent-samples t-test. Learn the full pairing table, because examiners rotate through it: the paired t-test pairs with the Wilcoxon signed-rank test (Wilcoxon, 1945); one-way ANOVA with the Kruskal-Wallis H test (William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis, 1952); repeated-measures ANOVA with the Friedman test (Milton Friedman, 1937); Pearson r with Spearman's rho or Kendall's tau. Chi-square (Karl Pearson, 1900) is itself non-parametric and works on frequencies in categories, so it is nobody's 'equivalent' in this table. The quick rule: two independent groups, Mann-Whitney; two related measures, Wilcoxon signed-rank; three or more groups, Kruskal-Wallis or Friedman. Option a is right because the Mann-Whitney U test compares two independent groups, exactly the design served by the independent-samples t-test.","traps":{"b":"The paired (dependent-samples) t-test compares two related sets of scores, such as the same patients before and after therapy, or matched pairs. Its non-parametric counterpart is the Wilcoxon signed-rank test (Frank Wilcoxon, 1945), which ranks the absolute differences within pairs and compares the sums of positive and negative ranks. It tempts because Wilcoxon's name is also attached to the rank-sum test that is equivalent to Mann-Whitney U.","c":"One-way ANOVA compares the means of three or more independent groups on one factor. Its rank-based counterpart is the Kruskal-Wallis H test (William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis, 1952), which is in effect Mann-Whitney extended beyond two groups; with exactly two groups the two tests agree. It tempts because both are rank tests for independent groups, and the deciding detail, the number of groups, is easy to overlook.","d":"The chi-square test of Karl Pearson (1900) compares observed and expected frequencies in categories, testing goodness of fit or the association between two nominal variables. It is already a non-parametric test, so it is not the parametric original that Mann-Whitney replaces, and it works on counts rather than ranks. It tempts because chi-square is the best-known non-parametric test, so a candidate who links 'non-parametric' with 'chi-square' picks it."}},{"q":"Sensitivity of a diagnostic test is defined as:","concept":"Diagnostic accuracy is worked out from a 2 by 2 table that crosses the test result (positive or negative) with true disease status, set by a reference or 'gold' standard. The four cells are true positives (TP, ill and test positive), false negatives (FN, ill but test negative), false positives (FP, healthy but test positive) and true negatives (TN, healthy and test negative). The terms sensitivity and specificity were introduced by the biostatistician Jacob Yerushalmy (1947). Sensitivity is the proportion of truly ill people whom the test catches: TP/(TP+FN), the true positive rate. Specificity is the proportion of truly healthy people whom the test clears: TN/(TN+FP), the true negative rate. Both read down the disease-status columns, so they are properties of the test and do not change with prevalence. The predictive values read across the test-result rows: positive predictive value (PPV) is TP/(TP+FP) and negative predictive value (NPV) is TN/(TN+FN). These answer the clinician's question ('my patient tested positive; how likely is illness?') and do change with prevalence; in a low-prevalence population even a good test has a low PPV. Two memory aids from evidence-based medicine: SnNout (a highly Sensitive test, when Negative, rules the disorder out) and SpPin (a highly Specific test, when Positive, rules it in). Variants also ask for 1 minus specificity, the false positive rate plotted on the x-axis of an ROC curve. A screening instrument should above all be sensitive. Option d is right because sensitivity divides true positives by everyone who actually has the disorder, TP/(TP+FN).","traps":{"a":"TN/(TN+FN) is the negative predictive value (NPV): of all people who test negative, the proportion who are truly free of the disorder. It reads across the 'test negative' row of the 2 by 2 table and depends on prevalence. It does not answer this stem because sensitivity concerns people who have the disorder. It tempts because FN appears in both formulas, and a candidate who remembers only 'false negatives go in sensitivity' grabs it.","b":"TN/(TN+FP) is specificity, the true negative rate: of all people who are truly healthy, the proportion the test correctly calls negative. A highly specific test rarely mislabels healthy people, so a positive result rules the disorder in (SpPin). It tempts because specificity is the mirror twin of sensitivity, and under exam pressure these two formulas are the most commonly swapped pair in the whole topic.","c":"TP/(TP+FP) is the positive predictive value (PPV): of all people who test positive, the proportion who truly have the disorder. It shares the numerator TP with sensitivity but divides by test positives, not by disease positives, so it rises and falls with prevalence. It tempts because a candidate who remembers only that sensitivity starts with true positives cannot separate it from PPV without knowing the denominator."}},{"q":"Cohen's kappa is used to assess:","concept":"Cohen's kappa was introduced by Jacob Cohen (1960) to measure agreement between two raters who sort the same cases into the same set of categories, for example two clinicians assigning diagnoses to the same patients. Raw percentage agreement overstates reliability, because some agreement happens by chance alone: if both raters call most cases 'depressed', they will often agree even when guessing. Kappa removes this: kappa = (Po minus Pe) / (1 minus Pe), where Po is the observed proportion of agreement and Pe the agreement expected by chance from each rater's marginal totals. A kappa of 1 means perfect agreement, 0 means agreement no better than chance, and negative values mean worse than chance. The widely quoted benchmarks of J. Richard Landis and Gary Koch (1977) call 0.41 to 0.60 moderate, 0.61 to 0.80 substantial and above 0.80 almost perfect. Related indices form the variant pool: weighted kappa (Cohen, 1968) for ordered categories, where near misses earn partial credit; Fleiss' kappa (Joseph L. Fleiss, 1971) for more than two raters; and the intraclass correlation coefficient (ICC) for continuous ratings. Kappa is an inter-rater reliability index, so keep it apart from the other reliability coefficients: Cronbach's alpha (Lee J. Cronbach, 1951) for internal consistency, the test-retest correlation for stability over time, and split-half reliability with the Spearman-Brown formula. Kappa was used in the DSM-III field trials to show improved diagnostic reliability. Option a is right because kappa is a chance-corrected index of agreement between raters making categorical judgments.","traps":{"b":"Internal consistency is the extent to which the items of one scale measure the same construct, usually estimated with Cronbach's alpha (Lee J. Cronbach, 1951), KR-20 for items scored right or wrong, or split-half reliability corrected with the Spearman-Brown formula. It concerns items within a test, not raters. It tempts because alpha and kappa are both reliability coefficients, both written as Greek letters, and both have a maximum of 1.","c":"Effect size for ANOVA is expressed as eta-squared (the proportion of total variance explained by the factor), partial eta-squared, omega-squared, or Cohen's f. It tells how large a group difference is, not whether raters agree. It tempts because Jacob Cohen also gave us Cohen's d and Cohen's f, so a candidate who links the name 'Cohen' with effect sizes picks this option.","d":"Convergent validity is evidence that a test correlates highly with other measures of the same construct, formalised alongside discriminant validity in the multitrait-multimethod matrix of Donald T. Campbell and Donald W. Fiske (1959). It is shown with correlation coefficients, not kappa. It tempts because 'agreement between two measures' sounds like 'agreement between two raters', but convergent validity concerns validity, while kappa concerns reliability."}},{"q":"A 95% confidence interval for a mean indicates:","concept":"A confidence interval (CI) estimates a population parameter, such as a mean, with a range instead of a single number. The framework was set out by Jerzy Neyman (1937) within frequentist statistics, where the population mean is a fixed but unknown constant and only the sample, and hence the interval, varies. For a mean, the 95% CI is roughly the sample mean plus or minus 1.96 standard errors, where the standard error is the standard deviation divided by the square root of n. The '95%' is a property of the procedure, not of any one interval. Imagine drawing 100 random samples and building a CI from each: about 95 of those intervals will capture the true mean and about 5 will miss it. Once a particular interval is calculated, the true mean is either inside it or not; frequentist theory gives no probability to a fixed parameter. The tempting phrase 'there is a 95% probability that the mean lies in this interval' belongs to the Bayesian credible interval, which treats the parameter as having a probability distribution. Exam variants also test these points: a wider interval means less precision; a larger n narrows it; raising confidence to 99% widens it (z of 2.58); and a 95% CI for a difference that excludes zero corresponds to p below .05 in a two-tailed test. Do not confuse it with the range covering 95% of individual scores, which uses the standard deviation, not the standard error. Option b is right because it states the long-run, repeated-sampling meaning of a frequentist 95% confidence interval.","traps":{"a":"This is the most common misreading of a confidence interval. Saying the mean has a 95% probability of lying in this particular interval treats the parameter as random, which is the logic of a Bayesian credible interval. In frequentist statistics the mean is fixed and the 95% describes how often the method succeeds. It tempts because it sounds like plain English, and many textbooks loosely phrase it this way.","c":"The range covering about 95% of individual observations is a reference (normal) range: in a normal distribution, the mean plus or minus about 1.96 standard deviations. A confidence interval for the mean uses the standard error, which is much smaller and shrinks as n grows, so it covers far fewer than 95% of individual scores. It tempts because both ranges use 1.96 and the 95% figure.","d":"Significance is a property of a hypothesis test, not of a mean. A 95% CI can be used to judge a test (if the CI for a difference excludes zero, p is below .05 two-tailed), but the interval itself does not give a mean a 'chance of being significant'. It tempts because CIs and p-values are taught together and both revolve around the 95% or .05 figure."}},{"q":"The p-value represents:","concept":"The p-value comes from the significance-testing tradition of Ronald A. Fisher, set out in Statistical Methods for Research Workers (1925). It is a conditional probability: assuming the null hypothesis (H0) is true, how likely is a result at least as extreme as the one observed? Formally, p = P(data this extreme or more | H0). A small p means the data would be surprising if H0 were true, which counts as evidence against H0. It does not give the probability that H0 is true, P(H0 | data), because that is the inverse conditional and needs Bayesian reasoning with prior probabilities. Nor does it give the probability that the alternative hypothesis (H1) is true, the size of the effect, or the chance that the result will replicate. In the decision framework of Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1933), the researcher fixes alpha (usually .05) in advance and rejects H0 when p falls below alpha; alpha is then the long-run Type I error rate, the risk of rejecting a true null. A Type II error (beta) is failing to reject a false null, and power equals 1 minus beta. Because p depends heavily on sample size, a trivial effect can be highly significant in a huge sample, so effect sizes such as Cohen's d and confidence intervals should also be reported. The American Statistical Association statement by Ronald Wasserstein and Nicole Lazar (2016) restated these limits. Option b is right because it gives the textbook definition: the probability of a result this extreme or more, assuming H0 is true.","traps":{"a":"The probability that the null hypothesis is true is P(H0 | data), the reverse of what a p-value computes, P(data | H0). Turning one conditional into the other needs Bayes' theorem and a prior probability for H0, which classical tests never use. It tempts because it is the single most common misinterpretation of p, the inverse probability fallacy, and it sounds like exactly what a researcher wants to know.","c":"The size of an effect is reported by effect-size statistics such as Cohen's d, r, eta-squared or an odds ratio. A p-value mixes effect size with sample size: a tiny effect in a sample of 10,000 can give p below .001, while a large effect in 10 people can miss .05. It tempts because people wrongly read a smaller p as a bigger or more important finding.","d":"The probability that the alternative hypothesis (H1) is true is also a Bayesian posterior probability, and the p-value cannot supply it; 1 minus p is not the probability that H1 is correct. A small p only says that the data fit poorly with H0. It tempts because it is the mirror image of the first option and seems to follow naturally from rejecting the null hypothesis."}},{"q":"One-way ANOVA tests:","concept":"Analysis of variance (ANOVA) was developed by Ronald A. Fisher in the 1920s, and the F statistic was later named in his honour. One-way ANOVA tests whether the means of a continuous dependent variable differ across the levels of one categorical independent variable (one 'factor'), for example anxiety scores in CBT, medication and waiting-list groups. Despite its name it compares means; variance is the tool. Total variability is split into between-group variability (how far group means lie from the grand mean) and within-group variability (the scatter of individuals around their own group mean, treated as error). F = MS between / MS within. If H0 (all population means equal) is true, F is near 1; a large F suggests at least one mean differs. A significant F is an omnibus result: it does not say which groups differ, so post hoc tests such as Tukey's HSD or Scheffe's test, or planned contrasts, follow. With only two groups, one-way ANOVA gives the same p-value as the independent t-test, since F equals t squared; ANOVA is preferred for three or more groups because running many t-tests inflates the Type I error rate. Its assumptions are normality, independence and homogeneity of variance (checked with Levene's test). Know the neighbours: two-way ANOVA has two factors and tests their interaction; repeated-measures ANOVA uses the same participants; ANCOVA adds a covariate; MANOVA has several dependent variables; Kruskal-Wallis is the rank-based alternative. Option a is right because one-way ANOVA tests differences in group means across two or more levels of a single independent variable.","traps":{"b":"Association between two categorical variables, such as gender and diagnosis, is tested with the chi-square test of independence (Karl Pearson, 1900), which compares observed with expected cell frequencies in a contingency table. ANOVA needs a continuous dependent variable whose means can be compared. It tempts because the independent variable in ANOVA is categorical, so a candidate may forget that its outcome must be continuous.","c":"Correlation between two continuous variables, such as hours of sleep and mood score, is measured with Pearson's product-moment r (Karl Pearson) or examined with regression. It shows the strength and direction of a linear relationship, with no groups involved. It tempts because ANOVA and correlation both belong to the general linear model and both talk about explained variance, but one-way ANOVA always compares group means.","d":"Testing whether groups differ only in their spread is the job of Levene's test, Bartlett's test or Hartley's F-max test, which check the homogeneity of variance assumption before ANOVA is run. ANOVA itself uses variances as a means to an end; its hypothesis is about means. It tempts because the name 'analysis of variance' literally suggests that variances themselves are what is being compared."}},{"q":"Bonferroni correction is used to:","concept":"Every significance test run at alpha = .05 carries a 5% risk of a false positive (Type I error) when the null is true. Run many tests on the same data, for example comparing six groups pairwise (15 comparisons) or correlating 20 questionnaire items, and the chance that at least one result is falsely significant climbs quickly. This is the family-wise error rate (FWER): for k independent tests it equals 1 minus (1 minus alpha) to the power k, so 10 tests give about 40%. The Bonferroni correction controls the FWER by dividing alpha by the number of tests: with 10 tests each must reach p below .005 (equivalently, each p is multiplied by k and compared with .05). It rests on an inequality of the Italian mathematician Carlo Emilio Bonferroni and was popularised for multiple comparisons of means by Olive Jean Dunn (1961), hence the name Dunn-Bonferroni. It is simple and valid even when tests are correlated, but conservative: stricter thresholds lower power and raise Type II errors, especially when k is large. Variant questions use the neighbours: the Holm step-down method (Sture Holm, 1979), more powerful while still controlling the FWER; the Benjamini-Hochberg procedure (Yoav Benjamini and Yosef Hochberg, 1995), which controls the false discovery rate instead and is popular in genetics and brain imaging; and ANOVA post hoc tests such as Tukey's HSD and Scheffe's test, which build the correction into pairwise comparisons. Option c is right because Bonferroni is a multiple-comparisons adjustment that keeps the family-wise Type I error rate at or below alpha.","traps":{"a":"Bonferroni does the opposite of increasing power. By shrinking the alpha for each test, it makes significance harder to reach, so power falls and Type II errors (missed true effects) rise. Power is increased by larger samples, larger effects, less noisy measures, a higher alpha or one-tailed tests. It tempts because 'correction' sounds like an improvement in the analysis, and a careless reader equates improvement with power.","b":"Unequal group sizes are handled inside the test itself, for instance through Welch's t-test or the Tukey-Kramer version of Tukey's HSD, or by sample-size planning before the study. Bonferroni divides alpha by the number of tests and ignores group sizes. It tempts because the correction adjusts something using a count, and the candidate guesses that the count is participants rather than comparisons.","d":"Transforming skewed data with a logarithm, square root or reciprocal, or with the Box-Cox family, is done to meet the normality assumption of parametric tests or to stabilise variance. It changes the data, not the significance threshold. It tempts because transformations and corrections both appear in the 'assumptions and adjustments' part of a statistics syllabus, so the two kinds of fix get mixed up."}},{"q":"Number Needed to Treat (NNT) is calculated as:","concept":"The number needed to treat (NNT) was introduced by Andreas Laupacis, David Sackett and Robin Roberts (1988) to turn trial results into a figure clinicians can use: how many patients must receive the treatment, rather than the control, for one extra patient to benefit or avoid a bad outcome. Start with event rates. The control event rate (CER) is the proportion of the control group with the outcome, say relapse in 20% (0.20); the experimental event rate (EER) is the proportion in the treated group, say 15% (0.15). The absolute risk reduction (ARR) is CER minus EER, here 0.05, or 5 percentage points. NNT = 1/ARR = 1/0.05 = 20, usually rounded up to the next whole number. The same data give a relative risk (RR) of EER/CER = 0.75 and a relative risk reduction (RRR) of ARR/CER = 0.25, so the drug 'cuts relapse by 25%'. That relative figure sounds bigger and stays the same whether baseline risk is high or low, while the NNT reflects actual baseline risk; this is why evidence-based medicine prefers absolute measures. A lower NNT means a more effective treatment, and an NNT of 1 would mean every treated patient benefits. Its mirror image is the number needed to harm (NNH), 1 divided by the absolute risk increase for an adverse effect, so benefit and harm can be weighed. Odds ratios need conversion, using a baseline risk, before an NNT can be derived. Option a is right because the NNT is by definition the reciprocal of the absolute risk reduction.","traps":{"b":"The relative risk reduction (RRR) is the absolute risk reduction divided by the control event rate, for example 0.05/0.20 = 0.25. Its reciprocal, here 4, is not the NNT, because the RRR has lost the information about baseline risk. It tempts because the RRR is the figure most often quoted in drug advertisements and abstracts, and the option differs from the correct one by a single word.","c":"Relative risk multiplied by the control event rate simply returns the experimental event rate, since RR = EER/CER. In the example, 0.75 times 0.20 gives 0.15, the proportion relapsing on treatment. That is an event rate, a proportion, not a count of patients needed for one extra success. It tempts because it uses two genuine terms from the same 2 by 2 table and looks like a plausible formula.","d":"Odds ratio minus 1 does not give the NNT. An odds ratio compares the odds of an outcome in two groups and is the usual measure in case-control studies and logistic regression; OR minus 1 roughly expresses the proportional change in odds, which is still a relative measure. It tempts because odds ratios are familiar effect measures, and subtracting 1 looks like the arithmetic used for risk reductions."}},{"q":"Allocation concealment in an RCT refers to:","concept":"In a randomised controlled trial (RCT), randomisation has two steps: generating an unpredictable allocation sequence (for example by computer) and then implementing it so that no one can foresee or alter the next assignment. The second step is allocation concealment. It protects the sequence until the moment a participant is irreversibly enrolled. If the clinician recruiting patients knows that the next envelope says 'active drug', they may, consciously or not, enrol a more promising patient now and delay a sicker one, which creates selection bias and destroys the comparability that randomisation was meant to create. Adequate methods include central telephone or web-based randomisation, pharmacy-controlled allocation, and sequentially numbered, opaque, sealed envelopes. Alternation, date of birth or an open list are inadequate because they are predictable. Kenneth Schulz and colleagues (1995) showed that trials with inadequate or unclear concealment reported exaggerated treatment effects. Keep it separate from blinding (masking), which happens after allocation and hides the assigned arm from participants, care providers, outcome assessors or analysts, to prevent performance and detection bias. Concealment is always possible; blinding is not, for example when psychotherapy is compared with medication. Variant questions use cues such as 'sealed opaque envelopes', 'selection bias' and 'before assignment' for concealment, and 'double-blind', 'placebo' and 'outcome assessor' for blinding. The CONSORT statement asks authors to report the two separately. Option d is right because allocation concealment means that the people enrolling participants cannot know or predict the upcoming assignment.","traps":{"a":"Keeping patients unaware of their assigned arm is participant blinding, often achieved with a matched placebo. It operates after randomisation and guards against performance bias and placebo-driven reporting. Allocation concealment operates before and at the moment of assignment, protecting the sequence from those who enrol patients. It tempts because both involve hiding information about group assignment, and many students wrongly use the two terms interchangeably.","b":"Locking or sealing the analysis dataset is a data-management step near the end of a trial: once the database is locked, no data can be altered, and analysts may also be kept blind to group labels. It protects the integrity of the analysis, not the randomisation sequence. It tempts because 'sealing' echoes the sealed envelopes used for concealment, so the word triggers a false association.","c":"Hiding the identity of outcome assessors is not a standard trial safeguard. What matters for outcome assessment is that assessors are blind to which arm each participant is in, which prevents detection (ascertainment) bias. That is blinding, and it happens after allocation, not concealment of the sequence. It tempts because it sounds like a procedural safeguard and borrows the vocabulary of hiding and concealing."}},{"q":"Intention-to-treat (ITT) analysis in an RCT means:","concept":"Intention-to-treat (ITT) analysis follows the rule 'as randomised, so analysed': every participant is analysed in the group to which they were randomly allocated, whether they took the treatment, stopped it, crossed over to the other arm, or broke protocol. The logic is that randomisation creates groups balanced on known and unknown prognostic factors, and that balance survives only if the groups are kept intact. People who drop out or stop a drug usually differ from those who stay (they may be sicker, have more side effects, or be less motivated), so removing them means the comparison is no longer randomised. ITT therefore estimates the effect of offering or prescribing a treatment in real conditions, an effectiveness question, and in superiority trials it tends to be conservative, pulling estimates towards no difference. Its competitors appear as distractors. Per-protocol analysis includes only participants who adhered to the protocol; it estimates efficacy under ideal adherence but risks selection bias and often overestimates benefit. Completers-only analysis drops everyone who did not finish. As-treated analysis regroups people by the treatment actually received, breaking randomisation. A full ITT analysis needs outcome data on everyone, so missing data must be handled, for example by multiple imputation; older methods such as last observation carried forward are now criticised. 'Modified ITT' excludes some randomised people by a stated rule. The CONSORT statement asks authors to say which analysis was used and how many participants were analysed in each group. Option a is right because ITT keeps every participant in the originally randomised group regardless of later adherence.","traps":{"b":"Excluding everyone who deviated from the protocol gives a per-protocol analysis. It answers an efficacy question (what the treatment does when taken exactly as planned) but discards a non-random subset, so the groups are no longer comparable and benefit is often overestimated. It tempts because excluding deviators sounds like cleaning the data, and per-protocol results are often reported alongside ITT in the same paper.","c":"Including only those who completed the trial is a completers-only analysis, a cruder form of per-protocol analysis. Dropouts are rarely random: patients with side effects or no improvement tend to leave early, so completers flatter the treatment. It tempts because analysing people with full data feels natural and simple, and missing data is a real practical problem in every trial.","d":"Analysing participants by the treatment they actually received is an as-treated analysis. A patient randomised to therapy who never attended would be moved into the control group, which reintroduces confounding because the reasons for switching are linked to prognosis. It tempts because grouping people by what they actually got sounds more accurate, but it throws away the protection that randomisation provides."}},{"q":"Spearman's rho is appropriate when:","concept":"Spearman's rank-order correlation (rho, written rs) was introduced by Charles Spearman (1904), the psychologist better known for the g factor. It is Pearson's correlation computed on ranks instead of raw scores. Each variable is ranked separately, and when there are no ties the formula rho = 1 minus 6Σd² / n(n² minus 1) is used, where d is the difference between a person's two ranks. Because only order matters, rho measures how consistently one variable rises or falls as the other rises, a monotonic relation, whether or not that relation is a straight line. It is chosen when at least one variable is ordinal (class rank, Likert-type ratings, severity grades), when the relation is curved but monotonic, when data are badly skewed, or when outliers would distort Pearson's r. Like r, it runs from minus 1 to plus 1. The right coefficient depends on the level of measurement, a scheme from S. S. Stevens (1946): Pearson's r for two interval or ratio variables in a linear relation with roughly normal data; Kendall's tau (Maurice Kendall, 1938), another rank coefficient, often preferred for small samples with many ties; point-biserial r for one true dichotomy and one continuous variable; phi for two dichotomous variables; and Cramér's V or the contingency coefficient for nominal variables with more categories. Neither r nor rho captures a U-shaped relation, where the variables are related but not monotonically. Option d is right because Spearman's rho is designed for ordinal data and for relationships that are monotonic but not linear.","traps":{"a":"Two continuous variables in a linear relation are the ideal case for Pearson's product-moment correlation r, developed by Karl Pearson, which also assumes roughly bivariate normal data and no extreme outliers. Spearman's rho could still be calculated, but it throws away information by reducing scores to ranks, so Pearson's r is the more appropriate and more powerful choice. It tempts because rho and r are both correlation coefficients running from minus 1 to plus 1.","b":"When both variables are dichotomous (relapsed or not, male or female), the usual index is the phi coefficient, which is Pearson's r computed on two binary variables and is closely linked to the chi-square of a 2 by 2 table. If both dichotomies are artificial cuts of underlying continuous traits, the tetrachoric correlation is used. It tempts because the option's odd wording looks like a special case that a rank method might handle.","c":"Nominal variables, such as religion or diagnostic category, have no inherent order, so they cannot be ranked and rho cannot be computed. Their association is tested with chi-square and summarised with Cramér's V (Harald Cramér, 1946), phi or the contingency coefficient. It tempts because Spearman's rho is called a non-parametric statistic, and candidates wrongly stretch 'non-parametric' to cover every level of measurement below interval."}},{"q":"Stratified random sampling differs from cluster sampling in that:","concept":"Stratified and cluster sampling are both probability methods (every member of the population has a known, non-zero chance of selection), and both begin by dividing the population into groups. They then use the groups in opposite ways. In stratified random sampling, the population is divided into strata on a variable linked to the outcome (gender, age band, residence), and a separate random sample is drawn from every stratum, either in proportion to stratum size (proportionate) or by deliberately oversampling small strata (disproportionate). Strata should be internally homogeneous and different from one another. Because every stratum is represented, precision is usually better than a simple random sample of the same size. In cluster sampling, the population is divided into naturally occurring groups such as villages or schools; a random sample of clusters is chosen, and then everyone in the chosen clusters is studied (one-stage) or a subsample is drawn within them (two-stage or multistage). Clusters should ideally be internally varied, each a mini-population. The motive is cost and feasibility when no list of individuals exists; the price is lower precision, because people within a cluster resemble each other, which inflates the design effect. A quick test: if some groups are left out entirely, it is cluster sampling; if every group contributes, it is stratified. Option a is right because stratified sampling draws a random sample from every stratum, while cluster sampling randomly selects only some clusters and then studies everyone in them.","traps":{"b":"Both methods are probability sampling, because chance decides which stratum members or which clusters are chosen. Non-probability methods are convenience, purposive, snowball and quota sampling. Quota sampling is the real look-alike of stratified sampling: it also fills subgroups in set proportions, but the researcher picks the individuals non-randomly. It tempts because quota sampling makes candidates think stratified sampling can be non-probability.","c":"There is a real difference. Stratified sampling includes every subgroup and gains precision; cluster sampling includes only some groups and gains economy at the cost of precision. Strata are built to be internally homogeneous, clusters are ideally internally heterogeneous. It tempts because both designs start by dividing the population into groups, so on a quick read they look like two names for the same procedure.","d":"Sample size is set by the researcher in both designs, so cluster sampling does not always yield a larger sample. What is true is that cluster samples often need to be larger to reach the same precision, because members of a cluster resemble each other (the design effect). It tempts because candidates picture whole villages or schools being surveyed and assume this must mean more people."}},{"q":"Cronbach's α of 0.40 indicates:","concept":"Cronbach's coefficient alpha, described by Lee J. Cronbach (1951), estimates internal-consistency reliability: how strongly the items of a scale vary together, as if each were a parallel measure of the same construct. It depends on the number of items and the average inter-item correlation, and it generalises the Kuder-Richardson formula 20 (G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson, 1937), which applies to items scored right or wrong. Alpha usually runs from 0 to 1 (it can turn negative if items are miscoded). Commonly quoted rules of thumb label 0.9 and above excellent, 0.8 good, 0.7 acceptable, 0.6 questionable, 0.5 poor and below 0.5 unacceptable. Jum Nunnally (1978) recommended about 0.70 for research purposes and at least 0.90 when important decisions are made about individuals, as in clinical diagnosis. On any of these scales 0.40 is clearly poor: much of the variance in total scores is error, and the items do not hang together. Causes include too few items, items tapping different constructs, or reverse-scored items left unrecoded. Key exam points: alpha rises with the number of items, so a long test can show a high alpha without being unidimensional; a very high alpha (above about 0.95) may signal redundant items; and alpha is reliability, not validity. Reliability limits validity, since a test's correlation with a criterion cannot exceed the square root of its reliability, but high reliability never proves validity. Option a is right because an alpha of 0.40 falls well below every accepted threshold, indicating poor internal consistency.","traps":{"b":"High-stakes clinical use, such as diagnosing a patient or deciding treatment from one score, demands very high reliability, usually 0.90 or more, as Jum Nunnally (1978) recommended. With alpha at 0.40 the standard error of measurement is so large that an individual's score cannot be trusted. It tempts because the clinical wording appeals to clinical candidates, and some confuse alpha with correlation effect sizes, where 0.40 counts as a fair association.","c":"Excellent internal consistency corresponds to an alpha of about 0.90 or above, meaning the items are highly intercorrelated and total scores contain little error. An alpha of 0.40 sits at the opposite end of the scale. It tempts only a candidate who reverses the scale or confuses alpha with a p-value, where smaller numbers are 'better'; for reliability coefficients, higher values mean more consistency.","d":"Construct validity is the degree to which a test measures the theoretical construct it claims to measure, a concept set out by Lee J. Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955). It is shown through convergent and discriminant correlations, factor analysis and theory-based predictions, not through alpha. Low reliability in fact limits validity. It tempts because Cronbach's name is attached to both alpha and construct validity."}},{"q":"Effect size r of −0.30 indicates:","concept":"The Pearson correlation coefficient r is often used as an effect size because it is already standardised. It ranges from minus 1 to plus 1. The sign gives direction: a positive r means the variables rise together, while a negative r means one tends to fall as the other rises (an inverse relationship). The absolute size gives strength. Jacob Cohen (1988), in Statistical Power Analysis for the Behavioral Sciences, proposed conventional benchmarks for r: 0.10 small, 0.30 medium, 0.50 large. The sign does not affect strength, so r = minus 0.30 is exactly as strong as plus 0.30: a medium effect in the negative direction. Squaring r gives the coefficient of determination: 0.30 squared is 0.09, so the two variables share only 9% of their variance. Variant questions swap in Cohen's other benchmarks, so keep them apart: Cohen's d (standardised mean difference) 0.2 small, 0.5 medium, 0.8 large; eta-squared 0.01, 0.06, 0.14; Cohen's f 0.10, 0.25, 0.40. Cohen himself warned that these labels are rough conventions for use when a field has no better guide. Remember also that r measures only linear association: r near 0 can coexist with a strong curved relation, and outliers or a restricted range can distort it. Correlation never establishes causation, because of third variables and the directionality problem. Option b is right because an r of minus 0.30 meets Cohen's benchmark for a medium effect, and the negative sign shows an inverse linear relationship.","traps":{"a":"No relationship corresponds to an r of 0, or values so close to 0 (below about 0.10 in absolute size) that the association is trivial. An r of minus 0.30 is a real, medium-sized association, and in a reasonable sample it would be statistically significant. It tempts because the negative sign makes some candidates think the value is below zero and therefore means nothing, but negative means inverse, not absent.","c":"A perfect inverse relationship is r = minus 1, where every point lies exactly on a straight line sloping downward and one variable can be predicted from the other without error. At minus 0.30 the scatterplot is a loose cloud with a mild downward trend, and only 9% of variance is shared. It tempts because 'inverse' correctly describes the direction, so a candidate who notices the sign but ignores the size is misled.","d":"Causation cannot be inferred from a correlation of any size or sign. A third variable may drive both, or the direction of influence may run the other way. Causal claims need experimental manipulation with random assignment, or strong designs backed by theory. It tempts because a negative correlation is easily narrated as 'more X causes less Y', a classic error that examiners love to test."}},{"q":"The CONSORT statement is a reporting guideline for:","concept":"Reporting guidelines are checklists, usually with a flow diagram, that tell authors what to report about a particular study design so that readers can judge its validity and reproduce it. CONSORT stands for Consolidated Standards of Reporting Trials. It was first published in 1996 by an international group of trialists, methodologists and journal editors, and has been revised since; CONSORT 2010 has a 25-item checklist plus a participant flow diagram that tracks people through enrolment, allocation, follow-up and analysis. It applies to randomised controlled trials (RCTs) and asks for details such as how the random sequence was generated, how allocation was concealed, who was blinded, and whether analysis was by intention to treat. Extensions cover cluster, non-inferiority, pragmatic and non-pharmacological (for example psychotherapy) trials. Variant questions pair each design with its guideline, so learn the set, most of it collected by the EQUATOR Network: PRISMA for systematic reviews and meta-analyses (it replaced the older QUOROM statement); STROBE for observational studies (cohort, case-control, cross-sectional); STARD for diagnostic accuracy studies; SPIRIT for trial protocols; CARE for case reports; COREQ for qualitative interview and focus-group research; and MOOSE for meta-analyses of observational studies. Note the difference between reporting guidelines and quality-appraisal tools such as the Cochrane risk-of-bias tool: CONSORT tells authors what to write, not how to score a trial's quality. Option d is right because CONSORT is the reporting standard designed specifically for randomised controlled trials.","traps":{"a":"Systematic reviews and meta-analyses are reported using PRISMA (Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses), first published in 2009 and updated in 2020, with its own flow diagram of records identified, screened and included. A review may pool many RCTs, but the review itself follows PRISMA, not CONSORT. It tempts because both guidelines use a flow diagram and both belong to evidence-based medicine.","b":"Observational studies, namely cohort, case-control and cross-sectional designs, are reported with STROBE (Strengthening the Reporting of Observational Studies in Epidemiology), published in 2007. These studies do not randomise participants, so checklist items on sequence generation and allocation concealment do not apply. It tempts because observational studies are the commonest designs in psychiatric research, and candidates assume the best-known guideline must cover them.","c":"Diagnostic accuracy studies, which compare an index test with a reference standard to estimate sensitivity and specificity, are reported with STARD (Standards for Reporting of Diagnostic Accuracy Studies), first published in 2003 and updated in 2015. Its flow diagram tracks participants through the index test and the reference standard. It tempts because STARD and CONSORT both contain the words 'standards' and 'reporting' in their full names."}},{"q":"Random assignment in an experiment primarily controls for:","concept":"Random assignment (randomisation) means that chance alone, such as a coin toss or a computer-generated sequence, decides which participant goes into which condition. Ronald A. Fisher championed it for experiments in The Design of Experiments (1935). Its power is that, on average, it balances the groups on every pre-existing characteristic: age, severity, motivation, genes, and variables the researcher never measured or even thought of. Any later difference in outcome can then be attributed to the manipulated independent variable rather than to who ended up in which group. In the threats-to-validity scheme of Donald T. Campbell and Julian Stanley (1963), this removes the 'selection' threat and the confounding it brings, which is why true experiments and RCTs sit high in the evidence hierarchy. Examiners twist two points. First, random assignment is not random sampling: random sampling (choosing who enters the study) supports generalisation to the population, or external validity, while random assignment (allocating those already in the study) supports causal inference, or internal validity. Second, randomisation balances groups only in expectation; small samples can still be unbalanced by chance, which is why stratified or block randomisation is used. Randomisation does not fix other biases: measurement error needs reliable instruments and blinded assessment, recall bias belongs to retrospective designs, attrition calls for intention-to-treat analysis, and publication bias is a problem of the literature. Quasi-experiments lack random assignment and must control confounders statistically. Option c is right because random assignment primarily controls selection bias and confounding by balancing groups on known and unknown variables.","traps":{"a":"Measurement error is the random or systematic gap between an observed score and the true score, the focus of classical test theory. It is reduced by reliable, valid instruments, trained and blinded assessors and standardised procedures. Randomisation spreads such error evenly across groups but does not reduce it. It tempts because both appear as methodological controls in research design chapters, and any listed bias can look plausible.","b":"Recall bias is a systematic difference in how accurately groups remember past exposures, typical of case-control studies: mothers of children with a disorder may search their memories harder than control mothers. It arises from retrospective self-report, so it is reduced by using records or prospective designs. It tempts because it is a well-known named bias, and candidates assume randomisation fixes every kind of bias.","d":"Publication bias is the tendency for studies with significant or positive results to be published more often than null results, which Robert Rosenthal (1979) called the file drawer problem. It distorts the literature and meta-analyses, not the internal validity of a single trial. It is detected with funnel plots and countered by trial registration. It tempts because it is another familiar named bias in evidence-based practice."}},{"q":"A p-value of .049 vs .051 indicates:","concept":"A p-value is a continuous measure of how surprising the data would be if the null hypothesis were true. Ronald A. Fisher (1925) suggested .05 as a convenient reference point for judging whether a result deserved attention, not as a rigid law. The later decision framework of Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1933) fixes alpha in advance and makes a binary choice, reject or do not reject, and journals hardened this into 'significant' versus 'not significant'. Yet .049 and .051 differ by only .002. They reflect almost identical evidence against the null, and would come from nearly identical effect estimates and confidence intervals, one just excluding zero and the other just including it. Calling one a discovery and the other a failure is an artefact of the cut-off. Ralph Rosnow and Robert Rosenthal (1989) put it memorably: 'surely, God loves the .06 nearly as much as the .05.' Andrew Gelman and Hal Stern (2006) added that the difference between 'significant' and 'not significant' is not itself statistically significant. The American Statistical Association statement by Ronald Wasserstein and Nicole Lazar (2016) warned that scientific conclusions should not rest only on whether p passes a threshold. Dichotomising also encourages p-hacking, in which analyses are adjusted until p dips below .05. Good practice is to report the exact p, the effect size and its confidence interval, and to judge clinical importance separately. Option c is right because p-values of .049 and .051 represent essentially the same strength of evidence, and the .05 line is a convention, not a natural boundary.","traps":{"a":"Clinical significance concerns whether an effect is large enough to matter to patients, judged by effect sizes, confidence intervals, the minimal clinically important difference, or the reliable change index of Neil Jacobson and Paula Truax (1991). A p-value says nothing about practical importance; with a huge sample a trivial difference gives a tiny p. It tempts because 'significant' in everyday speech means important.","b":"Treating .049 as a positive finding and .051 as a negative one is the very dichotomisation error the question targets. Under a rigid alpha of .05 they would be labelled differently, but the evidence behind them is nearly identical. It tempts because textbooks and journals have trained students to treat .05 as a cliff edge, so the two numbers seem to fall on opposite sides of a real line.","d":"Replication failure means that a new, independent study does not reproduce an earlier finding, the central issue of the replication crisis highlighted by the Open Science Collaboration (2015). Two p-values sitting on either side of .05 do not by themselves show a failure to replicate; the underlying estimates may be almost identical. It tempts because the replication crisis is often discussed alongside p-value thresholds."}},{"q":"Choose the synonym of 'BENEVOLENT':","concept":"A synonym question asks for the word closest in meaning to the given word. 'Benevolent' comes from the Latin bene, 'well', and volens, 'wishing', from velle, 'to wish'. A benevolent person literally wishes others well: kind, generous, charitable and caring in intention. The usual synonyms are kind, kindly, compassionate, charitable, altruistic, humane and well-meaning. Its direct antonym is 'malevolent', from male, 'badly', plus the same root, meaning wishing harm to others; 'malicious' and 'spiteful' sit close to malevolent. Learning these roots unlocks a family of words that examiners like. Bene gives benefit, benefactor (one who does good for others), beneficent (actively doing good, whereas benevolent means wishing good), benign (gentle, not harmful, as in a benign tumour) and benediction (a blessing). Mal gives malice, malign, malignant, malpractice and malediction (a curse). The vol root gives voluntary, volition and, in psychopathology, avolition, the negative symptom of schizophrenia in which the person lacks the will to begin purposeful activity. For vocabulary items, a reliable method is to define the word in your own words first, then check each option against that definition and its emotional tone. Here the definition is 'kindly wishing good to others', which is warm and positive, so any cold, neutral or harsh word can be ruled out at once. 'Compassionate' (from com, 'with', and pati, 'to suffer') means feeling for another's suffering and wanting to help. Option a is right because 'compassionate' shares benevolent's core sense of warm, caring goodwill towards others.","traps":{"b":"'Malicious' means intending to harm, upset or hurt others, from the Latin malitia, 'badness', from malus, 'bad'. It is the near opposite of benevolent, close in meaning to malevolent and spiteful. It tempts in two ways: a hurried reader mixes up synonym and antonym questions, and examiners deliberately place a strong opposite word to catch candidates who notice only that both words describe intentions.","c":"'Indifferent' means having no particular interest, concern or sympathy: uncaring, unconcerned or neutral. A benevolent person actively wishes others well, so indifference is the absence of the very quality that benevolence names, not a synonym of it. It tempts because it is not obviously negative like 'malicious', so a candidate unsure of the meaning of benevolent may choose the milder-sounding option.","d":"'Severe' means strict, harsh or stern in manner, or very serious and intense, as in a severe illness or severe depression. It carries a cold, punitive tone, the opposite of the warm goodwill in benevolent. It tempts candidates who link benevolent with authority figures, as in the phrase 'benevolent dictator', and so think of firmness, but the word itself means kindly."}},{"q":"Choose the antonym of 'ELOQUENT':","concept":"An antonym question asks for the word most nearly opposite in meaning. 'Eloquent' comes from the Latin eloqui, 'to speak out', from e (ex), 'out', and loqui, 'to speak'. An eloquent speaker or writer expresses ideas fluently, forcefully and persuasively; the word stresses both fluency and skill, and it can describe things that 'speak' without words, as in 'an eloquent silence'. Its synonyms are articulate, fluent, expressive, persuasive and silver-tongued. Its true antonym is 'inarticulate': unable to express ideas clearly or fluently, from in, 'not', plus articulatus, 'divided into distinct parts', from articulus, 'small joint'. Articulate speech is speech broken into clear, distinct units. The loqu root is worth learning as a family: loquacious (talkative), soliloquy (speaking alone, as on stage), circumlocution (talking around a point), colloquial (conversational), ventriloquist and obloquy (strong public criticism). Note the trap: 'loquacious' is about the quantity of speech, while 'eloquent' is about its quality; the antonym of loquacious is 'taciturn' or 'reticent', meaning saying little. In clinical work these distinctions matter when describing speech in a mental status examination, such as pressured speech in mania or poverty of speech in depression and schizophrenia. A good method for antonym items is to eliminate the synonyms first, because examiners nearly always include two or three. Here 'articulate' and 'persuasive' are synonyms, and 'loquacious' shares the root but not the meaning. Option d is right because 'inarticulate', unable to express oneself clearly, is the direct opposite of eloquent.","traps":{"a":"'Articulate' means able to express thoughts clearly and effectively in words, as in 'an articulate witness'. It is a near synonym of eloquent, and the correct answer is simply this word with the negative prefix in. It tempts because it looks almost identical to the right answer, so a candidate reading quickly, or confusing antonym with synonym, picks it.","b":"'Persuasive' means good at convincing others to believe or do something, and persuasiveness is part of what makes speech eloquent. So it is a synonym or close associate, not an opposite. It tempts because it is a plausible, sophisticated word, and a candidate who only half remembers the meaning of 'eloquent' senses a connection without checking whether the relation is sameness or opposition.","c":"'Loquacious' means very talkative, from the same Latin root loqui, 'to speak'. It describes the amount of speech, not its quality: a loquacious person can ramble on without being eloquent at all. Its own antonyms are 'taciturn' and 'reticent'. It tempts because it shares the root and the topic of speech, and its difficulty makes it look like a deliberate pick."}},{"q":"Choose the correctly punctuated sentence:","concept":"This item tests the punctuation of relative clauses, which begin with who, which, that, whose or whom. A restrictive (defining) clause identifies which person or thing is meant and is essential to the meaning: 'The patient who was anxious refused medication' implies several patients, of whom the anxious one refused. It takes no commas. A non-restrictive (non-defining) clause adds extra information about a noun already identified; remove it and the main sentence still makes complete sense. It must be enclosed in a pair of commas, like brackets: 'The patient, who was anxious, refused medication' means there is one patient, who happened to be anxious, and that patient refused. Non-restrictive clauses use who or which, never that. Three rules follow, and each wrong option here breaks one. First, commas around a mid-sentence non-restrictive clause come in pairs; opening with one and not closing it is an error. Second, a single comma must never separate a subject from its verb: 'The patient who was anxious, refused' splits the subject from 'refused'. Third, a semicolon joins two independent clauses, each able to stand as a sentence, so it cannot close a dependent relative clause. Similar items test 'which' versus 'that' and the comma splice. A useful check is to read the sentence without the clause: if 'The patient refused medication' still works and the clause is extra detail, use paired commas. Option d is right because it encloses the non-restrictive clause 'who was anxious' in a matching pair of commas, with no lone comma between subject and verb.","traps":{"a":"This sentence opens the non-restrictive clause with a comma but closes it with a semicolon. A semicolon links two independent clauses, each able to stand alone, or separates items in a complex list. 'The patient, who was anxious' is not a complete sentence, so the semicolon is wrong. It tempts because it at least marks both ends of the clause, and candidates unsure of the semicolon's job treat it as a stronger comma.","b":"Here the comma before 'who' opens a non-restrictive clause, but no comma closes it after 'anxious'. Commas around a mid-sentence parenthetical element must come in pairs, just like brackets; with only one, the reader cannot tell where the extra information ends. It tempts because it looks almost identical to the correct answer, and a missing second comma is one of the commonest real writing errors.","c":"Without a comma before 'who', the clause reads as restrictive, which is acceptable on its own. The error is the lone comma after 'anxious', which separates the subject 'The patient who was anxious' from its verb 'refused'. A single comma must not come between a subject and its verb. It tempts because a speaker naturally pauses after a long subject, and many writers wrongly place a comma wherever they would pause."}},{"q":"Choose the correct preposition: 'The therapist insisted ___ a follow-up after two weeks.'","concept":"Many English verbs combine with a fixed preposition, called a dependent preposition; the pairing is a matter of idiom and has to be learned, not worked out from logic. 'Insist' means to demand something forcefully or to keep stating something firmly. When it is followed by a noun or a gerund, it takes 'on' (or the more formal 'upon'): 'The therapist insisted on a follow-up', 'She insisted on paying', 'He insisted upon strict confidentiality'. When it is followed by a clause, it takes 'that', often with the subjunctive in formal usage: 'The therapist insisted that the patient return after two weeks'. 'Insist for', 'insist with' and 'insist to' are not standard ways of naming the thing demanded. A related error is 'request for': the verb takes a direct object (request a meeting), while the noun takes 'for' (a request for leave). Verbs that also take 'on' or 'upon' are worth grouping: depend on, rely on, count on, focus on, concentrate on, comment on, agree on (a decision). Other pairs that examiners test: persist in, persevere in, succeed in, consist of, accuse of, approve of, comply with, cope with, adhere to, object to, refer to, abstain from, refrain from and differ from. Another common error type is the unnecessary preposition, as in 'discuss about', 'emphasise on' or 'reach to'. Option c is right because 'insist' takes the dependent preposition 'on' before a noun phrase, so 'insisted on a follow-up' is the standard idiom.","traps":{"a":"'For' is the preposition of verbs such as ask for, wait for, apply for, hope for and long for. 'Insist for' is non-standard English and is marked wrong in exams. It tempts because demanding something feels close to asking for it, so the preposition of 'ask for' gets borrowed. A related error is 'request for': the verb request takes a direct object, though the noun takes 'for'.","b":"'With' pairs with verbs such as agree with, comply with, cope with, deal with and plead with. 'Insist with' is not idiomatic English for demanding something, and 'insisted with a follow-up' would not be understood as a demand. It tempts because 'with' feels natural when another person is involved, and a candidate may picture the therapist pressing the point with the patient.","d":"'To' pairs with verbs such as object to, adhere to, refer to and consent to. With 'insist', 'to' can only introduce the listener before a that-clause ('he insisted to the doctor that he was fine'); it cannot introduce the thing demanded, so 'insisted to a follow-up' is wrong. It tempts because 'to' is the commonest preposition, and learners often default to it."}},{"q":"Choose the correctly formed conditional: 'If I ___ his number, I would have called him.'","concept":"Conditional sentences join an if-clause (the condition) to a main clause (the result), and English uses four core patterns plus a mixed type. The zero conditional states general truths: 'If you heat ice, it melts' (present + present). The first conditional covers real, likely futures: 'If it rains, I will stay home' (present + will). The second conditional covers unreal or imagined present situations: 'If I knew his number, I would call him' (simple past + would + base verb); here the past form signals unreality, not past time. The third conditional covers unreal past situations, things that did not happen and can no longer change: 'If I had known his number, I would have called him' (had + past participle, then would have + past participle). It means I did not know the number, so I did not call. The mixed conditional joins a past condition to a present result: 'If I had taken the medicine, I would feel better now.' A formal variant drops 'if' and inverts: 'Had I known his number, I would have called him.' Two rules decide most exam items. First, match the if-clause to the result clause: 'would have + past participle' in the result demands the past perfect in the condition. Second, 'would' belongs in the result clause, not the if-clause, except in polite requests such as 'If you would wait here'. Option d is right because the result clause 'I would have called him' is third conditional, so the if-clause needs the past perfect 'had known'.","traps":{"a":"'Have known' is the present perfect, which links a past event to the present ('I have known him for years'). It never forms the if-clause of an unreal past conditional, because it lacks the remote, contrary-to-fact signal that the past perfect carries. It tempts because 'have known' looks like a shortened 'had known' and echoes the 'have' in 'would have called'.","b":"'Knew' is the simple past, used in the second conditional for an unreal present: 'If I knew his number, I would call him' (I do not know it now). It pairs with 'would + base verb', not 'would have + past participle'. It tempts because the call belongs to the past, but in conditionals the simple past signals unreality, not past time.","c":"'Would have known' puts 'would' inside the if-clause, the classic double-would error ('If I would have known, I would have called'). It is common in casual speech, but standard written grammar, the kind tested here, keeps 'would' in the result clause only. It tempts because it mirrors the main clause exactly and sounds natural when spoken."}},{"q":"Identify the part of speech of 'inconclusive' in: 'The evidence was inconclusive.'","concept":"Parts of speech classify a word by the job it does in a sentence, not by its look alone. Traditional grammar names eight: noun (names a person, place, thing or idea), pronoun (stands in for a noun), verb (an action or state), adjective (describes a noun), adverb (modifies a verb, adjective or another adverb), preposition (shows a relation, such as 'on' or 'in'), conjunction (joins words or clauses) and interjection. Adjectives work in two positions. In attributive position they sit before the noun: 'the inconclusive evidence'. In predicative position they follow a linking verb such as be, seem, become, appear or feel and describe the subject: 'The evidence was inconclusive.' An adjective in this slot is called a predicate adjective or subject complement. Suffixes give quick clues. Adjectives often end in -ive, -ous, -able, -al, -ful or -less (decisive, anxious, reliable). Adverbs often end in -ly, nouns in -ness, -ion or -ity, and verbs in -ize, -ify or -ate. Build the word family to check: conclude (verb), conclusion (noun), conclusive and inconclusive (adjectives), inconclusively (adverb), inconclusiveness (noun). Two simple tests confirm an adjective: it fits between 'the' and a noun, and it can take 'very' or 'more'. The negative prefix 'in-' changes the meaning, not the part of speech. Psychology writing uses this word constantly, as in 'the findings were inconclusive'. Option b is right because 'inconclusive' follows the linking verb 'was' and describes the noun 'evidence', so it is a predicate adjective.","traps":{"a":"A verb expresses an action or a state of being and carries tense. The only verb in this sentence is 'was', the past tense of 'be', working as a linking verb. The verb in this word family is 'conclude' ('The panel concluded that the drug was safe'). It tempts because 'inconclusive' is built from a verb, so its root looks like an action word.","c":"An adverb modifies a verb, an adjective or another adverb and answers how, when, where or to what degree. The adverb form here is 'inconclusively' ('The trial ended inconclusively'). Linking verbs such as 'was' take adjectives, not adverbs, so 'The evidence was inconclusively' is ungrammatical. It tempts because the word sits right after a verb, the usual place for adverbs.","d":"A noun names a person, place, thing or idea. The noun in this sentence is 'evidence', the subject. The noun forms in this family are 'conclusion' and 'inconclusiveness'. It tempts because the slot after 'was' can also hold a noun ('The evidence was a letter'), but here the word describes the subject instead of renaming it."}},{"q":"Choose the sentence with correct subject–verb agreement:","concept":"Subject-verb agreement means a verb must match its subject in number: a singular subject takes a singular verb ('the patient was') and a plural subject takes a plural verb ('the patients were'). Trouble starts with compound subjects. Subjects joined by 'and' are normally plural: 'The therapist and the patients were satisfied.' Correlative pairs that offer alternatives, 'either...or', 'neither...nor' and 'not only...but also', follow the proximity rule: the verb agrees with the subject nearer to it. So 'Neither the patients nor the therapist was satisfied', but 'Neither the therapist nor the patients were satisfied.' Style guides advise putting the plural subject second because the plural verb then sounds natural. A different rule governs phrases such as 'as well as', 'along with', 'together with' and 'in addition to': they do not create a compound subject, so the verb agrees with the first noun ('The therapist, along with the patients, was satisfied'). Collective nouns such as team or committee usually take a singular verb in American usage and may take a plural one in British usage. 'Neither' used alone as a pronoun is formally singular ('Neither of them was ready'), although informal speech often uses a plural verb. To solve an item, find the subject nearest the verb, check its number, and then confirm the tense fits any context. In this item only one option pairs the plural nearer subject with a plural verb. Option c is right because 'were' is plural and agrees with the nearer subject 'patients', as the proximity rule requires.","traps":{"a":"'Was' is singular, so it agrees with the far subject 'therapist' instead of the nearer plural 'patients'. Under the proximity rule for 'neither...nor', the verb looks at the closest subject. The sentence would be correct only if the order were reversed: 'Neither the patients nor the therapist was satisfied.' It tempts because 'neither' feels singular and the sentence opens with a singular noun.","b":"'Is' is the singular present form, so it agrees with the far subject 'therapist' rather than the nearer plural 'patients'. Under the proximity rule the verb must look at the closest subject, which needs 'are' in the present or 'were' in the past. It tempts because 'neither' feels singular and the sentence opens with a singular noun, so a singular verb sounds right on a quick read.","d":"'Has been' is the singular present perfect (the plural is 'have been'), so it agrees with the far subject 'therapist', not the nearer plural 'patients'. Changing the tense does not rescue it, because number is still wrong. It tempts because the present perfect sounds formal and test-like, and because 'neither' is often wrongly treated as always taking a singular verb."}},{"q":"Choose the correctly used homophone: 'Her advice had a strong ___ on his recovery.'","concept":"Homophones are words that sound the same, or nearly the same, but differ in spelling and meaning. 'Affect' and 'effect' are the most tested pair. In everyday use, 'affect' is a verb meaning to influence ('Stress affects sleep'), and 'effect' is a noun meaning a result or outcome ('Stress has an effect on sleep'). A common memory aid is RAVEN: Remember, Affect is a Verb, Effect is a Noun. The quickest grammatical test is the slot: after an article (a, an, the) or an adjective ('a strong', 'a lasting', 'the side'), you need a noun, so 'effect'. Fixed collocations also help: 'have an effect on', 'take effect', 'side effects', 'in effect'. Psychology students must know two exceptions. First, 'affect' is a noun in psychology and psychiatry, stressed on the first syllable, meaning the observable expression of emotion: flat, blunted, labile and inappropriate affect are recorded in the mental status examination. Second, 'effect' can be a verb meaning to bring about: 'The new policy effected change' means it caused change, while 'affected change' means it influenced a change already under way. Related forms follow the same split: 'effective' and 'effectively' come from the result noun, while 'affective' (as in affective disorders) comes from the emotion noun. Statistical 'effect size' also uses the noun. Option b is right because the slot follows 'a strong', which needs a singular noun meaning result, and that noun is 'effect'.","traps":{"a":"'Affect' is normally a verb meaning to influence ('Her advice affected his recovery'). As a noun it means displayed emotion in clinical usage (flat affect), which makes no sense here, since advice cannot have a strong emotional expression on recovery. It tempts because the sentence is about influence, exactly what the verb 'affect' means, so learners pick the meaning and ignore the grammar.","c":"'Effects' is the right word in the wrong number. The article 'a' requires a singular noun, so 'a strong effects' is ungrammatical. The plural would need to drop the article: 'Her advice had strong effects on his recovery.' It tempts because the candidate correctly rejects 'affect', spots the noun 'effect', and stops checking agreement with the article.","d":"'Affected' is the past tense or past participle of the verb 'affect' ('His recovery was affected by her advice'). A verb form cannot follow 'a strong', which needs a noun. As an adjective it means artificial or put on ('an affected accent'), which also does not fit. It tempts because it carries the influence meaning and looks like a past-tense match for 'had'."}},{"q":"Which sentence uses the apostrophe correctly?","concept":"The apostrophe has two jobs in English. It shows possession in nouns ('the patient's file', 'the patients' files') and it marks missing letters in contractions ('don't' for 'do not'). Personal pronouns are the exception: their possessive forms are already possessive, so they never take an apostrophe. The full set is my, your, his, her, its, our and their, the independent forms mine, yours, hers, ours and theirs, plus the relative 'whose'. That is why 'its' (belonging to it) has no apostrophe, just as 'his' and 'hers' do not. The form 'it's' is always a contraction, of 'it is' ('It's raining') or 'it has' ('It's been a long day'). The reliable test is to expand the word. If 'it is' or 'it has' makes sense, write 'it's'; if not, write 'its'. Here 'It is coat is wet' is nonsense, so the possessive 'its' is needed. The same trap repeats in other pairs: whose and who's (who is), your and you're (you are), their, they're (they are) and there. Two further points help with variants. 'Its'' with a trailing apostrophe is not a word in any context. And the other possessive pronouns never take apostrophes either, so 'her's', 'our's' and 'your's' are always wrong. Indefinite pronouns are different and do take one: 'someone's bag', 'everybody's turn'. Option d is right because the sentence means the coat belonging to it, which needs the possessive pronoun 'its' with no apostrophe.","traps":{"a":"'It's' is the contraction of 'it is' or 'it has'. Expanding it gives 'It is coat is wet', which is nonsense, so it cannot show possession. It is the most common apostrophe error in English, because nouns show possession with apostrophe-s ('the dog's coat'), and writers wrongly extend that noun rule to the pronoun 'it'.","b":"'Its's' is not an English word. It adds a possessive apostrophe-s to 'its', which is already possessive, so possession would be marked twice. No pronoun takes this double marking ('his's' and 'hers's' are equally wrong). It tempts candidates who half-remember that possession needs apostrophe-s and try to merge the two forms they have seen.","c":"'Its'' with a trailing apostrophe imitates the plural possessive of nouns ('the nurses' station'), where the apostrophe follows an s that marks a plural. 'Its' is not a plural noun, and possessive pronouns never take an apostrophe in any position. It tempts because it looks like a compromise between 'its' and 'it's' and copies a familiar noun pattern."}},{"q":"Choose the word closest in meaning to 'LACONIC':","concept":"A synonym question asks for the word closest in meaning, not merely a related one. 'Laconic' means using very few words; a laconic reply is brief and to the point, sometimes to the edge of bluntness. The word comes from Laconia, the region of ancient Greece whose capital was Sparta. Spartans were famous for terse speech. In a well-known anecdote, Philip II of Macedon warned them that if he invaded Laconia he would destroy them, and they answered with the single word 'If'. The synonym cluster is concise, terse, succinct, pithy, brief and curt; note that 'curt' adds rudeness, while 'concise' and 'succinct' praise economy. The antonym cluster is verbose, wordy, prolix, loquacious and garrulous. Close confusables are worth separating. 'Reticent' means reluctant to reveal thoughts or feelings, so a reticent person may say little about one topic, while a laconic person uses few words in general. 'Taciturn' means habitually silent, describing a temperament more than a style of speech. 'Eloquent' means fluent and persuasive, which concerns skill, not length. Psychiatric description uses a related idea: markedly reduced speech output in schizophrenia is called poverty of speech or alogia, a negative symptom, which is a clinical sign rather than a personal style. A useful method for synonym items is to rule out the antonym first, then remove words from a different meaning area. Option d is right because 'concise' means expressing much in few words, which is exactly the core meaning of 'laconic'.","traps":{"a":"'Verbose' means using more words than needed: wordy and long-winded. It is the direct antonym of 'laconic', and setters plant antonyms in synonym questions because a candidate who knows the word concerns speech, but not its direction, will grab it. Related antonyms are prolix, loquacious and garrulous. A verbose report pads; a laconic one cuts.","b":"'Reluctant' means unwilling or hesitant to do something ('reluctant to start medication'). It describes attitude toward an action, not the amount of speech. It tempts because it sounds like 'reticent', which does mean reserved and unwilling to speak about something, and a laconic person can seem unwilling to talk. Laconic is about brevity, not unwillingness.","c":"'Eloquent' means fluent, expressive and persuasive in speech or writing. It concerns skill and power with words, not quantity; an eloquent speaker may talk at great length. It tempts because both words describe a manner of speaking, and a skilled short reply can be both laconic and eloquent, but the two words do not mean the same thing."}},{"q":"Identify the correctly worded comparative: 'She is ___ than her sister.'","concept":"Adjectives have three degrees. The positive degree describes one thing ('tall'), the comparative compares two ('taller'), and the superlative picks one out of three or more ('the tallest'). The word 'than' is the signal for a comparative, while 'the' with 'of' or 'in' signals a superlative ('the tallest in the class'). How the forms are built depends mainly on length. One-syllable adjectives add -er and -est: tall, taller, tallest; fast, faster, fastest. A short vowel before a single final consonant doubles it: big, bigger, biggest. Two-syllable adjectives ending in -y change y to i: happy, happier, happiest. Most other two-syllable adjectives and all longer ones use 'more' and 'most': more anxious, most reliable. A few two-syllable words accept both ('cleverer' or 'more clever'). Irregular forms must be memorised: good, better, best; bad, worse, worst; far, farther or further, farthest or furthest; little, less, least; many and much, more, most. The most tested error is the double comparative, using both 'more' and -er ('more taller', 'more better'), and its partner the double superlative ('most tallest'). Each form already marks degree once, so a second marker is redundant. Another tested error is using a superlative for two people ('the tallest of the two sisters'), which formal grammar marks wrong although many speakers say it. Option d is right because 'than her sister' compares two people, and the comparative of the one-syllable adjective 'tall' is 'taller'.","traps":{"a":"'More taller' is a double comparative: the -er suffix already makes 'tall' comparative, so adding 'more' marks the degree twice. Use either -er or 'more', never both. It is a frequent error in learner writing and casual speech ('more better', 'more easier'). It tempts because it sounds emphatic, as if the extra word adds force to the comparison.","b":"'Tallest' is the superlative, used to single out one member of a group of three or more, usually with 'the': 'She is the tallest of the three sisters.' It cannot pair with 'than', which signals a two-way comparison. It tempts because it belongs to the correct word family and sounds strong, so a hurried reader picks the most emphatic form.","c":"'More tall' applies the 'more + adjective' pattern meant for longer adjectives (more anxious, more beautiful) to a one-syllable word, which normally takes -er. It is acceptable only when comparing two qualities of one thing ('the box is more tall than wide'), not two people. It tempts because candidates who know that 'more' forms comparatives overgeneralise the rule."}},{"q":"Choose the antonym of 'BENIGN':","concept":"An antonym is a word of opposite meaning. 'Benign' comes from Latin 'benignus', kind, built on 'bene', well. In everyday use it means gentle, kindly or harmless ('a benign smile', 'a benign climate'). In medicine a benign tumour is non-cancerous: it grows locally, does not invade surrounding tissue and does not spread. 'Malignant' comes from 'malus', bad, and means harmful or evil in intent; a malignant tumour is cancerous, invades nearby tissue and can metastasise to distant sites. The bene- and mal- roots build many antonym pairs that exams like: benevolent and malevolent, beneficent and maleficent, benediction and malediction, benefactor and malefactor. Both words also appear in clinical psychology and psychiatry. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome is a rare, life-threatening reaction to antipsychotic drugs, marked by high fever, muscle rigidity, altered consciousness and autonomic instability. Erich Fromm (1964) coined 'malignant narcissism', later developed by Otto Kernberg, for a severe form combining narcissism with antisocial traits, paranoid features and sadistic aggression. 'Benign' appears in medical terms such as benign paroxysmal positional vertigo, a common cause of dizziness. When an antonym item offers three near-synonyms of the stem word and one opposite, the near-synonyms are the trap. Check each option by asking whether it could replace the stem word in a sentence. If it could, it is a synonym, not an antonym. Option d is right because 'malignant', meaning harmful or cancerous, is the direct opposite of 'benign', meaning harmless or non-cancerous.","traps":{"a":"'Harmless' is a synonym of 'benign', not its opposite: a benign growth is harmless in that it does not invade or spread. It tempts candidates who read quickly, see a matching word, and forget that the question asks for the antonym. The substitution test settles it: 'a harmless tumour' means much the same as 'a benign tumour', so it cannot be the opposite.","b":"'Tranquil' means calm, peaceful and free from disturbance ('a tranquil lake'). It overlaps with the gentle, mild sense of 'benign' instead of opposing it. Related words are serene and placid; its antonyms would be agitated or turbulent. It tempts because it is a soft, positive word, and candidates may sense that it differs from 'benign' without checking for true opposition.","c":"'Cordial' means warm, friendly and polite ('a cordial welcome'). Like 'benign' in its everyday sense, it describes a kindly manner, so it is a near-synonym, not an antonym. Its opposites would be hostile or unfriendly. It tempts because it is less familiar than 'harmless', and candidates unsure of its meaning may guess that it is the odd one out."}},{"q":"Identify the analogy: 'Psychologist : Counselling :: Surgeon : ___'","concept":"An analogy item gives one pair of words and asks you to complete a second pair that shares the same relationship. The best method is to build a precise bridge sentence for the first pair and then test it on each option. Here the bridge is 'A psychologist's characteristic professional activity is counselling.' Applied to the second pair, 'A surgeon's characteristic professional activity is ___' gives 'operation'. Examiners build variants from a fixed set of relationships, and each distractor here stands for one of them: worker to action (psychologist : counselling, surgeon : operation); worker to tool (surgeon : scalpel, carpenter : saw); worker to workplace (surgeon : hospital, teacher : school); worker to client or recipient (surgeon : patient, lawyer : client); and worker to product (author : book). Other common relations are part to whole, cause to effect, degree (warm : hot), synonym and antonym. Analogies matter in psychology too. Charles Spearman (1923) described intelligence in terms of the 'eduction of relations and correlates', which are exactly the two steps of an analogy: find the relation in the first pair, then find the term that completes it in the second. Robert J. Sternberg (1977) broke analogical reasoning into components including encoding, inference, mapping and application. Analogy items therefore appear in many verbal reasoning and intelligence tests. If two options seem to fit, make the bridge sentence more specific until only one survives. Option a is right because a surgeon's characteristic professional action is an operation, just as a psychologist's is counselling.","traps":{"b":"'Hospital' is where a surgeon works, so it gives a worker to workplace relation (like teacher : school or judge : court). The first pair is worker to action, because counselling is something a psychologist does, not a place. It tempts because 'surgeon' and 'hospital' are strongly associated, and candidates who think in loose associations instead of exact relations pick it.","c":"'Scalpel' is the surgeon's cutting instrument, so it gives a worker to tool relation (like painter : brush or writer : pen). Counselling is an activity, not an instrument, so the relationship does not match. It tempts because the scalpel is the most vivid image linked to surgery, and both counselling and a scalpel can be loosely seen as the means of helping.","d":"'Patient' is the person who receives the surgeon's work, a worker to recipient relation (like lawyer : client or teacher : student). The matching first pair would have been 'psychologist : client'. It tempts because psychologists and surgeons both help patients, and candidates focus on who is helped instead of what the professional characteristically does."}},{"q":"Choose the sentence with the correctly placed modifier:","concept":"A modifier is a word or phrase that describes another word, and it must sit next to what it describes. A participial phrase is a modifier built on a participle, such as 'Walking down the corridor' or 'Tired after the session'. When it opens a sentence, the rule is strict: it attaches to the subject of the main clause that follows the comma. So that subject must be the person or thing doing the action. 'Walking down the corridor, I noticed the painting' works because 'I' was walking. Three related errors appear in exams. A dangling modifier has no proper word to attach to, so it latches onto the wrong subject: 'Walking down the corridor, the painting caught my eye' says the painting was walking. A misplaced modifier has its target in the sentence but sits next to the wrong word: 'She served sandwiches to the children on paper plates.' A squinting modifier sits between two elements and could modify either: 'Patients who exercise often sleep better' (exercise often, or often sleep better?). There are two standard fixes for a dangling modifier. Make the doer the subject of the main clause ('Walking down the corridor, I noticed the painting'), or turn the phrase into a full clause with its own subject ('As I walked down the corridor, the painting caught my eye'). Always check that the result is logical and natural, not just parseable. Option a is right because the opening phrase is followed directly by 'I', the person who was actually walking, so the modifier attaches correctly.","traps":{"b":"This is the classic dangling modifier. The phrase 'Walking down the corridor' attaches to the subject after the comma, which is 'the painting', so the sentence literally says the painting walked. The real walker appears only as 'my' inside the object. It tempts because it sounds natural in speech and the intended meaning is obvious, so readers repair it without noticing.","c":"This sentence is grammatically well formed, with a clear subject and two verbs, but it is absurd: paintings do not walk. It does not fix the modifier problem; it simply states the dangling reading as fact and changes the meaning. It tempts candidates who check only grammar and not sense, since nothing in its structure is technically broken.","d":"Here the phrase 'walking down the corridor' is jammed between the verb 'noticed' and its object 'the painting'. That splits a verb from its object, which English avoids, and creates ambiguity: it could mean I noticed someone walking, or that the painting was walking. It is a misplaced modifier. It tempts because it keeps 'I' as subject, which looks like the right fix."}},{"q":"Choose the closest synonym for 'EPHEMERAL':","concept":"'Ephemeral' means lasting a very short time. It comes from the Greek 'ephemeros', literally 'lasting only a day', from 'epi' (on) and 'hemera' (day). Biology uses the root in Ephemeroptera, the order of mayflies, whose adults often live only a day or so. In ordinary English, fame, fashions, moods and online trends can all be called ephemeral. The synonym cluster is transient, transitory, fleeting, momentary, short-lived, evanescent and passing. The antonym cluster is eternal, everlasting, permanent, enduring, perennial and abiding. Psychology uses the idea constantly. Sensory memory is ephemeral: in the partial-report experiments of George Sperling (1960), iconic memory faded within about a second. Clinical writing separates transient features from enduring ones; for example, the criteria for borderline personality disorder include transient, stress-related paranoid ideation, and a transient ischaemic attack causes brief neurological symptoms that resolve. Personality research distinguishes states, which are passing conditions, from traits, which are stable dispositions, as in the State-Trait Anxiety Inventory. Two exam tips help. First, separate time words from substance words: 'ephemeral' answers 'how long?', while 'tangible' answers 'can it be touched?' and 'genuine' answers 'is it real?'. Second, watch for the antonym planted among the options, here 'eternal', which tempts anyone who remembers that the word is about time but forgets the direction. Also do not confuse 'ephemeral' with 'ethereal', which means light, airy or heavenly. Option d is right because 'transient' means passing quickly and lasting only briefly, which matches 'ephemeral' almost exactly.","traps":{"a":"'Eternal' means lasting forever, without beginning or end. It is the direct antonym of 'ephemeral', placed to catch candidates who know the word concerns time but not which end of the scale. Related antonyms are permanent, everlasting and perennial. A quick check: an ephemeral feeling passes in moments, while an eternal one never passes. It tempts precisely because it belongs to the same meaning area.","b":"'Genuine' means real, authentic or sincere ('a genuine apology', 'a genuine signature'). It answers the question 'is it real?', not 'how long does it last?', so it shares no meaning with 'ephemeral'. Its opposite would be fake or counterfeit. It tempts only candidates who do not know the stem word and fall back on any positive-sounding option.","c":"'Tangible' means able to be touched, or concrete enough to be measured ('tangible benefits', 'tangible evidence'). It concerns substance, not duration, and its opposite is intangible or abstract. It tempts candidates who confuse 'ephemeral' with 'ethereal' (light, airy, otherworldly), because the airy-versus-solid contrast makes 'tangible' feel related, though it is a synonym of neither word."}},{"q":"Read the passage and answer: 'Although the trial reported a statistically significant reduction in symptoms, the effect size was small and the dropout rate was 35%. Replication has been inconsistent.' The author's tone is best described as:","concept":"Tone is the writer's attitude toward the subject, inferred from what the writer chooses to stress and from signal words. Mood, by contrast, is the feeling a text creates in the reader. Tone options in exams range from strongly positive (enthusiastic, laudatory) through neutral (objective, indifferent) to strongly negative (dismissive, scornful), with qualified tones such as cautious, guarded or sceptical in between. Here the opening word 'Although' signals a concession: the writer grants a positive point but will qualify it. Three caveats follow, each a standard weakness in research appraisal. A statistically significant result shows only that the finding is unlikely to be due to chance; it says nothing about size. Jacob Cohen (1988) proposed conventions for effect size (d of 0.2 small, 0.5 medium, 0.8 large), and clinical significance asks whether patients change meaningfully, as in the reliable change index of Neil Jacobson and Paula Truax (1991). A 35% dropout rate risks attrition bias, since completers may differ from those who left; intention-to-treat analysis partly guards against this. Inconsistent replication matters because the Open Science Collaboration (2015) repeated 100 psychology studies and obtained significant results in only about 36% of them. The writer neither rejects the finding nor praises it; the stance is qualified doubt. In tone questions, distrust extreme options with words like 'wholly' or 'entirely' unless the passage is equally extreme. Option c is right because the writer accepts that a significant result exists but doubts its strength and reliability, which is a cautiously sceptical stance.","traps":{"a":"'Wholly enthusiastic' would need unqualified praise, such as calling the treatment a breakthrough. The passage does the opposite: it concedes one positive point and then lists three weaknesses. The word 'wholly' makes the option extreme, and extreme tone words are usually wrong for qualified passages. It tempts candidates who stop reading after 'statistically significant reduction in symptoms'.","b":"'Dismissive' means rejecting something as unworthy of serious attention. The writer does not do that; they accept that the reduction was statistically significant and engage carefully with the evidence. Pointing out weaknesses is critical evaluation, not dismissal. It tempts because most of the passage is negative in content, and candidates confuse doubt about strength with outright rejection.","d":"'Indifferent' means having no interest or opinion. The writer is clearly engaged: they select specific weaknesses (effect size, dropout, replication) and weigh them against the positive result, which shows a definite evaluative stance. It tempts candidates who equate a calm, academic style with having no attitude. Neutral wording is not indifference; tone shows in what is emphasised."}},{"q":"Choose the correctly used word: 'The therapist gave a ___ to the patient's improvement.'","concept":"'Compliment' and 'complement' are homophones: they sound the same but differ in spelling and meaning. A compliment, spelled with an i, is an expression of praise or admiration ('She paid him a compliment'). It is also a verb: 'The supervisor complimented the trainee.' A complement, spelled with an e, is something that completes or goes well with something else ('The two therapies complement each other'). The e links it to 'complete', which is the standard memory aid; for the other, remember 'I like compliments'. The adjectives follow the same split. 'Complimentary' means praising and, by extension, free of charge ('complimentary breakfast'). 'Complementary' means completing or supplying what the other lacks: complementary colours, complementary angles that add up to 90 degrees, and complementary and alternative medicine. 'Complement' also has a fixed noun use meaning the full number needed: 'a full complement of staff'. In grammar, a subject complement is the word after a linking verb that completes the subject ('She is a psychologist'). Other confusable pairs tested the same way are principal and principle, stationary and stationery, discreet and discrete, elicit and illicit, and council and counsel. For each, fix one meaning with a link word and the other follows. In this sentence the therapist is expressing praise about the patient's progress, so the praise word is needed. The more idiomatic phrasing would be 'paid the patient a compliment on his improvement', but the keyed choice is still clear. Option d is right because 'compliment' means an expression of praise, which is what the therapist gave.","traps":{"a":"'Complimentation' is not a standard English word. The noun for praise is simply 'compliment', and the act is described with the verb 'compliment'. Adding a noun suffix to a word that is already a noun creates a fake form. It tempts because it looks formal and academic, and suffixes like -ation are common in psychology vocabulary (motivation, habituation).","b":"'Complement' with an e means something that completes or enhances something else ('Medication and therapy complement each other') or a full set ('a full complement of staff'). It carries no sense of praise, so 'gave a complement to the patient's improvement' does not say the therapist praised anything. It tempts because it sounds identical to the right word and fits the grammar slot.","c":"'Complement of' combines the wrong word with a broken structure. 'Gave a complement of to the patient's improvement' puts two prepositions together with no object for 'of', so it is ungrammatical as well as wrong in meaning. The phrase 'a complement of' works only before a noun ('a complement of ten nurses'). It tempts because it echoes that familiar set phrase."}},{"q":"Choose the sentence with correct article use:","concept":"English has three article choices: the indefinite 'a' or 'an', the definite 'the', and the zero article (no article). 'A' or 'an' introduces a singular countable noun that is one of many or not yet specific ('a psychologist'). 'The' points to something specific and identifiable to both speaker and listener ('the psychologist we met'). The zero article is used for general plural or uncountable nouns ('Psychologists value evidence'). A singular countable noun such as 'psychologist' almost never stands alone without a determiner. Several rules require 'the'. Superlatives (the best, the tallest, the most experienced) need 'the' because they pick out one unique member of a group. Ordinal numbers (the first session), 'only' and 'same' (the only option, the same test), and unique things (the sun, the internet) also take 'the'. Superlatives are usually followed by 'in' for places or groups ('the best in the city', 'the best in the team') and 'of' for a set ('the best of all the trainees'). Two exceptions help with variants. 'A most' can mean 'very' and is not a true superlative: 'It was a most interesting case.' And when no noun follows, 'the' may drop: 'This method is best for anxiety' or 'She works best alone.' Article omission is a common feature of learner English, which is why examiners test it. Option c is right because 'best' is a superlative before a singular countable noun, so it requires the definite article 'the' to mark one unique psychologist in the city.","traps":{"a":"'He is best psychologist in the city' drops the article before a superlative and a singular countable noun, and both features demand 'the'. The sentence is understandable, which is why it tempts: article omission is common in spoken learner English, and candidates hear it so often that it sounds acceptable, but formal grammar marks it wrong.","b":"'A best' pairs the indefinite article with a superlative. 'A' means one of many, not specified, while 'best' means the single top member, so the two contradict each other. The only similar correct pattern is 'a most' meaning 'very' ('a most skilled psychologist'), which is not a true superlative. It tempts because 'a' is the usual article for naming someone's profession.","d":"'He is best psychologist of the city' still omits 'the' before the superlative, and changing 'in' to 'of' does not repair it. With places, 'in' is the natural preposition after a superlative ('the best in the city'), while 'of' suits a set ('the best of the trainees'). It tempts because the changed preposition gives a look of correction while the real error remains."}},{"q":"Choose the meaning of the idiom 'to throw in the towel':","concept":"An idiom is a fixed expression whose meaning cannot be worked out from the meanings of its individual words. 'To throw in the towel' means to give up, surrender or admit defeat. It comes from boxing, where a fighter's corner throws a towel into the ring to stop the fight and concede. An older variant is 'to throw up the sponge', from the sponge used to wipe the boxer between rounds. Examiners usually set the figurative meaning against literal readings ('clean up', suggested by 'towel') and against opposite meanings ('begin a new effort'). Neighbouring idioms help with variants. Give up: throw in the sponge, call it quits, raise the white flag. Stop for now, without defeat: call it a day. Persist: hang in there, stick it out. Begin afresh: turn over a new leaf. Face something unpleasant bravely: bite the bullet. Ease initial awkwardness: break the ice. The idea of giving up matters in clinical psychology. Martin Seligman and Steven Maier (1967) found that dogs exposed to inescapable shock later failed to escape shock they could avoid, which they called learned helplessness, later used as a model of depression. In addiction work, G. Alan Marlatt and Judith Gordon (1985) described the abstinence violation effect, where a single slip leads a client to abandon recovery entirely; relapse prevention teaches clients not to throw in the towel after one lapse. Option d is right because 'to throw in the towel' means to surrender or give up, from the boxing practice of conceding a fight.","traps":{"a":"'To begin a new effort' is close to the opposite of the idiom; idioms with this meaning include 'turn over a new leaf', 'make a fresh start' and 'get the ball rolling'. Throwing in the towel ends an effort instead of starting one. It tempts because 'throw' suggests energetic starting, as in 'throw yourself into a task', and candidates who do not know the boxing origin may read it that way.","b":"'To make a joke' has no connection with the idiom. Idioms about joking include 'pull someone's leg' (tease), 'crack a joke' and 'tongue in cheek' (said ironically). Nothing in the boxing origin of 'throw in the towel' involves humour. It tempts only by elimination, when candidates who do not know the idiom guess that a light-sounding phrase must be playful.","c":"'To clean up after a session' is a literal reading suggested by the word 'towel'. Idioms are figurative by definition, so a literal guess built from one word in the phrase is usually wrong. It is the standard literal distractor in idiom questions. It tempts candidates who try to decode the idiom word by word instead of recalling it as a single unit."}},{"q":"Choose the antonym of 'TRANSPARENT':","concept":"An antonym is a word of opposite meaning. 'Transparent' comes from Latin 'trans' (through) and 'parere' (to appear): light passes through it so clearly that objects behind it can be seen, as with clear glass or still water. Materials fall on a three-step scale. Transparent: light passes through with little scattering, and you can see clearly through it. Translucent: light passes through but is scattered, so shapes behind it are blurred, as with frosted glass or tracing paper. Opaque: no light passes through, as with wood, metal or a brick wall. Since translucent lies in the middle, the true antonym of transparent is the other end of the scale, opaque. Gradable scales such as hot, warm, cool and cold work the same way: the true opposite of hot is cold, not cool. Both words also have figurative senses that appear in exams. A transparent process is open and easy to understand ('transparent reporting of research methods'), and a transparent excuse is easily seen through. Opaque, figuratively, means hard to understand or deliberately unclear ('an opaque explanation'). In psychology, the open science movement asks for transparent methods, shared data and preregistration, while machine-learning models whose reasoning cannot be inspected are described as opaque or black-box models. For antonym items, first remove synonyms of the stem word, then choose the extreme opposite, not an intermediate term. Option b is right because 'opaque' means letting no light through, the complete opposite of 'transparent', which lets light through clearly.","traps":{"a":"'Clear' is a synonym of 'transparent': clear glass and clear water let light through so you can see through them. Figuratively both can also mean easy to understand. Because it means nearly the same, it cannot be the opposite. It tempts candidates who scan for the first related word and forget that the question asks for an antonym, not a synonym.","c":"'Translucent' means letting light through but scattering it, so shapes behind are blurred, as with frosted glass or a lampshade. It is the midpoint between transparent and opaque, not the opposite end. It tempts because it looks and sounds like 'transparent', and because it is less see-through, so candidates treat a difference in degree as a true opposite.","d":"'Visible' means able to be seen. It describes whether an object can be seen, not whether a material lets light pass through, so it belongs to a different meaning area. Its own antonym is 'invisible'. In fact a perfectly transparent object can be hard to see. It tempts because 'see-through' and 'can be seen' sound closely related."}},{"q":"Choose the correctly punctuated sentence:","concept":"A conjunctive adverb is an adverb that links two complete ideas and shows how they relate: however and nevertheless (contrast), therefore, thus and consequently (result), moreover and furthermore (addition), meanwhile (time). The clause on each side is independent, meaning it could stand alone as a sentence. Because a conjunctive adverb is not a true conjunction, a comma alone cannot join the two clauses. The standard pattern is a semicolon before it and a comma after it: 'The session ended; however, the patient lingered.' A full stop also works: 'The session ended. However, the patient lingered.' Contrast this with the seven coordinating conjunctions, remembered as FANBOYS (for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so), which can join independent clauses with just a comma: 'The session ended, but the patient lingered.' Three errors are tested. A comma splice joins two independent clauses with only a comma ('The session ended, however the patient lingered'). A run-on or fused sentence joins them with no punctuation at all. A missing comma after 'however' can also change meaning, because 'however' without a comma means 'in whatever way' ('However the patient lingered, the clinic stayed open'). When 'however' falls inside one clause, set it off with a pair of commas: 'The patient, however, lingered.' A quick test: if you can move the word to another position in the second clause, it is a conjunctive adverb, not a conjunction. Option c is right because it puts a semicolon before the conjunctive adverb 'however', which joins two independent clauses, and a comma after it.","traps":{"a":"This version has the correct semicolon before 'however' but omits the comma after it. Without that comma, 'however the patient lingered' can be read as 'in whatever way the patient lingered', which changes the meaning. Standard punctuation sets off 'however' in its contrast sense with a comma. It tempts because the semicolon, the harder part of the rule, is already right.","b":"Using only a comma before 'however' to join two independent clauses creates a comma splice, one of the most tested punctuation errors. A comma can join independent clauses only together with a coordinating conjunction such as 'but' ('The session ended, but the patient lingered'). It tempts because 'however' feels like 'but', so writers punctuate it the same way.","d":"With no punctuation at all, the two independent clauses run together into a fused sentence, also called a run-on. It is also ambiguous, since 'ended however the patient lingered' could be read as 'ended in whatever way the patient lingered'. It needs a semicolon or full stop before 'however' and a comma after. It tempts because it reads smoothly when spoken aloud."}},{"q":"Identify the most concise rewrite: 'Due to the fact that the patient was anxious, she was unable to sleep.'","concept":"Concise writing expresses an idea in the fewest words needed without losing meaning or clarity. It is not brevity at any cost: the concise version must keep all the information of the original. William Strunk Jr. and E. B. White (1959) put the rule as 'Omit needless words' in The Elements of Style, and the Publication Manual of the American Psychological Association (7th edition, 2020) asks psychology writers to cut wordiness and redundancy. The commonest source of padding is a wordy phrase that stands in for a single word: 'due to the fact that', 'owing to the fact that', 'on account of the fact that' and 'in view of the fact that' all mean 'because' or 'since'; 'in spite of the fact that' means 'although'; 'at this point in time' means 'now'; 'in order to' means 'to'. Other sources are redundancy ('past history', 'end result', 'completely eliminate'), nominalisation ('make a decision' for 'decide') and unnecessary passive voice ('sleep was not possible' for 'she could not sleep'). To answer a conciseness item, first check that each option keeps the full meaning, then eliminate any that keep a wordy phrase or add passive voice, then choose the shortest grammatical survivor. The original here contains two facts (she was anxious; she could not sleep) and a causal link between them. Option c is right because 'Because she was anxious, the patient could not sleep' keeps all of that meaning while replacing the padded opener with the single word 'because'.","traps":{"a":"'Owing to the fact that' is just another wordy phrase meaning 'because', so this rewrite swaps one padded opener for an equally padded one. It is grammatical and keeps the meaning, but it is barely shorter than the original. It tempts because 'owing to' sounds formal and different, and candidates notice the change without counting the words saved.","b":"'On account of the fact that' is even longer than the original opener, and the ending 'sleep was not possible' turns the patient's experience into an impersonal statement, weakening the link to her. It is wordier, not more concise. It tempts because it sounds formal and clinical, and candidates sometimes confuse a formal tone with good writing.","d":"'Sleep was unable to be achieved by her' is an awkward passive construction that adds words and is also illogical, because 'unable' describes a person, not sleep. The semicolon and 'therefore' are correctly used, which gives the option a polished look, but the second clause is bloated. It tempts candidates who equate complex structure with better writing."}},{"q":"Choose the word meaning 'speaking little; reserved':","concept":"This item tests a vocabulary cluster about how much a person talks. 'Taciturn' means habitually silent, saying little and reserved in speech. It comes from Latin 'tacitus' (silent), the same root as 'tacit', meaning understood without being stated ('tacit agreement', 'tacit knowledge'). The opposite end of the scale has several words. 'Loquacious' comes from Latin 'loqui' (to speak), the root of eloquent, colloquial, soliloquy and circumlocution. 'Garrulous' means excessively talkative, often about trivial matters, and tends to be critical. 'Voluble' means speaking easily, rapidly and at length, from Latin 'volvere' (to roll), as if words roll out. Related words on the quiet side are reticent (unwilling to reveal thoughts), laconic (using few words), reserved and uncommunicative. On the talkative side are verbose, prolix and chatty. These words are useful in mental status examinations. Reduced talk can reflect poverty of speech or alogia, a negative symptom of schizophrenia, or the slowed, sparse speech of depression. Excessive talk includes the pressured speech of mania and logorrhoea. A taciturn person, however, may be entirely healthy, because taciturnity is a personality style, not a symptom. When three options are near-synonyms of each other and one stands apart, the odd one is often the answer, but always confirm by meaning, not by pattern alone. Option c is right because 'taciturn' means speaking little and being reserved, which matches the definition in the stem exactly.","traps":{"a":"'Loquacious' means very talkative, from Latin 'loqui', to speak (as in eloquent and colloquial). It describes someone who talks a lot and easily, the opposite of the reserved person in the stem. It tempts because it is a long, formal word that could sound like it means 'quiet', and candidates who do not know the root may guess.","b":"'Garrulous' means excessively talkative, especially about trivial or boring matters, and is often used critically ('a garrulous old neighbour'). It is the opposite of speaking little. It tempts because it is an uncommon word whose sound gives no clue to its meaning, so candidates unsure of it may treat it as a possible match.","d":"'Voluble' means speaking fluently, rapidly and at length, from Latin 'volvere', to roll, as though words roll out. It describes a ready flow of talk, the opposite of reserved. It tempts because it looks like 'volume' or 'voluntary', so candidates may guess it concerns loudness or choice instead of the amount a person talks."}},{"q":"Choose the sentence in correct active voice:","concept":"Voice tells you whether the subject of a sentence performs the action or receives it. In the active voice the subject is the doer: 'The team (doer) discussed (verb) the case (receiver).' In the passive voice the receiver becomes the subject and the doer moves into a 'by' phrase or disappears: 'The case was discussed (by the team).' The passive is formed with a form of 'be' plus the past participle, and 'be' carries the tense: is discussed (present), was discussed (past), is being discussed (present continuous), has been discussed (present perfect), will be discussed (future). Tense and voice are separate, so a passive sentence stays passive in every tense. Only transitive verbs, which take an object, can be made passive; 'The patient slept' has no passive form. Three quick checks identify the passive: a form of 'be' followed by a past participle; a 'by' phrase, or one that could be added; and a subject that receives the action. The passive is grammatically correct and useful when the doer is unknown or unimportant ('The scale was standardised on 500 adults'), which is why older lab reports used it heavily. The APA Publication Manual (7th edition, 2020) encourages the active voice for clearer, more direct writing ('We standardised the scale'). To convert passive to active, move the 'by' phrase to the front as the subject and turn 'be + participle' into an ordinary verb. Option a is right because in 'The team discussed the case' the subject, the team, performs the action, which defines the active voice.","traps":{"b":"'The case was discussed by the team' is the simple past passive: 'was' plus the past participle 'discussed', with the doer in a 'by' phrase. The subject 'the case' receives the action. It is perfectly grammatical, which is the trap: candidates may choose it because it sounds formal and correct, forgetting that the question asks about voice, not correctness.","c":"'The case has been discussed by the team' is the present perfect passive: 'has been' plus the past participle. The subject still receives the action and the doer appears in the 'by' phrase. It tempts because the change of tense makes it look different from the other passive options, and candidates confuse tense with voice. Every tense has both an active and a passive form.","d":"'The case is being discussed by the team' is the present continuous passive: 'is being' plus the past participle. The ongoing action does not make it active; the case is still the receiver. Its active equivalent is 'The team is discussing the case.' It tempts because the -ing form looks active and lively, but here the -ing belongs to 'being', the passive auxiliary."}},{"q":"Read the passage and answer: 'The author argues that early intervention prevents most cases of chronic psychosis. However, the studies cited used small samples and lacked control groups.' The author's main point about the cited studies is that they:","concept":"Reading comprehension items about an author's point ask what the passage actually does with its evidence, not what the topic makes you think. Signal words guide this. 'However', 'but', 'yet' and 'although' mark a contrast or qualification, so the sentence after them usually carries the real evaluative position. Here the first sentence reports a strong claim (early intervention prevents most cases of chronic psychosis), and 'However' introduces two flaws in the studies behind it. To judge such flaws, recall basic research design. Small samples reduce statistical power, which raises the risk of Type II errors and makes any significant effects unstable and often inflated. Without a control group, improvement cannot be credited to the intervention, because threats to internal validity such as history, maturation and regression to the mean, classified by Donald T. Campbell and Julian C. Stanley (1963), are not ruled out; some patients would also improve without treatment. Studies with these flaws do not prove the claim false; they simply cannot carry the weight placed on them. Examiners build options along a scale: confirm decisively (too strong), provide weak support (qualified), irrelevant (off-topic) and refute entirely (too strong in the other direction). The right answer usually matches the strength of the passage's own language. Note also the gap between 'most cases' in the claim and the modest evidence, a classic overgeneralisation. Option d is right because the passage criticises the studies' small samples and missing control groups, so they offer only weak methodological support for the argument.","traps":{"a":"'Confirm decisively' is the opposite of what the passage says. Small samples and missing control groups weaken evidence; they cannot make it decisive. Decisive confirmation would need large, controlled, replicated trials. It tempts candidates who read the strong claim in the first sentence ('prevents most cases'), take it as settled, and skip the qualifying 'However'.","b":"'Irrelevant' would mean the studies do not bear on the argument at all. They do: they are the very evidence cited for it, and they address the same question of early intervention and psychosis outcomes. Their problem is quality, not relevance. It tempts candidates who confuse weak evidence with no evidence, reasoning that flawed studies count for nothing.","c":"'Refute entirely' would require evidence that early intervention does not prevent chronic psychosis. The passage offers none; it only says the supporting studies are methodologically weak. Absence of strong support is not proof of the opposite. It tempts because criticism of studies feels like an attack on the argument, and the extreme word 'entirely' looks forceful and decisive."}}]});
